window.SUBJECT_DATA["pharma"]=[{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The branch of medical science and scientific study of the origin, nature, chemistry, effects and uses of drugs is known as:","options":["Pharmacognosy","Pharmacology","Toxicology","Pharmacovigilance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pharmacology is defined as the branch of medical science and scientific study of the origin, nature, chemistry, effects and uses of drugs.  Why A wrong: Pharmacognosy is the study of crude drugs from natural sources.  Why C wrong: Toxicology deals with poisons.  Why D wrong: Pharmacovigilance is the study of side effects of drugs."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The word \"Pharmacology\" is derived from which components?","options":["Pharma (drugs) and vigilance (side effects)","Pharmacon (Drugs) and Logos (study)","Pharmaco (drugs) and kinetics (movement)","Pharmaco (drugs) and dynamic (power)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pharmacology = Pharmacon (Drugs) → and Logos (study).  Why A wrong: That describes pharmacovigilance.  Why C wrong: That describes pharmacokinetics.  Why D wrong: That describes pharmacodynamics."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Who is regarded as the Father of Pharmacology?","options":["Oswald Schmiedeberg","Ramnath Chopra","Jonathan Pereira","Paul Ehrlich"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Jonathan Pereira is the Father of pharmacology.  Why A wrong: Oswald Schmiedeberg is the Father of modern pharmacology.  Why B wrong: Ramnath Chopra is the Father of Indian pharmacology.  Why D wrong: Paul Ehrlich is not listed as father of pharmacology on this page."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The Father of modern pharmacology is:","options":["Jonathan Pereira","Oswald Schmiedeberg","Ramnath Chopra","Claude Bernard"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oswald Schmiedeberg is the Father of modern pharmacology.  Why A wrong: Jonathan Pereira is the Father of pharmacology.  Why C wrong: Ramnath Chopra is the Father of Indian pharmacology.  Why D wrong: Not mentioned on the page."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Ramnath Chopra is known as the Father of:","options":["Modern pharmacology","Pharmacology","Indian pharmacology","Toxicology"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Ramnath Chopra is the Father of Indian pharmacology.  Why A wrong: That is Oswald Schmiedeberg.  Why B wrong: That is Jonathan Pereira.  Why D wrong: No such designation given."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The study of the mechanism of action of drugs and their pharmacological effects on the human body is called:","options":["Pharmacokinetics","Pharmacodynamics","Pharmacognosy","Pharmacovigilance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pharmacodynamics (\"dynamic\" means power) is the study of mechanism of action of drugs and their pharmacological effects on the human body.  Why A wrong: Pharmacokinetics is movement of drugs (ADME).  Why C wrong: Pharmacognosy studies crude natural drugs.  Why D wrong: Pharmacovigilance is study of side effects."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"In the term \"Pharmacodynamic\", the word \"dynamic\" means:","options":["Movement","Power","Side effect","Study"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In pharmacodynamic, \"pharmaco\" means drugs and \"dynamic\" means power.  Why A wrong: Movement refers to kinetics.  Why C wrong: Side effect refers to vigilance.  Why D wrong: Study refers to logos."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Pharmacokinetics is the study of which processes of drugs?","options":["Mechanism of action and receptor binding","Absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion","Side effects only","Origin and chemistry"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pharmacokinetics (kinetic = movement) is the study of absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion of drugs.  Why A wrong: That is pharmacodynamics.  Why C wrong: That is pharmacovigilance.  Why D wrong: That is part of the general pharmacology definition."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"In the term \"Pharmacokinetics\", the word \"kinetic\" means:","options":["Power","Movement","Side effect","Poison"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Pharmaco\" means drugs and \"kinetic\" means movements.  Why A wrong: Power = dynamic.  Why C wrong: Side effect = vigilance.  Why D wrong: Poison relates to toxicology."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The branch of pharmacology that studies the side effects of drugs/medicines is:","options":["Pharmacognosy","Pharmacovigilance","Pharmacokinetics","Pharmacodynamics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pharmacovigilance (\"vigilance\" means side effects) is the study of side effects of drugs/medicines.  Why A wrong: Pharmacognosy studies natural crude drugs.  Why C wrong: Kinetics = drug movement.  Why D wrong: Dynamics = mechanism of action."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The study of medicines or crude drugs produced from natural sources such as plants, microbes and animals is:","options":["Pharmacovigilance","Pharmacognosy","Toxicology","Pharmacokinetics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pharmacognosy is the study of medicines/crude drugs from natural sources (plants, microbes, animals) including analysis of their biological, chemical, biochemical and physical properties.  Why A wrong: Pharmacovigilance is study of side effects.  Why C wrong: Toxicology deals with poisons.  Why D wrong: Kinetics is drug movement."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which of the following are studied under pharmacokinetic genetic polymorphisms? (Select all that apply)","options":["Absorption","Distribution","Receptors","Metabolism","Excretion"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Under genetic polymorphisms, the pharmacokinetic branch includes Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism and Excretion.  Why C wrong: Receptors fall under the pharmacodynamic branch (along with ion channels, enzymes, immune system)."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"In the genetic polymorphisms diagram, which components belong to the pharmacodynamic branch?","options":["Absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion","Receptors, ion channels, enzymes, immune system","Onset, peak, duration","Affinity, efficacy, potency"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pharmacodynamic branch of genetic polymorphisms includes Receptors, Ion Channels, Enzymes and Immune System.  Why A wrong: These are pharmacokinetic components.  Why C wrong: Onset/peak/duration are drug time-course terms, not the diagram branches.  Why D wrong: Not the diagram categories."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which term is defined as \"any substance taken exogenously that can affect the normal physiology of the body, which may be therapeutic and non-therapeutic\"?","options":["Medicine","Drug","Antidote","Agonist"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the glossary, drugs are any exogenous substance that can affect normal body physiology, therapeutic or non-therapeutic.  Why A wrong: Medicine is used for therapeutic purpose with minimum side effect.  Why C wrong: Antidote counteracts poisoning.  Why D wrong: Agonist activates a receptor."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Any substance used for therapeutic purpose with minimum side effect is called:","options":["Drug","Medicine","Toxin","Antidote"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Medicine is defined as any substance used for therapeutic use with minimum side effect.  Why A wrong: Drug is broader and may be non-therapeutic.  Why C wrong: Toxin is a poison.  Why D wrong: Antidote counteracts poisoning."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The branch of pharmacology dealing with poisonous substances, their effects and manner of prevention is:","options":["Pharmacognosy","Toxicology","Pharmacovigilance","Biotransformation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Toxicology is the branch of pharmacology dealing with poison substances, their effects and manner of prevention.  Why A wrong: Pharmacognosy studies natural crude drugs.  Why C wrong: Pharmacovigilance studies side effects.  Why D wrong: Biotransformation is drug modification/removal."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The rate and extent to which the active ingredient is absorbed from a drug product and becomes available at the site of action (fraction reaching systemic circulation in unchanged form) is called:","options":["Potency","Bioavailability","Efficacy","Affinity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bioavailability is the rate and extent to which the active ingredient/moiety is absorbed and becomes available at the site of action / fraction reaching systemic circulation unchanged.  Why A wrong: Potency is the dose required to produce a specific intensity of effect.  Why C wrong: Efficacy is the maximum effect a drug is capable of.  Why D wrong: Affinity is strength of drug-receptor binding."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The process by which drugs are either modified from one chemical to another or get easily removed from the body is:","options":["Excretion","Biotransformation","Distribution","Absorption"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Biotransformation is the process by which drugs are modified from one chemical to another or get easily removed from the body.  Why A wrong: Excretion is the final elimination of byproducts/metabolites.  Why C wrong: Distribution is dispersal throughout the body.  Why D wrong: Absorption is entry into circulation."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"A drug that activates a receptor to produce a biological effect similar to that of the physiological molecule is called a/an:","options":["Antagonist","Agonist","Antidote","Partial blocker"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Agonists are drugs which activate a receptor to produce a similar biological effect to that of the physiological molecule.  Why A wrong: Antagonists oppose the action of receptor or agonists.  Why C wrong: Antidote counteracts poisoning.  Why D wrong: Not the defined term."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Drugs which oppose the action of the receptor or of agonists are called:","options":["Agonists","Antagonists","Antidotes","Enzymes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Antagonists are drugs which oppose the action of the receptor or agonists.  Why A wrong: Agonists activate receptors.  Why C wrong: Antidote counteracts poisoning.  Why D wrong: Enzymes are biological catalysts, not the definition."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"A substance that can counteract a form of poisoning is called a/an:","options":["Agonist","Antidote","Antagonist","Placebo"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Antidote is a substance that can counteract a form of poisoning.  Why A wrong: Agonist activates a receptor.  Why C wrong: Antagonist opposes receptors/agonists but is not specifically for poisoning.  Why D wrong: Placebo is an inactive substance, not defined here."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The ability of a drug to produce a harmful effect on the fetus when administered during pregnancy is called:","options":["Teratogenicity","Bioavailability","Toxicity","Idiosyncrasy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Teratogenicity is the ability of a drug to produce harmful effect on the fetus if administered during pregnancy.  Why B wrong: Bioavailability is fraction reaching circulation.  Why C wrong: Toxicity is a general poisonous effect.  Why D wrong: Idiosyncrasy is not defined on the page."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"\"A mixture of two or more liquids that are normally immiscible, e.g. mixture of water and oil\" defines which term? (Milk is a natural example)","options":["Suspension","Emulsion","Solution","Colloid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Emulsion is a mixture of two or more normally immiscible liquids (e.g., water and oil); milk is a natural emulsion.  Why A wrong: Suspension is not defined here.  Why C wrong: Solution is a homogeneous mixture.  Why D wrong: Colloid is not the given definition."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"An inhibitory mechanism where one drug blocks or reduces the effect of another drug is termed:","options":["Synergism","Antagonism","Potentiation","Additive effect"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Antagonism is an inhibitory mechanism where one drug blocks or reduces the effect of another drug.  Why A wrong: Synergism enhances effects.  Why C wrong: Potentiation increases effect of another drug.  Why D wrong: Additive effect is sum of effects."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The first stage of pharmacokinetics, in which medications enter the body and travel from the site of administration into the body's circulation, is:","options":["Distribution","Absorption","Metabolism","Excretion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Absorption is the first stage of pharmacokinetics — medications enter the body and travel from the administration site into circulation.  Why A wrong: Distribution is the second stage.  Why C wrong: Metabolism is drug breakdown.  Why D wrong: Excretion is the final stage."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"An unintended and potentially dangerous pharmacological effect occurring when a medication is administered correctly is termed a/an:","options":["Side effect","Adverse effect","Idiosyncratic reaction","Allergy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Adverse effect is an unintended and potentially dangerous pharmacological effect occurring even when the medication is administered correctly.  Why A wrong: Side effect is an effect other than the desired one, sometimes in another organ.  Why C wrong/D wrong: Not the defined terms here."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The strength of binding between a drug and its receptor is called:","options":["Efficacy","Affinity","Potency","Bioavailability"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Affinity is the strength of binding between drug and receptor.  Why A wrong: Efficacy is the maximum effect a drug can produce.  Why C wrong: Potency is dose required for a specific intensity of effect.  Why D wrong: Bioavailability is fraction reaching circulation."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The nearly impenetrable barricade built from a tightly woven mesh of capillaries that protects the brain from poisons and viruses is the:","options":["Meninges","Blood-Brain Barrier","Placental barrier","Choroid plexus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Blood-Brain Barrier is a nearly impenetrable barricade from a tightly woven mesh of capillaries protecting the brain from poisons and viruses.  Why A wrong: Meninges are protective membranes, not the defined term.  Why C wrong: Placental barrier protects the fetus.  Why D wrong: Choroid plexus produces CSF."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The second stage of pharmacokinetics, the process by which medication is distributed throughout the body, is:","options":["Absorption","Distribution","Metabolism","Excretion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Distribution is the second stage of pharmacokinetics — the process by which medication is distributed throughout the body.  Why A wrong: Absorption is the first stage.  Why C wrong: Metabolism is drug breakdown.  Why D wrong: Excretion is the final stage."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"According to the Dose-Response concept, as the dose of a drug increases:","options":["The response should decrease","The response should also increase","The response remains unchanged","The response becomes unpredictable"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In dose-response, as the dose increases the response should also increase; the slope of the curve is characteristic of the drug-receptor interaction.  Why A/C/D wrong: They contradict the defined direct dose-response relationship."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The length of time that a medication is producing its desired therapeutic effect is called:","options":["Onset","Peak","Duration","Half-life"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Duration is the length of time that a medication is producing its desired therapeutic effect.  Why A wrong: Onset is when a medication first begins to work.  Why B wrong: Peak is when maximum concentration is in the blood.  Why D wrong: Half-life is not defined on this page."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The maximum effect of which a drug is capable is termed its:","options":["Potency","Efficacy","Affinity","Bioavailability"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Efficacy is the maximum effect of which the drug is capable.  Why A wrong: Potency is dose required to produce a specific intensity of effect.  Why C wrong: Affinity is strength of drug-receptor binding.  Why D wrong: Bioavailability is fraction reaching circulation."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The final stage of pharmacokinetics, the process whereby drug byproducts and metabolites are eliminated from the body, is:","options":["Metabolism","Excretion","Distribution","Absorption"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Excretion is the final stage of pharmacokinetics — elimination of drug byproducts and metabolites from the body.  Why A wrong: Metabolism is drug breakdown.  Why C wrong: Distribution is the second stage.  Why D wrong: Absorption is the first stage."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The inactivation of orally or enterally administered drugs in the liver and intestines is known as:","options":["Bioavailability","First Pass Effect","Enterohepatic circulation","Biotransformation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: First Pass Effect is the inactivation of orally/enterally administered drugs in the liver and intestines.  Why A wrong: Bioavailability is fraction reaching circulation.  Why C wrong: Enterohepatic circulation is not defined here.  Why D wrong: Biotransformation is chemical modification/removal generally."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"\"How a medication works at a cellular level within the body\" defines:","options":["Mechanism of Action","Metabolism","Potency","Pharmacognosy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mechanism of Action is how a medication works at a cellular level within the body.  Why B wrong: Metabolism is drug breakdown via enzymes.  Why C wrong: Potency is dose for a given intensity of effect.  Why D wrong: Pharmacognosy is study of natural crude drugs."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Metabolism refers to the breakdown of a drug molecule via enzymes, occurring primarily in the:","options":["Kidney","Liver","Lungs","Stomach"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Metabolism is the breakdown of a drug molecule via enzymes in the liver (primarily) or intestines (secondarily).  Why A wrong: Kidney is primarily excretion.  Why C wrong: Lungs are not the primary metabolic site.  Why D wrong: Stomach is a site of absorption, not primary metabolism."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The term describing when a medication first begins to work and exerts a therapeutic effect is:","options":["Peak","Onset","Duration","Potency"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Onset is when a medication first begins to work and exerts a therapeutic effect.  Why A wrong: Peak is maximum concentration in the bloodstream.  Why C wrong: Duration is length of therapeutic effect.  Why D wrong: Potency relates to dose."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"\"When the maximum concentration of a drug is in the bloodstream\" defines:","options":["Onset","Peak","Trough","Duration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Peak is when the maximum concentration of a drug is in the bloodstream.  Why A wrong: Onset is when it begins to work.  Why C wrong: Trough is not defined here.  Why D wrong: Duration is length of effect."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug dose required to produce a specific intensity of effect is called:","options":["Efficacy","Potency","Affinity","Bioavailability"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Potency is the drug dose required to produce a specific intensity of effect.  Why A wrong: Efficacy is the maximum capable effect.  Why C wrong: Affinity is binding strength.  Why D wrong: Bioavailability is fraction reaching circulation."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"An effect of a drug other than the desired effect, sometimes in an organ other than the target organ, is a/an:","options":["Adverse effect","Side effect","Toxic effect","Allergic reaction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Side Effect is an effect of a drug other than the desired effect, sometimes in an organ other than the target organ.  Why A wrong: Adverse effect is unintended and potentially dangerous even when given correctly.  Why C/D wrong: Not the defined term."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which route of administration has, by definition, 100% bioavailability and the most rapid onset?","options":["Intramuscular (IM)","Oral (PO)","Intravenous (IV)","Subcutaneous (SC)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Intravenous (IV) route has 100% bioavailability (by definition) and the most rapid onset.  Why A wrong: IM has 75 to <100% bioavailability.  Why B wrong: Oral has 5 to <100% due to first-pass effect.  Why D wrong: SC has 75 to <100%."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"What is the bioavailability range of the intramuscular (IM) route?","options":["100%","75 to <100%","5 to <100%","30 to <100%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IM route has 75 to <100% bioavailability; large volumes often feasible but may be painful.  Why A wrong: 100% is IV only.  Why C wrong: 5 to <100% is oral/inhalation.  Why D wrong: 30 to <100% is rectal."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The oral (PO) route has which characteristic per the bioavailability table?","options":["Most rapid onset","Most convenient, but first pass effect may be important (bioavailability 5 to <100%)","Bioavailability of 100% by definition","Very slow absorption with no first-pass effect"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oral (PO) is most convenient with bioavailability 5 to <100%, and first pass effect may be important.  Why A wrong: Most rapid onset is IV.  Why C wrong: 100% is IV.  Why D wrong: Very slow absorption/no first-pass is transdermal."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The rectal (PR) route has a bioavailability range of ______ and is characterized by:","options":["30 to <100%; less first-pass effect than oral","100%; most rapid onset","5 to <100%; often very rapid onset","80 to ≤100%; very slow absorption"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rectal (PR) route has 30 to <100% bioavailability and less first-pass effect than oral.  Why B wrong: That describes IV.  Why C wrong: That describes inhalation.  Why D wrong: That describes transdermal."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which route is characterized by \"often very rapid onset\" with bioavailability of 5 to <100%?","options":["Rectal","Inhalation","Transdermal","Subcutaneous"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inhalation has 5 to <100% bioavailability and often very rapid onset.  Why A wrong: Rectal is 30 to <100%, less first-pass effect.  Why C wrong: Transdermal is 80 to ≤100%, very slow.  Why D wrong: SC is 75 to <100%."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The transdermal route has a bioavailability of 80 to ≤100% and is characterized by:","options":["Most rapid onset","Usually very slow absorption, used for lack of first-pass effect, with prolonged duration of action","Painful large volumes","Significant first-pass metabolism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Transdermal is usually very slow absorption, used for lack of first-pass effect and prolonged duration action, bioavailability 80 to ≤100%.  Why A wrong: Rapid onset describes IV.  Why C wrong: Large painful volumes describes IM.  Why D wrong: It is used to avoid first-pass, not cause it."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"According to the \"Should Remember\" box, 100% bioavailability is usually assured only for which route of medication?","options":["Intramuscular","Subcutaneous","Intravenous","Oral"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Usually 100% bioavailability is assured only for IV medications, not for IM or SC routes, because IV directly reaches the blood circulation.  Why A/B/D wrong: These routes do not guarantee 100% bioavailability."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"According to the \"Should Remember\" box, which organ is the major drug-metabolising organ in the human body?","options":["Kidney","Liver","Lungs","Spleen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The liver is a major drugs-metabolising organ in the human body.  Why A wrong: Kidney is mainly for excretion.  Why C/D wrong: Not the major metabolising organ."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Penicillins, cephalosporins, aminoglycosides and fluoroquinolones share which type of antibiotic action?","options":["Bacteriostatic","Bactericidal","Antifungal","Antiviral"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the drug-group table, penicillins, cephalosporins, aminoglycosides and fluoroquinolones are all bactericidal.  Why A wrong: Sulfonamides, chloramphenicol and tetracyclines are bacteriostatic.  Why C/D wrong: These are antibacterial groups."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"What is the mode of action of penicillins?","options":["Inhibit protein synthesis","Inhibit cell wall synthesis","Inhibit DNA replication","Inhibit folic acid synthesis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Penicillins are bactericidal and inhibit cell wall synthesis.  Why A wrong: Aminoglycosides/macrolides/tetracyclines inhibit protein synthesis.  Why C wrong: Fluoroquinolones inhibit DNA replication.  Why D wrong: Sulfonamides inhibit folic acid synthesis."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"How long does the action of Benzyl penicillin-G last?","options":["4-6 hours","12-24 hours","1 week","48 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Benzyl penicillin-G action lasts for 4-6 hrs.  Why B wrong: Procaine penicillin-G lasts 12-24 hr.  Why C wrong: Benzathine penicillin (penidura) is given one injection in a week.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The action of Procaine penicillin-G lasts for approximately:","options":["4-6 hours","12-24 hours","1 week","30 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Procaine penicillin-G action lasts for 12-24 hr.  Why A wrong: 4-6 hrs is Benzyl penicillin-G.  Why C wrong: 1 week (one injection) is Benzathine penicillin.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Benzathine penicillin (penidura) is typically administered:","options":["Every 4-6 hours","Every 12-24 hours","One injection in a week","Continuously by IV infusion"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Benzathine penicillin (penidura) is one injection in a week.  Why A wrong: 4-6 hrs is Benzyl penicillin-G.  Why B wrong: 12-24 hr is Procaine penicillin-G.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which of the following are classified as synthetic penicillins?","options":["Benzyl penicillin-G, Procaine penicillin-G","Methicillin sodium, cloxacillin sodium, oxacillin sodium","Ampicillin, carbenicillin","Amoxycillin, phenoxymethyl penicillin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Synthetic penicillins include Methicillin sodium, cloxacillin sodium and oxacillin sodium.  Why A wrong: These are natural penicillins.  Why C wrong: Ampicillin and carbenicillin are broad spectrum penicillins.  Why D wrong: Phenoxymethyl penicillin and amoxycillin are semisynthetic penicillins."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Ampicillin and carbenicillin are examples of which penicillin group?","options":["Natural penicillins","Semisynthetic penicillin","Synthetic penicillin","Broad spectrum penicillin"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Ampicillin and carbenicillin are broad spectrum penicillins.  Why A wrong: Natural penicillins are benzyl/procaine/benzathine penicillin.  Why B wrong: Semisynthetic are phenoxymethyl penicillin, amoxycillin.  Why C wrong: Synthetic are methicillin/cloxacillin/oxacillin sodium."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which is the MOST common adverse effect of penicillins?","options":["Nephrotoxicity","Hypersensitivity","Ototoxicity","Tooth discoloration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For penicillins, hypersensitivity is the most common adverse effect (also skin rashes, anaphylaxis shock).  Why A wrong: Nephrotoxicity is an adverse effect of cephalosporins/aminoglycosides.  Why C wrong: Ototoxicity is with aminoglycosides/fluoroquinolones.  Why D wrong: Tooth discoloration is with tetracyclines."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which of the following are listed adverse effects of penicillins? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hypersensitivity","Skin rashes","Anaphylaxis shock","Tooth discoloration","Ototoxicity"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Penicillins' adverse effects are hypersensitivity (most common), skin rashes and anaphylaxis shock.  Why D wrong: Tooth discoloration is with tetracyclines.  Why E wrong: Ototoxicity is with aminoglycosides/fluoroquinolones."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Cefazolin, cefadroxil and cephalexin belong to which generation of cephalosporins?","options":["First generation","Second generation","Third generation","Fourth generation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: First generation cephalosporins include cefazolin, cefadroxil and cephalexin.  Why B wrong: Second gen: cefuroxime, cefotetan, cefoxitin.  Why C wrong: Third gen: cefotaxime, ceftriaxone, cefexime, ceftazidime.  Why D wrong: Fourth gen: cefpirome, cefepime."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Cefuroxime, cefotetan and cefoxitin are examples of which cephalosporin generation?","options":["First generation","Second generation","Third generation","Fourth generation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Second generation cephalosporins include cefuroxime, cefotetan and cefoxitin.  Why A wrong: First gen: cefazolin, cefadroxil, cephalexin.  Why C wrong: Third gen: cefotaxime, ceftriaxone, cefexime, ceftazidime.  Why D wrong: Fourth gen: cefpirome, cefepime."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Cefotaxime, ceftriaxone, cefexime and ceftazidime belong to which cephalosporin generation?","options":["First generation","Second generation","Third generation","Fourth generation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Third generation cephalosporins include cefotaxime, ceftriaxone, cefexime and ceftazidime.  Why A/B wrong: First/second gen are listed differently.  Why D wrong: Fourth gen is cefpirome and cefepime."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Injection Cefpirome and injection Cefepime are examples of which cephalosporin generation?","options":["First generation","Second generation","Third generation","Fourth generation"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Fourth generation cephalosporins include inj. Cefpirome and inj. Cefepime.  Why A/B/C wrong: These belong to earlier generations as listed."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which adverse effects are associated with cephalosporins? (Select all that apply)","options":["Nephrotoxicity","Phlebitis","GI upset","Tooth discoloration","Optic neuritis"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Cephalosporins' adverse effects listed are nephrotoxicity, phlebitis and GI upset.  Why D wrong: Tooth discoloration is with tetracyclines.  Why E wrong: Optic neuritis is an ethambutol side effect (TB section)."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"What is the mode of action of aminoglycosides such as gentamycin and streptomycin?","options":["Inhibit cell wall synthesis","Inhibit protein synthesis","Inhibit DNA replication","Inhibit folic acid synthesis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aminoglycosides are bactericidal and inhibit protein synthesis.  Why A wrong: Penicillins/cephalosporins inhibit cell wall synthesis.  Why C wrong: Fluoroquinolones inhibit DNA replication.  Why D wrong: Sulfonamides inhibit folic acid synthesis."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which of the following is an aminoglycoside antibiotic?","options":["Ciprofloxacin","Gentamycin","Erythromycin","Sulphadiazine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aminoglycosides include Gentamycin, Amikacin, Kanamycin, Tobramycin, Neomycin, Sisomycin, Netilmycin and Streptomycin.  Why A wrong: Ciprofloxacin is a fluoroquinolone.  Why C wrong: Erythromycin is a macrolide.  Why D wrong: Sulphadiazine is a sulfonamide."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which adverse effects are associated with aminoglycosides?","options":["Nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity","Tooth discoloration","Hypersensitivity and skin rashes","Diarrhoea and GI upset"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Aminoglycosides cause nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity.  Why B wrong: Tooth discoloration is tetracyclines.  Why C wrong: Hypersensitivity/rashes are penicillins.  Why D wrong: Diarrhoea/GI upset are macrolides/chloramphenicol."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"What is the mode of action of fluoroquinolones such as ciprofloxacin and ofloxacin?","options":["Inhibit cell wall synthesis","Inhibit protein synthesis","Inhibit DNA replication","Inhibit folic acid synthesis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Fluoroquinolones are bactericidal and inhibit DNA replication.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are the mechanisms of other listed antibiotic classes."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which of the following is a fluoroquinolone antibiotic?","options":["Norfloxocin","Cefazolin","Tetracycline","Erythromycin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fluoroquinolones include Ciprofloxacin, Ofloxocin, Norfloxocin, Levofloxacin, Lomefloxacin and Nalidixic acid.  Why B wrong: Cefazolin is a cephalosporin.  Why C wrong: Tetracycline is a tetracycline.  Why D wrong: Erythromycin is a macrolide."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Sulfonamides such as sulphadiazine and sulfamethoxazole are bacteriostatic and act by:","options":["Inhibiting cell wall synthesis","Inhibiting protein synthesis","Inhibiting DNA replication","Inhibiting folic acid synthesis"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Sulfonamides are bacteriostatic and inhibit folic acid synthesis.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are mechanisms of other classes."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which of the following drugs is a sulfonamide?","options":["Sulfamethoxazole","Azithromycin","Doxycycline","Amikacin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sulfonamides include Sulphadiazine, sulfamerazine, Sulfamethoxazole, Sulfamethizole, Sulfacetamide sodium and sulfaguanidine.  Why B wrong: Azithromycin is a macrolide.  Why C wrong: Doxycycline is a tetracycline.  Why D wrong: Amikacin is an aminoglycoside."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Erythromycin, azithromycin and clarithromycin belong to which bactericidal antibiotic class that inhibits protein synthesis?","options":["Macrolides","Tetracyclines","Sulfonamides","Aminoglycosides"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Macrolides (bactericidal, inhibit protein synthesis) include Erythromycin, Azithromycin, Roxithromycin, Clindamycin, Lincomycin, Spiramycin, Spectinomycin and Vancomycin.  Why B wrong: Tetracyclines are bacteriostatic.  Why C wrong: Sulfonamides inhibit folic acid synthesis.  Why D wrong: Aminoglycosides are a separate class."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which adverse effects are associated with macrolides and chloramphenicol?","options":["Diarrhoea and GI upset","Nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity","Tooth discoloration","Anaphylaxis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Macrolides and chloramphenicol are associated with diarrhoea and GI upset.  Why B wrong: Nephro/ototoxicity are aminoglycosides.  Why C wrong: Tooth discoloration is tetracyclines.  Why D wrong: Anaphylaxis is penicillins."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Chloramphenicol is classified as which type of antibiotic?","options":["Bactericidal, inhibits cell wall synthesis","Bacteriostatic, inhibits protein synthesis","Bactericidal, inhibits DNA replication","Bacteriostatic, inhibits folic acid synthesis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chloramphenicol is bacteriostatic and inhibits protein synthesis.  Why A/C/D wrong: They describe other classes."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Tetracycline and doxycycline are bacteriostatic drugs that act by:","options":["Inhibiting protein synthesis","Inhibiting cell wall synthesis","Inhibiting DNA replication","Inhibiting folic acid synthesis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tetracyclines are bacteriostatic and inhibit protein synthesis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are mechanisms of other listed classes."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"A well-known adverse effect of tetracyclines in children is:","options":["Nephrotoxicity","Tooth discoloration and growth retardation","Persistent dry cough","Ototoxicity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tetracyclines cause tooth discoloration in children and growth retardation.  Why A wrong: Nephrotoxicity is aminoglycosides/cephalosporins.  Why C wrong: Dry cough is ACE inhibitors.  Why D wrong: Ototoxicity is aminoglycosides."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"What is the antidote for paracetamol poisoning?","options":["N-Acetylcysteine","Naloxone","Protamine sulphate","Flumazenil"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote for paracetamol poisoning is N-Acetylcysteine.  Why B wrong: Naloxone is for opioids.  Why C wrong: Protamine sulphate is for heparin.  Why D wrong: Flumazenil is for benzodiazepines."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The antidote for heparin overdose is:","options":["Vitamin K","Protamine sulphate","N-Acetylcysteine","Naloxone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Protamine sulphate is the antidote for heparin.  Why A wrong: Vitamin K + fresh blood is the antidote for warfarin.  Why C wrong: N-Acetylcysteine is for paracetamol.  Why D wrong: Naloxone is for opioids."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The antidote for warfarin toxicity is:","options":["Protamine sulphate","Vitamin K + fresh blood","Digibind","Atropine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Warfarin antidote is Vitamin K + fresh blood.  Why A wrong: Protamine sulphate is for heparin.  Why C wrong: Digibind is for digoxin.  Why D wrong: Atropine is for mushroom/organophosphate poisoning."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The antidote for digoxin toxicity is:","options":["Naloxone","Digibind or Digoxin immune Fab","Atropine","Calcium gluconate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Digoxin antidote is Digibind or Digoxin immune Fab.  Why A wrong: Naloxone is for opioids.  Why C wrong: Atropine is for mushroom/organophosphate/neostigmine.  Why D wrong: Calcium gluconate is for MgSO4/oxytocin."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The antidote for opioid (e.g. morphine, fentanyl) overdose is:","options":["Naloxone","Flumazenil","Protamine sulphate","Deferoxamine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Naloxone is the antidote for opioids such as morphine and fentanyl.  Why B wrong: Flumazenil is for benzodiazepines.  Why C wrong: Protamine sulphate is for heparin.  Why D wrong: Deferoxamine is for iron toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The antidote for oxytocin is:","options":["Inj. Calcium Gluconate 10%","Atropine","Naloxone","Glucose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote for oxytocin is Inj. Calcium Gluconate 10%.  Why B wrong: Atropine is for mushroom poisoning/OPS.  Why C wrong: Naloxone is for opioids.  Why D wrong: Glucose is for insulin overdose."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The antidote for mushroom poisoning is:","options":["Atropine","Naloxone","Hyperbaric oxygen","Calcium carbonate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote for mushroom poisoning is Atropine.  Why B wrong: Naloxone is for opioids.  Why C wrong: Hyperbaric oxygen is for CO poisoning.  Why D wrong: Calcium carbonate is for beta-blocker overdose."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The antidote/treatment for carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning is:","options":["Atropine","Hyperbaric oxygen","Calcium gluconate","Dimercaprol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CO poisoning is treated with Hyperbaric oxygen.  Why A wrong: Atropine is for mushroom/OPS.  Why C wrong: Calcium gluconate is for MgSO4/oxytocin.  Why D wrong: Dimercaprol is for iron/arsenic."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The antidote for magnesium sulphate (MgSO4) toxicity is:","options":["Calcium gluconate 10%","Protamine sulphate","Naloxone","Vitamin K"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote for MgSO4 is Calcium Gluconate 10%.  Why B wrong: Protamine sulphate is for heparin.  Why C wrong: Naloxone is for opioids.  Why D wrong: Vitamin K is for warfarin."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The antidote for beta-blocker overdose listed is:","options":["Calcium carbonate","Atropine","Glucose","Flumazenil"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote listed for B-blocker is Calcium carbonate.  Why B wrong: Atropine is for mushroom/OPS.  Why C wrong: Glucose is for insulin overdose.  Why D wrong: Flumazenil is for benzodiazepines."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The antidote for organophosphorous (OPS) poisoning is:","options":["Atropine","Dimercaprol","Sodium thiosulphate","Calcium disodium edentate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote for organophosphorous (OPS) poisoning is Atropine.  Why B wrong: Dimercaprol is for iron.  Why C wrong: Sodium thiosulphate is for iodine.  Why D wrong: Calcium disodium edentate is for lead poisoning."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The antidote for lead poisoning is:","options":["Calcium disodium edentate","Dimercaprol","Deferoxamine","Atropine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote for lead poisoning is Calcium disodium edentate.  Why B wrong: Dimercaprol is for iron.  Why C wrong: Deferoxamine is for iron toxicity.  Why D wrong: Atropine is for mushroom/OPS."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The antidote for iron poisoning is:","options":["Dimercaprol","Deferoxamine","Calcium gluconate","Glucose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote listed for Iron (poisoning) is Dimercaprol; for iron toxicity Deferoxamine is used.  Why B wrong: Deferoxamine is listed specifically for \"Iron toxicity\" while Dimercaprol matches \"Iron.\"  Why C wrong: Calcium gluconate is for MgSO4/oxytocin.  Why D wrong: Glucose is for insulin."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The antidote for neostigmine bromide toxicity is:","options":["Atropine sulphate","Glucose","Sodium thiosulphate","Flumazenil"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote for neostigmine bromide is Atropine sulphate.  Why B wrong: Glucose is for insulin.  Why C wrong: Sodium thiosulphate is for iodine.  Why D wrong: Flumazenil is for benzodiazepines."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The antidote for insulin overdose (hypoglycemia) is:","options":["Glucose","Atropine","Naloxone","Calcium gluconate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote for insulin is Glucose.  Why B wrong: Atropine is for mushroom/OPS.  Why C wrong: Naloxone is for opioids.  Why D wrong: Calcium gluconate is for MgSO4/oxytocin."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The antidote for iodine poisoning is:","options":["Sodium thiosulphate","Deferoxamine","Dimercaprol","Carbimazole"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote for Iodine is Sodium thiosulphate.  Why B wrong: Deferoxamine is for iron toxicity.  Why C wrong: Dimercaprol is for iron/arsenic.  Why D wrong: Carbimazole is for thyroid toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"According to the antidotes table, the antidote for aspirin overdose is:","options":["Furosemide + Vit. K with sodium bicarbonate","N-Acetylcysteine","Naloxone","Protamine sulphate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the aspirin antidote as Furosemide + Vit. K with sodium bicarbonate.  Why B wrong: N-Acetylcysteine is for paracetamol.  Why C wrong: Naloxone is for opioids.  Why D wrong: Protamine sulphate is for heparin."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The antidote for iron toxicity is:","options":["Deferoxamine","Dimercaprol","Calcium disodium edentate","Carbimazole"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote for iron toxicity is Deferoxamine.  Why B wrong: Dimercaprol is listed for iron/arsenic.  Why C wrong: Calcium disodium edentate is for lead.  Why D wrong: Carbimazole is for thyroid toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The antidote for thyroid toxicity is:","options":["Carbimazole","Sodium thiosulphate","Dimercaprol","Glucose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote for thyroid toxicity is Carbimazole.  Why B wrong: Sodium thiosulphate is for iodine.  Why C wrong: Dimercaprol is for iron/arsenic.  Why D wrong: Glucose is for insulin."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The antidote/treatment for snake bite is:","options":["ASV (Anti-Snake Venom)","Dimercaprol","Atropine","Flumazenil"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The treatment for snake bite is ASV (Anti-Snake Venom).  Why B wrong: Dimercaprol is for iron/arsenic.  Why C wrong: Atropine is for mushroom/OPS.  Why D wrong: Flumazenil is for benzodiazepines."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"According to the antidotes table, hypokalaemia is managed with:","options":["Insulin+dextrose/NaHCO3/calcium gluconate","Aminocaproic acid","Dimercaprol","Flumazenil"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists hypokalaemia management as Insulin+dextrose/NaHCO3/calcium gluconate. (Note: this combination is classically used for hyperkalaemia; as printed, it is keyed to hypokalaemia in the source.)  Why B wrong: Aminocaproic acid is for thrombolytic reversal.  Why C wrong: Dimercaprol is for iron/arsenic.  Why D wrong: Flumazenil is for benzodiazepines."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The antidote used to reverse a thrombolytic agent is:","options":["Aminocaproic acid","Protamine sulphate","Vitamin K","Deferoxamine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote for thrombolytic is Aminocaproic acid.  Why B wrong: Protamine sulphate is for heparin.  Why C wrong: Vitamin K is for warfarin.  Why D wrong: Deferoxamine is for iron toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The antidote for arsenic poisoning is:","options":["Dimercaprol (Dimercapol)","Sodium thiosulphate","Atropine","Naloxone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote for arsenic is Dimercapol (Dimercaprol).  Why B wrong: Sodium thiosulphate is for iodine.  Why C wrong: Atropine is for mushroom/OPS.  Why D wrong: Naloxone is for opioids."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The antidote for benzodiazepine overdose is:","options":["Flumazenil","Naloxone","Atropine","Protamine sulphate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote for benzodiazepine is Flumazenil.  Why B wrong: Naloxone is for opioids.  Why C wrong: Atropine is for mushroom/OPS.  Why D wrong: Protamine sulphate is for heparin."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice for fever is:","options":["Paracetamol","Aspirin","Morphine","Adrenaline"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for fever is Paracetamol.  Why B wrong: Aspirin is prophylaxis of MI.  Why C wrong: Morphine is for myocardial infarction.  Why D wrong: Adrenaline is for anaphylactic shock."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice for anaphylactic shock is:","options":["Adrenaline","Dopamine","Ondansetron","Morphine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for anaphylactic shock is Adrenaline.  Why B wrong: Dopamine is for cardiogenic shock.  Why C wrong: Ondansetron is for chemotherapy-induced vomiting.  Why D wrong: Morphine is for MI."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice for myocardial infarction (pain relief) is:","options":["Morphine","Aspirin","NTG","Adrenaline"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for myocardial infarction is Morphine.  Why B wrong: Aspirin is for prophylaxis of MI.  Why C wrong: NTG is for angina.  Why D wrong: Adrenaline is for anaphylactic shock."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice for prophylaxis of myocardial infarction is:","options":["Aspirin","Morphine","Digoxin","Dopamine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for prophylaxis of MI is Aspirin.  Why B wrong: Morphine is for acute MI.  Why C wrong: Digoxin is for heart failure.  Why D wrong: Dopamine is for cardiogenic shock."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice to reduce increased intracranial pressure (ICP) is:","options":["Mannitol (Osmotic Diuretic)","MgSO4","Furosemide","Dopamine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For increased ICP, the drug of choice is Mannitol (an osmotic diuretic).  Why B wrong: MgSO4 is for eclampsia.  Why C wrong: Furosemide is a loop diuretic (not the listed DOC for ICP here).  Why D wrong: Dopamine is for cardiogenic shock."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice for eclampsia is:","options":["MgSO4 (Magnesium Sulphate)","Mannitol","Lithium","Ceftriaxone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for eclampsia is MgSO4 (Magnesium Sulphate).  Why B wrong: Mannitol is for increased ICP.  Why C wrong: Lithium is for mania.  Why D wrong: Ceftriaxone is for typhoid/PID."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice for typhoid is:","options":["Ceftriaxone","Tetracycline","Metronidazole","Albendazole"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for typhoid is Ceftriaxone.  Why B wrong: Tetracycline is for cholera.  Why C wrong: Metronidazole is for bacterial vaginosis/trichomoniasis.  Why D wrong: Albendazole is for worm infestation."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice for heart failure is:","options":["Digoxin","NTG","Dopamine","Morphine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for heart failure is Digoxin.  Why B wrong: NTG is for angina.  Why C wrong: Dopamine is for cardiogenic shock.  Why D wrong: Morphine is for MI."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice for angina is:","options":["NTG (Nitroglycerin)","Digoxin","Aspirin","Dopamine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for angina is NTG.  Why B wrong: Digoxin is for heart failure.  Why C wrong: Aspirin is prophylaxis of MI.  Why D wrong: Dopamine is for cardiogenic shock."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice for cardiogenic shock is:","options":["Dopamine","Adrenaline","Digoxin","Morphine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for cardiogenic shock is Dopamine.  Why B wrong: Adrenaline is for anaphylactic shock.  Why C wrong: Digoxin is for heart failure.  Why D wrong: Morphine is for MI."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice for chemotherapy-induced vomiting is:","options":["Ondansetron","Metoclopramide","Lithium","Fluoxetine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for chemotherapy induced vomiting is Ondansetron.  Why B wrong: Metoclopramide is not the listed choice.  Why C wrong: Lithium is for mania.  Why D wrong: Fluoxetine is for phobia/depression/anxiety disorders."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice for cholera is:","options":["Tetracycline","Ceftriaxone","Metronidazole","Azithromycin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for cholera is Tetracycline.  Why B wrong: Ceftriaxone is for typhoid/PID.  Why C wrong: Metronidazole is for bacterial vaginosis/trichomoniasis.  Why D wrong: Azithromycin is for chlamydia/chancroid."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drugs of choice for bed wetting (enuresis) are:","options":["Imipramine and Desmopressin","Lithium","Chlordiazepoxide","Fluoxetine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For bed wetting (enuresis), the drugs of choice are Imipramine and Desmopression (Desmopressin).  Why B wrong: Lithium is for mania.  Why C wrong: Chlordiazepoxide is for alcohol withdrawal.  Why D wrong: Fluoxetine is for phobia/depression."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice for mania is:","options":["Lithium","Fluoxetine","Chlordiazepoxide","Imipramine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for mania is Lithium.  Why B wrong: Fluoxetine is for phobia/depression/anorexia/bulimia/OCD.  Why C wrong: Chlordiazepoxide is for alcohol withdrawal.  Why D wrong: Imipramine is for enuresis."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice for phobia, depression, anorexia nervosa, bulimia nervosa and OCD is:","options":["Fluoxetine","Lithium","Chlordiazepoxide","Oseltamivir"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For phobia/depression/anorexia nervosa/bulimia nervosa/OCD, the drug of choice is Fluoxetine (printed as \"Fluexitime\").  Why B wrong: Lithium is for mania.  Why C wrong: Chlordiazepoxide is for alcohol withdrawal.  Why D wrong: Oseltamivir is for swine flu."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice for alcohol withdrawal symptoms is:","options":["Chlordiazepoxide","Lithium","Fluoxetine","Naloxone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for alcohol withdrawal symptoms is Chlordiazepoxide.  Why B wrong: Lithium is for mania.  Why C wrong: Fluoxetine is for depression/OCD.  Why D wrong: Naloxone is an opioid antidote."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice for swine flu is:","options":["Oseltamivir (Tamiflu)","Azithromycin","Metronidazole","Albendazole"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for swine flu is Oseltamivir (Temiflu/Tamiflu).  Why B wrong: Azithromycin is for chlamydia/chancroid.  Why C wrong: Metronidazole is for bacterial vaginosis/trichomoniasis.  Why D wrong: Albendazole is for worm infestation."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice for bacterial vaginosis is:","options":["Metronidazole","Azithromycin","Ceftriaxone","Albendazole"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for bacterial vaginosis is Metronidazole.  Why B wrong: Azithromycin is for chlamydia/chancroid.  Why C wrong: Ceftriaxone is for PID/typhoid.  Why D wrong: Albendazole is for worm infestation."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice for chlamydia is:","options":["Azithromycin","Metronidazole","Ceftriaxone","Tetracycline"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for chlamydia is Azithromycin.  Why B wrong: Metronidazole is for bacterial vaginosis/trichomoniasis.  Why C wrong: Ceftriaxone is for PID.  Why D wrong: Tetracycline is for cholera."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice for chancroid is:","options":["Azithromycin","Metronidazole","Cotrimoxazole","Ceftriaxone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for chancroid is Azithromycin.  Why B wrong: Metronidazole is for bacterial vaginosis/trichomoniasis.  Why C wrong: Cotrimoxazole is for vaginal candidiasis (with fluconazole).  Why D wrong: Ceftriaxone is for PID/typhoid/gonorrhoea."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice for filariasis is:","options":["Diethylcarbamazine citrate (DEC)","Albendazole","Metronidazole","Azithromycin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for filariasis is Diethylcarbamazine citrate (DEC).  Why B wrong: Albendazole is for worm infestation.  Why C wrong: Metronidazole is for bacterial vaginosis/trichomoniasis.  Why D wrong: Azithromycin is for chlamydia/chancroid."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice for pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) is:","options":["Ceftriaxone","Metronidazole","Albendazole","Lithium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for PID is Ceftriaxone.  Why B wrong: Metronidazole is for bacterial vaginosis/trichomoniasis.  Why C wrong: Albendazole is for worm infestation.  Why D wrong: Lithium is for mania."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice for trichomoniasis is:","options":["Metronidazole","Azithromycin","Ceftriaxone","Albendazole"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for trichomoniasis is Metronidazole.  Why B wrong: Azithromycin is for chlamydia/chancroid.  Why C wrong: Ceftriaxone is for PID/typhoid.  Why D wrong: Albendazole is for worm infestation."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice for gonorrhoea is:","options":["Ceftriaxone (Cetriaxone)","Metronidazole","Azithromycin","Albendazole"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for gonorrhoea is Ceftriaxone (printed \"Cetriaxone\").  Why B wrong: Metronidazole is for bacterial vaginosis/trichomoniasis.  Why C wrong: Azithromycin is for chlamydia/chancroid.  Why D wrong: Albendazole is for worm infestation."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drugs of choice for vaginal candidiasis are:","options":["Cotrimoxazole and Fluconazole","Metronidazole and Azithromycin","Ceftriaxone and DEC","Albendazole and Tetracycline"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drugs of choice for vaginal candidiasis are Cotrimoxazole and Fluconazole.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are drugs of choice for other conditions in the table."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The drug of choice for worm infestation is:","options":["Albendazole","Metronidazole","DEC","Azithromycin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for worm infestation is Albendazole.  Why B wrong: Metronidazole is for bacterial vaginosis/trichomoniasis.  Why C wrong: DEC is for filariasis.  Why D wrong: Azithromycin is for chlamydia/chancroid."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Under the FDA Pregnancy Categories, which category means \"Controlled studies in humans show no risk to the fetus\"?","options":["Category A","Category B","Category C","Category X"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Category A = controlled human studies show no risk to the fetus (e.g., levothyroxine, folic acid, MgSO4, paracetamol, amoxicillin).  Why B wrong: Category B = no evidence of risk in humans.  Why C wrong: Category C = risk cannot be ruled out.  Why D wrong: Category X = absolutely contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which drugs are examples of FDA Pregnancy Category A?","options":["Levothyroxine, folic acid, MgSO4, paracetamol, amoxicillin","Penicillins, cephalosporins, macrolides, metformin","Zidovudine, albuterol, CCB, morphine, atropine","Thalidomide, isotretinoin, warfarin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Category A examples are levothyroxine, folic acid, MgSO4, paracetamol and amoxicillin.  Why B wrong: Those are Category B.  Why C wrong: Those are Category C.  Why D wrong: Those are Category X."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"FDA Pregnancy Category B is defined as:","options":["No evidence of risk in humans","Controlled studies show no risk to fetus","Positive evidence of risk to the fetus","Absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Category B = no evidence of risk in humans (e.g., penicillins, cephalosporins, macrolides, metformin).  Why B wrong: That is Category A.  Why C wrong: That is Category D.  Why D wrong: That is Category X."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Penicillins, cephalosporins, macrolides and metformin are examples of which FDA pregnancy category?","options":["Category A","Category B","Category C","Category D"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: These are Category B (no evidence of risk in humans).  Why A wrong: Category A includes folic acid, paracetamol, etc.  Why C wrong: Category C includes zidovudine, albuterol, CCB, morphine, atropine.  Why D wrong: Category D includes tetracyclines, aminoglycosides, phenytoin, lithium, valproic acid."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"FDA Pregnancy Category C means:","options":["Risk cannot be ruled out; no well-controlled human studies (could be safe or potentially harmful)","No evidence of risk in humans","Positive evidence of risk to the fetus","No risk shown in controlled human studies"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Category C = risk cannot be ruled out, no well-controlled studies in humans; could be safe/probably safe or potentially harmful.  Why B wrong: That is Category B.  Why C wrong: That is Category D.  Why D wrong: That is Category A."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Zidovudine, albuterol, CCB, morphine and atropine are examples of which FDA pregnancy category?","options":["Category A","Category B","Category C","Category X"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: These drugs are Category C examples.  Why A/B wrong: Categories A and B have different examples.  Why D wrong: Category X includes thalidomide, isotretinoin, fluoroquinolones, warfarin, ACE inhibitors."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"FDA Pregnancy Category D indicates:","options":["Positive evidence of risk to the fetus, but potential benefits may outweigh risks","No risk to the fetus","Risk cannot be ruled out","Absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Category D = positive evidence of risk from investigational/post-marketing data, but potential benefits may outweigh risks.  Why B wrong: That is Category A.  Why C wrong: That is Category C.  Why D wrong: That is Category X."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which of the following are examples of FDA Pregnancy Category D drugs? (Select all that apply)","options":["Tetracyclines","Aminoglycosides (amikacin, gentamycin)","Phenytoin","Lithium","Valproic acid"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Category D examples are tetracyclines, aminoglycosides (amikacin, gentamycin), phenytoin, lithium and valproic acid.  All listed options are correct per the table."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"FDA Pregnancy Category X means:","options":["Absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy; fetal risk clearly outweighs any benefits","No evidence of risk in humans","Positive evidence of risk but benefits may outweigh risks","Controlled studies show no risk"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Category X = absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy; potential fetal risk clearly outweighs any benefits.  Why B wrong: That is Category B.  Why C wrong: That is Category D.  Why D wrong: That is Category A."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which of the following are FDA Pregnancy Category X drugs? (Select all that apply)","options":["Thalidomide","Isotretinoin","Fluoroquinolones","Warfarin","ACE inhibitors"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Category X examples are thalidomide, isotretinoin, fluoroquinolones, warfarin and ACE inhibitors.  All listed options are correct per the table."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"In the Indian Drug Schedules, which schedule is a list of drugs that don't need a prescription (Over the Counter/OTC medicines)?","options":["Schedule G","Schedule H","Schedule X","Schedule Y"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Schedule G is a list of drugs that don't need a prescription or OTC medicines.  Why B wrong: Schedule H drugs need a prescription.  Why C wrong: Schedule X is for psychotropic drugs needing special license.  Why D wrong: Schedule Y is guidelines on clinical trials."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which Indian drug schedule lists drugs that can be sold only when a prescription is produced, and to which most drugs belong?","options":["Schedule G","Schedule H","Schedule P","Schedule W"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Schedule H is the list of drugs sold only on prescription; most drugs belong to this category.  Why A wrong: Schedule G is OTC.  Why C wrong: Schedule P concerns drug expiry date rules.  Why D wrong: Schedule W is generic-name-only drugs."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which Indian drug schedule explains rules about drug expiry date?","options":["Schedule P","Schedule W","Schedule X","Schedule Y"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Schedule P explains rules about drug expiry date.  Why B wrong: Schedule W is drugs marketed under generic names only.  Why C wrong: Schedule X is psychotropic drugs needing special license.  Why D wrong: Schedule Y is clinical-trial guidelines."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Schedule W in the Indian drug schedules refers to:","options":["Drugs marketed under generic names only","OTC drugs","Psychotropic drugs","Clinical trial guidelines"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Schedule W is the list of drugs marketed under generic names only.  Why B wrong: OTC = Schedule G.  Why C wrong: Psychotropic = Schedule X.  Why D wrong: Clinical trials = Schedule Y."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which Indian drug schedule lists psychotropic drugs that need a special license for manufacture and sale?","options":["Schedule H","Schedule P","Schedule X","Schedule Y"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Schedule X is the list of psychotropic drugs which need special license for manufacture and sale.  Why A wrong: Schedule H is prescription-only drugs.  Why B wrong: Schedule P is drug expiry rules.  Why D wrong: Schedule Y is clinical-trial guidelines."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Schedule Y in the Indian drug schedules provides:","options":["Guidelines on clinical trials, import and manufacture of new drugs","Rules about drug expiry date","List of OTC medicines","List of psychotropic drugs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Schedule Y provides guidelines on clinical trials, import and manufacture of new drugs.  Why B wrong: That is Schedule P.  Why C wrong: That is Schedule G.  Why D wrong: That is Schedule X."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which teratogenic drug is associated with fetal hydantoin syndrome?","options":["Phenytoin","Warfarin","Thalidomide","Lithium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Phenytoin causes fetal hydantoin syndrome.  Why B wrong: Warfarin causes fetal warfarin syndrome.  Why C wrong: Thalidomide causes phocomelia.  Why D wrong: Lithium causes Epstein anomaly."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Warfarin taken during pregnancy is associated with which congenital anomaly?","options":["Fetal warfarin syndrome","Fetal hydantoin syndrome","Neural tube defect","Phocomelia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Warfarin causes fetal warfarin syndrome.  Why B wrong: Fetal hydantoin syndrome is caused by phenytoin.  Why C wrong: Neural tube defect is caused by valproic acid.  Why D wrong: Phocomelia is caused by thalidomide."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Thalidomide is associated with which congenital anomaly?","options":["Phocomelia (anomalies in arms & legs)","Deafness","Tooth decay","Neural tube defect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thalidomide causes phocomelia (anomalies in arms & legs).  Why B wrong: Deafness/8th cranial nerve damage is from streptomycin.  Why C wrong: Tooth decay is from tetracycline.  Why D wrong: Neural tube defect is from valproic acid."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Streptomycin taken during pregnancy can cause:","options":["Deafness or damage to the 8th cranial nerve","Tooth decay","Fetal warfarin syndrome","Neural tube defect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Streptomycin causes deafness or damage to the 8th cranial nerve.  Why B wrong: Tooth decay is from tetracycline.  Why C wrong: Fetal warfarin syndrome is from warfarin.  Why D wrong: Neural tube defect is from valproic acid."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Tetracycline used during pregnancy is associated with which fetal anomaly?","options":["Tooth decay","Phocomelia","Epstein anomaly","Carcinogenic effect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tetracycline causes tooth decay.  Why B wrong: Phocomelia is from thalidomide.  Why C wrong: Epstein anomaly is from lithium.  Why D wrong: Carcinogenic effect is from vitamin A."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Lithium taken during pregnancy is associated with:","options":["Epstein anomaly (malformation of tricuspid valve)","Neural tube defect","Tooth decay","Deafness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lithium causes Epstein anomaly (malformation of tricuspid valve).  Why B wrong: Neural tube defect is from valproic acid.  Why C wrong: Tooth decay is from tetracycline.  Why D wrong: Deafness is from streptomycin."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Valproic acid taken during pregnancy is associated with which congenital anomaly?","options":["Neural tube defect","Phocomelia","Tooth decay","Carcinogenic effect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Valproic acid causes neural tube defect.  Why B wrong: Phocomelia is from thalidomide.  Why C wrong: Tooth decay is from tetracycline.  Why D wrong: Carcinogenic effect is from vitamin A."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Vitamin A in excess during pregnancy is associated with:","options":["Carcinogenic effect","Neural tube defect","Epstein anomaly","Fetal hydantoin syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin A is associated with a carcinogenic effect.  Why B wrong: Neural tube defect is from valproic acid.  Why C wrong: Epstein anomaly is from lithium.  Why D wrong: Fetal hydantoin syndrome is from phenytoin."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which of the following are FIRST-line medicines for tuberculosis? (Select all that apply)","options":["Isoniazid","Rifampin","Ethambutol","Pyrazinamide","Cycloserine"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: First line TB medicines are Isoniazid, Rifampin, Ethambutol, Pyrazinamide, Rifabutin and Rifapentine.  Why E wrong: Cycloserine is a second line TB medicine."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which of the following is a SECOND-line tuberculosis medicine?","options":["Isoniazid","Rifampin","Amikacin","Pyrazinamide"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Second line TB medicines include Amikacin, Capreomycin sulphate, Cycloserine, Ethionamide, Kanamycin, Levofloxacin, Moxifloxacin and Streptomycin.  Why A/B/D wrong: Isoniazid, Rifampin and Pyrazinamide are first-line."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Why is Isoniazid contraindicated in a client with liver disease?","options":["It can cause hepatotoxicity","It causes nephrotoxicity","It causes ototoxicity","It causes optic neuritis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Isoniazid is contraindicated in liver disease because it can cause hepatotoxicity; the nurse should observe for yellowish discolouration of the sclera and urine (jaundice/liver damage) before the next dose.  Why B/C wrong: These are not the reason given for isoniazid liver contraindication.  Why D wrong: Optic neuritis is an ethambutol effect."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Before the next scheduled dose of isoniazid, the nurse should observe the client for which sign indicating jaundice or liver damage?","options":["Yellowish discolouration of the sclera and urine","Red-green colour blindness","Hearing loss","Peripheral tingling"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should observe for yellowish discolouration of the sclera and urine that may indicate jaundice or liver damage.  Why B wrong: Red/green colour vision change is with ethambutol.  Why C wrong: Hearing loss/deafness is with streptomycin.  Why D wrong: Tingling relates to peripheral neuritis, a separate isoniazid effect."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Besides hepatotoxicity, which are other adverse effects of isoniazid?","options":["Peripheral neuritis and neurotoxicity","Tooth discoloration","Optic neuritis","Persistent dry cough"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Peripheral neuritis and neurotoxicity are other adverse effects of isoniazid.  Why B wrong: Tooth discoloration is with tetracyclines.  Why C wrong: Optic neuritis is with ethambutol.  Why D wrong: Dry cough is with ACE inhibitors."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Isoniazid causes deficiency of which vitamin, so it should be supplemented during therapy?","options":["Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine)","Vitamin K","Vitamin B12","Vitamin C"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Isoniazid causes Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine) deficiency, so vitamin B6 should be offered to clients on isoniazid therapy.  Why B wrong: Vitamin K relates to warfarin/clotting.  Why C/D wrong: Not the vitamin depleted by isoniazid."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The major side effects of ethambutol are:","options":["Optic neuritis and retrobulbar neuritis / colour vision changes (Red & Green)","Hepatotoxicity","8th cranial nerve damage","Red-orange discolouration of secretions"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Major side effects of ethambutol are optic neuritis and retrobulbar neuritis or colour vision changes (Red & Green).  Why B wrong: Hepatotoxicity is with isoniazid.  Why C wrong: 8th cranial nerve damage is with streptomycin.  Why D wrong: Red-orange discolouration of secretions is with rifampicin."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Why should ethambutol be avoided in children under 6 years of age?","options":["Because it is difficult to identify colour vision changes in children","Because it causes tooth decay","Because it causes hepatotoxicity","Because it causes deafness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ethambutol should be avoided in children <6 years because it is difficult to identify colour vision changes in children.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are effects of other drugs, not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Red-orange discolouration of body secretions (urine, sweat and tears) is a side effect of which TB drug? (AIIMS, GMC H, DSSSB 2019)","options":["Rifampicin","Isoniazid","Ethambutol","Streptomycin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Red orange discolouration of body secretions (urine, sweat, tears) is a side effect of Rifampicin.  Why B wrong: Isoniazid causes hepatotoxicity/neuritis.  Why C wrong: Ethambutol affects colour vision.  Why D wrong: Streptomycin causes 8th cranial nerve damage."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Streptomycin toxicity can cause damage to which cranial nerve, leading to deafness?","options":["8th cranial nerve","5th cranial nerve","2nd cranial nerve","10th cranial nerve"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Streptomycin toxicity can cause 8th cranial nerve damage, leading to deafness, so it should be avoided in pregnant women and children.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the affected nerve."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Streptomycin is contraindicated in clients with which conditions?","options":["Myasthenia gravis and Parkinsonism","Hypertension and diabetes","Asthma and COPD","Peptic ulcer and GERD"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Streptomycin is contraindicated in clients with Myasthenia gravis and Parkinsonism.  Why B/C/D wrong: These conditions are not listed as contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"What is the primary action of anticoagulants?","options":["Prevent further clot formation and extension by inhibiting clotting factor function","Dissolve existing clots","Increase platelet aggregation","Promote fibrin formation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anticoagulants prevent further clot formation and extension by inhibiting the clotting factors' function.  Why B wrong: They do not dissolve existing clots (that is thrombolytics).  Why C wrong: They prevent platelet aggregation.  Why D wrong: They prevent fibrin formation."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Anticoagulants prevent formation of which end product of haemostasis? (AIIMS-2017)","options":["Fibrin","Thrombin","Platelets","Plasmin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anticoagulants prevent fibrin formation; fibrin is the end product of haemostasis.  Why B wrong: Thrombin is an enzyme, not the end product.  Why C wrong: Platelets are cells.  Why D wrong: Plasmin dissolves clots."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Anticoagulants are contraindicated in bleeding disorders EXCEPT which condition?","options":["DIC (Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation)","Haemophilia","Thrombocytopenia","Peptic ulcer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anticoagulants are contraindicated in bleeding disorders except DIC.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are bleeding-risk conditions where anticoagulants would generally be contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Low Molecular Weight Heparin (LMWH) is administered by which route at which most common site, and should NOT be aspirated because it comes in a prefilled syringe?","options":["SC route at the abdominal wall","IV route at the antecubital fossa","IM route at the deltoid","Intradermal at the forearm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: LMWH is available in a prefilled syringe (don't aspirate) and is administered by SC route at the abdominal wall (most common site); it has a longer half-life than heparin.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated route/site for LMWH."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which of the following are listed side effects of anticoagulant therapy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Haemorrhage, petechiae, purpura and ecchymosis","Haematuria, epistaxis and bleeding gums","Thrombocytopenia and hypotension","Tinnitus and hearing loss with aspirin therapy","Persistent dry cough"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Side effects include haemorrhage/petechiae/purpura/ecchymosis, haematuria/epistaxis/bleeding gums, thrombocytopenia/hypotension, and tinnitus/hearing loss with aspirin therapy.  Why E wrong: Persistent dry cough is a side effect of ACE inhibitors, not anticoagulants."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Heparin sodium acts on which factor, thereby preventing fibrinogen from converting into fibrin?","options":["Thrombin","Plasmin","Vitamin K","Calcium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Heparin acts on thrombin, so fibrinogen is unable to convert into fibrin.  Why B wrong: Plasmin dissolves clots.  Why C wrong: Vitamin K is targeted by warfarin.  Why D wrong: Calcium is a clotting factor but not heparin's target here."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"By which routes is heparin given?","options":["IV and SC (SuQ) route","IM and oral","Oral only","Intradermal only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Heparin is given by IV and SuQ (subcutaneous) route.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated routes; heparin is not given orally."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"What is the antidote for heparin therapy? (FAQs)","options":["Protamine sulphate","Vitamin K","Naloxone","Aminocaproic acid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote of heparin therapy is protamine sulphate.  Why B wrong: Vitamin K is the antidote for warfarin.  Why C wrong: Naloxone is for opioids.  Why D wrong: Aminocaproic acid reverses thrombolytics."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Does heparin dissolve existing clots?","options":["No, it only prevents new clot formation","Yes, it dissolves existing clots completely","Yes, it dissolves clots and prevents new ones","Only when combined with aspirin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Heparin does not dissolve existing clots; it only prevents new clot formation and prevents further thrombus/thromboembolism formation.  Why B/C/D wrong: They contradict the stated action."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which laboratory test should be monitored during heparin therapy? (NORCET 5.0 Mains, PGI, GMCH, JIPMER)","options":["aPTT (Activated Partial Thromboplastin Time)","PT and INR","Bleeding time only","Platelet count only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: During heparin therapy, aPTT (Activated Partial Thromboplastin Time) level should be monitored.  Why B wrong: PT and INR are monitored during warfarin therapy.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the primary test for heparin monitoring."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"What is the normal aPTT level? (BSF-2018)","options":["20-36 seconds","9.5-11.5 seconds","60-80 seconds","2-3 seconds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The normal aPTT level is 20-36 seconds.  Why B wrong: 9.5-11.5 seconds is the normal PT level.  Why C wrong: 80 seconds relates to therapeutic maximum aPTT during therapy.  Why D wrong: 2-3 relates to INR."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The therapeutic aPTT value during heparin therapy is 1.5 to 2.5 times normal, which means a maximum of:","options":["80 seconds","36 seconds","25 seconds","100 seconds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The normal value of aPTT during therapy is 1.5 to 2.5 times, meaning a maximum of 80 seconds.  Why B wrong: 36 seconds is the upper normal aPTT.  Why C wrong: 25 sec relates to therapeutic PT maximum.  Why D wrong: 100 seconds is above the therapeutic maximum."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"If the aPTT level is more than 80 seconds during heparin therapy, the nurse should:","options":["Immediately withhold the heparin therapy or lower the dose as per order","Increase the dose","Continue at the same dose","Administer protamine sulphate immediately without an order"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If aPTT is more than 80 seconds, heparin therapy should immediately be withheld or the dose lowered as per order.  Why B wrong: Dose increase is for a short aPTT (<60 sec).  Why C wrong: Continuing risks bleeding.  Why D wrong: The page indicates dose adjustment per order, not unordered antidote administration."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"If the aPTT value is too short (less than 60 seconds), the heparin dose should be:","options":["Increased","Decreased","Withheld","Unchanged"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If the aPTT value is too short, less than 60 sec, then the dose should increase.  Why B/C wrong: These apply when aPTT is too high (>80 sec).  Why D wrong: A short aPTT requires a dose change."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"How frequently should the aPTT level be monitored during heparin therapy?","options":["Initially every 6 hours, then every day","Every 12 hours only","Once a week","Only at the start of therapy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Initially every 6 hours the aPTT level should be monitored, then every day.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated monitoring schedule."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Warfarin is which type of anticoagulant?","options":["An oral anticoagulant","An IV anticoagulant only","A subcutaneous anticoagulant","A topical anticoagulant"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Warfarin is an oral anticoagulant therapy used for long-term anticoagulation.  Why B/C/D wrong: Warfarin is given orally, not via these routes."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Warfarin acts as an antagonist of which vitamin?","options":["Vitamin K","Vitamin B6","Vitamin C","Vitamin D"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Warfarin acts as an antagonist of Vitamin K, meaning it suppresses the action of vitamin K.  Why B/C/D wrong: Warfarin does not antagonize these vitamins."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Vitamin K is necessary for clotting factor formation in the liver, especially which clotting factors (also called vitamin-K dependent factors)? (FAQs)","options":["Factors 2, 7, 9, 10","Factors 1, 5, 8, 13","Factors 3, 4, 6, 11","Factors 12, 13"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin K is necessary for clotting factors 2, 7, 9, 10, which are called vitamin-K dependent factors.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the vitamin-K dependent factors listed."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"What is the antidote for warfarin therapy? (FAQs)","options":["Vitamin K or phytonadione","Protamine sulphate","Naloxone","Aminocaproic acid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote of warfarin therapy is Vitamin K or phytonadione.  Why B wrong: Protamine sulphate is for heparin.  Why C wrong: Naloxone is for opioids.  Why D wrong: Aminocaproic acid reverses thrombolytics."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which lab tests should be monitored during warfarin therapy? (GMCH-2016, JIPMER-2020)","options":["Prothrombin time (PT) and INR","aPTT","Bleeding time","Platelet count"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: During warfarin therapy, prothrombin time (PT) and INR should be monitored.  Why B wrong: aPTT is monitored for heparin.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the primary tests for warfarin."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"What is the normal PT (prothrombin time) level? (CGHS-2018)","options":["9.5-11.5 seconds","20-36 seconds","60-80 seconds","2-3 seconds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The normal PT level is 9.5-11.5 seconds.  Why B wrong: 20-36 seconds is the normal aPTT.  Why C wrong: 60-80 sec relates to therapeutic aPTT range.  Why D wrong: 2-3 relates to INR."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The therapeutic value of PT is 1.5 to 2 times normal, which means a maximum of:","options":["25 seconds","80 seconds","11.5 seconds","36 seconds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The therapeutic value of PT is 1.5 to 2 times normal, meaning a maximum of 25 sec.  Why B wrong: 80 sec is the therapeutic maximum for aPTT.  Why C wrong: 11.5 sec is upper normal PT.  Why D wrong: 36 sec is upper normal aPTT."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The normal therapeutic value of INR during warfarin therapy is:","options":["2 to 3 times normal","1.5 to 2.5 times normal","9.5-11.5","20-36"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The normal value of INR is 2 to 3 times normal.  Why B wrong: 1.5-2.5 refers to therapeutic aPTT ratio.  Why C wrong: 9.5-11.5 is normal PT in seconds.  Why D wrong: 20-36 is normal aPTT in seconds."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"In nursing care for anticoagulant therapy, which lab value is monitored for the patient on heparin versus warfarin?","options":["aPTT for heparin; PT & INR for warfarin","PT & INR for heparin; aPTT for warfarin","Bleeding time for both","Platelet count only for both"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor aPTT when the patient is on heparin therapy; monitor PT & INR when on warfarin therapy.  Why B wrong: The tests are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the primary monitoring tests specified."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"As part of anticoagulant nursing care, the nurse should observe for bleeding in which areas? (Select all that apply)","options":["Bleeding gums","Nose bleed","Haematuria","Haematemesis","Occult blood in the stool"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes observing for bleeding gums, nose bleed, haematuria, haematemesis and occult blood in the stool (also monitor platelet counts and vital signs).  All listed options are correct per the page."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"According to the \"Also Remember\" box, which is the best and most common site for subcutaneous heparin administration?","options":["Abdomen near the umbilical region","Outer aspect of upper arm","Scapular portion of the back","Anterior thigh"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Heparin SC sites include outer aspect of upper arm, abdomen near umbilical region (best and most common), scapular portion of back and anterior thigh.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are alternative sites but not the \"best and most common\" one."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"After administration of subcutaneous heparin, the nurse should:","options":["Not aspirate or massage the area","Massage the area vigorously","Aspirate before injecting","Apply heat to the site"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After administration of heparin, do not aspirate or massage the area.  Why B/C wrong: Both aspiration and massage are contraindicated.  Why D wrong: Not indicated."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"A patient on anticoagulant therapy should avoid which analgesic drug?","options":["Aspirin or ASA","Paracetamol","Amoxicillin","Metformin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Avoid aspirin or ASA when the patient is on anticoagulant therapy (it increases bleeding risk).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the drug singled out to avoid in this context."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"A patient on warfarin therapy should avoid excessive intake of which dietary sources and which juice?","options":["Dietary sources of Vitamin K and cranberry juice","Dietary sources of Vitamin C and orange juice","High-protein foods and milk","Iron-rich foods and grape juice"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Avoid excessive intake of dietary sources of Vitamin K and cranberry juice when taking warfarin therapy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the dietary items to avoid per the page."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"To maintain safety and prevent bleeding in anticoagulant therapy, the nurse should advise the patient to use:","options":["An electric razor","A straight (blade) razor","A firm-bristled toothbrush","Intramuscular injections for other medications"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Maintain safety precautions to prevent bleeding, such as use of an electric razor.  Why B/C/D wrong: These increase bleeding/injury risk and are not advised."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"What is the mechanism of action of cardiac glycosides such as digoxin and digitalis?","options":["Inhibit the sodium-potassium pump, increasing intracellular calcium, causing more effective heart muscle contraction","Block calcium entry into blood vessels","Inhibit ACE","Block beta receptors"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiac glycosides inhibit the sodium-potassium pump, increasing intracellular calcium, which causes heart muscles to contract more effectively/efficiently. Examples: digoxin, digitalis.  Why B wrong: That is the mechanism of CCBs.  Why C wrong: That is ACE inhibitors.  Why D wrong: That is beta blockers."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The positive inotropic effect of digoxin means it:","options":["Increases the force and strength of cardiac muscle contraction (acts on ventricles)","Decreases heart rate","Decreases speed of conduction","Increases heart rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Positive inotropic effect = increase force & strength of cardiac muscles, acting on ventricles (AIIMS M.Sc. Ent. 2022).  Why B wrong: That is the negative chronotropic effect.  Why C wrong: That is the negative dromotropic effect.  Why D wrong: Digoxin decreases, not increases, heart rate."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The negative chronotropic effect of digoxin refers to:","options":["Decreased heart rate & rhythm, acting on the SA node","Increased force of contraction","Decreased speed of conduction at the AV node","Increased conduction speed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Negative chronotropic effect = decrease heart rate & rhythm, acting on the SA node.  Why B wrong: That is positive inotropic effect.  Why C wrong: That is negative dromotropic effect.  Why D wrong: Digoxin decreases conduction."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The negative dromotropic effect of digoxin refers to:","options":["Decreased speed of conduction, acting on the AV node","Decreased heart rate at the SA node","Increased force of contraction","Increased heart rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Negative dromotropic effect = decrease speed of conduction, acting on the AV node.  Why B wrong: That is negative chronotropic (SA node).  Why C wrong: That is positive inotropic.  Why D wrong: Digoxin does not increase heart rate."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Digoxin increases cardiac output by increasing the effectiveness of the heart pump, so it effectively treats which conditions? (Select all that apply)","options":["Heart failure","Atrial flutter","Atrial fibrillation","Ventricular fibrillation"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Digoxin effectively treats heart failure, atrial flutter and fibrillation.  Why D wrong: Ventricular dysrhythmias (VT and VF) are contraindications for digoxin, not indications."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Digoxin is the drug of choice for which condition? (NIMHANS-2016)","options":["Heart failure","Angina","Hypertension","Anaphylaxis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Digoxin is the drug of choice for heart failure.  Why B wrong: NTG is DOC for angina.  Why C wrong: Thiazide/CCB are first-line for HTN.  Why D wrong: Adrenaline is DOC for anaphylaxis."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"While a client is on digoxin therapy, the apical pulse should be assessed for how long?","options":["Full 1 minute","15 seconds","30 seconds","2 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Assess the apical pulse for a full 1 minute while the client is on digoxin therapy.  Why B/C wrong: A shorter count is not recommended for digoxin.  Why D wrong: The stated duration is one full minute."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which of the following are contraindications to digoxin? (Select all that apply)","options":["Ventricular dysrhythmias (VT and VF)","Second & third degree heart block","Bradycardia","Heart failure"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Contraindications include ventricular dysrhythmias (VT and VF), second & third degree heart block, and bradycardia.  Why D wrong: Heart failure is an indication (digoxin is the DOC), not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which of the following is an early side effect of digoxin due to malabsorption of nutrients?","options":["Nausea, vomiting and anorexia","Persistent dry cough","Hyperglycaemia","Tooth discoloration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nausea, vomiting and anorexia are early side effects (due to malabsorption of nutrients); other side effects include diarrhoea and bradycardia.  Why B wrong: Dry cough is with ACE inhibitors.  Why C wrong: Hyperglycaemia is with thiazide diuretics.  Why D wrong: Tooth discoloration is with tetracyclines."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"During digoxin therapy, the nurse should withhold the dose if the heart rate in an ADULT goes below:","options":["60","70","90","100"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Withhold digoxin if the heart rate goes below 60 in an adult.  Why B wrong: Below 70 applies to children.  Why C wrong: Below 90 applies to infants.  Why D wrong: Not a stated threshold."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"During digoxin therapy, the dose should be withheld if the heart rate in a CHILD goes below:","options":["60","70","90","100"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Withhold digoxin if the heart rate goes below 70 in children.  Why A wrong: Below 60 applies to adults.  Why C wrong: Below 90 applies to infants.  Why D wrong: Not a stated threshold."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"During digoxin therapy, the dose should be withheld if the heart rate in an INFANT goes below:","options":["60","70","90","100"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Withhold digoxin if the heart rate goes below 90 in an infant.  Why A wrong: Below 60 applies to adults.  Why B wrong: Below 70 applies to children.  Why D wrong: Not a stated threshold."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"What is the FIRST nursing action in digoxin toxicity? (FAQs)","options":["Withhold the next scheduled dose and immediately inform the duty doctor","Administer more digoxin","Give potassium immediately","Increase the dose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The first nursing action in digoxin toxicity is withholding the next scheduled dose and immediately informing the duty doctor.  Why B/D wrong: More digoxin would worsen toxicity.  Why C wrong: Not the first action stated."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"During digoxin therapy, the serum potassium level should be maintained between: (AIIMS-2020)","options":["3.5-5.1 mEq/L","0.5-2 ng/mL","0.6-1.2 mEq/L","9.5-11.5 mEq/L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor serum potassium level; it should be maintained between 3.5-5.1 mEq/L.  Why B wrong: 0.5-2 ng/mL is the therapeutic digoxin level.  Why C wrong: 0.6-1.2 mEq/L is the lithium therapeutic range (not on this page).  Why D wrong: Not a valid potassium range."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Hypokalemia has what effect on digoxin toxicity? (NORCET-2021, PGI, GMCH-2022)","options":["It increases the chance of digoxin toxicity","It decreases the chance of digoxin toxicity","It has no effect","It reverses digoxin toxicity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypokalemia increases the chance of digoxin toxicity, so encourage intake of a potassium-rich diet to prevent hypokalaemia.  Why B/C/D wrong: They contradict the stated relationship."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"The therapeutic digoxin level should be maintained between: (KPSC-2022)","options":["0.5-2 ng/mL","3.5-5.1 mEq/L","20-36 seconds","9.5-11.5 ng/mL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor for therapeutic digoxin level; it should be maintained between 0.5-2 ng/mL.  Why B wrong: 3.5-5.1 mEq/L is the serum potassium target.  Why C wrong: 20-36 sec is normal aPTT.  Why D wrong: Incorrect range/units."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"A serum digoxin level above which value indicates toxicity, requiring the nurse to withhold the next dose and inform the doctor?","options":["More than 2 ng/mL","More than 5.1 ng/mL","More than 1 ng/mL","More than 0.5 ng/mL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If serum digoxin levels are more than 2 ng/ml, it indicates toxicity; withhold the next scheduled dose and inform the doctor.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated toxicity threshold."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which of the following is an early sign of digoxin toxicity due to stimulation of the chemoreceptor zone of the medulla oblongata?","options":["Anorexia, nausea & vomiting","Xanthopsia","Premature ventricular complexes","Hyperkalaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anorexia, nausea & vomiting are an early sign of digoxin toxicity due to stimulation of the chemoreceptor zone of the medulla oblongata.  Why B wrong: Xanthopsia (yellow vision) is a visual effect on the cone.  Why C wrong: Premature ventricular complexes are a cardiac toxicity sign, not the earliest chemoreceptor-mediated one.  Why D wrong: Digoxin toxicity is associated with hypokalaemia, not hyperkalaemia."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Xanthopsia (yellow vision) seen in digoxin toxicity is due to: (IGIMS-2018)","options":["Effect on the visual cone","Effect on the SA node","Malabsorption of nutrients","Renal impairment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Xanthopsia or yellow vision is due to the effect on the visual cone.  Why B wrong: SA node effect causes bradycardia.  Why C wrong: Malabsorption relates to GI side effects.  Why D wrong: Not the stated cause."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which of the following are signs to monitor for digoxin toxicity? (Select all that apply)","options":["Premature ventricular complexes","Xanthopsia (yellow vision)","Blurred vision due to CNS effect","Muscle weakness due to CNS effect","Hypokalaemia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Digoxin toxicity signs include anorexia/nausea/vomiting, premature ventricular complexes, xanthopsia (yellow vision), blurred vision (CNS effect), muscle weakness (CNS effect) and hypokalaemia (AIIMS, PGI, JIPMER, ESIC).  All listed options are correct per the page."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"What is the antidote for digoxin toxicity?","options":["Digoxin immune fab","Protamine sulphate","Vitamin K","Naloxone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote for digoxin toxicity is Digoxin immune fab.  Why B wrong: Protamine sulphate is for heparin.  Why C wrong: Vitamin K is for warfarin.  Why D wrong: Naloxone is for opioids."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"What are the first-line medicines for stage 1 hypertension?","options":["Thiazide diuretics & CCBs","Beta blockers & ACE inhibitors","Loop diuretics & digoxin","Alpha blockers & nitrates"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Initially, first-line medicines for stage 1 hypertension are thiazide diuretics & CCBs.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not stated as first-line for stage 1 HTN."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Loop diuretics act directly on which part of the nephron, e.g. frusemide (Lasix) and torsemide?","options":["Loop of Henle","Distal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct","Proximal tubule"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Loop diuretics act directly on the Loop of Henle, such as frusemide (Lasix) and torsemide.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not where loop diuretics act."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which of the following are thiazide diuretics?","options":["Hydrochlorothiazide & chlorothiazide","Frusemide & torsemide","Spironolactone & amiloride","Amlodipine & nifedipine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thiazide diuretics include hydrochlorothiazide and chlorothiazide.  Why B wrong: Frusemide and torsemide are loop diuretics.  Why C wrong: These are potassium-sparing diuretics.  Why D wrong: These are CCBs."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Potassium-sparing diuretics increase absorption of potassium, so which high-potassium foods should be avoided during therapy?","options":["Cauliflower, spinach & raisins","White rice & bread","Chicken & eggs","Apples & pears"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Potassium sparing diuretics increase absorption of potassium, so high potassium containing foods such as cauliflower, spinach & raisins should be avoided during therapy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the high-potassium foods listed."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Loop diuretics should be given slowly because rapid administration can cause:","options":["Ototoxicity and deafness","Hyperglycaemia","Hyperkalaemia","Persistent dry cough"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Loop diuretics are given slowly because they can cause ototoxicity and deafness.  Why B wrong: Hyperglycaemia is caused by thiazide diuretics.  Why C wrong: Hyperkalaemia is caused by potassium-sparing diuretics.  Why D wrong: Dry cough is caused by ACE inhibitors."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"All thiazide diuretics cause which metabolic side effect?","options":["Hyperglycaemia","Hypoglycaemia","Hyperkalaemia","Ototoxicity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: All thiazide diuretics cause hyperglycaemia.  Why B wrong: They cause hyper-, not hypo-, glycaemia.  Why C wrong: Hyperkalaemia is from potassium-sparing diuretics.  Why D wrong: Ototoxicity is from loop diuretics."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Potassium-sparing diuretics are contraindicated in renal and liver failure and electrolyte imbalances such as: (NORCET-2020)","options":["Hyperkalaemia","Hypokalaemia","Hyperglycaemia","Hypernatraemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Potassium sparing diuretics are contraindicated in renal and liver failure and electrolyte imbalances e.g. hyperkalaemia.  Why B wrong: They tend to cause hyperkalaemia, so hypokalaemia is not the contraindication.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which statement about diuretics and potassium is correct?","options":["All diuretics cause hypokalaemia, but potassium-sparing diuretics cause hyperkalaemia","All diuretics cause hyperkalaemia","Potassium-sparing diuretics cause hypokalaemia","Loop diuretics cause hyperkalaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: All diuretics cause hypokalaemia but potassium-sparing diuretics cause hyperkalaemia.  Why B/C/D wrong: They contradict the stated rule."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"What is the mechanism of action of calcium channel blockers (CCBs)?","options":["They block the entry of calcium into blood vessels and muscles","They block beta receptors","They inhibit ACE","They antagonize vitamin K"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CCBs block the entry of calcium into blood vessels and muscles.  Why B wrong: That is beta blockers.  Why C wrong: That is ACE inhibitors.  Why D wrong: That is warfarin."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"According to the \"trick\" on the page, the names of most CCBs end with which suffix?","options":["-pine","-pril","-sartan","-olol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The trick is that \"-pine\" is the last word of CCBs (e.g., amlodipine, nifedipine, nimodipine).  Why B wrong: \"-pril\" is the suffix for ACE inhibitors.  Why C wrong: \"-sartan\" is for angiotensin-II receptor antagonists.  Why D wrong: \"-olol\" is for beta blockers (not stated here)."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which of the following are dihydropyridine CCBs?","options":["Amlodipine, nifedipine and nimodipine","Diltiazem and verapamil","Captopril and enalapril","Telmisartan and losartan"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dihydropyridine CCBs are amlodipine (most commonly used), nifedipine and nimodipine.  Why B wrong: Diltiazem and verapamil are non-dihydropyridine CCBs.  Why C wrong: These are ACE inhibitors.  Why D wrong: These are ARBs."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which is the most commonly used dihydropyridine CCB?","options":["Amlodipine","Nifedipine","Verapamil","Diltiazem"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Amlodipine is the most commonly used dihydropyridine CCB.  Why B wrong: Nifedipine is also a dihydropyridine but not stated as most common.  Why C/D wrong: Verapamil and diltiazem are non-dihydropyridine CCBs."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which of the following are non-dihydropyridine CCBs?","options":["Diltiazem and verapamil","Amlodipine and nifedipine","Nimodipine and amlodipine","Captopril and ramipril"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Non-dihydropyridine CCBs are diltiazem and verapamil.  Why B/C wrong: These are dihydropyridine CCBs.  Why D wrong: These are ACE inhibitors."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"How do ACE inhibitors prevent peripheral vasoconstriction?","options":["By blocking the conversion of angiotensin-I into angiotensin-II","By blocking calcium entry into vessels","By antagonizing vitamin K","By blocking beta receptors"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ACE inhibitors prevent peripheral vasoconstriction by blocking conversion of angiotensin-I into angiotensin-II, because angiotensin-II causes vasoconstriction & HTN.  Why B wrong: That is CCBs.  Why C wrong: That is warfarin.  Why D wrong: That is beta blockers."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"What is the MOST common side effect of ACE inhibitors? (JIPMER-2016)","options":["Persistent dry cough","Hyperglycaemia","Ototoxicity","Tooth discoloration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common side effect of ACE inhibitors is persistent dry cough.  Why B wrong: Hyperglycaemia is with thiazide diuretics.  Why C wrong: Ototoxicity is with loop diuretics/aminoglycosides.  Why D wrong: Tooth discoloration is with tetracyclines."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"According to the page's trick, the names of ACE inhibitors end with which suffix (e.g., captopril, enalapril, ramipril, fosinopril)?","options":["-pril","-pine","-sartan","-olol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"-pril\" is the last word of ACE inhibitors, e.g., captopril, analapril (enalapril), ramipril and fosinopril.  Why B wrong: \"-pine\" is CCBs.  Why C wrong: \"-sartan\" is ARBs.  Why D wrong: \"-olol\" is beta blockers."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Telmisartan, losartan and candesartan are examples of which drug class?","options":["Angiotensin-II receptor antagonists (ARBs)","ACE inhibitors","Calcium channel blockers","Beta blockers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Telmisartan, losartan and candesartan are angiotensin-II receptor antagonists.  Why B wrong: ACE inhibitors end in \"-pril.\"  Why C wrong: CCBs end in \"-pine.\"  Why D wrong: Beta blockers block beta receptors."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Beta blockers, also called adrenergic blocking agents, reduce blood pressure by:","options":["Blocking all types of beta receptors","Blocking calcium channels","Inhibiting ACE","Antagonizing vitamin K"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Beta blockers (adrenergic blocking agents) block all types of beta receptors, reducing blood pressure.  Why B wrong: That is CCBs.  Why C wrong: That is ACE inhibitors.  Why D wrong: That is warfarin."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Beta-1 receptors are located mainly in which organs? (AIIMS Delhi-2016)","options":["Heart & kidney","Lungs & liver","Uterus & skeletal muscle","Brain & spinal cord"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Beta-1 receptors are located in the heart & kidney.  Why B/C wrong: Beta-2 receptors are in lungs, liver, GIT, uterus and skeletal muscle.  Why D wrong: Not the stated location."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Beta-2 receptors are located mainly in the lungs and also in which other tissues? (Select all that apply)","options":["Liver","GIT","Uterus","Skeletal muscles","Heart"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Beta-2 receptors are located mainly in the lungs, other- liver, GIT, uterus and skeletal muscles.  Why E wrong: The heart contains beta-1 receptors (heart & kidney)."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"Which pharmacodynamic components in the genetic polymorphisms diagram are shown besides receptors and enzymes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Ion Channels","Immune System","Absorption","Metabolism"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The pharmacodynamic branch shows Receptors, Ion Channels, Enzymes and Immune System.  Why C/D wrong: Absorption and Metabolism belong to the pharmacokinetic branch."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Pharmacology intro & branches, glossary/definitions…","question":"In warfarin therapy, warfarin acts by increasing which blood clotting parameter?","options":["Clotting time (CT) of the blood","Platelet count","Haemoglobin","Fibrinogen level"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Warfarin acts by increasing the clotting time (CT) of the blood.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the parameters warfarin increases per the page."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Beta-3 adrenergic receptors are primarily located in which tissue?","options":["Cardiac muscle","Adipose tissue (adipocytes)","Bronchial smooth muscle","Renal juxtaglomerular cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states beta-3 receptors are located in adipose tissue (adipocytes).  Why A wrong: Beta-1 receptors predominate in cardiac muscle.  Why C wrong: Beta-2 receptors are found in bronchial smooth muscle.  Why D wrong: The source does not place beta-3 receptors in the kidney."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the most common side effect of beta blockers?","options":["Tachycardia","Hypertension","Bradycardia","Hyperglycaemia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common side effect is bradycardia.  Why A wrong: Beta blockers slow, not speed, the heart rate.  Why B wrong: Beta blockers lower blood pressure.  Why D wrong: Beta blockers mask signs of hypoglycaemia rather than causing hyperglycaemia."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Beta blockers can cause bronchospasm and also mask the signs of which condition?","options":["Hyperthyroidism","Hypoglycaemia","Hyperkalaemia","Dehydration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states beta blockers cause bronchospasm and mask signs of hypoglycaemia.  Why A wrong: Not mentioned in the source.  Why C wrong: Not mentioned in the source.  Why D wrong: Not mentioned in the source."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Using the trick that \"-lol is the last word,\" which of the following is an example of a beta blocker?","options":["Amlodipine","Metoprolol","Diltiazem","Verapamil"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists atenolol, metoprolol, propranolol, and timolol as beta blockers (the \"-lol\" ending).  Why A wrong: Amlodipine is a calcium channel blocker.  Why C wrong: Diltiazem is a calcium channel blocker.  Why D wrong: Verapamil is a calcium channel blocker."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Vasodilators used as anti-hypertensives are indicated in which situation?","options":["Routine long-term maintenance","Only in emergency situations","Only in paediatric patients","Only in pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states vasodilators are used only in emergency situations.  Why A wrong: They are reserved for emergencies, not routine maintenance.  Why C wrong: Not restricted to paediatrics.  Why D wrong: Not restricted to pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which drug is the drug of choice (DOC) for malignant hypertension?","options":["Hydralazine","Sodium nitroprusside","Isosorbide dinitrate","Atenolol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states sodium nitroprusside is the DOC for malignant HTN (Delhi AIIMS-2015).  Why A wrong: Hydralazine is the DOC for gestational HTN.  Why C wrong: Isosorbide dinitrate is the DOC in an angina attack.  Why D wrong: Atenolol is a beta blocker, not the DOC for malignant HTN."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which drug is the drug of choice (DOC) for gestational hypertension?","options":["Sodium nitroprusside","Hydralazine","Nitroglycerin","Propranolol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states hydralazine is the DOC for gestational HTN.  Why A wrong: Sodium nitroprusside is the DOC for malignant HTN.  Why C wrong: NTG (nitroglycerin) is a nitrate for angina.  Why D wrong: Propranolol is a beta blocker."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Isosorbide dinitrate is the drug of choice in which condition?","options":["Malignant hypertension","Gestational hypertension","Angina attack","Congestive heart failure"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states nitrates like isosorbide dinitrate are the DOC in an angina attack (along with NTG).  Why A wrong: Sodium nitroprusside is used for malignant HTN.  Why B wrong: Hydralazine is used for gestational HTN.  Why D wrong: Not specified as DOC for CHF in this section."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"During anti-hypertensive therapy, in which positions should blood pressure be measured?","options":["Sitting only","Standing and supine both","Prone only","Lying with legs elevated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states BP should be measured in both standing and supine positions.  Why A wrong: Sitting alone is incomplete per the source.  Why C wrong: Prone is not indicated.  Why D wrong: Not the stated positions."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Because IV nitrates are photosensitive, what nursing action is recommended during administration?","options":["Warm the solution before infusion","Wrap the bottle with a black-colour cover to protect from light","Infuse rapidly to reduce light exposure","Add the drug to a clear glass bottle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states IV nitrates must be protected from light, so a black-colour cover should be wrapped on the bottle as nitrates are photosensitive.  Why A wrong: Warming is not indicated.  Why C wrong: Rapid infusion is not the stated protective measure.  Why D wrong: A clear bottle would not protect from light."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which is the most common complication during anti-hypertensive therapy?","options":["Hypertensive crisis","Orthostatic (postural) hypotension","Bradycardia","Pulmonary oedema"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states orthostatic hypotension is the most common complication during therapy.  Why A wrong: The goal of therapy is to lower BP, not cause a crisis.  Why C wrong: Bradycardia is not listed as the most common complication of anti-hypertensives here.  Why D wrong: Not the most common complication."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"To prevent injury from orthostatic hypotension, what should the client be instructed to do?","options":["Stand up quickly to restore blood flow","While standing, sit on the bed for a few seconds or change position slowly","Avoid all movement for one hour after the dose","Take an extra dose if dizzy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs the client to sit on the bed for a few seconds or change position slowly.  Why A wrong: Standing up quickly worsens orthostatic hypotension.  Why C wrong: Immobility is not advised.  Why D wrong: Extra doses are dangerous and not advised."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which dietary instruction is given to a client on anti-hypertensive therapy?","options":["High sodium diet","Low sodium diet","High protein diet","Fluid restriction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs a low sodium diet and to take all medicines daily at the right time.  Why A wrong: High sodium worsens hypertension.  Why C wrong: Not the stated instruction.  Why D wrong: Not the stated instruction."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Thrombolytics are also called by which name because they act on fibrin?","options":["Anticoagulants","Fibrinolytics","Antiplatelets","Vasodilators"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states thrombolytics are also called fibrinolytics, which act on fibrin.  Why A wrong: Anticoagulants prevent clot formation but are a distinct class.  Why C wrong: Antiplatelets inhibit platelet aggregation.  Why D wrong: Vasodilators widen vessels."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"In haemostasis, fibrin is described as the:","options":["First product","End/last product","Precursor to plasminogen","Enzyme that dissolves clots"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states fibrin is the end/last product of haemostasis.  Why A wrong: It is the last, not first, product.  Why C wrong: Fibrin is not a precursor to plasminogen.  Why D wrong: Plasmin dissolves clots, not fibrin."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Thrombolytic medicines produce their effect by activating which substance?","options":["Fibrin","Plasminogen","Thrombin","Prothrombin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states thrombolytic medicines activate plasminogen and accelerate its conversion into plasmin.  Why A wrong: Fibrin is acted upon by plasmin, not activated by thrombolytics.  Why C wrong: Thrombin is not the activated target.  Why D wrong: Prothrombin is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the role of plasmin in thrombolysis?","options":["It forms new clots","It dissolves clots by acting on fibrin","It activates platelets","It converts fibrinogen to fibrin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states plasmin is a product that dissolves clots by acting on fibrin.  Why A wrong: Plasmin dissolves, not forms, clots.  Why C wrong: Plasmin does not activate platelets.  Why D wrong: That is thrombin's action, not plasmin's."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"By which route(s) are thrombolytic medicines given?","options":["Oral only","IV or intra-arterially","Subcutaneously only","Sublingually"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states thrombolytics are given by IV or intra-arterially.  Why A wrong: They are not given orally.  Why C wrong: Subcutaneous is not the stated route.  Why D wrong: Sublingual is not the stated route."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"The initial dose of a thrombolytic is given along with which drug?","options":["Aspirin","Heparin","Warfarin","Clopidogrel"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the initial dose is given with heparin.  Why A wrong: Aspirin is not the stated co-drug.  Why C wrong: Warfarin is not mentioned.  Why D wrong: Clopidogrel is an antiplatelet, not the stated co-drug."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Thrombolytic medicines should be given in a myocardial infarction (MI) within what time window to limit myocardial damage?","options":["Within 1-2 hours","Within 4-6 hours","Within 12-24 hours","Within 48 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states thrombolytics are given in MI within 4-6 hours to limit myocardial damage, restore blood flow, and prevent death.  Why A wrong: The stated window is 4-6 hours.  Why C wrong: Too late per the source.  Why D wrong: Too late per the source."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which of the following is an example of a thrombolytic medicine?","options":["Streptokinase","Aspirin","Clopidogrel","Metoprolol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists streptokinase and tissue plasminogen activator (t-PA) such as alteplase and reteplase as thrombolytics.  Why B wrong: Aspirin is an antiplatelet.  Why C wrong: Clopidogrel is an antiplatelet.  Why D wrong: Metoprolol is a beta blocker."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the most common major side effect of thrombolytic medicines?","options":["Constipation","Bleeding","Weight gain","Hyperglycaemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists bleeding (most common), dysrhythmias, and allergic reactions as major side effects.  Why A wrong: Not a listed side effect.  Why C wrong: Not a listed side effect.  Why D wrong: Not a listed side effect."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Before initiating thrombolytic therapy, invasive procedures such as venipuncture should be:","options":["Delayed until after therapy","Completed before initiating therapy","Performed during therapy","Avoided entirely"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to complete all invasive procedures like venipuncture before initiating therapy (to reduce bleeding risk).  Why A wrong: They should be done before, not after.  Why C wrong: Performing during therapy increases bleeding risk.  Why D wrong: They should be completed beforehand, not entirely avoided."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which laboratory values should be monitored during thrombolytic therapy?","options":["Blood glucose and HbA1c","PTT and fibrinogen blood level","Serum creatinine and BUN","Serum sodium and potassium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to observe for bleeding and monitor PTT and fibrinogen blood level.  Why A wrong: These are diabetes markers.  Why C wrong: These are renal markers.  Why D wrong: These are electrolyte markers."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the antidote for thrombolytics?","options":["Naloxone","Aminocaproic acid","Protamine sulphate","Vitamin K"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the antidote of thrombolytics is aminocaproic acid.  Why A wrong: Naloxone is the antidote for opioids.  Why C wrong: Protamine sulphate is the antidote for heparin.  Why D wrong: Vitamin K is the antidote for warfarin."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"After thrombolytic administration, for how long should direct pressure be applied to a puncture site?","options":["5-10 minutes","20-30 minutes","1-2 minutes","60 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to apply direct pressure on the puncture site for 20-30 minutes.  Why A wrong: Insufficient per the source.  Why C wrong: Far too short.  Why D wrong: Not the stated duration."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Anti-platelet medicines act by which mechanism?","options":["Dissolving existing clots","Inhibiting the aggregation of platelets, prolonging bleeding time","Activating plasminogen","Increasing fibrinogen levels"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states antiplatelets inhibit platelet aggregation (gathering), thereby prolonging bleeding time.  Why A wrong: Dissolving clots is the action of thrombolytics.  Why C wrong: Activating plasminogen is a thrombolytic action.  Why D wrong: Antiplatelets do not increase fibrinogen."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Anti-platelet medicines are contraindicated in which condition?","options":["Diabetes","Bleeding disorders","Hypertension","Asthma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states antiplatelets are contraindicated in bleeding disorders.  Why A wrong: Not the stated contraindication.  Why C wrong: Not the stated contraindication.  Why D wrong: Not the stated contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which of the following is an example of an anti-platelet medicine?","options":["Aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid)","Streptokinase","Heparin","Warfarin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid), clopidogrel, and dipyridamole as antiplatelets.  Why B wrong: Streptokinase is a thrombolytic.  Why C wrong: Heparin is an anticoagulant.  Why D wrong: Warfarin is an anticoagulant."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the most common side effect of anti-platelet medicines?","options":["GI bleeding","Constipation","Weight gain","Bradycardia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states side effects include GI bleeding (most common), bruising, haematuria, and tarry/black stool (melena).  Why B wrong: Not a listed side effect.  Why C wrong: Not a listed side effect.  Why D wrong: Not a listed side effect."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Blood in the urine, a side effect of anti-platelet medicines, is termed:","options":["Melena","Haematuria","Haematemesis","Epistaxis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source identifies haematuria as blood in the urine.  Why A wrong: Melena is tarry/black stool.  Why C wrong: Haematemesis is vomiting blood (not listed here).  Why D wrong: Epistaxis is a nosebleed (not listed here)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Tarry or black stool caused by anti-platelet medicines is called:","options":["Steatorrhoea","Melena","Haematochezia","Haematuria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source identifies tarry or black stool as melena.  Why A wrong: Steatorrhoea is fatty stool.  Why C wrong: Haematochezia is bright red blood (not the term used here).  Why D wrong: Haematuria is blood in urine."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which is the most common side effect associated with taking aspirin?","options":["Tinnitus","Constipation","Bradycardia","Hypertension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states tinnitus is the most common side effect when taking aspirin.  Why B wrong: Not the stated aspirin side effect.  Why C wrong: Not the stated aspirin side effect.  Why D wrong: Not the stated aspirin side effect."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Nitrates exert their anti-anginal effect primarily by:","options":["Vasodilation","Increasing heart rate","Increasing preload","Blocking calcium channels"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states nitrates act as vasodilation, decreasing preload, afterload, and myocardial oxygen consumption.  Why B wrong: Nitrates do not primarily increase heart rate.  Why C wrong: Nitrates decrease preload, not increase it.  Why D wrong: Calcium channel blockade is a different class's mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Nitrates reduce which of the following, thereby lowering cardiac work?","options":["Preload, afterload and myocardial oxygen consumption","Blood glucose","Serum cholesterol","Intracranial pressure only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states nitrates decrease preload, afterload, and reduce myocardial oxygen consumption.  Why B wrong: Not a nitrate effect.  Why C wrong: That is the action of anti-lipidemics.  Why D wrong: Nitrates can increase ICP (contraindicated in raised ICP)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which of the following is a contraindication to nitrate therapy?","options":["Hypertension","Hypotension and increased ICP","Angina pectoris","Hyperlipidaemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists contraindications as hypotension, increased ICP, anaemia, and use of erectile dysfunction medicines.  Why A wrong: Nitrates are used to lower BP; hypotension (not hypertension) is the contraindication.  Why C wrong: Angina is the indication for nitrates.  Why D wrong: Not a stated contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"A client on nitrates who is also taking erectile dysfunction medicines is at risk because nitrates are contraindicated with them. What is the primary concern?","options":["Severe hypotension","Hyperglycaemia","Bradycardia","Increased clotting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists erectile dysfunction medicines as a contraindication with nitrates (both lower BP, risking severe hypotension).  Why B wrong: Not the concern.  Why C wrong: Not the primary concern here.  Why D wrong: Nitrates do not increase clotting."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What are the most common side effects of nitrates?","options":["Headache, orthostatic hypotension, flushing, dizziness, and fainting","Constipation and weight gain","Bradycardia and hyperglycaemia","Tinnitus and haematuria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists headache, orthostatic hypotension, flushing, dizziness, and fainting.  Why B wrong: Not the listed nitrate side effects.  Why C wrong: Not the listed nitrate side effects.  Why D wrong: Tinnitus/haematuria are antiplatelet/aspirin effects."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which is an example of a sublingual nitrate medication?","options":["Isosorbide dinitrate (Sorbitrate)","Metformin","Streptokinase","Mannitol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives isosorbide dinitrate (Sorbitrate) as the sublingual example.  Why B wrong: Metformin is an oral hypoglycaemic.  Why C wrong: Streptokinase is a thrombolytic.  Why D wrong: Mannitol is an osmotic diuretic."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Before giving a sublingual nitrate, why should the nurse offer sips of water?","options":["To help swallow the tablet","Because dryness of the mouth may inhibit drug absorption","To dilute the medication","To prevent nausea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to offer sips of water before giving because dryness may inhibit drug absorption.  Why A wrong: Sublingual tablets are not swallowed.  Why C wrong: Water is not to dilute the drug.  Why D wrong: Not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the correct instruction for administering a sublingual nitrate tablet?","options":["Chew the tablet thoroughly","Place the medicine under the tongue and leave until fully dissolved; do not swallow","Swallow with a full glass of water","Crush and mix with food"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs to place the medicine under the tongue and leave until fully dissolved and not swallow the tablet.  Why A wrong: It is not chewed.  Why C wrong: It is not swallowed.  Why D wrong: It is not crushed/mixed."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"For a trans-lingual (spray) nitrate medication, the client should be instructed to:","options":["Inhale the spray deeply","Direct the spray against the oral mucosa and not inhale it","Spray it onto the skin","Swallow the spray"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs to direct the spray against the oral mucosa and not inhale the spray.  Why A wrong: The client should not inhale the spray.  Why C wrong: That would be transdermal, not trans-lingual.  Why D wrong: Not the stated instruction."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"A transdermal nitrate patch should be applied to what type of skin area? (NORCET-4.0)","options":["Hairy, moist area","Hairless, dry, and clean area","Broken or irritated skin","Directly over the heart only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to apply the patch on a hairless, dry, and clean area (NORCET-4.0).  Why A wrong: Hair impairs adhesion/absorption.  Why C wrong: Broken skin should be avoided.  Why D wrong: Not the stated requirement."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the most common site for applying a transdermal nitrate patch?","options":["Outer upper arm and chest","Inner thigh","Sole of the foot","Back of the neck"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common site is the outer upper arm and chest.  Why B wrong: Not the stated site.  Why C wrong: Not the stated site.  Why D wrong: Not the stated site."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"During an anginal attack, what is the priority (first) nursing action at the hospital?","options":["Immediately assess the client","Administer IV fluids","Call the family","Document the event"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists \"Immediately assess the client\" as the first priority nursing action during an anginal attack.  Why B wrong: Not the first listed action.  Why C wrong: Not a priority action.  Why D wrong: Documentation follows care, not first."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"During an anginal attack, which medication is given by the sublingual route?","options":["Nitroglycerin/sorbitrate tablet","Aspirin tablet","Metformin","Heparin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to give a nitroglycerin/sorbitrate tablet by sub-lingual route.  Why B wrong: Aspirin is not given sublingually in this protocol.  Why C wrong: Metformin is an oral hypoglycaemic.  Why D wrong: Heparin is parenteral."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"After giving a sublingual nitrate during an anginal attack, the nurse should stay with the client and reassess after how many minutes?","options":["1 minute","5 minutes","15 minutes","30 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to stay with the client and reassess after 5 minutes.  Why A wrong: Too soon per the source.  Why C wrong: 15 minutes is the total maximum-dose window, not the reassessment interval.  Why D wrong: Not the stated interval."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"If pain is not resolved 5 minutes after the first NTG dose, the nurse should give another NTG tablet provided that:","options":["The heart rate is above 100","The blood pressure is stable","The client requests it","Oxygen is running"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to give another NTG tablet after 5 minutes if pain is not resolved and BP is stable.  Why A wrong: The condition is stable BP, not high HR.  Why C wrong: Not the stated criterion.  Why D wrong: Not the stated criterion."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Why should blood pressure be checked before giving additional NTG tablets?","options":["Because NTG can cause hypertension","Because NTG can cause hypotension","Because NTG increases heart rate","Because NTG causes bradycardia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states BP should be checked before giving 2 tablets because NTG medicines can cause hypotension.  Why A wrong: NTG lowers, not raises, BP.  Why C wrong: Not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: Not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the maximum dose of nitroglycerin/sorbitrate that may be given during an anginal attack?","options":["5 mg (three tablets) within 15 minutes","10 mg (two tablets) within 30 minutes","3 mg (one tablet) within 5 minutes","15 mg (five tablets) within 15 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the maximum dose of nitroglycerin/sorbitrate is 5 mg, three tablets, within 15 minutes.  Why B wrong: Incorrect dose and interval.  Why C wrong: Incorrect dose and interval.  Why D wrong: Incorrect dose."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"If pain is not relieved after the third nitroglycerin tablet, what should the nurse do?","options":["Give a fourth tablet immediately","Reassess the client and contact the doctor","Discharge the client","Withhold all medications for one hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to reassess the client and contact the doctor if the 3rd tablet also does not relieve the pain.  Why A wrong: The maximum is three tablets.  Why C wrong: Unsafe and not indicated.  Why D wrong: Not the stated action."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"During anginal therapy with nitrates, how should the foot end of the bed be positioned to prevent hypotension?","options":["Kept flat","Kept lightly elevated","Lowered below the head","Fully raised at 90 degrees"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the foot end of the bed should be kept lightly elevated to prevent hypotension.  Why A wrong: Not the stated position.  Why C wrong: Would worsen hypotension.  Why D wrong: Not the stated position."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Anti-dysrhythmic medicines are used to treat:","options":["Abnormal variation in heart rate and rhythm (dysrhythmia)","High cholesterol","Bleeding disorders","Seizures"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states anti-dysrhythmic medicines treat abnormal variation in heart rate and rhythm and dysrhythmia.  Why B wrong: That is anti-lipidemics.  Why C wrong: Not their use.  Why D wrong: That is anti-convulsants."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Class-I anti-dysrhythmic medicines act as which type of channel blockers?","options":["Sodium channel blockers","Potassium channel blockers","Calcium channel blockers","Beta blockers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Class-I are sodium channel blockers.  Why B wrong: Potassium channel blockers are Class-III.  Why C wrong: Calcium channel blockers are Class-IV.  Why D wrong: Beta blockers are Class-II."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which drugs are examples of Class-IA anti-dysrhythmics?","options":["Disopyramide, procainamide, quinidine sulphate","Lidocaine, phenytoin","Flecainide","Amiodarone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists disopyramide, procainamide, and quinidine sulphate as Class-IA.  Why B wrong: Lidocaine and phenytoin are Class-IB.  Why C wrong: Flecainide is Class-IC.  Why D wrong: Amiodarone is Class-III."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which drugs are examples of Class-IB anti-dysrhythmics?","options":["Lidocaine and phenytoin","Disopyramide and quinidine","Flecainide","Verapamil and diltiazem"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists lidocaine and phenytoin as Class-IB.  Why B wrong: These are Class-IA.  Why C wrong: Flecainide is Class-IC.  Why D wrong: These are Class-IV (calcium channel blockers)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which drug is an example of a Class-IC anti-dysrhythmic?","options":["Flecainide","Lidocaine","Amiodarone","Esmolol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists flecainide as Class-IC.  Why B wrong: Lidocaine is Class-IB.  Why C wrong: Amiodarone is Class-III.  Why D wrong: Esmolol is Class-II (beta blocker)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Class-II anti-dysrhythmic medicines belong to which drug class?","options":["Beta blockers","Sodium channel blockers","Potassium channel blockers","Calcium channel blockers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Class-II are beta blockers.  Why B wrong: Class-I are sodium channel blockers.  Why C wrong: Class-III are potassium channel blockers.  Why D wrong: Class-IV are calcium channel blockers."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which of the following are examples of Class-II (beta blocker) anti-dysrhythmics?","options":["Esmolol, propranolol and metoprolol","Amiodarone","Verapamil, diltiazem","Lidocaine, phenytoin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists esmolol, propranolol, and metoprolol as Class-II examples.  Why B wrong: Amiodarone is Class-III.  Why C wrong: Verapamil and diltiazem are Class-IV.  Why D wrong: Lidocaine and phenytoin are Class-IB."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Class-III anti-dysrhythmic medicines act as which type of channel blockers?","options":["Potassium channel blockers","Sodium channel blockers","Calcium channel blockers","Beta blockers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Class-III are potassium channel blockers (example: amiodarone).  Why B wrong: That is Class-I.  Why C wrong: That is Class-IV.  Why D wrong: That is Class-II."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Amiodarone is an example of which class of anti-dysrhythmic?","options":["Class-III (potassium channel blocker)","Class-I (sodium channel blocker)","Class-II (beta blocker)","Class-IV (calcium channel blocker)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists amiodarone as an example of Class-III potassium channel blockers.  Why B wrong: Incorrect class.  Why C wrong: Incorrect class.  Why D wrong: Incorrect class."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Class-IV anti-dysrhythmic medicines are which type of blockers?","options":["Calcium channel blockers","Potassium channel blockers","Sodium channel blockers","Beta blockers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Class-IV are calcium channel blockers (examples: verapamil, diltiazem, amlodipine).  Why B wrong: That is Class-III.  Why C wrong: That is Class-I.  Why D wrong: That is Class-II."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which drugs are examples of Class-IV (calcium channel blocker) anti-dysrhythmics?","options":["Verapamil, diltiazem and amlodipine","Esmolol, propranolol","Amiodarone","Quinidine, procainamide"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists verapamil, diltiazem, and amlodipine as Class-IV examples.  Why B wrong: These are Class-II beta blockers.  Why C wrong: Amiodarone is Class-III.  Why D wrong: These are Class-IA."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the most common side effect of all anti-dysrhythmic medicines?","options":["Hypotension due to decreased cardiac output and vasodilation","Hyperglycaemia","Tinnitus","Constipation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common side effect is hypotension due to decreased cardiac output and vasodilation.  Why B wrong: Not the stated common side effect.  Why C wrong: Not the stated common side effect.  Why D wrong: Not the stated common side effect."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Class-II beta blockers, when used as anti-dysrhythmics, most notably cause:","options":["Bradycardia","Tachycardia","Hyperglycaemia","Bronchodilation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Class-II beta blockers cause bradycardia.  Why B wrong: Beta blockers slow the heart.  Why C wrong: Not the stated effect.  Why D wrong: Beta blockers can cause bronchospasm, not bronchodilation."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Nursing care during anti-dysrhythmic therapy specifically includes monitoring which vital parameters?","options":["Heart rate and blood pressure","Respiratory rate and temperature only","Blood glucose and urine output","Weight and height"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to monitor vital parameters, especially HR and BP, plus general nursing care.  Why B wrong: HR and BP are specified.  Why C wrong: Not the specified parameters.  Why D wrong: Not the specified parameters."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Anti-lipidemic medicines reduce the serum level of which substances?","options":["Cholesterol, triglyceride, or LDL","Glucose and insulin","Sodium and potassium","Fibrinogen and platelets"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states anti-lipidemics reduce serum cholesterol, triglyceride, or LDL.  Why B wrong: These are diabetes-related.  Why C wrong: These are electrolytes.  Why D wrong: These relate to clotting."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"According to the source, total cholesterol level should be less than what value? (ESIC, RPSC-2009)","options":["Less than 200 mg/dl","Less than 100 mg/dl","Less than 300 mg/dl","Less than 40 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states total cholesterol level should be less than 200 mg/dl (ESIC, RPSC-2009).  Why B wrong: That is the target for LDL, not total cholesterol.  Why C wrong: Exceeds the healthy limit.  Why D wrong: That is a lower bound of the HDL range."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Increased cholesterol level is known as:","options":["Hyperlipidaemia","Hyperglycaemia","Hypercalcaemia","Hyperkalaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states increased cholesterol level is known as hyperlipidaemia.  Why B wrong: That is high blood glucose.  Why C wrong: That is high calcium.  Why D wrong: That is high potassium."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Bile sequestrants lower cholesterol by which mechanism?","options":["Binding acids in the intestine to prevent re-absorption of cholesterol","Inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase","Stimulating insulin release","Blocking sodium channels"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states bile sequestrants bind the acids in the intestine that prevent re-absorption of cholesterol (e.g. cholestyramine).  Why B wrong: That is the statin mechanism.  Why C wrong: That is a sulfonylurea action.  Why D wrong: That is a Class-I anti-dysrhythmic action."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which drug is an example of a bile sequestrant?","options":["Cholestyramine","Atorvastatin","Metformin","Amiodarone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives cholestyramine as the bile sequestrant example.  Why B wrong: Atorvastatin is a statin (HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor).  Why C wrong: Metformin is an oral hypoglycaemic.  Why D wrong: Amiodarone is an anti-dysrhythmic."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors are commonly known as:","options":["Statins","Fibrates","Sequestrants","Nitrates"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors are statins, most commonly used for lowering the cholesterol serum level, e.g. atorvastatin.  Why B wrong: Fibrates are a separate class not named here.  Why C wrong: Sequestrants bind bile acids.  Why D wrong: Nitrates are anti-anginals."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which drug is an example of an HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor (statin)?","options":["Atorvastatin","Cholestyramine","Glimepiride","Repaglinide"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives atorvastatin as the statin example.  Why B wrong: Cholestyramine is a bile sequestrant.  Why C wrong: Glimepiride is a sulfonylurea.  Why D wrong: Repaglinide is a meglitinide."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"According to the source, what is the most dangerous side effect of statins, requiring an annual eye check-up?","options":["Cataracts","Glaucoma","Retinal detachment","Macular degeneration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most dangerous side effect of statins is cataracts, so an annual eye check-up should be done. (Note: clinically, myopathy/rhabdomyolysis is the classic dangerous statin effect, but per source fidelity the answer is cataracts.)  Why B wrong: Not the stated side effect.  Why C wrong: Not the stated side effect.  Why D wrong: Not the stated side effect."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"HDL cholesterol is also known as:","options":["Good cholesterol","Bad cholesterol","Chylomicron","Triglyceride"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states HDL (high density lipoprotein) is also known as 'good cholesterol.'  Why B wrong: LDL is 'bad cholesterol.'  Why C wrong: ULDL is also known as chylomicrons.  Why D wrong: Triglyceride is a separate lipid."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the normal HDL level, and what level is considered good for health?","options":["40 to 60 mg/dl; >60 mg/dl for good health","Less than 100 mg/dl; >100 mg/dl for good health","30 mg/dl; >30 mg/dl for good health","Less than 200 mg/dl; >200 mg/dl for good health"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states HDL normal level is 40 to 60 mg/dl and should be >60 mg/dl for good health.  Why B wrong: That describes LDL values.  Why C wrong: That describes VLDL.  Why D wrong: That describes total cholesterol."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the function of HDL?","options":["It absorbs cholesterol and carries it back to the liver for re-filtration","It carries cholesterol from liver to peripheral tissues","It brings triglycerides from liver to peripheral organs","It transfers lipids from intestine to other organs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states HDL absorbs cholesterol and carries it back to the liver for re-filtration.  Why B wrong: That is the LDL function.  Why C wrong: That is the VLDL function.  Why D wrong: That is the ULDL function."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"LDL cholesterol is also known as:","options":["Bad cholesterol","Good cholesterol","Chylomicron","Triglyceride"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states LDL (low density lipoprotein) is also known as 'Bad Cholesterol.'  Why B wrong: HDL is good cholesterol.  Why C wrong: ULDL is chylomicrons.  Why D wrong: Triglyceride is a separate lipid."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the normal (healthy) LDL level, and what level is risky for MI?","options":["Less than 100 mg/dl normal; above 100 mg/dl risky for MI","40 to 60 mg/dl normal; above 60 mg/dl risky","30 mg/dl normal; above 30 mg/dl risky","Less than 200 mg/dl normal; above 200 mg/dl risky"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states LDL normal level for good health is less than 100 mg/dl, and above 100 mg/dl is risky for MI.  Why B wrong: That describes HDL.  Why C wrong: That describes VLDL.  Why D wrong: That describes total cholesterol."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the function of LDL?","options":["It carries cholesterol from the liver to the peripheral tissues or cells","It absorbs cholesterol and returns it to the liver","It brings triglycerides from liver to peripheral organs","It transfers lipids from intestine to other organs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states LDL carries cholesterol from liver to the peripheral tissues or cells.  Why B wrong: That is the HDL function.  Why C wrong: That is the VLDL function.  Why D wrong: That is the ULDL function."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the function of VLDL (very-low density lipoprotein)?","options":["Brings triglycerides from the liver to peripheral organs or cells","Returns cholesterol to the liver","Carries cholesterol from liver to peripheral cells","Transfers lipids from intestine to other organs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states VLDL brings triglycerides from the liver to peripheral organs or cells.  Why B wrong: That is HDL.  Why C wrong: That is LDL.  Why D wrong: That is ULDL."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the normal (healthy) VLDL level, and what level is risky for MI?","options":["30 mg/dl normal; above 30 mg/dl risky for MI","Less than 100 mg/dl normal; above 100 mg/dl risky","40 to 60 mg/dl normal; above 60 mg/dl risky","Less than 200 mg/dl normal; above 200 mg/dl risky"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states VLDL normal level for health is 30 mg/dl and risky for MI above 30 mg/dl.  Why B wrong: That describes LDL.  Why C wrong: That describes HDL.  Why D wrong: That describes total cholesterol."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"ULDL (ultra-low density lipoprotein) is also known as:","options":["Chylomicrons","Good cholesterol","Bad cholesterol","Bile acids"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ULDL is also known as chylomicrons.  Why B wrong: HDL is good cholesterol.  Why C wrong: LDL is bad cholesterol.  Why D wrong: Bile acids are unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"ULDL (chylomicrons) consist of approximately what percentage of triglycerides?","options":["80%","50%","30%","100%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ULDL consists of 80% triglycerides.  Why B wrong: Incorrect per source.  Why C wrong: Incorrect per source.  Why D wrong: Incorrect per source."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the function of ULDL (chylomicrons)?","options":["Transfer lipids from the intestine to other organs","Return cholesterol to the liver","Carry cholesterol from liver to peripheral cells","Bring triglycerides from liver to peripheral organs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ULDL transfers lipids from the intestine to other organs.  Why B wrong: That is HDL.  Why C wrong: That is LDL.  Why D wrong: That is VLDL."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Inotropic effects of the heart are related to which cardiac property?","options":["Cardiac contraction (force/strength)","Heart rate and rhythm","Conduction velocity","Heart relaxation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states inotropic effects are related to cardiac contraction; they act on calcium to increase or decrease muscular contraction strength.  Why B wrong: That is chronotropic.  Why C wrong: That is dromotropic.  Why D wrong: That is lusitropic."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Positive inotropes affect the heart by:","options":["Increasing the force or strength of muscular contraction","Decreasing cardiac contraction strength","Slowing conduction velocity","Decreasing heart rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states positive inotropes increase the force or strength of muscular contraction.  Why B wrong: That is negative inotropes.  Why C wrong: That is a dromotropic effect.  Why D wrong: That is a chronotropic effect."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which of the following is an example of a positive inotrope?","options":["Digoxin","Propranolol","Verapamil","Procainamide"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists digoxin, digitalis, dopamine, dobutamine, NORAD, adrenaline, calcium, and insulin as positive inotropes.  Why B wrong: Propranolol (beta blocker) is a negative inotrope.  Why C wrong: Verapamil (CCB) is a negative inotrope.  Why D wrong: Procainamide (Class-I anti-dysrhythmic) is a negative inotrope."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a positive inotrope in the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Digoxin","Dobutamine","Insulin","Diltiazem","Adrenaline"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists digoxin, digitalis, dopamine, dobutamine, NORAD, adrenaline, calcium, and insulin as positive inotropes.  Why D wrong: Diltiazem (non-dihydropyridine CCB) is listed as a negative inotrope."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Negative inotropes affect the heart by:","options":["Decreasing the force/strength of contraction and decreasing cardiac workload","Increasing the force of contraction","Increasing heart rate","Increasing conduction velocity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states negative inotropes decrease the force/strength of contraction and decrease cardiac workload.  Why B wrong: That is positive inotropes.  Why C wrong: That is a chronotropic effect.  Why D wrong: That is a dromotropic effect."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which of the following are examples of negative inotropes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Beta blockers (propranolol, atenolol, timolol)","Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (diltiazem, verapamil)","Class-1 anti-dysrhythmics (procainamide, quinidine sulphate)","Digoxin","Dobutamine"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists beta blockers, non-dihydropyridine CCBs, and Class-1 anti-dysrhythmics as negative inotropes.  Why D wrong: Digoxin is a positive inotrope.  Why E wrong: Dobutamine is a positive inotrope."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Chronotropic effects of the heart are related to:","options":["Heart rate and rhythm","Cardiac contraction force","Conduction velocity","Heart relaxation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states chronotropic effects are related to heart rate and rhythm.  Why B wrong: That is inotropic.  Why C wrong: That is dromotropic.  Why D wrong: That is lusitropic."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Positive chronotropes act on which structure to increase heart rate and rhythm?","options":["The SA node","The AV node","The bundle of His","Purkinje fibres"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states positive chronotropes act on the electrical conduction system of the SA node and increase heart rate and rhythm.  Why B wrong: Negative chronotropes act on the AV node.  Why C wrong: Not the stated structure.  Why D wrong: Not the stated structure."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which of the following are examples of positive chronotropes?","options":["Atropine sulphate, epinephrine and dopamine","Diltiazem and verapamil","Digoxin and acetylcholine","Propranolol and atenolol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists atropine sulphate, epinephrine, and dopamine as positive chronotropes.  Why B wrong: These are negative chronotropes (non-dihydropyridine CCBs).  Why C wrong: These are negative chronotropes.  Why D wrong: Beta blockers are negative chronotropes."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Negative chronotropes act on which structure to decrease heart rate and rhythm?","options":["The AV node","The SA node","Purkinje fibres","The atria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states negative chronotropes act on the electrical conduction system of the AV node and decrease heart rate and rhythm.  Why B wrong: The SA node is acted on by positive chronotropes.  Why C wrong: Not the stated structure.  Why D wrong: Not the stated structure."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which of the following are examples of negative chronotropes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (diltiazem, verapamil)","Beta blockers (metoprolol, propranolol, atenolol)","Digoxin, digitalis, acetylcholine","Atropine sulphate","Vagal stimulation"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists non-dihydropyridine CCBs, beta blockers, digoxin/digitalis/acetylcholine, and vagal stimulation as negative chronotropes.  Why D wrong: Atropine sulphate is a positive chronotrope."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Dromotropic effects act on which cardiac property?","options":["Conduction velocity or speed of conductivity","Contraction force","Heart rate","Heart relaxation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states dromotropic effects act on the conduction velocity or speed of conductivity.  Why B wrong: That is inotropic.  Why C wrong: That is chronotropic.  Why D wrong: That is lusitropic."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Positive dromotropes act on the AV node to:","options":["Increase the speed of conductivity","Decrease the speed of conductivity","Increase contraction force","Slow the heart rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states positive dromotropes act on the AV node and increase speed of conductivity (e.g. epinephrine, vagal stimulation).  Why B wrong: That is negative dromotropes.  Why C wrong: That is inotropic.  Why D wrong: That is chronotropic."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which are examples of positive dromotropes?","options":["Epinephrine and vagal stimulation","Digoxin and diltiazem","Verapamil and digitalis","Propranolol and atenolol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists epinephrine and vagal stimulation as positive dromotropes. (Note: source lists vagal stimulation, though physiologically vagal stimulation slows AV conduction; keyed per source fidelity.)  Why B wrong: These are negative dromotropes.  Why C wrong: These are negative dromotropes.  Why D wrong: Not listed as positive dromotropes."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which of the following are examples of negative dromotropes?","options":["Digoxin, digitalis and non-dihydropyridine CCBs (diltiazem, verapamil)","Epinephrine and vagal stimulation","Atropine sulphate","Dopamine and dobutamine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists digoxin, digitalis, and non-dihydropyridine CCBs (diltiazem, verapamil) as negative dromotropes.  Why B wrong: These are positive dromotropes.  Why C wrong: Atropine is a positive chronotrope.  Why D wrong: These are positive inotropes."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Bathmotropic effects refer to the effect of cardiac nerves on which property?","options":["Cardiac excitability","Cardiac contraction","Heart rate","Conduction speed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states bathmotropic effects are the effects of the cardiac nerves on cardiac excitability.  Why B wrong: That is inotropic.  Why C wrong: That is chronotropic.  Why D wrong: That is dromotropic."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"A positive bathmotropic effect does what to muscle response to stimulation?","options":["Increases the response of muscle to stimulation","Decreases the response of muscle to stimulation","Slows conduction","Relaxes the heart"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states positive bathmotropic effects increase the response of muscle to stimulation.  Why B wrong: That is a negative bathmotropic effect.  Why C wrong: That relates to dromotropy.  Why D wrong: That relates to lusitropy."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"A negative bathmotropic effect does what to muscle response to stimulation?","options":["Decreases the response of muscle to stimulation","Increases the response of muscle to stimulation","Increases contraction force","Increases heart rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states negative bathmotropic effects decrease the response of muscle to stimulation.  Why B wrong: That is a positive bathmotropic effect.  Why C wrong: That is inotropy.  Why D wrong: That is chronotropy."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Lusitropic effects are related to which cardiac property?","options":["Heart relaxation","Heart rate","Contraction force","Conduction velocity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states lusitropic effects are related to heart relaxation.  Why B wrong: That is chronotropic.  Why C wrong: That is inotropic.  Why D wrong: That is dromotropic."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Adrenergic agonists produce which physiological effect?","options":["Stimulation or activation of sympathetic nervous system function","Activation of the parasympathetic nervous system","Blockade of beta receptors","Inhibition of insulin release"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states adrenergic agonists stimulate/activate sympathetic nervous system function and are a subtype of the sympathomimetic group.  Why B wrong: They act on the sympathetic, not parasympathetic, system.  Why C wrong: They are agonists, not blockers.  Why D wrong: Not their action."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Dopamine increases BP and cardiac output through which effect, and it also increases what blood flow?","options":["Positive inotropic effect; increases renal blood flow","Negative inotropic effect; increases cerebral blood flow","Vasodilation; decreases renal blood flow","Beta blockade; increases coronary flow"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states dopamine increases BP and cardiac output through a positive inotropic effect and increases renal blood flow.  Why B wrong: It is a positive, not negative, inotrope.  Why C wrong: It increases renal blood flow.  Why D wrong: Dopamine is not a beta blocker."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Dobutamine increases myocardial force and cardiac output through stimulation of which receptors and is the DOC for which condition?","options":["Beta receptors (positive inotropic); DOC in congestive heart failure","Alpha receptors; DOC in hypertension","Beta receptors; DOC in asthma","Dopamine receptors; DOC in Parkinson's"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states dobutamine increases myocardial force and cardiac output through stimulation of beta receptors (positive inotropic) and is the DOC in congestive heart failure.  Why B wrong: Not the stated mechanism or use.  Why C wrong: Not the stated use.  Why D wrong: Not the stated use."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Epinephrine is naturally a hormone of which gland, and is used for which cardiac emergency?","options":["Adrenal gland cortex; used for stimulation in cardiac arrest","Thyroid gland; used for bradycardia","Pituitary gland; used for hypotension","Pancreas; used for diabetes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states epinephrine is naturally an adrenal gland (cortex) hormone, used for stimulation in cardiac arrest, and for broncho-dilation in asthma patients.  Why B wrong: Wrong gland and use.  Why C wrong: Wrong gland.  Why D wrong: Wrong gland and use."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Besides cardiac arrest, epinephrine is used for what purpose in asthma patients?","options":["Broncho-dilation","Bronchoconstriction","Reducing mucus","Sedation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states epinephrine is used for broncho-dilation for asthma patients.  Why B wrong: It dilates, not constricts, bronchi.  Why C wrong: Not the stated use.  Why D wrong: Not the stated use."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Norepinephrine (NORAD) is used to stimulate the heart in cardiac arrest because it is a:","options":["Vasoconstrictor that increases BP in hypotension and shock","Vasodilator that lowers BP","Positive chronotrope only","Bronchodilator"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states NORAD stimulates the heart in cardiac arrest, is a vasoconstrictor, and increases BP in hypotension and shock.  Why B wrong: It is a vasoconstrictor, not vasodilator.  Why C wrong: Its stated action is vasoconstriction and increasing BP.  Why D wrong: Not the stated action."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Nursing care during adrenergic agonist/cardiac therapy includes monitoring which parameters?","options":["Vital signs (especially BP and HR), lung sounds, urinary output, and ECG","Blood glucose and HbA1c only","Serum cholesterol only","Fibrinogen levels only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to monitor vital signs (especially BP and HR), lung sounds, urinary output, ECG, and initiate general nursing care.  Why B wrong: Not the stated parameters.  Why C wrong: Not the stated parameters.  Why D wrong: Not the stated parameters."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Anaesthetics are defined as a group of drugs that produce:","options":["Reversible loss of pain, sensation, and consciousness","Permanent loss of consciousness","Increased pain sensitivity","Muscle contraction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states anaesthetics bring reversible loss of pain, sensation, and consciousness.  Why B wrong: The loss is reversible, not permanent.  Why C wrong: They reduce, not increase, pain.  Why D wrong: Not their action."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Opioid analgesics relieve pain by which mechanism?","options":["Suppressing pain impulses","Blocking sodium channels in the heart","Inhibiting platelet aggregation","Stimulating insulin release"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states opioid analgesics suppress pain impulses.  Why B wrong: That is a Class-I anti-dysrhythmic action.  Why C wrong: That is an antiplatelet action.  Why D wrong: That is a sulfonylurea action."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the major side effect of opioid analgesics?","options":["Respiratory depression","Hypertension","Hyperglycaemia","Tachycardia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the major side effect is respiratory depression, and they suppress coughing by acting on the respiratory and cough centre in the medulla of the midbrain.  Why B wrong: Not the major opioid side effect.  Why C wrong: Not the major opioid side effect.  Why D wrong: Not the major opioid side effect."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Because opioids suppress coughing, they are used in which type of preparation?","options":["Cough syrups","Anti-hypertensives","Insulin injections","Anticoagulants"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states due to their cough-suppression effect, opioids are used in cough syrups.  Why B wrong: Not a stated use.  Why C wrong: Not a stated use.  Why D wrong: Not a stated use."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Opioid analgesics can cause which of the following psychological/physical effects?","options":["Euphoria and physical dependency","Depression and weight loss","Insomnia and hyperactivity","Hyperglycaemia and polyuria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states opioid analgesics can cause euphoria and physical dependency.  Why B wrong: Not the stated effects.  Why C wrong: Not the stated effects.  Why D wrong: Not the stated effects."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Codeine sulphate is used in low dose primarily as a:","options":["Cough suppressant","Anticoagulant","Antihypertensive","Antidysrhythmic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states codeine sulphate is also used as a cough suppressant in low dose, with the most common side effect being constipation.  Why B wrong: Not its use.  Why C wrong: Not its use.  Why D wrong: Not its use."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Meperidine is used to treat which type of pain and as which type of medicine?","options":["Acute pain and as a preoperative medicine","Chronic pain only","Neuropathic pain only","Cancer pain only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states meperidine is used to treat acute pain and as a preoperative medicine.  Why B wrong: Not the stated use.  Why C wrong: Not the stated use.  Why D wrong: Morphine (not meperidine) is emphasized for cancer pain."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What are the most common side effects of meperidine?","options":["Increased ICP if head injury, hypotension, urinary retention, and tachycardia","Bradycardia and hyperglycaemia","Tinnitus and haematuria","Weight gain and lactic acidosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists increased ICP (if head injury), hypotension, urinary retention, and tachycardia as meperidine's most common side effects.  Why B wrong: Not the listed effects.  Why C wrong: Those are aspirin/antiplatelet effects.  Why D wrong: Those are diabetes-drug effects."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Meperidine is contraindicated in which conditions?","options":["Increased ICP, hypotension, shock, and alcoholism","Hypertension and diabetes","Asthma and glaucoma","Anaemia and bleeding disorders"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states meperidine is contraindicated in increased ICP, hypotension, shock, and alcoholism.  Why B wrong: Not the stated contraindications.  Why C wrong: Not the stated contraindications.  Why D wrong: Not the stated contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Nalbuphine is preferred for treating acute pain from MI and cancer because it:","options":["Reduces the oxygen needs of the heart without reducing blood pressure","Increases blood pressure","Increases heart rate","Has no side effects"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states nalbuphine is preferred for acute pain in MI and cancer because it reduces the oxygen needs of the heart without reducing blood pressure.  Why B wrong: Not the stated reason.  Why C wrong: Not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Hydromorphone acts to depress respiration and its most common side effect is:","options":["Constipation","Diarrhoea","Tachycardia","Hyperglycaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states hydromorphone acts as depress respiration, with the most common side effect being constipation.  Why B wrong: Opioids cause constipation, not diarrhoea.  Why C wrong: Not the stated side effect.  Why D wrong: Not the stated side effect."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Morphine sulphate is used for acute pain caused by MI or cancer, and also for dyspnoea caused by which condition?","options":["Pulmonary oedema, surgery, and preoperative medication","Asthma only","Pneumonia only","Bronchitis only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states morphine sulphate is used for acute pain by MI or cancer and for dyspnoea caused by pulmonary oedema, surgery, and preoperative medication.  Why B wrong: Not the stated use.  Why C wrong: Not the stated use.  Why D wrong: Not the stated use."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which of the following is the most severe side effect of opioids?","options":["Respiratory depression","Orthostatic hypotension","Constipation","Sedation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states respiratory depression is the most severe opioid side effect (along with orthostatic hypotension, constipation).  Why B wrong: A side effect but not the most severe.  Why C wrong: A side effect but not the most severe.  Why D wrong: A side effect but not the most severe."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Reduction in pupil size (miosis) or pinpoint pupils in a patient on opioids indicates:","options":["Morphine overdose","Opioid withdrawal","Hypoglycaemia","Cardiac arrest"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pinpoint pupil (miosis) indicates morphine overdose, along with nausea and vomiting.  Why B wrong: Withdrawal causes dilated (mydriatic) pupils.  Why C wrong: Not indicated by pupil size here.  Why D wrong: Not the stated indication."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Morphine is contraindicated in which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Severe respiratory disorders","Head injuries","Increased ICP","Mild headache","Severe renal, hepatic or pulmonary diseases"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source states morphine is contraindicated in severe respiratory disorders, head injuries, increased ICP, and severe renal, hepatic, or pulmonary diseases.  Why D wrong: Mild headache is not a listed contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"When monitoring a client on opioids, at what respiratory rate should the nurse withhold the medication and notify the doctor immediately?","options":["If respiratory rate is <12 breaths/minute in an adult","If respiratory rate is <20 breaths/minute","If respiratory rate is >20 breaths/minute","If respiratory rate is <30 breaths/minute"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to monitor respiratory rate and if it is <12 breaths/minute in an adult, withhold the medication and notify the doctor immediately.  Why B wrong: Incorrect threshold.  Why C wrong: A high rate is not the withholding criterion.  Why D wrong: Incorrect threshold."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Why should breath sounds be monitored in a client receiving opioids?","options":["Because opioids suppress the cough reflex","Because opioids cause bronchodilation","Because opioids increase secretions","Because opioids cause pneumonia directly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to monitor breath sounds because opioids suppress the cough reflex.  Why B wrong: Not the stated reason.  Why C wrong: Not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: Not the direct reason stated."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which activities are encouraged in a client on opioids to prevent atelectasis and pneumonia?","options":["Turning, deep breathing, and incentive spirometry","Bed rest with no movement","Fluid restriction","Chest percussion only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to encourage turning, deep breathing, and incentive spirometry to prevent atelectasis and pneumonia.  Why B wrong: Immobility increases risk.  Why C wrong: Not the stated action.  Why D wrong: Not the stated action."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the antidote for opioid toxicity? (AIIMS, NHM Staff Nurse)","options":["Naloxone","Aminocaproic acid","Protamine sulphate","Flumazenil"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the antidote for opioid toxicity is naloxone (AIIMS, NHM Staff Nurse).  Why B wrong: Aminocaproic acid is the antidote for thrombolytics.  Why C wrong: Protamine sulphate is the antidote for heparin.  Why D wrong: Flumazenil is the antidote for benzodiazepines (not stated here)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the mode of action of anti-convulsant medicines?","options":["To depress abnormal neuronal discharge and prevent spread of seizures to adjacent neurons","To stimulate neuronal discharge","To block platelet aggregation","To increase dopamine in the brain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states anti-convulsants depress abnormal neuronal discharge and prevent spread of seizures to adjacent neurons.  Why B wrong: They depress, not stimulate, discharge.  Why C wrong: That is an antiplatelet action.  Why D wrong: That is an anti-Parkinsonian action."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which of the following is an example of a hydantoin anti-convulsant?","options":["Fosphenytoin and phenytoin","Diazepam and lorazepam","Valproic acid","Levodopa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists fosphenytoin and phenytoin as hydantoin examples.  Why B wrong: These are benzodiazepines.  Why C wrong: Valproic acid is a valproate.  Why D wrong: Levodopa is a dopaminergic anti-Parkinsonian."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Phenytoin (a hydantoin) is used to treat which type of seizures?","options":["Partial and generalised tonic-clonic seizures","Absence seizures only","Febrile seizures only","Myoclonic seizures only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states phenytoin is used to treat partial and generalised tonic and clonic seizures.  Why B wrong: Benzodiazepines treat absence seizures per the source.  Why C wrong: Not the stated use.  Why D wrong: Not the stated use."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Besides seizures, phenytoin is also used to treat which condition?","options":["Dysrhythmias","Hypertension","Diabetes","Bleeding disorders"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states phenytoin is also used to treat dysrhythmias (it is a Class-IB anti-dysrhythmic).  Why B wrong: Not a stated use.  Why C wrong: Not a stated use.  Why D wrong: Not a stated use."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"The \"TRICK-7H\" for phenytoin side effects includes all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Hypertrophy of gingiva (gingival hyperplasia)","Hirsutism","Hyperglycaemia","Hypotension"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: TRICK-7H lists hypertrophy, hyperplasia of gingiva, hirsutism, hyperglycaemia, hypocalcaemia, hypersensitivity, and headache — hypotension is NOT one of the 7 Hs.  Why A wrong: It IS a listed 7H side effect.  Why B wrong: It IS a listed 7H side effect.  Why C wrong: It IS a listed 7H side effect."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which of the following are the \"7H\" side effects of phenytoin? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hypertrophy / hyperplasia of gingiva","Hirsutism","Hyperglycaemia","Hypocalcaemia","Hypersensitivity"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source's TRICK-7H lists hypertrophy, hyperplasia of gingiva, hirsutism, hyperglycaemia, hypocalcaemia, hypersensitivity, and headache — all listed options are correct.  Why (none wrong): All five options are among the 7 Hs."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Other side effects of phenytoin include all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Nystagmus and blurred vision","Alopecia","Decreased platelets and WBC's","Increased absorption of Vitamin B12"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The source states phenytoin causes DECREASED absorption of Vitamin B12 (not increased), along with nystagmus, blurred vision, alopecia, decreased platelets/WBCs, and purple glove syndrome.  Why A wrong: These ARE listed side effects.  Why B wrong: Alopecia IS a listed side effect.  Why C wrong: This IS a listed side effect."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"The characteristic limb complication associated with IV phenytoin administration is:","options":["Purple glove syndrome","Compartment syndrome","Cellulitis","Deep vein thrombosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists purple glove syndrome among phenytoin's side effects.  Why B wrong: Not mentioned in the source.  Why C wrong: Not mentioned in the source.  Why D wrong: Not mentioned in the source."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Injectable phenytoin should be diluted with which solution and given how? (NORCET-06, Prelims-2024)","options":["0.9% NS solution, given slowly to prevent hypotension and cardiac dysrhythmias","5% dextrose, given rapidly","Sterile water, given as a bolus","Ringer's lactate, given by IM route"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states injectable phenytoin should be diluted with 0.9% NS solution and given slowly to prevent hypotension and cardiac dysrhythmias (NORCET-06, Prelims-2024).  Why B wrong: Phenytoin precipitates in dextrose; not the stated diluent.  Why C wrong: Not the stated diluent/method.  Why D wrong: Not the stated diluent/route."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the normal therapeutic serum level of phenytoin?","options":["10-20 mcg/ml","0.5-2 ng/ml","0.6-1.2 mEq/L","50-100 mcg/ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the normal phenytoin serum level is 10-20 mcg/ml.  Why B wrong: That is the digoxin range.  Why C wrong: That is the lithium range.  Why D wrong: Incorrect range."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Benzodiazepines are used to treat which type of seizures?","options":["Absence seizures","Only febrile seizures","Only myoclonic seizures","Only partial seizures"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states benzodiazepines are used to treat absence seizures.  Why B wrong: Not the stated use.  Why C wrong: Not the stated use.  Why D wrong: Partial/generalised tonic-clonic are treated by phenytoin per source."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What are the drugs of choice for status epilepticus?","options":["Diazepam and lorazepam","Phenytoin and fosphenytoin","Valproic acid and divalproex","Levodopa and carbidopa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the DOC for status epilepticus is diazepam and lorazepam.  Why B wrong: These are hydantoins used for tonic-clonic seizures.  Why C wrong: These are valproates.  Why D wrong: These are anti-Parkinsonian drugs."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Besides seizures, benzodiazepines are also used for which purposes?","options":["Treatment of anxiety, muscle spasm, and inducing sleep","Treatment of hypertension","Treatment of diabetes","Treatment of dysrhythmias"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states benzodiazepines are also used for treatment of anxiety, muscle spasm, and inducing sleep.  Why B wrong: Not a stated use.  Why C wrong: Not a stated use.  Why D wrong: Not a stated use."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What are the side effects of benzodiazepines?","options":["Sedation, drowsiness, blurred vision, tolerance, and drug dependence","Hypertension and tachycardia","Hyperglycaemia and polyuria","Gingival hyperplasia and hirsutism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists sedation, drowsiness, blurred vision, tolerance, and drug dependence.  Why B wrong: Not the stated side effects.  Why C wrong: Not the stated side effects.  Why D wrong: Those are phenytoin side effects."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which of the following are examples of valproate anti-convulsants?","options":["Valproic acid and divalproex sodium","Diazepam and lorazepam","Phenytoin and fosphenytoin","Neostigmine and pyridostigmine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists valproic acid and divalproex sodium as valproate examples.  Why B wrong: These are benzodiazepines.  Why C wrong: These are hydantoins.  Why D wrong: These are anti-myasthenic drugs."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What are the side effects of valproates?","options":["Transient nausea, vomiting, sedation, drowsiness, and pancreatitis","Gingival hyperplasia and hirsutism","Respiratory depression","Lactic acidosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists transient nausea, vomiting, sedation, drowsiness, and pancreatitis as valproate side effects.  Why B wrong: Those are phenytoin side effects.  Why C wrong: That is an opioid side effect.  Why D wrong: That is a biguanide (metformin) side effect."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Anti-myasthenic medicines are also called by what other name?","options":["Anti-cholinesterase medications","Anticholinergic medications","Anticoagulant medications","Antiplatelet medications"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states anti-myasthenic medicines are also called anti-cholinesterase medications.  Why B wrong: Anticholinergics are a different (opposite) class.  Why C wrong: Not the same as anti-myasthenics.  Why D wrong: Not the same as anti-myasthenics."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the mode of action of anti-myasthenic (anti-cholinesterase) medications?","options":["Relieve muscle weakness in myasthenia gravis by blocking acetylcholine breakdown at the neuromuscular junction","Block acetylcholine receptors","Increase dopamine in the brain","Depress abnormal neuronal discharge"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states they relieve muscle weakness in myasthenia gravis by blocking acetylcholine breakdown at the neuromuscular junction.  Why B wrong: Blocking ACh receptors is the anticholinergic action, the opposite.  Why C wrong: That is an anti-Parkinsonian action.  Why D wrong: That is an anti-convulsant action."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which drugs are used for the treatment of myasthenia gravis?","options":["Neostigmine bromide, pyridostigmine, ambenonium chloride","Edrophonium bromide","Levodopa and carbidopa","Diazepam and lorazepam"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states neostigmine bromide, pyridostigmine, and ambenonium chloride are used for the treatment of myasthenia gravis.  Why B wrong: Edrophonium is used for diagnosis, not treatment.  Why C wrong: These are anti-Parkinsonian.  Why D wrong: These are benzodiazepines."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which drug is used for the diagnosis of myasthenia gravis?","options":["Edrophonium bromide","Neostigmine bromide","Pyridostigmine","Ambenonium chloride"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states edrophonium bromide is used for the diagnosis of myasthenia gravis.  Why B wrong: Neostigmine is used for treatment.  Why C wrong: Pyridostigmine is used for treatment.  Why D wrong: Ambenonium chloride is used for treatment."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"During anti-myasthenic therapy, the nurse should monitor for signs of medication overdose or under-dose, which respectively indicate:","options":["Cholinergic crisis (overdose) and myasthenia crisis (under-dose)","Myasthenia crisis (overdose) and cholinergic crisis (under-dose)","Thyroid crisis and adrenal crisis","Hypoglycaemia and hyperglycaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to monitor for overdose or cholinergic crisis and under-dose or myasthenia crisis.  Why B wrong: The associations are reversed.  Why C wrong: Not the stated crises.  Why D wrong: Not the stated crises."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the mode of action of anti-Parkinsonian medicines?","options":["Restore dopamine in the brain or restore the balance of acetylcholine and dopamine in the CNS","Block acetylcholine breakdown at the neuromuscular junction","Depress neuronal discharge","Inhibit platelet aggregation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states anti-Parkinsonian medicines restore dopamine in the brain for neuronal activity or restore the balance of acetylcholine and dopamine in the CNS.  Why B wrong: That is anti-myasthenic action.  Why C wrong: That is anti-convulsant action.  Why D wrong: That is antiplatelet action."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Anti-Parkinsonian medicines include which two categories?","options":["Dopaminergic and anticholinergic","Anticoagulant and antiplatelet","Sodium and calcium channel blockers","Statins and bile sequestrants"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states anti-Parkinsonian medicines include dopaminergic and anticholinergic drugs.  Why B wrong: Those are cardiac/blood drug classes.  Why C wrong: Those are anti-dysrhythmic classes.  Why D wrong: Those are anti-lipidemic classes."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the mode of action of dopaminergic anti-Parkinsonian medicines?","options":["Stimulate the dopamine receptors for dopamine production","Block dopamine receptors","Block acetylcholine receptors","Increase acetylcholine breakdown"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states dopaminergic medicines stimulate the dopamine receptors for dopamine production.  Why B wrong: They stimulate, not block, dopamine receptors.  Why C wrong: That is anticholinergic action.  Why D wrong: Not the stated action."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which of the following are examples of dopaminergic anti-Parkinsonian medicines?","options":["Levodopa, carbidopa, selegiline, amantadine, and bromocriptine","Benztropine, biperiden, trihexyphenidyl","Neostigmine, pyridostigmine","Diazepam, lorazepam"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists levodopa, carbidopa, selegiline, amantadine, and bromocriptine as dopaminergic medicines.  Why B wrong: These are anticholinergic medicines.  Why C wrong: These are anti-myasthenic drugs.  Why D wrong: These are benzodiazepines."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What are the adverse effects of dopaminergic medicines?","options":["Dyskinesia, hallucinations, orthostatic hypotension, and dry mouth","Gingival hyperplasia and hirsutism","Respiratory depression","Lactic acidosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists dyskinesia, hallucinations, orthostatic hypotension, and dry mouth as adverse effects.  Why B wrong: Those are phenytoin effects.  Why C wrong: That is an opioid effect.  Why D wrong: That is a metformin effect."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the mode of action of anticholinergic anti-Parkinsonian medicines?","options":["Block the cholinergic receptors and suppress acetylcholine activity, reducing tremors and drooling","Stimulate dopamine receptors","Block acetylcholine breakdown","Depress neuronal discharge"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states anticholinergic medicines block cholinergic receptors and suppress acetylcholine activity, reducing tremors and drooling.  Why B wrong: That is dopaminergic action.  Why C wrong: That is anti-myasthenic action.  Why D wrong: That is anti-convulsant action."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Anticholinergic medicines are contraindicated in clients with which condition?","options":["Glaucoma","Diabetes","Hypertension","Asthma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states anticholinergic medicines are contraindicated in clients with glaucoma.  Why B wrong: Not the stated contraindication.  Why C wrong: Not the stated contraindication.  Why D wrong: Not the stated contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which of the following are examples of anticholinergic anti-Parkinsonian medicines?","options":["Benztropine, biperiden, trihexyphenidyl","Levodopa, carbidopa","Selegiline, amantadine","Neostigmine, pyridostigmine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists benztropine, biperiden, and trihexyphenidyl as anticholinergic examples.  Why B wrong: These are dopaminergic.  Why C wrong: These are dopaminergic.  Why D wrong: These are anti-myasthenic."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What are the side effects of anticholinergic medicines?","options":["Blurred vision, dryness of mouth/nose/throat, increased pulse rate and RR, restlessness, confusion, and depression","Bradycardia and hypersalivation","Diarrhoea and constricted pupils","Lactic acidosis and diarrhoea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists blurred vision, dryness of mouth/nose/throat, increased pulse rate and RR, restlessness, confusion, and depression.  Why B wrong: Anticholinergics cause the opposite (tachycardia, dry mouth).  Why C wrong: Anticholinergics cause dilated pupils and constipation, not these.  Why D wrong: Those are metformin effects."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"If an anticholinergic medicine is discontinued abruptly, what may occur?","options":["Parkinsonism (rigidity, tremors, akinesia, bradykinesia, shuffling gait, mask face)","Hypertensive crisis","Hyperglycaemia","Bleeding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that if an anticholinergic medicine is discontinued abruptly, Parkinsonism may occur (rigidity, tremors, akinesia, bradykinesia, stopped forward gait, shuffling gait, mask face).  Why B wrong: Not the stated consequence.  Why C wrong: Not the stated consequence.  Why D wrong: Not the stated consequence."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the mechanism of action of osmotic diuretics?","options":["Increase pressure of glomerular filtrate, inhibiting re-absorption of water and electrolytes, causing osmotic diuresis","Block sodium channels in the distal tubule","Inhibit aldosterone","Block carbonic anhydrase"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states osmotic diuretics increase pressure of glomerular filtrate, inhibiting re-absorption of water and electrolytes and causing osmotic diuresis.  Why B wrong: Not the stated mechanism.  Why C wrong: That is a potassium-sparing diuretic action.  Why D wrong: That is a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor action."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Osmotic diuretics are commonly used for the treatment of which condition?","options":["Increased intracranial pressure (ICP)","Hypertension","Diabetes","Seizures"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states osmotic diuretics are commonly used for treatment of increased ICP.  Why B wrong: Not the stated primary use.  Why C wrong: Not the stated use.  Why D wrong: Not the stated use."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which drug is the best example of an osmotic diuretic?","options":["Mannitol","Furosemide","Spironolactone","Hydrochlorothiazide"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the best example of an osmotic diuretic is mannitol.  Why B wrong: Furosemide is a loop diuretic.  Why C wrong: Spironolactone is a potassium-sparing diuretic.  Why D wrong: Hydrochlorothiazide is a thiazide diuretic."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What are the side effects of osmotic diuretics?","options":["Electrolyte imbalances, pulmonary oedema, tachycardia, hyponatremia, and dehydration","Gingival hyperplasia and hirsutism","Respiratory depression","Lactic acidosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists electrolyte imbalances, pulmonary oedema, tachycardia, hyponatremia, and dehydration.  Why B wrong: Those are phenytoin effects.  Why C wrong: That is an opioid effect.  Why D wrong: That is a metformin effect."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Nursing care for a client on osmotic diuretics includes all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Change the client's position slowly to prevent orthostatic hypotension","Strictly monitor the I/O chart","Assess for signs of decreasing ICP","Encourage rapid position changes"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to change position slowly (not rapidly) to prevent orthostatic hypotension; rapid changes are contraindicated.  Why A wrong: This IS correct nursing care.  Why B wrong: This IS correct nursing care.  Why C wrong: This IS correct nursing care."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the mode of action of sulfonylureas?","options":["Stimulate beta cells to produce more insulin","Reduce hepatic glucose output","Slow digestion of carbohydrates","Improve insulin sensitivity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states sulfonylureas stimulate beta cells to produce more insulin.  Why B wrong: That is the biguanide (metformin) mechanism.  Why C wrong: That is the alpha-glucosidase inhibitor mechanism.  Why D wrong: That is the thiazolidinedione mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which of the following are examples of sulfonylureas?","options":["Glimepiride and glipizide","Metformin","Repaglinide","Pioglitazone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists glimepiride and glipizide as sulfonylureas.  Why B wrong: Metformin is a biguanide.  Why C wrong: Repaglinide is a meglitinide.  Why D wrong: Pioglitazone is a thiazolidinedione."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Sulfonylureas can cause which type of reaction if alcohol is ingested?","options":["A disulfiram-like reaction","An anaphylactic reaction","A serotonin syndrome","A cholinergic crisis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states sulfonylureas cause a disulfiram-like reaction if alcohol is ingested.  Why B wrong: Not the stated reaction.  Why C wrong: Not the stated reaction.  Why D wrong: Not the stated reaction."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"When should sulfonylureas be taken?","options":["Before or with breakfast","At bedtime only","Only after dinner","On an empty stomach at midnight"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states sulfonylureas should be taken before or with breakfast.  Why B wrong: Not the stated timing.  Why C wrong: Not the stated timing.  Why D wrong: Not the stated timing."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the most common side effect of sulfonylureas?","options":["Weight gain","Weight loss","Lactic acidosis","Diarrhoea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common side effect of sulfonylureas is weight gain.  Why B wrong: Not the stated effect.  Why C wrong: That is a biguanide side effect.  Why D wrong: That is a biguanide side effect."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the mode of action of biguanides?","options":["Reduce hepatic glucose output and increase uptake of glucose by the periphery, including skeletal muscle","Stimulate beta cells to produce insulin","Slow carbohydrate digestion","Improve insulin sensitivity only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states biguanides reduce hepatic glucose output and increase glucose uptake by the periphery, including skeletal muscle.  Why B wrong: That is the sulfonylurea mechanism.  Why C wrong: That is the alpha-glucosidase inhibitor mechanism.  Why D wrong: That is the thiazolidinedione mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"When is a biguanide (metformin) usually taken?","options":["Before breakfast","At bedtime","Only after dinner","During the night"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states biguanides are usually taken before breakfast.  Why B wrong: Not the stated timing.  Why C wrong: Not the stated timing.  Why D wrong: Not the stated timing."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which drug is the most commonly used biguanide?","options":["Metformin","Glimepiride","Pioglitazone","Acarbose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states metformin is the most commonly used biguanide.  Why B wrong: Glimepiride is a sulfonylurea.  Why C wrong: Pioglitazone is a thiazolidinedione.  Why D wrong: Acarbose is an alpha-glucosidase inhibitor."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What are the side effects of biguanides (metformin)?","options":["Lactic acidosis and diarrhoea","Weight gain","Gingival hyperplasia","Respiratory depression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists lactic acidosis and diarrhoea as biguanide side effects.  Why B wrong: Weight gain is a sulfonylurea side effect.  Why C wrong: That is a phenytoin effect.  Why D wrong: That is an opioid effect."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the mode of action of alpha-glucosidase inhibitors?","options":["Slow down the digestion of ingested carbohydrate and decrease absorption of glucose","Stimulate beta cells to produce insulin","Reduce hepatic glucose output","Improve insulin sensitivity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states alpha-glucosidase inhibitors slow the digestion of ingested carbohydrate and decrease absorption of glucose.  Why B wrong: That is the sulfonylurea mechanism.  Why C wrong: That is the biguanide mechanism.  Why D wrong: That is the thiazolidinedione mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which of the following are examples of alpha-glucosidase inhibitors?","options":["Miglitol, acarbose, voglibose","Metformin","Glimepiride","Repaglinide"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists miglitol, acarbose, and voglibose as alpha-glucosidase inhibitors.  Why B wrong: Metformin is a biguanide.  Why C wrong: Glimepiride is a sulfonylurea.  Why D wrong: Repaglinide is a meglitinide."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the mode of action of thiazolidinediones?","options":["Improve insulin sensitivity","Stimulate beta cells to produce insulin","Slow carbohydrate digestion","Reduce hepatic glucose output"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states thiazolidinediones improve insulin sensitivity.  Why B wrong: That is the sulfonylurea mechanism.  Why C wrong: That is the alpha-glucosidase inhibitor mechanism.  Why D wrong: That is the biguanide mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Pioglitazone, an example of a thiazolidinedione, is associated with which risk?","options":["Increased cardiovascular risk","Lactic acidosis","Gingival hyperplasia","Respiratory depression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pioglitazone is associated with increased cardiovascular risk.  Why B wrong: That is a metformin risk.  Why C wrong: That is a phenytoin effect.  Why D wrong: That is an opioid effect."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the mode of action of meglitinides?","options":["Stimulate quick release of insulin by beta cells of the pancreas","Reduce hepatic glucose output","Improve insulin sensitivity","Slow carbohydrate digestion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states meglitinides stimulate quick release of insulin by beta cells of the pancreas.  Why B wrong: That is the biguanide mechanism.  Why C wrong: That is the thiazolidinedione mechanism.  Why D wrong: That is the alpha-glucosidase inhibitor mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which drug is an example of a meglitinide?","options":["Repaglinide","Metformin","Glimepiride","Acarbose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists repaglinide as a meglitinide.  Why B wrong: Metformin is a biguanide.  Why C wrong: Glimepiride is a sulfonylurea.  Why D wrong: Acarbose is an alpha-glucosidase inhibitor."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"When should meglitinides be taken, and what happens if a meal is skipped?","options":["With or shortly before meals; if a meal is skipped, the medication is also skipped","At bedtime; the dose is doubled if a meal is skipped","After meals; an extra dose is added if a meal is skipped","Once daily regardless of meals"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states meglitinides should be taken with or shortly before meals to boost insulin response to each meal, and if a meal is skipped, the medication is also skipped.  Why B wrong: Not the stated instruction.  Why C wrong: Not the stated instruction.  Why D wrong: Not the stated instruction."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"During oral hypoglycaemic therapy, when should blood glucose level be assessed?","options":["Before and after meals or as per order","Only once a week","Only at bedtime","Only when symptomatic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to assess blood glucose level before and after meals or as per order.  Why B wrong: Not the stated frequency.  Why C wrong: Not the stated timing.  Why D wrong: Not the stated timing."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which specific instruction is given when a client is taking sulfonylureas (e.g. glimepiride)?","options":["Avoid alcohol","Increase alcohol intake","Avoid all carbohydrates","Take with a high-fat meal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to avoid alcohol when taking sulfonylureas (e.g. glimepiride), because of the disulfiram-like reaction.  Why B wrong: The opposite is advised.  Why C wrong: Not the stated instruction.  Why D wrong: Not the stated instruction."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Metformin should be withheld for how long before and after any radiological studies with dye or contrast?","options":["48 hours","24 hours","12 hours","72 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states metformin should be withheld 48 hours before and after any radiological studies with dye or contrasts.  Why B wrong: Incorrect duration.  Why C wrong: Incorrect duration.  Why D wrong: Incorrect duration."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Metformin may need to be discontinued how long before surgery? (Staff Nurse-2018)","options":["48 hours","24 hours","12 hours","1 week"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states metformin may need to be discontinued 48 hours before surgery (Staff Nurse-2018).  Why B wrong: Incorrect duration.  Why C wrong: Incorrect duration.  Why D wrong: Incorrect duration."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Metformin is usually the first-line medication used for the treatment of which condition? (JSSK-2019)","options":["Type 2 diabetes","Type 1 diabetes","Hypertension","Hyperlipidaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states metformin is usually the first-line medication used for treatment of type 2 diabetes (JSSK-2019).  Why B wrong: Type 1 diabetes requires insulin.  Why C wrong: Not its use.  Why D wrong: Not its use."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Insulin is secreted by which cells?","options":["Beta cells of the pancreas","Alpha cells of the pancreas","Delta cells of the pancreas","Hepatocytes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states insulin is secreted by beta cells of the pancreas.  Why B wrong: Alpha cells secrete glucagon.  Why C wrong: Delta cells secrete somatostatin.  Why D wrong: Hepatocytes do not secrete insulin."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Insulin primarily acts on which tissues?","options":["Liver, muscles, and adipose tissues","Brain and kidneys","Heart and lungs","Skin and bone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states insulin primarily acts on liver, muscles, and adipose tissues.  Why B wrong: Not the stated primary tissues.  Why C wrong: Not the stated primary tissues.  Why D wrong: Not the stated primary tissues."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Insulin is used in which types of diabetes?","options":["Both types (IDDM & NIDDM)","Only IDDM","Only NIDDM","Neither type"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states insulin is used in both types of diabetes (IDDM & BIDDM/NIDDM).  Why B wrong: Used in both, not only IDDM.  Why C wrong: Used in both, not only NIDDM.  Why D wrong: Insulin is used in diabetes."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"The onset, peak, and duration of action of insulin depend on:","options":["The type of insulin","The route of administration only","The patient's weight only","The time of day only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the onset, peak, and duration of action depend on the type of insulin.  Why B wrong: Not the stated determinant.  Why C wrong: Not the stated determinant.  Why D wrong: Not the stated determinant."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What is the total number of amino acids in insulin? (ISRO-2024)","options":["51","50","61","41"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the total number of amino acids in insulin is 51 (ISRO-2024).  Why B wrong: Incorrect count.  Why C wrong: Incorrect count.  Why D wrong: Incorrect count."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"In the liver, what is the mode of action of insulin?","options":["Decrease gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis, and increase glycogenesis","Increase gluconeogenesis","Increase glycogenolysis","No effect on the liver"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that in the liver, insulin decreases gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis and increases glycogenesis.  Why B wrong: Insulin decreases gluconeogenesis.  Why C wrong: Insulin decreases glycogenolysis.  Why D wrong: Insulin acts on the liver."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"In skeletal muscles, insulin has which effect?","options":["Increases glucose uptake and inhibits protein breakdown","Decreases glucose uptake","Increases protein breakdown","Has no effect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in skeletal muscles insulin increases glucose uptake and inhibits protein breakdown.  Why B wrong: Insulin increases glucose uptake.  Why C wrong: Insulin inhibits protein breakdown.  Why D wrong: Insulin acts on skeletal muscle."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"In adipose tissues, insulin has which effect?","options":["Increases glucose uptake (leading to weight gain) and inhibits fat breakdown","Decreases glucose uptake","Increases fat breakdown","Causes weight loss"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in adipose tissues insulin increases glucose uptake (leading to weight gain) and inhibits fat breakdown.  Why B wrong: Insulin increases glucose uptake.  Why C wrong: Insulin inhibits fat breakdown.  Why D wrong: It leads to weight gain, not loss."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"For subcutaneous insulin injection in thin patients (the most common route), what needle angle is used?","options":["45° angle","90° angle","15° angle","60° angle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states subcutaneous injection uses a 45° angle in thin patients (most common route).  Why B wrong: 90° is used intramuscularly in thick/obese patients.  Why C wrong: Incorrect angle.  Why D wrong: Incorrect angle."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"For intramuscular insulin injection in thick or obese patients, what needle angle is used?","options":["90° angle","45° angle","15° angle","30° angle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states intramuscular injection uses a 90° angle in thick or obese patients.  Why B wrong: 45° is for subcutaneous in thin patients.  Why C wrong: Incorrect angle.  Why D wrong: Incorrect angle."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which of the following is a correct principle for storing insulin? (Select all that apply)","options":["Avoid exposing insulin to extreme temperature","Insulin should not be frozen or kept in direct sunlight or a hot place","Before administration, insulin should be normalised at room temperature","Insulin should always be frozen for long-term storage","A vial of insulin can be used up to 1 month, otherwise it should be refrigerated"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to avoid extreme temperatures, not freeze or keep in sunlight/hot place, normalise to room temp before administration, and that a vial can be used up to 1 month (then refrigerate).  Why D wrong: Insulin should NOT be frozen — this contradicts the source."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"How long can an in-use vial of insulin be kept at room temperature before it should be refrigerated?","options":["Up to 1 month","Up to 1 week","Up to 3 months","Up to 48 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a vial of insulin can be used up to 1 month; it may be kept at room temperature, otherwise it should be refrigerated.  Why B wrong: Incorrect duration.  Why C wrong: Incorrect duration.  Why D wrong: Incorrect duration."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Glargine (Lantus) is which type of insulin?","options":["Long-acting insulin","Rapid-acting insulin","Short-acting insulin","Intermediate-acting insulin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states glargine (Lantus) is long-acting insulin.  Why B wrong: Rapid-acting includes glulisine, aspart, lispro.  Why C wrong: Short-acting is regular insulin.  Why D wrong: Intermediate-acting is NPH."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"The trick \"GAL\" for remembering rapid-acting insulins stands for:","options":["Glulisine, Aspart, and Lispro","Glargine, Aspart, Lispro","Glulisine, Aspart, Lantus","Glargine, Aspart, Lantus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the trick \"GAL\" for rapid-acting insulin is glulisine, aspart, and lispro.  Why B wrong: Glargine is long-acting, not rapid.  Why C wrong: Lantus is glargine (long-acting).  Why D wrong: Both glargine and Lantus are long-acting."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What are the onset, peak, and duration of rapid-acting insulin (e.g. glulisine, aspart, lispro)?","options":["Onset 10-15 min, peak 1-1.5 hrs, duration 3-5 hrs","Onset 30-45 min, peak 2-3 hrs, duration 6.5 hrs","Onset 1-3 hrs, peak 5-8 hrs, duration 14-18 hrs","Onset 1-2 hrs, peak 8-10 hrs, duration 12-24 hrs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's insulin table gives rapid-acting: onset 10-15 min, peak 1-1.5 hrs, duration 3-5 hrs.  Why B wrong: Those are short-acting (regular) values.  Why C wrong: Those are intermediate (NPH) values.  Why D wrong: Those are long-acting (glargine) values."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What are the onset, peak, and duration of short-acting (regular) insulin?","options":["Onset 30-45 min, peak 2-3 hrs, duration 6.5 hrs","Onset 10-15 min, peak 1-1.5 hrs, duration 3-5 hrs","Onset 1-3 hrs, peak 5-8 hrs, duration 14-18 hrs","Onset 1-2 hrs, peak 8-10 hrs, duration 12-24 hrs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's table gives short-acting (regular): onset 30-45 min, peak 2-3 hrs, duration 6.5 hrs.  Why B wrong: Those are rapid-acting values.  Why C wrong: Those are intermediate (NPH) values.  Why D wrong: Those are long-acting values."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"Which insulin is the example of intermediate-acting insulin, and what are its onset, peak, and duration?","options":["NPH; onset 1-3 hrs, peak 5-8 hrs, duration 14-18 hrs","Regular; onset 30-45 min, peak 2-3 hrs, duration 6.5 hrs","Glargine; onset 1-2 hrs, peak 8-10 hrs, duration 12-24 hrs","Lispro; onset 10-15 min, peak 1-1.5 hrs, duration 3-5 hrs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's table gives intermediate: NPH, onset 1-3 hrs, peak 5-8 hrs, duration 14-18 hrs.  Why B wrong: That is short-acting (regular).  Why C wrong: That is long-acting (glargine).  Why D wrong: That is rapid-acting (lispro)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Beta-3 receptors & beta blockers, vasodilators…","question":"What are the onset, peak, and duration of long-acting insulin (glargine)?","options":["Onset 1-2 hrs, peak 8-10 hrs, duration 12-24 hrs","Onset 10-15 min, peak 1-1.5 hrs, duration 3-5 hrs","Onset 30-45 min, peak 2-3 hrs, duration 6.5 hrs","Onset 1-3 hrs, peak 5-8 hrs, duration 14-18 hrs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's table gives long-acting (glargine): onset 1-2 hrs, peak 8-10 hrs, duration 12-24 hrs.  Why B wrong: Those are rapid-acting values.  Why C wrong: Those are short-acting values.  Why D wrong: Those are intermediate values."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Why should the insulin administration site be changed with every injection (prick)?","options":["To reduce needle pain","To prevent lipodystrophy","To speed up absorption","To prevent infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rotating the insulin site with every prick prevents lipodystrophy (fatty tissue changes at repeated injection sites).  Why A wrong: Site rotation is not primarily for pain reduction.  Why C wrong: Rotation prevents tissue damage, not to speed absorption.  Why D wrong: Infection prevention relies on asepsis, not rotation."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Heat, massage and exercise of the insulin-injected area should be avoided because they can:","options":["Decrease absorption rate and cause hyperglycaemia","Increase absorption rate and cause hypoglycaemia","Cause lipodystrophy","Denature the insulin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Heat, massage and exercise increase absorption rate and may result in hypoglycaemia, so they should be avoided.  Why A wrong: They increase (not decrease) absorption, leading to hypoglycaemia, not hyperglycaemia.  Why C wrong: Lipodystrophy is related to not rotating sites.  Why D wrong: These activities do not denature the insulin."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which insulin CANNOT be mixed with any other type of insulin?","options":["Regular insulin","NPH insulin","Insulin glargine (Lantus)","Insulin aspart"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Insulin glargine (Lantus) cannot be mixed with any other type of insulin.  Why A wrong: Regular (short-acting) insulin is commonly mixed with NPH.  Why B wrong: NPH can be mixed with short-acting insulin.  Why D wrong: Aspart is a rapid-acting insulin that can be mixed with certain insulins."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"When mixing two types of insulin, which should be drawn up first?","options":["Long-acting insulin first, then short-acting","Shortest-acting insulin first, then long-acting","Either order is acceptable","NPH first, then glargine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Draw up the shortest-acting insulin first, then the last (long) acting — to avoid contaminating the clear short-acting vial with the cloudy long-acting one.  Why A wrong: This reverses the correct sequence.  Why C wrong: Order matters to prevent contamination.  Why D wrong: Glargine is never mixed with any insulin."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"A mixed dose of insulin should be administered within what time frame after preparation?","options":["Within 30–60 minutes","Within 5–15 minutes","Within 1–2 hours","Immediately, within 1 minute"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A mixed dose should be given within 5–15 minutes of preparation; after that the short-acting insulin binds with NPH insulin and its action is reduced.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source-stated 5–15 minute window."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Regarding aspiration during insulin injection, the nurse should:","options":["Always aspirate before injecting","Avoid aspiration after needle insertion","Aspirate twice for confirmation","Aspirate only for IV insulin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aspiration after needle insertion should be avoided when giving insulin subcutaneously.  Why A/C wrong: Aspiration is not recommended for insulin.  Why D wrong: The source states aspiration should be avoided; IV route is separately discussed."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which types of insulin are the ONLY ones that can be administered intravenously (IV)?","options":["Long-acting and intermediate-acting","NPH and glargine","Rapid and short acting insulin","All insulins can be given IV"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Only rapid and short acting insulin can be administered via the intravenous (IV) route.  Why A/B wrong: Long/intermediate-acting and NPH/glargine are not given IV.  Why D wrong: Not all insulins are IV-compatible."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which is the most common and reliable insulin injection site giving the FASTEST absorption?","options":["Anterior thigh","Abdomen (umbilical region / belly area)","Hips and buttocks","Upper outer arm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The abdomen (umbilical region/belly area) is the most common and reliable site for fastest absorption.  Why A wrong: Anterior & outer thigh gives slower absorption.  Why C wrong: Hips and buttocks absorb more slowly.  Why D wrong: Posterior/upper outer arm is an option but not the fastest."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which insulin injection site is associated with SLOW absorption?","options":["Abdomen","Anterior & outer surface of thigh","Umbilical region","Belly area"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The anterior & outer surface of the thigh has slow absorption.  Why A/C/D wrong: Abdomen/umbilical/belly are the fastest-absorbing sites."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Glucagon is a pancreatic hormone secreted by which cells?","options":["Beta cells of islets of Langerhans","Alpha cells of islets of Langerhans","Delta cells of islets of Langerhans","Acinar cells of the pancreas"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Glucagon is secreted by the alpha cells of the islets of Langerhans of the pancreas.  Why A wrong: Beta cells secrete insulin.  Why C wrong: Delta cells secrete somatostatin.  Why D wrong: Acinar cells secrete digestive enzymes."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Glucagon increases blood glucose level by stimulating which process in the liver?","options":["Glycolysis","Glycogenolysis","Lipogenesis","Ketogenesis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Glucagon increases blood glucose by stimulating glycogenolysis in the liver.  Why A wrong: Glycolysis breaks down glucose (lowers it).  Why C/D wrong: These are not the described glucose-raising mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"By which routes can glucagon be administered?","options":["Oral only","IM, IV & SC","IV only","Inhalation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Glucagon is administered by IM, IV & SC routes.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated routes."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Glucagon is primarily used to treat:","options":["Insulin-induced hypoglycaemia","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Hyperglycaemia","Hypertension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Glucagon is used to treat insulin-induced hypoglycaemia.  Why B/C wrong: It raises glucose, so it is not for hyperglycaemia/DKA.  Why D wrong: It has no role in treating hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"After administration, glucagon's effect on blood glucose begins to rise within:","options":["1–2 minutes","5–20 minutes","30–60 minutes","1–2 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Glucagon's effect on blood glucose begins to rise within 5–20 minutes after administration.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 5–20 minute onset."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"What is the endogenous mineralocorticoid in the human body?","options":["Cortisol","Aldosterone","Fludrocortisone","Dexamethasone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aldosterone is the endogenous mineralocorticoid.  Why A wrong: Cortisol is a glucocorticoid.  Why C wrong: Fludrocortisone is the exogenous (example) mineralocorticoid.  Why D wrong: Dexamethasone is a glucocorticoid."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"An example of an exogenous mineralocorticoid used in hypoaldosteronism is:","options":["Fludrocortisone","Prednisone","Hydrocortisone","Betamethasone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fludrocortisone is the example exogenous mineralocorticoid, used in hypoaldosteronism (underproduction of endogenous aldosterone).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are glucocorticoids, not exogenous mineralocorticoids."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"What is the mode of action of mineralocorticoids on the renal tubules?","options":["Excretion of sodium and retention of potassium","Absorption of sodium/chloride and excretion of potassium & hydrogen","Excretion of both sodium and potassium","Retention of hydrogen ions only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mineralocorticoids enhance absorption of sodium and chlorides and promote excretion of potassium & hydrogen from the renal tubules.  Why A/C/D wrong: These reverse or misstate the electrolyte handling."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Exogenous mineralocorticoids are used in Addison's disease because of:","options":["Overproduction of aldosterone","Underproduction of endogenous aldosterone (hypoaldosteronism)","Excess cortisol","Excess ADH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: They are used in Addison's disease because of underproduction of endogenous aldosterone (hypoaldosteronism).  Why A/C/D wrong: Addison's involves adrenal insufficiency (deficiency), not excess."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which set of side effects is associated with mineralocorticoid use?","options":["Hyponatremia, hyperkalaemia, weight loss","Sodium and water retention, hypokalaemia, hypocalcaemia, osteoporosis, weight gain","Hypoglycaemia and dehydration","Metabolic acidosis and hyperkalaemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mineralocorticoid side effects include sodium and water retention (hypernatremia & water intoxication), hypokalaemia and hypocalcaemia, osteoporosis, and weight gain.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated retention/electrolyte profile."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which diet is recommended for a client on mineralocorticoid therapy?","options":["High sodium, low potassium diet","High potassium diet with sodium restriction (low sodium)","High calcium, high sodium diet","Low potassium, high sodium diet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Specific nursing care is to offer a high potassium diet and restrict/low sodium diet, and monitor I/O and daily weight.  Why A/D wrong: Sodium should be restricted and potassium increased.  Why C wrong: Sodium should be restricted."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"What is the nature of glucocorticoids?","options":["Peptide hormones","Steroid hormones","Catecholamines","Amino acid derivatives"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Glucocorticoids are steroid hormones.  Why A/C/D wrong: They are not peptides, catecholamines, or simple amino acid derivatives."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Glucocorticoids affect which metabolic processes?","options":["Only fat metabolism","Glucose, protein and bone metabolism","Only water and electrolyte balance","Only nucleic acid synthesis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Glucocorticoids affect glucose, protein and bone metabolism.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are incomplete or incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Glucocorticoids alter normal immune function by:","options":["Increasing immunity","Decreasing immunity (immunosuppression)","Having no effect on immunity","Stimulating antibody production"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Glucocorticoids alter normal immune function or decrease immunity.  Why A/C/D wrong: They suppress, not enhance, immune function."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Glucocorticoids primarily act as:","options":["Pro-inflammatory agents","Anti-inflammatory agents (decrease inflammation)","Antipyretics only","Vasoconstrictors only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Glucocorticoids act as anti-inflammatory agents or decrease inflammation.  Why A/C/D wrong: They reduce inflammation; the others are wrong or incomplete."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which is a therapeutic use of glucocorticoids?","options":["Hypertension","Allergy, asthma, adrenal insufficiency, inflammatory and autoimmune disorders","Hyperkalaemia","Peptic ulcer disease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Therapeutic uses include allergy, asthma, adrenal insufficiency, inflammatory and autoimmune disorders.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not listed indications."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which glucocorticoids are classified as LONG acting (duration 36–72 hours)?","options":["Cortisone, hydrocortisone","Methylprednisolone, prednisone, prednisolone","Dexamethasone, betamethasone","Fludrocortisone, aldosterone"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Long-acting glucocorticoids (36–72 hours) are dexamethasone and betamethasone.  Why A wrong: Cortisone/hydrocortisone are short-acting (8–12 hrs).  Why B wrong: Methylprednisolone/prednisone/prednisolone are intermediate-acting (12–36 hrs).  Why D wrong: These are mineralocorticoids."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"The intermediate-acting glucocorticoids with a duration of action of 12–36 hours include:","options":["Dexamethasone, betamethasone","Methylprednisolone, prednisone, prednisolone","Cortisone, hydrocortisone","Fludrocortisone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Intermediate-acting (12–36 hrs) glucocorticoids are methylprednisolone, prednisone, prednisolone.  Why A wrong: Those are long-acting (36–72 hrs).  Why C wrong: Those are short-acting (8–12 hrs).  Why D wrong: Fludrocortisone is a mineralocorticoid."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Cortisone and hydrocortisone are classified as which duration category of glucocorticoids?","options":["Long acting (36–72 hrs)","Intermediate acting (12–36 hrs)","Short acting (8–12 hrs)","Ultra-long acting (>72 hrs)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Short-acting glucocorticoids have a duration of 8–12 hours; examples are cortisone and hydrocortisone.  Why A/B/D wrong: These durations do not match cortisone/hydrocortisone."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"A key nursing consideration for a client on glucocorticoids is that the medication:","options":["Should be stopped abruptly when symptoms improve","Should NOT be stopped abruptly","Requires no blood pressure monitoring","Requires a low potassium diet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: All glucocorticoids should not be stopped abruptly (to avoid adrenal crisis); they are tapered.  Why A wrong: Abrupt cessation is dangerous.  Why C wrong: Regular BP monitoring is required.  Why D wrong: A high potassium diet with sodium restriction is recommended."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which routine monitoring is part of nursing care for glucocorticoid therapy?","options":["Regular monitoring of RBS and blood pressure","Only temperature monitoring","No monitoring needed","Only urine output"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes regular monitoring of RBS (random blood sugar) and blood pressure, plus vitals, I/O and electrolytes.  Why B/C/D wrong: Monitoring is more comprehensive than these options."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"The dietary advice for a client on glucocorticoids is to:","options":["Restrict potassium, add sodium","Restrict sodium and offer a high potassium diet","Restrict both sodium and potassium","High sodium, high calcium diet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes restricting sodium and offering a high potassium diet.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated dietary care."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Hypertension seen with glucocorticoid use is primarily due to:","options":["Potassium excretion","Sodium & water retention","Increased carbohydrate catabolism","Immune suppression"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Glucocorticoid-induced hypertension is due to sodium & water retention.  Why A wrong: Potassium excretion causes hypokalaemia.  Why C wrong: That causes hyperglycaemia.  Why D wrong: Immune suppression causes proneness to infection."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Hyperglycaemia caused by glucocorticoids is due to:","options":["Decreased carbohydrate intake","Increased carbohydrate catabolism and gluconeogenesis","Potassium excretion","Sodium retention"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperglycaemia is due to increased carbohydrate catabolism and gluconeogenesis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated mechanism of hyperglycaemia."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Hypokalaemia seen with glucocorticoids results from:","options":["Potassium excretion","Sodium excretion","Calcium retention","Water intoxication"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypokalaemia is due to potassium excretion.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not explain low potassium."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Hypocalcaemia associated with glucocorticoid use is due to:","options":["Increased calcium intake","Decreased absorption of calcium from the intestine","Increased renal calcium reabsorption","Bone deposition of calcium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypocalcaemia is due to decreased absorption of calcium from the intestine.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not cause low serum calcium."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Steroid-induced osteoporosis in glucocorticoid therapy occurs due to:","options":["Low bone density & low calcium level","High calcium level","Increased vitamin D absorption","Sodium retention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Steroid-induced osteoporosis is due to low bone density & low calcium level.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe osteoporosis mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Weight gain with central (truncal) obesity from glucocorticoids is caused by:","options":["Potassium excretion","Sodium & water retention","Decreased carbohydrate metabolism","Immune suppression"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Weight gain is due to sodium & water retention, with central obesity seen.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not describe the weight-gain mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Mood changes in a client on glucocorticoids are attributed to:","options":["CNS deterioration","Hypokalaemia","Increased ocular pressure","Sodium retention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mood changes are due to CNS deterioration.  Why B/C/D wrong: These relate to other listed side effects."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Glaucoma and cataracts from long-term glucocorticoid use are due to:","options":["Increased ocular pressure and long-time hyperglycaemia","Sodium retention","Immune suppression","Low calcium level"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Glaucoma and cataracts are due to increased ocular pressure and long-time hyperglycaemia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are unrelated to the ocular side effects listed."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Decreased wound healing in glucocorticoid therapy is due to:","options":["Long-time high blood sugar level","Sodium excretion","Increased immunity","Low bone density"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased wound healing is due to long-time high blood sugar level.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not explain impaired wound healing."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which cluster of clinical findings is characteristic of Cushingoid appearance from glucocorticoids?","options":["Moon face, buffalo hump, truncal obesity","Weight loss and cachexia","Bradycardia and hypotension","Exophthalmos and goiter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cushingoid clinical findings include moon face, buffalo hump, truncal obesity.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not Cushingoid features."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Proneness to infection during glucocorticoid therapy is due to:","options":["Immune suppression","Sodium retention","Hyperglycaemia only","CNS deterioration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Prone to infection is due to immune suppression.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated cause of infection proneness."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Hypercortisolemia (Cushing syndrome) as a side effect is caused by:","options":["Prolonged and excessive use of exogenous glucocorticoids","Sudden withdrawal of glucocorticoids","Underproduction of aldosterone","Low-dose short-term steroid use"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypercortisolemia/Cushing syndrome is due to prolonged and excessive use of exogenous glucocorticoids.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not cause hypercortisolemia."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Sympathomimetic bronchodilators relieve respiratory symptoms by:","options":["Constricting the bronchi","Relaxing the smooth muscles of the bronchi and dilating the airway","Increasing mucus secretion","Suppressing the cough reflex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sympathomimetic bronchodilators relax the smooth muscles of the bronchi and dilate the airway of the respiratory tract.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are opposite or unrelated actions."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which side effects are expected with sympathomimetic bronchodilators?","options":["Bradycardia and hypoglycaemia","Palpitation, tachycardia, restlessness, nervousness, hyperglycaemia, mouth dryness","Constipation and drowsiness","Diarrhoea and salivation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Side effects include palpitation, tachycardia (from sympathetic stimulation), restlessness, nervousness, hyperglycaemia and mouth dryness.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the sympathomimetic profile."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"The normal serum theophylline level is:","options":["5–10 mcg/ml","10–20 mcg/ml","20–30 mcg/ml","0.5–2 mcg/ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal serum theophylline level is 10–20 mcg/ml; if >20 mcg it is considered theophylline toxicity.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated therapeutic range."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"A serum theophylline level above which value is considered toxic?","options":[">10 mcg/ml",">15 mcg/ml",">20 mcg/ml",">30 mcg/ml"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A level greater than 20 mcg is considered theophylline toxicity.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated toxic threshold."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which are early signs of theophylline toxicity?","options":["Bradycardia and drowsiness","Restlessness, nervousness, tremors, palpitations and tachycardia","Constipation and dry mouth","Hypotension and bradypnea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Early signs of theophylline toxicity include restlessness, nervousness, tremors, palpitations and tachycardia.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the listed early toxicity signs."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which drugs are examples of SHORT-acting Beta2 agonist bronchodilators?","options":["Salmeterol, formoterol","Salbutamol, terbutaline","Theophylline, aminophylline","Ipratropium, tiotropium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Short-acting Beta2 agonists are salbutamol and terbutaline.  Why A wrong: Salmeterol/formoterol are long-acting.  Why C wrong: Those are methylxanthines.  Why D wrong: Those are anticholinergics."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Salmeterol and formoterol are classified as:","options":["Short-acting Beta2 agonists","Long-acting bronchodilators","Methylxanthines","Anticholinergics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Salmeterol and formoterol are long-acting bronchodilators (Beta2 agonists).  Why A/C/D wrong: They are not short-acting, methylxanthines, or anticholinergics."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Theophylline and aminophylline belong to which class of bronchodilators?","options":["Beta2 agonists","Methylxanthines","Anticholinergics","Leukotriene medicines"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Theophylline and aminophylline are methylxanthines.  Why A/C/D wrong: They are not in these classes."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Ipratropium and tiotropium are examples of which bronchodilator class?","options":["Methylxanthines","Anticholinergics","Beta2 agonists","Monoclonal antibodies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ipratropium and tiotropium are anticholinergics.  Why A/C/D wrong: They belong to none of these classes."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which are examples of inhaled glucocorticoids used in respiratory medicine?","options":["Salbutamol and terbutaline","Beclomethasone dipropionate, budesonide, fluticasone","Montelukast and zafirlukast","Ipratropium and tiotropium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inhaled glucocorticoids include beclomethasone dipropionate, budesonide, and fluticasone.  Why A wrong: Those are Beta2 agonists.  Why C wrong: Those are leukotriene medicines.  Why D wrong: Those are anticholinergics."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Leukotriene medicines such as montelukast and zafirlukast are used for:","options":["Acute asthma attack","Treatment of bronchial asthma (not for acute attack)","Chronic constipation","Reversing anaphylaxis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Leukotriene medicines are used for the treatment of bronchial asthma, NOT for acute asthma attack; they reduce bronchoconstriction & bronchial oedema.  Why A wrong: They are explicitly not for acute attacks.  Why C/D wrong: Unrelated indications."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Omalizumab, a monoclonal antibody, is used for:","options":["Acute asthma attack","Chronic allergic related asthma","COPD exacerbation","Peptic ulcer disease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The monoclonal antibody omalizumab is used in chronic allergic related asthma.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not its stated use."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"(NORCET Prelims-2023) If two inhaled medications are prescribed and one contains a glucocorticoid/corticosteroid, the correct administration sequence is:","options":["Corticosteroid first, then bronchodilator","Bronchodilator first, wait 5 minutes, then corticosteroid second","Both simultaneously","Corticosteroid first, wait 30 minutes, then bronchodilator"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Administer the bronchodilator first and wait 5 minutes, then administer the corticosteroid second (bronchodilator opens airways so steroid penetrates better). (NORCET Prelims-2023)  Why A/C/D wrong: These reverse the sequence or state the wrong interval."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Anti-histamines are also called:","options":["H2 blockers","Histamine antagonists or H1 blockers","Proton pump inhibitors","Leukotriene antagonists"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anti-histamines are also called histamine antagonists or H1 blockers.  Why A wrong: H2 blockers are a separate GI class.  Why C/D wrong: Different drug classes."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Anti-histamines are used for all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Common cold and rhinitis","Nausea & vomiting and motion sickness","Peptic ulcer disease","Urticaria and as a sleep aid"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Anti-histamines are used in common cold, rhinitis, nausea & vomiting, motion sickness, urticaria and as a sleep aid — not peptic ulcer disease (that's H2 blockers/PPIs).  Why A/B/D wrong: These are listed uses of anti-histamines."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"The MOST common side effect of anti-histamines is:","options":["Hypertension","CNS depression","Diarrhoea","Tachycardia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common side effect of anti-histamines is CNS depression.  Why A/C/D wrong: Common side effects are drowsiness, dizziness, blurred vision, constipation, dry mouth — not these."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which is a common side effect of anti-histamines?","options":["Drowsiness, dizziness, blurred vision, constipation, dry mouth","Diarrhoea and hypersalivation","Bradycardia and hypotension","Increased alertness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Common side effects include drowsiness, dizziness, blurred vision, constipation, dry mouth (and sleep aid effect).  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the anticholinergic/sedative profile."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which of the following is an example of an anti-histamine?","options":["Cimetidine","Cetirizine, levocetirizine, chlorpheniramine, diphenhydramine, fexofenadine","Omeprazole","Salbutamol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anti-histamine examples are cetirizine, levocetirizine, chlorpheniramine, diphenhydramine, fexofenadine.  Why A wrong: Cimetidine is an H2 blocker.  Why C wrong: Omeprazole is a PPI.  Why D wrong: Salbutamol is a bronchodilator."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"What is the mode of action of antacids?","options":["Increase gastric acid production","Neutralise gastric acid, inactivate pepsin and enhance mucosal protection (but do not coat the ulcer)","Coat the ulcer directly","Suppress the proton pump"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Antacids neutralise gastric acid, inactivate pepsin, and enhance mucosal protection but do not coat the ulcer.  Why A wrong: They neutralise, not increase, acid.  Why C wrong: They do not coat the ulcer (that is sucralfate).  Why D wrong: That is the PPI mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Antacids are used to treat:","options":["Peptic ulcer diseases and GERD","Hypertension","Asthma","Urinary tract infection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Antacids are used for peptic ulcer diseases and GERD.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not antacid indications."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"When should antacids be taken relative to meals?","options":["30 minutes before meal","After 1 to 3 hours of meal","With the first bite of food","Only at bedtime"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Antacids should be taken after 1 to 3 hours of a meal.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated timing."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Liquid antacid preparations should be:","options":["Diluted with milk","Shaken well before being taken","Warmed before use","Taken with citrus juice"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Liquid preparations should be shaken before taken.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not indicated instructions."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"The most common adverse effect of aluminium hydroxide antacids is (Trick: Aluminium-Constipation = AC):","options":["Diarrhoea","Constipation","Flatulence","Milk alkali syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aluminium components (aluminium hydroxide) most commonly cause constipation (Trick: Aluminium-Constipation = AC).  Why A wrong: Diarrhoea is caused by magnesium antacids.  Why C wrong: Flatulence is linked to calcium/sodium bicarbonate (CO2 release).  Why D wrong: Milk alkali syndrome is a calcium antacid effect."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"The most common adverse effect of magnesium hydroxide antacids is (Trick: Magnesium-Diarrhoea MD):","options":["Constipation","Diarrhoea","Belching","Milk alkali syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Magnesium components (magnesium hydroxide) most commonly cause diarrhoea (Trick: Magnesium-Diarrhoea MD).  Why A wrong: Constipation is caused by aluminium antacids.  Why C wrong: Belching is linked to calcium carbonate (CO2 release).  Why D wrong: Milk alkali syndrome relates to calcium."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Calcium carbonate antacids are rapid acting; the released CO2 leads to:","options":["Constipation only","Flatulence & belching","Diarrhoea","Metabolic acidosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Calcium carbonate is rapid acting and CO2 released leads to flatulence & belching.  Why A wrong: Its common side effect is constipation and milk alkali syndrome, but CO2 release specifically causes flatulence/belching.  Why C/D wrong: These are not caused by CO2 release from calcium carbonate."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"A common side effect specific to calcium carbonate antacids includes:","options":["Diarrhoea and hypotension","Constipation and milk alkali syndrome","Peripheral neuropathy","Agranulocytosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Calcium carbonate's common side effect is constipation and milk alkali syndrome.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not associated with calcium carbonate antacids."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"A client taking calcium carbonate antacids should be instructed to avoid:","options":["High-fiber foods","Milk products & vitamin-D supplements","Green leafy vegetables","Citrus fruits"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Clients should avoid milk products & vitamin-D supplements (to prevent milk alkali syndrome/hypercalcaemia).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated instruction."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Sodium bicarbonate antacid is rapid acting and, besides causing flatulence from CO2 release, is also used for treating:","options":["Peptic ulcer only","Acidosis","Alkalosis","Hypertension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sodium bicarbonate is rapid acting, causes CO2-related flatulence, and is also used for treating acidosis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not stated uses; it treats acidosis, not alkalosis."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Histamine H2 receptor antagonists suppress gastric acid secretion by acting on:","options":["Proton pump at the terminal stage","H2 histamine receptors at the parietal cells","H1 receptors in the CNS","Prostaglandin synthesis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: H2 antagonists suppress gastric acid secretion by acting on H2 histamine at the parietal cells.  Why A wrong: That is the PPI mechanism.  Why C/D wrong: These are different mechanisms."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"H2 receptor antagonists are contraindicated in clients with:","options":["Diabetes","Hypertension","Asthma","Renal stones"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: H2 blockers are used for peptic ulcer disease and GERD but are contraindicated in hypertensive clients.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which are examples of H2 receptor antagonists?","options":["Omeprazole, pantoprazole","Cimetidine, ranitidine, famotidine, nizatidine","Cetirizine, fexofenadine","Aluminium hydroxide, magnesium hydroxide"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: H2 antagonists include cimetidine, ranitidine, famotidine, nizatidine.  Why A wrong: Those are PPIs.  Why C wrong: Those are anti-histamines (H1 blockers).  Why D wrong: Those are antacids."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Cimetidine can cross the BBB and cause which neuronal side effects?","options":["Mental confusion, agitation, psychosis, depression & disorientation","Peripheral neuropathy only","Cancer-like fatal effects","Retinal damage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cimetidine passes the BBB (Blood Brain Barrier); neuronal side effects include mental confusion, agitation, psychosis, depression & disorientation.  Why B wrong: Not the stated cimetidine effect.  Why C wrong: That describes ranitidine (banned).  Why D wrong: Not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Ranitidine has been banned by WHO and GOI due to:","options":["Causing hypertension","Its cancer-like fatal side effects","Peripheral neuropathy","Crossing the blood brain barrier"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ranitidine, formerly commonly used, is now banned by WHO as well as GOI due to its cancer-like fatal side effects.  Why A/C wrong: Those are not the reason for the ban.  Why D wrong: That describes cimetidine."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"What is the mode of action of proton pump inhibitors (PPIs)?","options":["Neutralise gastric acid","Suppress gastric acid secretion by acting at the terminal stage of production (the proton pump)","Block H2 histamine receptors","Coat the ulcer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PPIs suppress gastric acid secretion/production, acting at the terminal stage of gastric acid production, which is the proton pump.  Why A wrong: That is antacids.  Why C wrong: That is H2 antagonists.  Why D wrong: That is sucralfate."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"With long-term use of PPIs, the most lethal side effect is:","options":["Bone marrow depression","Constipation","Milk alkali syndrome","Peripheral neuropathy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If PPIs are used for a long time, the most lethal side effect is bone marrow depression.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated lethal effect."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which of the following are examples of proton pump inhibitors?","options":["Cimetidine, ranitidine","Pantoprazole, omeprazole, lansoprazole, rabeprazole","Aluminium hydroxide, calcium carbonate","Cetirizine, loperamide"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PPI examples are pantoprazole, omeprazole (commonly used), lansoprazole & rabeprazole.  Why A wrong: Those are H2 antagonists.  Why C wrong: Those are antacids.  Why D wrong: Those are anti-histamine/anti-diarrheal."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Bulk forming laxatives act by:","options":["Stimulating intestinal motility","Absorbing water into the feces to increase bulk, producing large & soft stools","Creating a protective barrier","Lowering ammonia levels"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bulk forming laxatives absorb water into the feces and increase bulk to produce large and soft stools.  Why A wrong: That describes stimulant laxatives.  Why C wrong: That is sucralfate.  Why D wrong: That is lactulose."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Bulk forming laxatives are contraindicated in:","options":["Bowel disease","Hypertension","Renal impairment","Asthma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bulk forming laxatives are contraindicated in bowel disease.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which are examples of bulk forming laxatives?","options":["Bisacodyl and senna","Psyllium and methylcellulose","Lactulose and loperamide","Ondansetron and dexamethasone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bulk forming laxative examples are psyllium and methylcellulose.  Why A wrong: Those are stimulant laxatives.  Why C wrong: Those are for hepatic encephalopathy/diarrhoea.  Why D wrong: Those are anti-emetics."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Stimulant laxatives act by:","options":["Absorbing water into the feces","Stimulating the activity/motility of the large intestine","Neutralising gastric acid","Decreasing peristalsis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stimulant laxatives stimulate the activity or motility of the large intestine.  Why A wrong: That is bulk forming.  Why C/D wrong: Opposite/unrelated actions."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Examples of stimulant laxatives include:","options":["Psyllium and methylcellulose","Bisacodyl and senna","Loperamide","Lactulose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stimulant laxative examples are bisacodyl and senna.  Why A wrong: Those are bulk forming.  Why C wrong: That is anti-diarrheal.  Why D wrong: That is used for hepatic encephalopathy/constipation."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"A client receiving laxatives should be advised to:","options":["Restrict fluid intake","Increase fluid intake to prevent dehydration","Avoid all fiber","Take laxatives with milk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A client receiving laxatives needs to increase fluid intake to prevent dehydration.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated nursing note."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Anti-emetics are used to treat/control:","options":["Diarrhoea and constipation","Vomiting and motion sickness","Peptic ulcer","Hypertension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anti-emetics are used to treat or control vomiting and motion sickness.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not anti-emetic indications."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Because anti-emetics can cause drowsiness, the priority nursing intervention is to:","options":["Encourage ambulation","Protect the client from injury","Restrict fluids","Monitor for constipation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anti-emetics can cause drowsiness, so the priority intervention is protection from injury.  Why A wrong: Ambulation may risk injury when drowsy.  Why C/D wrong: Not the priority safety intervention."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Ondansetron (EMSET, ONSET) is an example of which class of anti-emetic?","options":["Serotonin antagonist","Glucocorticoid","Benzodiazepine","Dopamine antagonist"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ondansetron (EMSET, ONSET) is a serotonin antagonist anti-emetic.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are other anti-emetic classes with different examples."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which glucocorticoid is used as an anti-emetic?","options":["Prednisone","Dexamethasone","Hydrocortisone","Betamethasone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dexamethasone is the glucocorticoid listed as an anti-emetic.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the listed anti-emetic glucocorticoid."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which benzodiazepines are listed as anti-emetics?","options":["Lorazepam and diazepam","Ondansetron and dexamethasone","Chlorpromazine and prochlorperazine","Codeine and morphine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The benzodiazepine anti-emetics listed are lorazepam and diazepam.  Why B wrong: Serotonin antagonist/glucocorticoid.  Why C wrong: Those are dopamine antagonists.  Why D wrong: Those are opioids."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Chlorpromazine and prochlorperazine used as anti-emetics belong to which class?","options":["Serotonin antagonists","Dopamine antagonists","Benzodiazepines","Glucocorticoids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chlorpromazine and prochlorperazine are dopamine antagonist anti-emetics.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are different anti-emetic classes."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Centrally acting skeletal muscle relaxants work by:","options":["Depressing neuron activity in the spinal cord and brain","Interfering with calcium release from muscle tubules","Blocking the neuromuscular junction directly","Neutralising gastric acid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Centrally acting muscle relaxants depress neuron activity in the spinal cord and brain.  Why B wrong: That describes peripherally acting relaxants.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Peripherally acting muscle relaxants act directly on skeletal muscles by:","options":["Depressing the CNS","Interfering with calcium release from muscle tubules and preventing muscular contraction","Neutralising acid","Blocking serotonin receptors"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Peripherally acting muscle relaxants act directly on skeletal muscles, interfering with calcium release from muscle tubules and preventing muscular contraction.  Why A wrong: That is central action.  Why C/D wrong: Unrelated mechanisms."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Skeletal muscle relaxants are used for:","options":["Muscle spasms, muscle spasticity and acute musculoskeletal pain","Peptic ulcer disease","Hypertension","Diarrhoea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Uses include muscle spasms, muscle spasticity and acute musculoskeletal pain.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not indications."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which of the following are examples of skeletal muscle relaxants?","options":["Baclofen, chlorzoxazone, dantrolene","Salbutamol, terbutaline","Colchicine, allopurinol","Ondansetron, dexamethasone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Examples are baclofen, chlorzoxazone, and dantrolene.  Why B wrong: Bronchodilators.  Why C wrong: Antigout drugs.  Why D wrong: Anti-emetics."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Why should liver function be monitored in a client taking skeletal muscle relaxants?","options":["They cause nephrotoxicity","They cause hepatotoxicity","They cause bone marrow depression","They cause crystalluria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Liver function should be monitored because these medicines cause hepatotoxicity.  Why A/C/D wrong: Hepatotoxicity, not these, is the stated concern."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"A client taking chlorzoxazone should be told that the medicine may cause the urine to turn:","options":["Blue-green","Red or orange","Brown","Colourless"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor urine colour with chlorzoxazone because it can cause red, orange colour urine.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated urine colour change."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Dantrolene acts directly on skeletal muscles to relieve muscle spasticity; its most severe complication is:","options":["Nephrotoxicity","Liver dysfunction","Agranulocytosis","Crystalluria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dantrolene's most severe complication is liver dysfunction.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated most severe complication."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"In antigout therapy, colchicine works by:","options":["Reducing uric acid production","Increasing uric acid excretion","Preventing stone formation in kidneys","Neutralising gastric acid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Colchicine increases uric acid excretion.  Why A wrong: That is allopurinol.  Why C wrong: That is probenecid.  Why D wrong: Unrelated antacid action."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Allopurinol reduces gout symptoms by:","options":["Increasing uric acid excretion","Reducing uric acid production","Preventing kidney stones","Blocking prostaglandin synthesis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Allopurinol reduces uric acid production.  Why A wrong: That is colchicine.  Why C wrong: That is probenecid.  Why D wrong: That describes NSAIDs."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Probenecid is used in gout to:","options":["Reduce uric acid production","Prevent stone formation in the kidneys","Increase uric acid excretion","Neutralise stomach acid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Probenecid prevents stone formation in the kidneys.  Why A wrong: That is allopurinol.  Why C wrong: That is colchicine.  Why D wrong: Unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Besides colchicine, allopurinol and probenecid, which other medicines are used in gout?","options":["NSAIDs and glucocorticoids","Opioids and antacids","Fluoroquinolones","Anti-histamines"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Other medicines also used in gout are NSAIDs and glucocorticoids.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not listed antigout options."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which is a listed side effect of antigout medicines?","options":["Metallic taste, bone marrow depression, sore gums","Nephrotoxicity only","Milk alkali syndrome","Peripheral neuropathy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Side effects include metallic taste, bone marrow depression, sore gums and other common effects.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated antigout side effects."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"A client with gout should be instructed to avoid foods high in purine such as:","options":["Wine, alcohol, organ meats, sardines, salmon, scallops and gravy","Bread and rice","Green leafy vegetables","Citrus fruits"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The client should avoid foods high in purine such as wine, alcohol, organ meats, sardines, salmon, scallops and gravy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the listed high-purine foods."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Why should a client on allopurinol NOT take vitamin C?","options":["Because kidney stones may occur","Because it causes bone marrow depression","Because it raises uric acid production","Because it causes hepatotoxicity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The client should not take vitamin C with allopurinol because kidney stones may occur.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Why should aspirin be avoided in a client with gout?","options":["It facilitates uric acid absorption","It increases uric acid excretion","It causes nephrotoxicity","It neutralises uric acid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Aspirin should be avoided because it facilitates uric acid absorption.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which NSAID is the first choice of treatment for arthritis?","options":["Ibuprofen","Aspirin","Paracetamol","Diclofenac"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NSAID ibuprofen is the first choice of treatment for arthritis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not stated as the first choice for arthritis."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which glucocorticoids are used as anti-arthritic medicines?","options":["Dexamethasone and hydrocortisone","Fludrocortisone and aldosterone","Prednisone and betamethasone","Cortisone only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The anti-arthritic glucocorticoids listed are dexamethasone and hydrocortisone.  Why B wrong: Those are mineralocorticoids.  Why C/D wrong: Not the specifically listed drugs here."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Auranofin, a gold salt drug used in arthritis, works by:","options":["Decreasing the progression of the disease","Increasing uric acid excretion","Neutralising gastric acid","Blocking histamine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Gold salt drugs (example: Auranofin) decrease the progression of the disease.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated action."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Hydroxychloroquine, used in rheumatoid arthritis, also works as an:","options":["Anti-malarial and is the safest drug for treating rheumatoid arthritis","Antacid","Anti-emetic","Anticoagulant"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hydroxychloroquine also works as an anti-malarial and is the safest drug for treating rheumatoid arthritis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not its stated roles."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Osteoporosis is defined as a condition with:","options":["Increased bone mass","Decreased bone mass and fragility","Excess calcium in bone","Increased bone density"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Osteoporosis is a condition of decreased bone mass and fragility.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are the opposite of osteoporosis."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which supplementation can reduce the risk of osteoporosis?","options":["Iron and vitamin C","Calcium and vitamin-D supplementation","Potassium and sodium","Vitamin K and folate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Calcium and vitamin-D supplementation can reduce the risk of osteoporosis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated preventive supplements."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Atopic dermatitis is also known as:","options":["Psoriasis","Eczema","Urticaria","Acne"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Atopic dermatitis is also known as eczema.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are different skin conditions."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which topical immunosuppressants (with concentrations) are used for atopic dermatitis?","options":["Tacrolimus 0.1% and pimecrolimus 0.1%","Clindamycin 1% and benzoyl peroxide","Nitrofurazone and silver sulfadiazine","Tretinoin and isotretinoin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Topical immunosuppressants for atopic dermatitis are tacrolimus 0.1% and pimecrolimus 0.1%.  Why B wrong: Those are acne medicines.  Why C wrong: Those are burn medicines.  Why D wrong: Those are acne treatments."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"What is the mode of action of topical glucocorticoids in atopic dermatitis?","options":["Anti-inflammatory, antipruritic and vasoconstrictive actions","Antibacterial only","Increased peristalsis","Proton pump inhibition"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Topical glucocorticoids have anti-inflammatory, antipruritic and vasoconstrictive actions.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not their stated action."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Topical glucocorticoids are absorbed to a GREATER degree in which areas?","options":["Palms and soles","Scalp, axilla, neck, eyelid & perineum","Anterior thigh","Lower back"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Topical glucocorticoids are absorbed greater in areas such as scalp, axilla, neck, eyelid & perineum.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated high-absorption areas."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Before applying a topical glucocorticoid, the area should be:","options":["Left dry and unwashed","Washed to increase medication penetration","Covered with an occlusive dressing first","Massaged with oil"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Before applying, wash the area to increase medication penetration.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated instruction."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which are examples of topical glucocorticoids for atopic dermatitis?","options":["Dexamethasone, betamethasone","Tacrolimus, pimecrolimus","Clindamycin, tretinoin","Nitrofurazone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of topical glucocorticoids are dexamethasone, betamethasone.  Why B wrong: Those are topical immunosuppressants.  Why C wrong: Those are acne medicines.  Why D wrong: That is a burn medicine."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Initial management of acne includes non-pharmacological measures such as:","options":["Washing two to three times daily and avoiding oil-based moisturised products","Applying heavy oil-based creams","Oral antibiotics only","Isotretinoin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Acne is initially treated with non-pharmacological measures such as washing 2–3 times daily and avoiding oil-based moisturised products.  Why B wrong: Oil-based products should be avoided.  Why C/D wrong: These are pharmacological, not initial non-pharmacological measures."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Mild acne is treated with topical medicines such as:","options":["Isotretinoin and doxycycline","Clindamycin, benzoyl peroxide, tretinoin","Spironolactone","Silver sulfadiazine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mild acne is treated by topical medicines such as clindamycin, benzoyl peroxide, tretinoin.  Why A wrong: Those are for moderate acne (oral).  Why C wrong: That is a hormonal option.  Why D wrong: That is a burn medicine."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Moderate acne is treated by oral medications such as:","options":["Clindamycin and benzoyl peroxide","Isotretinoin, doxycycline","Silver sulfadiazine","Nitrofurazone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Moderate acne is treated by oral medications such as isotretinoin, doxycycline.  Why A wrong: Those are topical (mild acne).  Why C/D wrong: Those are burn medicines."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Severe acne is usually treated with isotretinoin, which is a derivative of:","options":["Vitamin A","Vitamin C","Vitamin D","Vitamin K"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Severe acne is usually treated with isotretinoin (it is a vitamin-A derivative).  Why B/C/D wrong: Isotretinoin is a vitamin-A, not C/D/K, derivative."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which hormonal medicines may be prescribed for acne in female clients?","options":["OCP (oral contraceptive pills) and spironolactone","Fludrocortisone","Levothyroxine","Insulin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hormonal medicines for female clients include OCP and spironolactone.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated hormonal acne treatments."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Isotretinoin should be avoided during pregnancy because it is:","options":["Highly teratogenic","Nephrotoxic","Hepatotoxic","Ototoxic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Isotretinoin is highly teratogenic, so it should be avoided during pregnancy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reason for avoidance in pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"(AIIMS Rishikesh-2018) Nitrofurazone burn medicine is topically applied as a film of what thickness?","options":["1/8 inch","1/16 inch","1/4 inch","1/32 inch"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nitrofurazone is topically applied as a 1/16 inch film directly on the burn area (AIIMS Rishikesh-2018).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 1/16 inch."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"The mode of action of nitrofurazone used in burns is:","options":["Anti-bacterial","Anti-viral","Anti-fungal","Anti-inflammatory"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nitrofurazone acts anti-bacterially and is applied topically.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not its stated mode of action."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Silver sulfadiazine (SS) cream has a broad spectrum of activity against:","options":["Only gram-negative bacteria","Gram-negative and gram-positive bacteria and yeast","Only viruses","Only fungi"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SS cream has broad spectrum activity against gram-negative and gram-positive bacteria and yeast.  Why A/C/D wrong: Its spectrum is broader than these single categories."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Silver sulfadiazine is most commonly used in burn patients to:","options":["Prevent sepsis in clients with burn","Neutralise gastric acid","Increase peristalsis","Reduce fever"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SS cream prevents sepsis in clients with burn and is the most commonly used burn cream.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not its stated purposes."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Silver sulfadiazine should be applied as a:","options":["1/16 firm, thin film for maximum absorption","Thick 1/4 inch layer","Dry powder","Spray only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Apply SS cream as a 1/16 firm, thin film for maximum absorption; keep the burn area covered at all times with silver sulfadiazine.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated application."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Side effects of silver sulfadiazine include:","options":["Leukopenia and interstitial nephritis","Constipation and dry mouth","Tachycardia and hyperglycaemia","Milk alkali syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Side effects of silver sulfadiazine include leukopenia and interstitial nephritis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated SS side effects."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"What is the mode of action of urinary tract antiseptics?","options":["Inhibit the growth of bacteria in the urine","Neutralise urinary acid","Increase urine output","Dissolve kidney stones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Urinary tract antiseptics inhibit the growth of bacteria in the urine and act as disinfectants in the urinary tract.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not their stated action."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which is the most commonly used urinary tract antiseptic?","options":["Ciprofloxacin","Nitrofurantoin","Sulfamethoxazole","Cyclosporine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Examples include nitrofurantoin (most commonly used), fosfomycin, methenamine.  Why A wrong: Ciprofloxacin is a fluoroquinolone.  Why C wrong: Sulfamethoxazole is a sulphonamide.  Why D wrong: Cyclosporine is an immunosuppressant."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Methenamine (a urinary antiseptic) should NOT be used in clients with renal impairment because it can cause:","options":["Crystalluria","Agranulocytosis","Peripheral neuropathy","Milk alkali syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Methenamine can cause crystalluria and should not be used in clients with renal impairment.  Why B/C wrong: Those are nitrofurantoin effects.  Why D wrong: Unrelated to methenamine."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Nitrofurantoin can cause which hematologic/blood disorders?","options":["Agranulocytosis, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia and megaloblastic anaemia","Polycythemia","Milk alkali syndrome","Hypernatremia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nitrofurantoin can cause agranulocytosis, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia and megaloblastic anaemia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated nitrofurantoin adverse effects."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Nitrofurantoin can also cause which neurological adverse effect?","options":["Peripheral neuropathy","Seizures","Meningitis","Stroke"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nitrofurantoin can also cause peripheral neuropathy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated effect."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Nitrofurantoin may change the urine colour to:","options":["Brown colour","Red or orange","Blue-green","Colourless"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nitrofurantoin may change the urine colour to brown.  Why B wrong: Red/orange is associated with chlorzoxazone.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated colour change."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"What is the mode of action of sulphonamides?","options":["Suppress bacterial growth by inhibiting the synthesis of folic acids","Inhibit DNA synthesis enzymes","Inhibit prostaglandin synthesis","Neutralise urinary acid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sulphonamides suppress bacterial growth by inhibiting the synthesis of folic acids.  Why B wrong: That is the fluoroquinolone mechanism.  Why C wrong: That is NSAIDs.  Why D wrong: Unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which are examples of sulphonamides?","options":["Sulfamethoxazole, trimethoprim","Ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin","Nitrofurantoin, methenamine","Ibuprofen, naproxen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Examples are sulfamethoxazole and trimethoprim.  Why B wrong: Those are fluoroquinolones.  Why C wrong: Those are urinary antiseptics.  Why D wrong: Those are NSAIDs."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"The most serious complication of sulphonamides is:","options":["Stevens-Johnson syndrome (skin rashes, fever & toxaemia)","Nephrotoxicity","Peripheral neuropathy","Milk alkali syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Stevens-Johnson syndrome (skin rashes, fever & toxaemia) is the most serious complication of sulphonamides.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated most serious complication."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Sulphonamides should be withheld if which sign is noted?","options":["Skin rashes","Diarrhoea","Dry mouth","Constipation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sulphonamides should be withheld if skin rashes are noted (early sign of Stevens-Johnson syndrome).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated warning sign."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"What is the mode of action of fluoroquinolones?","options":["Suppress bacterial growth by inhibiting an enzyme necessary for DNA synthesis","Inhibit folic acid synthesis","Inhibit prostaglandin synthesis","Neutralise gastric acid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fluoroquinolones suppress bacterial growth by inhibiting an enzyme necessary for DNA synthesis.  Why B wrong: That is sulphonamides.  Why C/D wrong: Unrelated mechanisms."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which are examples of fluoroquinolones?","options":["Ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin, norfloxacin, ofloxacin","Sulfamethoxazole, trimethoprim","Nitrofurantoin, fosfomycin","Morphine, codeine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fluoroquinolone examples are ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin, norfloxacin and ofloxacin.  Why B wrong: Those are sulphonamides.  Why C wrong: Those are urinary antiseptics.  Why D wrong: Those are opioids."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Norfloxacin should be taken:","options":["With food","On an empty stomach","With milk","With antacids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Norfloxacin should be taken on an empty stomach.  Why A/C/D wrong: These impair absorption or are not indicated."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"IV fluoroquinolones should be administered slowly to prevent:","options":["Vein irritation & discomfort","Nephrotoxicity","Hyperglycaemia","Constipation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IV fluoroquinolones should be administered slowly to prevent vein irritation & discomfort.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Cyclosporine is used to prevent rejection of which transplants?","options":["Only bone marrow","Allogeneic kidney, liver and heart transplant","Only cornea","Only skin grafts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cyclosporine is used to prevent rejection of allogeneic kidney, liver and heart transplant.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated transplants."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"The most common side effect of cyclosporine is:","options":["Nephrotoxicity","Hepatotoxicity","Bone marrow depression","Crystalluria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common side effect of cyclosporine is nephrotoxicity.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated most common side effect."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Cyclosporine is contraindicated in:","options":["Pregnancy, breastfeeding and with live vaccines","Diabetes only","Hypertension only","Elderly clients only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cyclosporine is contraindicated in pregnancy, breastfeeding and live vaccines.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Because cyclosporine is embryotoxic, women of childbearing age should:","options":["Use a mechanical form of contraception and avoid oral contraceptives","Use only oral contraceptives","Avoid all contraception","Take double the dose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cyclosporine is embryotoxic; women of childbearing age should use a mechanical form of contraception and avoid oral contraceptives.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated advice."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"(GMCH 2016) Grapefruit juice is contraindicated with cyclosporine because it:","options":["Raises cyclosporine levels and increases the risk of toxicity","Lowers cyclosporine levels","Causes nephrotoxicity independently","Neutralises the drug"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Grapefruit juice is contraindicated with cyclosporine because it raises cyclosporine levels and increases the risk of toxicity (GMCH 2016).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated interaction."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"What is the full form of NSAID?","options":["Non Steroidal Anti-Inflammatory Drugs","New Steroidal Anti-Inflammatory Drugs","Non Selective Anti-Infective Drugs","Neuro-Suppressive Anti-Inflammatory Drugs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NSAID stands for Non Steroidal Anti-Inflammatory Drugs.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"NSAIDs exert their action by inhibiting the synthesis of:","options":["Prostaglandin","Folic acid","DNA enzymes","Histamine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NSAIDs act by inhibiting the synthesis of prostaglandin.  Why B wrong: That is sulphonamides.  Why C wrong: That is fluoroquinolones.  Why D wrong: That is anti-histamines."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which is NOT one of the three listed modes of action of NSAIDs?","options":["Analgesic to relieve pain","Antipyretic to reduce body temperature","Anti-inflammatory to reduce inflammation","Antibiotic to kill bacteria"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: NSAIDs act as analgesic, antipyretic, and anti-inflammatory (and as an anticoagulant inhibiting platelet aggregation) — not as an antibiotic.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are the three listed modes of action."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"In addition to analgesic/antipyretic/anti-inflammatory effects, NSAIDs act as an anticoagulant by:","options":["Inhibiting platelet aggregation or collection","Increasing clotting factor synthesis","Neutralising acid","Inhibiting folic acid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NSAIDs act as an anticoagulant to inhibit platelet aggregation or collection.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated anticoagulant action."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which drugs are classified as non-selective COX inhibitors (traditional NSAIDs)?","options":["Aspirin, ibuprofen, naproxen, mefenamic acid, piroxicam, ketorolac, indomethacin, phenylbutazone","Nimesulide, diclofenac, meloxicam","Celecoxib, etoricoxib, parecoxib","Paracetamol, metamizole"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Non-selective COX inhibitors include aspirin, ibuprofen, naproxen, mefenamic acid, piroxicam, ketorolac, indomethacin, phenylbutazone.  Why B wrong: Those are preferential COX-2 inhibitors.  Why C wrong: Those are selective COX-2 inhibitors.  Why D wrong: Those are analgesic-antipyretics with poor anti-inflammatory action."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Nimesulide, diclofenac and meloxicam are classified as:","options":["Non-selective COX inhibitors","Preferential COX-2 inhibitors","Selective COX-2 inhibitors","Analgesic-antipyretics with poor anti-inflammatory action"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nimesulide, diclofenac, meloxicam are preferential COX-2 inhibitors.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are other NSAID categories."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which drugs are selective COX-2 inhibitors?","options":["Aspirin, ibuprofen","Nimesulide, diclofenac","Celecoxib, etoricoxib, parecoxib","Paracetamol, metamizole"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Selective COX-2 inhibitors are celecoxib, etoricoxib, parecoxib.  Why A wrong: Those are non-selective COX inhibitors.  Why B wrong: Those are preferential COX-2 inhibitors.  Why D wrong: Those are analgesic-antipyretics with poor anti-inflammatory action."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Paracetamol (acetaminophen) and metamizole are classified as:","options":["Analgesic-antipyretics with poor anti-inflammatory action","Selective COX-2 inhibitors","Non-selective COX inhibitors","Preferential COX-2 inhibitors"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Paracetamol (acetaminophen) and metamizole are analgesic-antipyretics with poor anti-inflammatory action.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are the COX-inhibitor categories."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"(AIIMS Bhopal-2018) Which is an EARLY sign of aspirin (Acetylsalicylic Acid, ASA) toxicity?","options":["Tinnitus & dizziness","Constipation","Bradycardia","Hypertension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tinnitus & dizziness are early signs of aspirin toxicity; other effects include bleeding, GI distress, heartburn, nausea & vomiting, visual changes and metabolic acidosis (AIIMS Bhopal-2018).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated early signs."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which acid-base disturbance is associated with aspirin toxicity?","options":["Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory alkalosis only","Respiratory acidosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Aspirin toxicity is associated with metabolic acidosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies metabolic acidosis."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"The most common adverse effect of NSAIDs is hepatotoxicity because NSAIDs are:","options":["Metabolised by the liver","Excreted unchanged by the kidney","Absorbed in the stomach","Bound to plasma proteins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NSAIDs commonly cause hepatotoxicity (most common) because NSAIDs are metabolised by the liver. Other effects: dysrhythmias, dizziness, gastric irritation, hypotension, pruritis, decreased renal function, sodium and water retention.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not explain the stated hepatotoxicity."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"NSAIDs are contraindicated in clients with disease of which organs/systems?","options":["Liver, GI, and renal diseases","Lung and skin only","Bone and joint only","Eye only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NSAIDs are contraindicated in clients with liver, GI, and renal diseases.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"A client taking anticoagulants should NOT take which drug?","options":["Aspirin","Paracetamol","Nitrofurantoin","Loperamide"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clients taking anticoagulants should not take aspirin (increased bleeding risk).  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifically warns against aspirin with anticoagulants."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Why should aspirin and NSAIDs NOT be taken together?","options":["Aspirin decreases the effectiveness of the NSAID and can increase the risk of bleeding","They cancel each other's analgesic effect entirely","They cause hyperglycaemia","They cause constipation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Aspirin and NSAIDs should not be taken together because aspirin decreases effectiveness of the NSAID and can increase the risk of bleeding.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Adolescents/children with flu-like symptoms or viral illness and varicella should NOT take aspirin because of the risk of:","options":["Reye's syndrome","Stevens-Johnson syndrome","Milk alkali syndrome","Cushing syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: They should not take aspirin because of the risk of Reye's syndrome.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the aspirin-related risk in this population."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"NSAIDs should NOT be taken:","options":["On an empty stomach","With food","With water","At bedtime"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NSAIDs should not be taken on an empty stomach (to reduce gastric irritation).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated instruction."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"NSAIDs are contraindicated in patients with which GI condition?","options":["Peptic ulcer disease","GERD only","Diarrhoea","Constipation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NSAIDs are contraindicated in a patient with peptic ulcer disease.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies peptic ulcer disease."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Besides analgesia, aspirin also produces:","options":["Inhibition of platelet aggregation","Increased platelet aggregation","Increased gastric acid","Bronchodilation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Aspirin also produces inhibition of platelet aggregation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are incorrect/opposite effects."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"What is the antidote for paracetamol (acetaminophen) overdose?","options":["N-Acetylcysteine","Naloxone","Vitamin K","Sodium bicarbonate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: N-Acetylcysteine is the antidote of paracetamol overdose (also called Mucomyst).  Why B wrong: Naloxone is for opioid overdose.  Why C/D wrong: Those are sometimes used for aspirin toxicity, not paracetamol."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"(DSSSB-2019) The nurse should monitor an NSAID/aspirin client for signs of bleeding such as:","options":["Tarry stool, bleeding gums, petechiae, ecchymosis and purpura","Dry mouth and constipation","Hyperglycaemia and tachycardia","Peripheral neuropathy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor for signs of bleeding such as tarry stool, bleeding gums, petechiae, ecchymosis and purpura (DSSSB-2019).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not signs of bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Aspirin should be discontinued how many days before any type of surgery to prevent unexpected bleeding?","options":["3 days","5 days","7 days","14 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Aspirin should be discontinued 7 days before any surgery to prevent unexpected bleeding.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated 7-day period."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which are examples of natural opium alkaloids (narcotic analgesics)?","options":["Morphine, codeine","Heroin, oxycodone","Pethidine, fentanyl","Methadone, tramadol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Natural opium alkaloids are morphine and codeine.  Why B wrong: Those are semi-synthetic opioids.  Why C/D wrong: Those are synthetic opioids."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which are examples of semi-synthetic opioids?","options":["Morphine, codeine","Diacetylmorphine (Heroin), ethylmorphine, oxycodone, hydrocodone","Pethidine, fentanyl, methadone, tramadol","Paracetamol, ibuprofen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Semi-synthetic opioids include diacetylmorphine (Heroin), ethylmorphine, oxycodone, hydrocodone.  Why A wrong: Those are natural opium alkaloids.  Why C wrong: Those are synthetic opioids.  Why D wrong: Those are NSAIDs."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which are examples of synthetic opioids?","options":["Morphine, codeine","Heroin, oxycodone","Pethidine, fentanyl, methadone, tramadol","Aspirin, naproxen"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Synthetic opioids are pethidine, fentanyl, methadone, tramadol.  Why A wrong: Those are natural opium alkaloids.  Why B wrong: Those are semi-synthetic.  Why D wrong: Those are NSAIDs."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which is a listed side effect of opioid analgesics?","options":["Respiratory depression, apnea, dizziness, euphoria, headache, mental clouding, constipation, urinary retention, decreased libido","Diarrhoea and increased libido","Hyperventilation and hypertension","Tinnitus and metabolic acidosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Side effects of opioid analgesics include respiratory depression, apnea, dizziness, euphoria, headache, mental clouding, insomnia, nausea, vomiting, constipation, dry mouth, skin rashes, laryngospasm, urinary retention and decreased libido.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the opioid profile."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Morphine is contraindicated in a client with head injury because it can cause:","options":["Further increase in ICP (intracranial pressure)","Decreased ICP","Hyperglycaemia","Hypertension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Morphine is contraindicated in clients with head injury because it can cause a further increase in ICP.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"(JIPMER-2023) The most common side effects of narcotic analgesics include:","options":["Sedation, euphoria, respiratory depression, constipation and physical dependence","Diarrhoea and hyperactivity","Hypertension and tachycardia","Crystalluria and nephrotoxicity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common side effects of narcotic analgesics include sedation, euphoria, respiratory depression, constipation, and physical dependence (JIPMER-2023).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated common effects."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"(DSSSB-2019 & AIIMS Nagpur-2020) What is the antidote for aspirin toxicity?","options":["No specific antidote available; sometimes sodium bicarbonate & vitamin-K are used","Naloxone","N-Acetylcysteine","Atropine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: There is no specific antidote for aspirin toxicity, but sometimes sodium bicarbonate & vitamin-K are used (DSSSB-2019 & AIIMS Nagpur-2020).  Why B wrong: Naloxone is for opioids.  Why C wrong: N-acetylcysteine is for paracetamol.  Why D wrong: Atropine is not the antidote here."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"What is the antidote for paracetamol (PCM) and acetaminophen overdose?","options":["Acetylcysteine or Mucomyst","Sodium bicarbonate","Vitamin-K","Naloxone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote for PCM and acetaminophen is acetylcysteine or Mucomyst.  Why B/C wrong: Those are used for aspirin toxicity.  Why D wrong: Naloxone is for opioid overdose."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Acetaminophen is described as an anti-inflammatory medicine that inhibits prostaglandin synthesis and is used for:","options":["Reducing pain and fever","Treating infection","Lowering blood glucose","Preventing clotting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Acetaminophen inhibits prostaglandin synthesis and is used for reducing pain and fever.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not its stated uses."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Nasal decongestants relieve congestion by:","options":["Shrinking nasal mucosal membranes and reducing fluid secretions","Increasing nasal secretions","Suppressing the cough reflex","Neutralising gastric acid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nasal decongestants shrink nasal mucosal membranes and reduce fluid secretions.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are opposite or unrelated actions."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Nasal decongestants are used for all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Allergic rhinitis, hay fever, acute coryza","Profuse nasal discharge","Severe nasal congestion/blockage from excessive secretion","Chronic constipation"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Nasal decongestants are used for allergic rhinitis, hay fever, acute coryza, profuse nasal discharge, and severe nasal congestion/blockage — not chronic constipation.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are listed indications."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which are examples of nasal decongestants?","options":["Naphazoline, oxymetazoline, budesonide, fluticasone, xylometazoline","Codeine, hydrocodone","Loperamide, ondansetron","Baclofen, dantrolene"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Examples include naphazoline, oxymetazoline, budesonide, fluticasone and xylometazoline.  Why B wrong: Those are anti-tussive/opioids.  Why C wrong: Anti-diarrheal/anti-emetic.  Why D wrong: Muscle relaxants."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Expectorants act by:","options":["Loosening bronchial secretions so they can be eliminated with coughing","Suppressing the cough reflex","Thinning mucus directly","Constricting the bronchi"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Expectorants loosen the bronchial secretions so they can be eliminated with coughing.  Why B wrong: That is anti-tussive.  Why C wrong: That is mucolytics.  Why D wrong: Unrelated action."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Expectorants are usually used for which type of cough?","options":["Dry-unproductive cough (to stimulate bronchial secretions)","Productive cough only","Whooping cough only","No cough at all"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Expectorants are usually used for a dry-unproductive cough and to stimulate bronchial secretions.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated use."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Mucolytic agents work by:","options":["Thinning mucus secretions to help make cough more productive","Suppressing the cough centre","Shrinking nasal membranes","Loosening secretions without thinning them"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mucolytic agents thin mucus secretions to help make the cough more productive.  Why B wrong: That is anti-tussive.  Why C wrong: That is decongestants.  Why D wrong: That describes expectorants."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Mucolytic agents with dextromethorphan should NOT be used by clients with COPD because they:","options":["Suppress the cough","Cause bronchospasm","Increase secretions","Cause hyperglycaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mucolytics with dextromethorphan should not be used by COPD clients because they suppress the cough.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which are examples of an expectorant and a mucolytic, respectively?","options":["Guaifenesin (expectorant) and acetylcysteine (mucolytic)","Codeine and dextromethorphan","Loperamide and ondansetron","Naphazoline and oxymetazoline"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Expectorant example is guaifenesin; mucolytic example is acetylcysteine.  Why B wrong: Those are anti-tussives.  Why C wrong: Anti-diarrheal/anti-emetic.  Why D wrong: Nasal decongestants."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Anti-tussive medicines act on the cough control centre in which part of the brain to suppress the cough reflex?","options":["Medulla","Cerebellum","Hypothalamus","Pituitary"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anti-tussives act on the cough control centre in the medulla to suppress the cough reflex.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated site of action."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Anti-tussive medicines are used for which type of cough?","options":["Non-productive or irritating cough","Productive cough","Cough with excessive mucus","All coughs equally"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anti-tussives are used for a cough that is non-productive or irritating.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated indication."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which are opioid anti-tussive examples?","options":["Codeine phosphate, hydrocodone","Dextromethorphan, diphenhydramine chloride","Guaifenesin, acetylcysteine","Loperamide"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Opioid anti-tussives are codeine phosphate and hydrocodone.  Why B wrong: Those are non-opioid anti-tussives.  Why C wrong: Expectorant/mucolytic.  Why D wrong: Anti-diarrheal."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which are non-opioid anti-tussive examples?","options":["Codeine phosphate, hydrocodone","Dextromethorphan, diphenhydramine chloride","Guaifenesin","Morphine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Non-opioid anti-tussives are dextromethorphan and diphenhydramine chloride.  Why A wrong: Those are opioid anti-tussives.  Why C wrong: Expectorant.  Why D wrong: Opioid analgesic."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Thyroid medicines are used to treat:","options":["Hypothyroidism and myxedema coma","Hyperthyroidism only","Diabetes","Osteoporosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thyroid medicines are used to treat hypothyroidism and myxedema coma.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated indications."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which is the MOST commonly used thyroid medicine?","options":["Levothyroxine sodium","Liothyronine sodium","Methimazole","Propylthiouracil"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Levothyroxine sodium is the most commonly used thyroid medicine (the other listed drug is liothyronine sodium).  Why B wrong: Liothyronine is listed but not marked as most common.  Why C/D wrong: These are not listed thyroid replacement medicines here."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which are specific side effects of thyroid medicines?","options":["Weight loss, nervousness, tremors, insomnia, sweating, heat intolerance, tachycardia, palpitations, chest pain, hypertension, headache","Weight gain, bradycardia, cold intolerance","Constipation and dry skin","Hypoglycaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Specific side effects include weight loss, nervousness, tremors, insomnia, sweating, heat intolerance, tachycardia, palpitations, chest pain, hypertension and headache.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reflect hypothyroid features, not thyroid-medicine side effects (which mimic hyperthyroidism)."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Thyroid hormone toxicity may cause:","options":["Hyperthyroidism","Hypothyroidism","Myxedema","Hypoglycaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thyroid hormone toxicity may cause hyperthyroidism.  Why B/C wrong: These are the conditions treated, not the toxicity effect.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which foods that inhibit thyroid secretion should a client on thyroid medicine avoid?","options":["Strawberries, pears, cabbage, spinach, cauliflower","Wine and organ meats","Milk and vitamin-D","Citrus fruits"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Avoid foods that inhibit thyroid secretion such as strawberries, pears, cabbage, spinach, cauliflower.  Why B wrong: Those are high-purine foods (gout).  Why C wrong: Those relate to calcium antacids.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"When should thyroid medicines be taken for best absorption?","options":["In an empty stomach, preferably in the early morning","With a full meal at night","With milk","With antacids"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thyroid medicines should be taken on an empty stomach, preferably in the early morning, because they absorb better on an empty stomach.  Why B/C/D wrong: These impair absorption or contradict the instruction."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Thyroid medicine's action is DECREASED by which drugs?","options":["Phenytoin & carbamazepine","Aspirin & ibuprofen","Loperamide & ondansetron","Morphine & codeine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thyroid medicine's action is decreased by phenytoin & carbamazepine.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated interacting drugs."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Thyroid medicine's ABSORPTION is decreased by all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Multivitamins","Aluminium & calcium hydroxide","Iron and sucralfate","Codeine"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Absorption is decreased by multivitamins, aluminium & calcium hydroxide, iron and sucralfate — not codeine.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are listed as decreasing thyroid absorption."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"If a patient is taking iron, antacids and multivitamins, thyroid medications should be given at what interval?","options":["At the same time","Within 1 hour","At a 4 hours interval","At a 12 hours interval"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: If a patient is taking iron, antacids and multivitamins, then thyroid medications should be given at a 4 hours interval.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated 4-hour interval."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Parathyroid hormone regulates which serum level?","options":["Serum calcium levels","Serum sodium levels","Serum potassium levels","Serum glucose levels"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Parathyroid hormone regulates serum calcium levels.  Why B/C/D wrong: PTH regulates calcium, not these."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"A low serum calcium level stimulates the parathyroid hormone to:","options":["Release into the bloodstream","Stop secretion","Increase renal excretion of calcium","Deposit calcium in bone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Low serum calcium level stimulates parathyroid hormone to release into the bloodstream.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are opposite/incorrect responses."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Hyperparathyroidism results in:","options":["High serum calcium level and bone demineralisation","Low serum calcium level","High serum sodium level","Low serum potassium level"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperparathyroidism results in high serum calcium level and bone demineralisation; medication is used to lower the serum calcium level.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe hyperparathyroidism."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which medicine is given for hypercalcaemia in hyperparathyroidism?","options":["Doxercalciferol","Calcium carbonate","Calcium gluconate","Ergocalciferol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The example medicine for hypercalcaemia is doxercalciferol (used to lower serum calcium in hyperparathyroidism).  Why B/C/D wrong: Those are used for hypocalcaemia (hypoparathyroidism)."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Hypoparathyroidism results in low serum calcium level, so treatment includes:","options":["Calcium and vitamin-D supplements","Sodium restriction","Potassium supplements","Iron supplements"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypoparathyroidism results in low serum calcium; treatment includes calcium and vitamin-D supplements.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not treat hypocalcaemia."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which medicines are used for hypocalcaemia?","options":["Calcium carbonate, calcium gluconate, cholecalciferol (Vita-D3) and ergocalciferol (Vita-D2)","Doxercalciferol only","Levothyroxine","Phenytoin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypocalcaemia medicines include calcium carbonate, calcium gluconate, cholecalciferol (Vita-D3) and ergocalciferol (Vita-D2).  Why B wrong: Doxercalciferol is for hypercalcaemia.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated drugs."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Lactulose is used in the prevention and treatment of:","options":["Hepatic encephalopathy and hepatic coma","Peptic ulcer disease","Urinary tract infection","Osteoporosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lactulose is used in the prevention and treatment of hepatic encephalopathy and hepatic coma; it is also used in chronic constipation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not lactulose indications."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Besides hepatic encephalopathy, lactulose is also used for:","options":["Chronic constipation","Diarrhoea","GERD","Hypertension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lactulose is also used in chronic constipation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not lactulose uses."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"What is the mode of action of lactulose in hepatic encephalopathy?","options":["Increased peristalsis and bowel evacuation, expelling ammonia from the colon and lowering the ammonia level","Neutralising gastric acid","Increasing ammonia absorption","Suppressing the cough reflex"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lactulose increases peristalsis and bowel evacuation, then expels ammonia from the colon, lowering the ammonia level.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not lactulose's mechanism; it lowers (not raises) ammonia."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"By which routes is lactulose given?","options":["Orally or rectally","IV only","IM only","Topically"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lactulose is given orally or rectally.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated routes."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"What is the mode of action of sucralfate?","options":["Create a protective barrier against acid and pepsin","Neutralise gastric acid","Suppress the proton pump","Block H2 receptors"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sucralfate creates a protective barrier against acid and pepsin.  Why B wrong: That is antacids.  Why C wrong: That is PPIs.  Why D wrong: That is H2 antagonists."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"How should sucralfate be taken?","options":["Orally and on an empty stomach","Orally with food","Rectally","IV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sucralfate is taken orally and it should be taken on an empty stomach.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated instruction."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"A common side effect of sucralfate is:","options":["Constipation","Diarrhoea","Hypertension","Tachycardia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A common side effect of sucralfate is constipation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated side effect."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"How do opioids act as effective anti-diarrheal medications?","options":["By decreasing intestinal motility & peristalsis","By increasing intestinal motility","By neutralising acid","By increasing secretions"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Opioids are effective anti-diarrheal medications that decrease intestinal motility & peristalsis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are opposite or unrelated actions."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Which is the most commonly used anti-diarrheal opioid?","options":["Loperamide","Morphine","Codeine","Fentanyl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Loperamide is the most commonly used anti-diarrheal (opioid-type) medicine.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are used as analgesics/anti-tussives, not the stated common anti-diarrheal."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"A nurse teaching a client on thyroid hormone replacement should advise the client to report signs of:","options":["Hyperthyroidism","Hypoglycaemia","Hypertension only","Hypocalcaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clients taking thyroid hormone should report signs of hyperthyroidism (indicating overdose/toxicity).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reporting instruction."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Insulin nursing care & injection sites, glucagon…","question":"Thyroid medicine can enhance or decrease the action of which medications?","options":["Anticoagulants, insulins and oral anti-diabetes medicines","Antibiotics only","Antacids only","Anti-emetics only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thyroid medicine enhances or decreases the action of anticoagulants, insulins and oral anti-diabetes medicines.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated interacting drug classes."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which agent is most commonly used for treatment of eclampsia and prophylaxis of eclampsia in patients with severe pre-eclampsia?","options":["Calcium gluconate","Magnesium sulphate (MgSO4)","Oxytocin","Ergometrine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Magnesium sulphate is the agent most commonly used for treatment of eclampsia and prophylaxis in severe pre-eclampsia, given IM or IV.  Why A wrong: Calcium gluconate is the antidote for magnesium toxicity, not the treatment for eclampsia.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin is a uterine stimulant used to induce/augment labour.  Why D wrong: Ergometrine is an ergot derivative for uterine haemorrhage, contraindicated in eclampsia."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"MgSO4 is described as the drug of choice for which condition?","options":["Postpartum haemorrhage","Eclampsia","Induction of labour","Patent ductus arteriosus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Magnesium sulphate is a drug of choice for eclampsia.  Why A wrong: Ergot derivatives/oxytocin manage PPH.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin/prostaglandins induce labour.  Why D wrong: Indomethacin is drug of choice for PDA."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Besides use in eclampsia, magnesium sulphate is widely used as a:","options":["Tocolytic (reduce uterine contractions and prevent pre-term labour)","Uterine stimulant","Vasopressor","Antibiotic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MgSO4 is used as a tocolytic to reduce uterine contractions and prevent pre-term labour.  Why B wrong: It relaxes rather than stimulates uterine muscle.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin exerts vasopressor effect, not MgSO4.  Why D wrong: It is not an antibiotic."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"How does magnesium sulphate act at the cellular level in eclampsia management?","options":["As a membrane stabiliser and neuro-protector","As a beta-adrenergic agonist","As a prostaglandin inhibitor","As a dopamine antagonist"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MgSO4 acts as a membrane stabiliser and neuro-protector.  Why B wrong: Terbutaline is the beta-adrenergic agonist.  Why C wrong: Indomethacin is the prostaglandin inhibitor.  Why D wrong: Antipsychotics are dopamine (D2) antagonists."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"What is the normal serum level of magnesium sulphate?","options":["0.6–1.2 mEq/L","1.5–2.6 mg/dl","4–7 mEq/L","10–25 mEq/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal serum level of magnesium sulphate is 1.5–2.6 mg/dl.  Why A wrong: 0.6–1.2 mEq/L is the lithium therapeutic range, not magnesium normal.  Why C wrong: 4–7 mEq/L is the therapeutic level, not the normal level.  Why D wrong: Levels exceeding 25 mEq/L cause cardiac arrest (toxic)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"The therapeutic level of magnesium sulphate is:","options":["4 to 7 mEq/L or 4.8–8.4 mg/dl","1.5–2.6 mg/dl","0.5–2 ng/mL",">10 mEq/L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Therapeutic level of MgSO4 is 4 to 7 mEq/L or 4.8–8.4 mg/dl.  Why B wrong: This is the normal serum level.  Why C wrong: 0.5–2 ng/mL is digoxin's range.  Why D wrong: >10 mEq/L causes respiratory depression (toxic)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"For IV administration, the concentration of MgSO4 should not exceed:","options":["10%","20%","50%","25%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For IV use the concentration should not exceed 20%; one part of 50% MgSO4 is diluted with 1.5 parts sterile water to make it 20%.  Why A wrong: 10% is the calcium gluconate strength (antidote), not the MgSO4 IV limit.  Why C wrong: 50% is the undiluted injection that must be diluted before IV use.  Why D wrong: 25% is not the stated IV concentration limit."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"To prepare a 20% MgSO4 solution for IV use, one part of 50% MgSO4 injection is diluted with how many parts of sterile water for injection?","options":["0.5 parts","1 part","1.5 parts","2 parts"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: One part 50% MgSO4 is diluted with 1.5 parts sterile water to make it 20%, then given IV slowly.  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated dilution ratio is 1:1.5, not 0.5, 1, or 2 parts."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"According to the loading dose guideline (Haryana CHO-2022), what is the total IM loading dose of MgSO4?","options":["10 gm","14 gm","5 gm","20 gm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IM regimen = 4 gm (20% solution) IV over 3–5 min followed by 10 gm (50%) deep IM (5 gm in each buttock); total loading dose = 14 gm.  Why A wrong: 10 gm is only the deep IM portion.  Why C wrong: 5 gm is the amount per buttock / maintenance dose.  Why D wrong: 20 gm is the solution concentration reference, not the loading dose."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"In the IM regimen for MgSO4, what is the maintenance dose?","options":["5 gm (50%) IM 4 hourly in alternate buttock","4 gm IV over 3–5 minutes","2 gm IV bolus over 5 minutes","1 to 2 gm/hour IV infusion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IM maintenance dose is 5 gm (50%) IM 4 hourly in alternate buttock.  Why B wrong: 4 gm IV over 3–5 min is part of the loading regimen.  Why C wrong: 2 gm IV bolus over 5 min is for seizure recurrence after 24 hrs.  Why D wrong: 1–2 gm/hour IV infusion is the IV-route maintenance dose."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"For the IV route, the loading dose of MgSO4 is:","options":["4 to 6 gm IV over 15–20 minutes","14 gm deep IM","5 gm 4 hourly","2 gm over 5 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IV loading dose is 4 to 6 gm IV over 15–20 minutes; maintenance is 1 to 2 gm/hour by infusion.  Why B wrong: 14 gm is the IM total loading dose.  Why C wrong: 5 gm 4 hourly is the IM maintenance dose.  Why D wrong: 2 gm over 5 min is the recurrence bolus."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"For the IV route, the maintenance dose of MgSO4 via infusion is:","options":["1 to 2 gm/hour IV","4 to 6 gm/hour IV","10 gm/hour IV","5 gm/hour IV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IV maintenance dose is 1 to 2 gm/hour via infusion.  Why B wrong: 4–6 gm is the IV loading dose given over 15–20 min.  Why C/D wrong: These rates are not the stated maintenance dose."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"During magnesium therapy in the critical phase, how often should blood pressure be monitored?","options":["Every 15 minutes","Every 30 minutes","Every 6 hours","Once daily"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BP is monitored every 15 minutes during the critical phase and every 1 hour after stabilisation.  Why B/D wrong: These intervals are not specified.  Why C wrong: Every 6 hours is the interval for assessing serum magnesium level."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"After the critical phase (stabilisation), how often is blood pressure monitored during magnesium therapy?","options":["Every 15 minutes","Every 1 hour","Every 4 hours","Every 6 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: BP is monitored every 1 hour after the critical phase/after stabilisation.  Why A wrong: Every 15 min applies during the critical phase.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated interval for BP."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Why is an indwelling Foley catheter inserted during magnesium sulphate therapy?","options":["To measure accurate hourly urine output","To administer the drug","To prevent infection","To reduce blood pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An indwelling Foley catheter is inserted to measure accurate hourly urine output (a key toxicity monitor).  Why B wrong: MgSO4 is given IM/IV, not via catheter.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated reasons."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"What is the laboratory hallmark of eclampsia that urine is assessed for during magnesium therapy?","options":["Uric acid","Glucose","Ketones","Bilirubin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Urine is assessed for uric acid (laboratory hallmark of eclampsia) and proteinuria.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not identified as the eclampsia hallmark."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"A repeat dose of MgSO4 is given only if the knee jerk reflex is present at what grade?","options":["+1 grade","+2 grade","+3 grade","0 grade"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Repeat dose is given only if knee jerk reflex is present at +2 grade, urine output >30 ml/hr, and respiration rate >12/minute.  Why A/C wrong: The stated criterion is specifically +2 grade.  Why D wrong: Grade 0 means no response/absent reflex — a sign of toxicity, so no repeat dose."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"A repeat dose of MgSO4 requires urine output to be:","options":[">30 ml/hr","<30 ml/hr",">12 ml/hr",">16 ml/hr"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Urine output must be >30 ml/hr for a repeat dose.  Why B wrong: Urine output <30 ml/hr is a sign of toxicity.  Why C/D wrong: These values are unrelated to the urine-output criterion."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"A repeat dose of MgSO4 requires the respiration rate to be:","options":[">12/minute","<12/minute",">30/minute","<16/minute"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Respiration rate must be >12/minute before repeating a dose.  Why B/D wrong: A respiratory rate less than 12–16/min signals toxicity.  Why C wrong: >30/minute is not the stated criterion."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which set of findings indicates a therapeutic response to magnesium therapy?","options":["+2 grade deep tendon reflexes, increased urine output (>30 ml/hr), and absence of seizure","Absent deep tendon reflexes and urine output <30 ml/hr","Respiratory rate <12/min and hypotension","Cardiac arrest and flushed face"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Therapeutic response = +2 grade DTRs, increased urine output (>30 ml/hr), and absence of seizure.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are signs of magnesium toxicity, not therapeutic response."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"According to serum-level parameters of MgSO4 toxicity, loss of deep tendon reflex occurs when the serum level exceeds:","options":["7 mEq/L","10 mEq/L","25 mEq/L","2.6 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Loss of deep tendon reflex occurs if serum MgSO4 exceeds 7 mEq/L.  Why B wrong: >10 mEq/L causes respiratory depression.  Why C wrong: >25 mEq/L causes cardiac arrest.  Why D wrong: 2.6 mg/dl is the upper normal serum level."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Respiratory depression from magnesium toxicity occurs when the serum MgSO4 level exceeds:","options":["7 mEq/L","10 mEq/L","25 mEq/L","4 mEq/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Respiratory depression occurs if serum MgSO4 exceeds 10 mEq/L.  Why A wrong: >7 mEq/L causes loss of deep tendon reflex.  Why C wrong: >25 mEq/L causes cardiac arrest.  Why D wrong: 4 mEq/L is within the therapeutic range."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Cardiac arrest from magnesium toxicity occurs when the serum MgSO4 level exceeds:","options":["7 mEq/L","10 mEq/L","25 mEq/L","15 mEq/L"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiac arrest occurs if serum MgSO4 exceeds 25 mEq/L.  Why A wrong: >7 mEq/L = loss of DTR.  Why B wrong: >10 mEq/L = respiratory depression.  Why D wrong: 15 mEq/L is not the stated cardiac-arrest threshold."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which of the following is a sign used to detect magnesium toxicity? (Select all that apply)","options":["Loss/depressed/absent deep tendon reflexes","Urine output less than 30 ml/hr","Decreased respiratory rate, less than 16 per min","Oxygen saturation greater than 95%","Chest pain, pulmonary oedema & heart block"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Detection signs include absent/depressed DTRs, urine output <30 ml/hr, respiratory rate <16/min, O2 saturation less than 95%, and chest pain/pulmonary oedema/heart block/flushed face.  Why D wrong: The toxicity sign is oxygen saturation LESS than 95%, not greater than 95%."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"In magnesium toxicity, oxygen saturation falls below what value?","options":["88%","90%","95%","98%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A detection sign of magnesium toxicity is oxygen saturation less than 95%.  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated threshold is 95%."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"What is the FIRST priority nursing intervention in magnesium toxicity?","options":["To stop magnesium therapy","Administer calcium gluconate","Administer IV fluids","Assess serum creatinine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The first priority is to stop magnesium therapy.  Why B wrong: Administering the antidote (calcium gluconate) is the next step, after stopping the drug.  Why C/D wrong: These are supportive/later interventions."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which drug is administered as the antidote for maternal MgSO4 toxicity (Haryana CHO-2021)?","options":["Injection Calcium Gluconate 10% (10 ml) IV over 10 minutes","Levallorphan (Lorfan)","Naloxone","Protamine sulphate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote for maternal magnesium toxicity is Injection Calcium Gluconate 10% (10 ml) IV over 10 minutes (10:10:10 formula).  Why B wrong: Levallorphan (Lorfan) is the antidote for the newborn.  Why C wrong: Naloxone reverses opioids.  Why D wrong: Protamine reverses heparin."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"The \"10:10:10 formula\" for administering the MgSO4 toxicity antidote refers to:","options":["Calcium gluconate 10% (10 ml) IV over 10 minutes","10 gm over 10 minutes for 10 doses","10 mEq/L target over 10 hours","10 mg IM every 10 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 10:10:10 = calcium gluconate 10% strength, 10 ml volume, over 10 minutes IV.  Why B/C/D wrong: These misrepresent the antidote formula."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"What is the antidote for magnesium sulphate toxicity in the NEWBORN?","options":["Calcium gluconate 10%","Levallorphan (Lorfan)","Vitamin K","Naloxone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the \"Should Remember\" box, the newborn antidote for magnesium toxicity is Levallorphan (Lorfan).  Why A wrong: Calcium gluconate (10%) is the antidote for the mother.  Why C wrong: Vitamin K prevents haemorrhagic disease of newborn, not magnesium toxicity.  Why D wrong: Naloxone reverses opioid depression."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"For how long is magnesium sulphate continued after the last seizure or delivery (whichever is later)?","options":["6 hours","12 hours","24 hours","48 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: MgSO4 is continued for 24 hours after the last seizure or delivery, whichever is later.  Why A wrong: 6 hours is the interval for assessing serum magnesium level.  Why B/D wrong: Not the stated duration."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"If seizure recurs after 24 hours of magnesium therapy, what further dose is given?","options":["2 gm IV bolus over 5 minutes","4 gm IM in each buttock","10 gm deep IM","6 gm over 15–20 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For seizure recurrence after 24 hours, a further 2 gm IV bolus is given over 5 minutes.  Why B/C wrong: These are loading-dose components.  Why D wrong: 6 gm over 15–20 min is the IV loading dose."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"During magnesium therapy, how often should the serum magnesium level be assessed?","options":["Every 15 minutes","Every 1 hour","Every 6 hours","Every 24 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Serum magnesium level is assessed every 6 hours (with periodic FHR assessment).  Why A wrong: 15 min is the BP interval in critical phase.  Why B wrong: 1 hour is post-stabilisation BP interval.  Why D wrong: 24 hours is the continuation duration."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"A client on magnesium therapy is placed in a side-lying position primarily to prevent:","options":["Supine hypotensive syndrome","Aspiration pneumonia","Pressure ulcers","Deep vein thrombosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Side-lying position prevents supine hypotensive syndrome caused by pressure of the gravid uterus on the vena cava.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reason for the side-lying position here."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Supine hypotensive syndrome in pregnancy occurs due to pressure of the gravid uterus on which structure?","options":["Aorta","Vena cava","Renal artery","Portal vein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: It occurs due to pressure of the gravid uterus on the vena cava.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifically names the vena cava."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which dietary recommendation is advised for a client on magnesium therapy?","options":["High protein diet with adequate intake of sodium","Low protein, sodium-restricted diet","High fat diet","Clear liquid diet only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A high protein diet with adequate sodium intake is offered.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated recommendation."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"In the gradation of deep tendon reflexes, Grade 0 indicates:","options":["No response, hyporeflexia — always an abnormal condition","Brisk, normal & active response","Very brisk/increased response","Hyperreflexia with clonus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Grade 0 = no response, hyporeflexia, always an abnormal condition.  Why B wrong: That describes +2.  Why C wrong: That describes +3.  Why D wrong: That describes +4."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which deep tendon reflex grade represents \"a brisk response, normal & active\"?","options":["0","+1","+2","+4"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: +2 = a brisk response, normal & active (the target for repeat dosing).  Why A wrong: 0 = no response.  Why B wrong: +1 = slight but definitely present response, diminished.  Why D wrong: +4 = hyperreflexia with clonus."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"In the deep tendon reflex gradation, +4 grade is described as:","options":["Diminished but present response","Brisk, normal & active","Very brisk/increased response","Hyperreflexia, very hyperactive response with clonus"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: +4 = hyperreflexia, very hyperactive response with clonus (rhythmic oscillations b/w flexion and extension).  Why A wrong: That is +1.  Why B wrong: That is +2.  Why C wrong: That is +3."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Deep tendon reflex grade +1 is best described as:","options":["No response, hyporeflexia","A slight but definitely present response, diminished — may or may not be normal","A brisk, normal & active response","Hyperreflexia with clonus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: +1 = a slight but definitely present response, diminished, may or may not be normal.  Why A wrong: That is grade 0.  Why C wrong: That is +2.  Why D wrong: That is +4."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"What is the primary purpose of oxytocic agents in obstetrics?","options":["Stimulate uterine activity to induce labour, increase contractions, reduce PPH risk, and expel fetal contents in incomplete abortion","Relax the uterus and prevent preterm labour","Promote fetal lung maturity","Prevent Rh isoimmunisation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocics stimulate uterine activity to induce labour, increase contractions, reduce PPH risk immediately after birth, and expel fetal contents in incomplete abortion.  Why B wrong: That describes tocolytics.  Why C wrong: That describes betamethasone/surfactant.  Why D wrong: That describes Anti-D immune globulin."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Endogenous oxytocic drugs are given routinely following C-section to prevent the development of:","options":["Uterine atony","Deep vein thrombosis","Wound infection","Placenta previa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: They are given routinely after C-section to prevent uterine atony.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated indication."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Examples of oxytocic agents include which of the following?","options":["Oxytocin, ergot derivatives, and prostaglandins","Terbutaline, indomethacin, and magnesium sulphate","Betamethasone and dexamethasone","Chlorpromazine and haloperidol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of oxytocic (uterine stimulant) agents include oxytocin, ergot derivatives, and prostaglandins.  Why B wrong: Those are tocolytics.  Why C wrong: Those are corticosteroids for lung maturity.  Why D wrong: Those are antipsychotics."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Where is oxytocin synthesised?","options":["Posterior pituitary gland","Supraoptic & paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus","Anterior pituitary gland","Adrenal cortex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin is synthesised in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus.  Why A wrong: The posterior pituitary is the storage site, not the synthesis site.  Why C/D wrong: Incorrect glands."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Oxytocin is stored in which gland?","options":["Hypothalamus","Anterior pituitary gland","Posterior pituitary gland","Thyroid gland"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin is stored in the posterior pituitary gland.  Why A wrong: The hypothalamus synthesises it.  Why B/D wrong: Incorrect storage sites."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"What is the half-life of oxytocin?","options":["3 to 4 minutes","20 minutes","60 minutes","1 to 2 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The half-life of oxytocin is 3 to 4 minutes.  Why B wrong: 20 minutes is the duration of action.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated half-life."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"What is the duration of action of oxytocin?","options":["3 to 4 minutes","20 minutes","40 minutes","24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Duration of action of oxytocin is 20 minutes.  Why A wrong: 3–4 minutes is the half-life.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated duration."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Oxytocin is sometimes referred to as which of the following?","options":["Love hormone","Stress hormone","Growth hormone","Sleep hormone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin is sometimes called the \"Love Hormone.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated nickname."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Oxytocin is metabolised and degraded by which enzyme?","options":["Oxytocinase","Monoamine oxidase","Acetylcholinesterase","Catechol-O-methyltransferase"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin is metabolised and degraded by oxytocinase.  Why B/C/D wrong: These enzymes act on other substrates, not oxytocin."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"How many amino acids make up oxytocin?","options":["8","9","10","11"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin has 9 amino acids.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated number is 9."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Synthetic oxytocin preparations Syntocinon and Pitocin are available in what concentration?","options":["5 IU/ml","40 units/ml","50 IU","10 IU/ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Syntocinon and Pitocin are both available as 5 IU/ml injections in ampoule.  Why B wrong: 40 units/ml is the oxytocin nasal solution.  Why C wrong: 50 IU is the buccal desamino-oxytocin dose.  Why D wrong: Not the stated concentration."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Syntometrine is a combination of:","options":["Syntocinon 5 IU + Ergometrine 0.5 mg, always given via IM route","Oxytocin + Misoprostol","Ergometrine + Carboprost","Oxytocin + Magnesium sulphate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Syntometrine = syntocinon 5 IU + ergometrine 0.5 mg, always given via IM route.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated composition."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Desamino-oxytocin is described as being how much more powerful than oxytocin, and by which route is it used?","options":["50–100 times more powerful; buccal route 50 IU","5–10 times more powerful; IV route","2 times more powerful; nasal route","20 times more powerful; IM route"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Desamino-oxytocin is 50–100 times more powerful/effective and is used via buccal route 50 IU.  Why B/C/D wrong: These misstate the potency and route."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"The oxytocin nasal solution is available in what concentration?","options":["5 IU/ml","40 units/ml","50 IU/ml","0.5 mg/ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin nasal solution is 40 units/ml.  Why A wrong: 5 IU/ml is the Syntocinon/Pitocin injection.  Why C wrong: 50 IU is the buccal desamino dose.  Why D wrong: 0.5 mg is ergometrine in Syntometrine."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Oxytocin belongs to which class of drugs?","options":["Uterine stimulants","Tocolytics","Beta-adrenergic agonists","Prostaglandin inhibitors"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Class of drug for oxytocin = uterine stimulants.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe tocolytics/terbutaline/indomethacin."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"At what temperature should oxytocin be stored?","options":["2° to 8°C","Room temperature (25°C)","−20°C","15° to 30°C"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin storage temperature is 2° to 8°C.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated storage temperature."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which best describes the mode of action of oxytocin on the uterus?","options":["Stimulates smooth muscle to increase force, frequency, and duration of uterine contractions","Produces tetanic contraction with complete loss of polarity","Relaxes uterine smooth muscle","Inhibits prostaglandin synthesis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin stimulates uterine smooth muscle and increases force, frequency, and duration of contractions.  Why B wrong: Loss of polarity/tetanic contraction describes ergot derivatives.  Why C wrong: Relaxation describes tocolytics.  Why D wrong: That describes indomethacin."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Oxytocin promotes milk ejection (let-down reflex) by eliciting contraction of which cells around the alveoli (IGIMS-2018)?","options":["Myoepithelial cells","Ductal epithelial cells","Adipocytes","Goblet cells"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin promotes milk ejection/let-down by contracting myoepithelial cells surrounding the alveoli and small intralobular ductules.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cells are not responsible for the let-down reflex."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which of the following are indications of oxytocin? (Select all that apply)","options":["Stimulate uterus to contract","To induce or augment labour","Control PPH bleeding by enhancing uterine contractions","Promote milk let-down for breast feeding (intranasal route)","Prevent preterm labour"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin indications include stimulating contraction, inducing/augmenting labour, controlling PPH, and promoting milk let-down via intranasal route, plus vasopressor/antidiuretic effects, incomplete/inevitable/missed abortion, cervical ripening, uterine inertia, and AMSTL via IM.  Why E wrong: Preventing preterm labour is a tocolytic indication; oxytocin stimulates rather than suppresses contractions."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Oxytocin promotes milk let-down and facilitates breast feeding usually by which route?","options":["Intranasal route","Intravenous route","Deep IM route","Buccal route"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: To promote milk let-down and facilitate breast feeding, oxytocin is usually given by the intranasal route.  Why B/C/D wrong: These routes are used for other indications, not the milk let-down indication described."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Besides uterine effects, oxytocin also exerts which additional effects?","options":["Vasopressor and anti-diuretic effects","Bronchodilator and hyperglycaemic effects","Sedative and antipyretic effects","Anticoagulant effects"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin exerts vasopressor and anti-diuretic effects.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not attributed to oxytocin."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Oxytocin is used for the Active Management of the Third Stage of Labor (AMSTL) via which route?","options":["IM","IV bolus","Buccal","Nasal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin is used for AMSTL via the IM route.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies IM for AMSTL."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"By the intramuscular route, oxytocin is given at what dose after birth of the baby as an alternative to ergometrine?","options":["5–10 units IM","20 units IM","40 units IM","0.5 mg IM"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IM route = 5–10 units IM after birth of the baby, as an alternative to ergometrine.  Why B/C wrong: These doses are not stated for the IM route.  Why D wrong: 0.5 mg is the ergometrine dose in Syntometrine."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"By the intravenous route, oxytocin is usually given by which method?","options":["Infusion pump","Rapid IV bolus","Slow direct push over 5 minutes","Piggyback without a pump"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IV oxytocin is usually given by infusion pump.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies an infusion pump for IV administration."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which routes are noted as being only \"occasionally used\" for oxytocin?","options":["Buccal route & nasal spray","IM and IV","Subcutaneous and intradermal","Rectal and vaginal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Buccal route and nasal spray are occasionally used.  Why B wrong: IM and IV are standard routes.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated for oxytocin."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which of the following is a maternal adverse effect of oxytocin?","options":["Water intoxication (which may cause seizure)","Hyperglycaemia","Constipation","Deafness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Maternal adverse effects of oxytocin include nausea & vomiting, tachycardia/headache, hypotension, water intoxication (may cause seizure), and uterine tetany/hyperstimulation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not listed maternal adverse effects of oxytocin."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Uterine tachysystole from oxytocin is defined as more than how many contractions in 10 minutes, which can lead to uterine rupture?","options":[">6 in 10 min",">12 in 10 min",">3 in 10 min",">20 in 10 min"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tachysystole = more than 6 contractions in 10 min, which can lead to uterine rupture.  Why B wrong: >12 in 20 min defines prostaglandin tachysystole.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated threshold."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Ergot derivatives are of which two types?","options":["Ergometrine and Methergine","Oxytocin and Pitocin","Misoprostol and Dinoprostone","Terbutaline and Indomethacin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The two types of ergot derivatives are Ergometrine and Methergine.  Why B wrong: Those are oxytocin preparations.  Why C wrong: Those are prostaglandins.  Why D wrong: Those are tocolytics."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Ergot derivatives act most strongly on which uterine layer, producing very strong contraction without relaxation in between?","options":["Myometrium","Endometrium","Perimetrium","Decidua basalis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ergot derivatives work most strongly on the myometrium, causing very strong uterine contraction without any relaxation in between.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the layer emphasised for ergot action."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Ergot derivatives are the drug of choice to manage uterine haemorrhage due to their:","options":["Haemostatic action","Bronchodilator effect","Neuro-protective effect","Prostaglandin-inhibiting effect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ergot derivatives are highly effective for haemostasis and are the drug of choice to manage uterine haemorrhage due to haemostatic action.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the mechanism behind their haemostatic use."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Ergot derivatives should NOT be used for which purpose?","options":["Induction of labor or abortion","Management of PPH","Control of uterine haemorrhage","Third-stage haemostasis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ergot derivatives should not be used in induction of labor or abortion.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are appropriate uses of ergot derivatives."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Ergot derivatives are contraindicated in hypertension, pre-eclampsia, and eclampsia because of their vaso-constrictive action. Which drug is a choice in these cases instead?","options":["Oxytocin","Methergine","Ergometrine","Carboprost"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Because of vaso-constrictive action, ergots are contraindicated in hypertension/pre-eclampsia/eclampsia; oxytocin is a choice in these cases.  Why B/C wrong: These are ergot derivatives — the contraindicated class.  Why D wrong: Carboprost is a prostaglandin, not the stated alternative here."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which of the following are contraindications to ergot derivatives? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hypertension, pre-eclampsia & eclampsia","MI & stroke, bronchospasm","Suspected multiple pregnancy","Rh negative mother; sepsis & vascular disorders","Postpartum haemorrhage"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Contraindications include hypertension/pre-eclampsia/eclampsia, MI & stroke, bronchospasm, suspected multiple pregnancy, Rh negative mother, sepsis & vascular disorders, and during pregnancy & labor (first & second stage).  Why E wrong: PPH is an indication for ergot derivatives, not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Repeated use of ergot derivatives in the puerperium may lead to low milk secretion following birth due to:","options":["Lowering of prolactin hormone level","Increase in oxytocin","Rise in estrogen","Suppression of oxytocinase"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Repeated puerperal use may lower milk secretion due to a lowering of prolactin hormone level.  Why B/C/D wrong: These mechanisms are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"According to the \"Should Remember\" box, ergot derivatives should NOT be used during pregnancy, first stage of labor, and second stage prior to:","options":["Crowning of the head","Rupture of membranes","Full dilatation","Placental delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ergot derivatives should not be used during pregnancy, first stage of labor, and second stage prior to crowning of the head.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated cut-off is crowning of the head."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Prostaglandins are also known as:","options":["Local hormones","Major tranquilizers","Mood elevators","Neuroleptics"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Prostaglandins are known as local hormones.  Why B/D wrong: Those describe antipsychotics.  Why C wrong: That describes antidepressants."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"The most common source of PGF2 alpha, which promotes myometrial contractibility, is the:","options":["Decidua","Amnion (fetal membranes)","Myometrium","Corpus luteum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Decidua is the most common source of PGF2 alpha, promoting myometrial contractibility.  Why B wrong: Amnion produces PGE2.  Why C wrong: Myometrium produces PGI2.  Why D wrong: Not stated as the PGF2 alpha source."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which prostaglandin, produced by fetal membranes (amnion), induces labor with cervical effacement and dilation?","options":["PGE2","PGF2 alpha","PGI2","PGE1"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fetal membranes (amnion) produce PGE2, which induces labor with cervical effacement and dilation.  Why B wrong: PGF2 alpha comes mainly from decidua.  Why C wrong: PGI2 comes from the myometrium.  Why D wrong: PGE1 (misoprostol) is not the amnion product cited."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"The myometrium of the uterus produces which prostaglandin?","options":["PGI2","PGE2","PGF2 alpha","PGE1"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The myometrium of the uterus produces PGI2.  Why B wrong: Amnion produces PGE2.  Why C wrong: Decidua produces PGF2 alpha.  Why D wrong: PGE1 is misoprostol."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Misoprostol (PGE1) is widely used for all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Medical management of abortion (MTP-Kit)","Induction of labor","AMTSL and peptic ulcer disease","Reversal of magnesium toxicity"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Misoprostol (PGE1) is used for medical abortion (MTP-Kit), induction of labor, AMTSL, and peptic ulcer disease — not for reversing magnesium toxicity (that is calcium gluconate).  Why A/B/C wrong: These are all listed uses of misoprostol."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"What is the mode of action of tocolytics?","options":["Promote uterine relaxation and suppress uterine activity","Stimulate uterine contraction","Increase prolactin secretion","Cause tetanic uterine contraction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tocolytics promote uterine relaxation and suppress uterine activity.  Why B/D wrong: Those describe oxytocics/ergots.  Why C wrong: Not a tocolytic action."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Tocolytics are indicated if uterine contractions or onset of labour start before how many weeks of gestation, to prevent preterm labour?","options":["37 weeks","34 weeks","40 weeks","28 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tocolytics are indicated if contractions/labour start before 37 weeks of gestation to prevent preterm labour.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated cut-off is 37 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which drugs are examples of tocolytics?","options":["Indomethacin, magnesium sulphate, and terbutaline","Oxytocin, ergometrine, and misoprostol","Dinoprostone, carboprost, and methergine","Chlorpromazine, clozapine, and risperidone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of tocolytics include indomethacin, magnesium sulphate, and terbutaline.  Why B/C wrong: Those are oxytocics/prostaglandins/ergots.  Why D wrong: Those are antipsychotics."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"What is the mode of action of indomethacin as a tocolytic?","options":["Prostaglandin inhibitor that relaxes uterine smooth muscle","Beta-adrenergic agonist","CNS depressant","Corticosteroid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Indomethacin is a prostaglandin inhibitor that relaxes uterine smooth muscle.  Why B wrong: That is terbutaline.  Why C wrong: That is magnesium sulphate.  Why D wrong: That is betamethasone."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which adverse effects are associated with indomethacin used as a tocolytic? (Select all that apply)","options":["ARDS","Premature closure of ductus arteriosus","Uterine tetany","Prolonged bleeding time only"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Adverse effects of indomethacin include ARDS and premature closure of the ductus arteriosus.  Why C wrong: Uterine tetany is an oxytocin/ergot effect, not indomethacin's stated adverse effect.  Why D wrong: Not listed as an indomethacin adverse effect here."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Indomethacin is the drug of choice for which neonatal cardiac condition?","options":["Patent Ductus Arteriosus (PDA)","Ventricular septal defect","Tetralogy of Fallot","Atrial septal defect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Indomethacin is the drug of choice for Patent Ductus Arteriosus (PDA), as it promotes ductal closure.  Why B/C/D wrong: Indomethacin is not the drug of choice for these defects."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"As a tocolytic, magnesium sulphate acts as a:","options":["CNS depressant that relaxes smooth muscles including uterine muscles","Prostaglandin inhibitor","Beta-adrenergic agonist","Dopamine antagonist"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Magnesium sulphate is a CNS depressant that relaxes smooth muscles including uterine muscles.  Why B wrong: That is indomethacin.  Why C wrong: That is terbutaline.  Why D wrong: That describes antipsychotics."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"As a tocolytic, magnesium sulphate is used to:","options":["Halt pre-term labour contractions for pre-eclamptic clients to prevent seizure","Induce labour at term","Ripen the cervix","Control postpartum haemorrhage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MgSO4 halts pre-term labour contractions for pre-eclamptic clients to prevent seizure.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are actions of oxytocics/prostaglandins, not tocolytic MgSO4."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which is a maternal side effect of magnesium sulphate as a tocolytic? (Select all that apply)","options":["Depressed deep tendon reflex","Respiratory depression and cardiac arrest","Hypotension and extreme muscle weakness","Decreased urine output","Hyperreflexia and hypertension"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Maternal side effects of MgSO4 include depressed DTR, respiratory depression, cardiac arrest, hypotension, extreme muscle weakness, and decreased urine output.  Why E wrong: MgSO4 causes depressed reflexes and hypotension, not hyperreflexia/hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"What is the newborn side effect of magnesium sulphate used as a tocolytic?","options":["Hypotonia","Hyperbilirubinemia","Hyperglycaemia","Tachycardia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The newborn side effect of MgSO4 is hypotonia.  Why B/D wrong: Those are terbutaline newborn effects.  Why C wrong: Not the stated MgSO4 newborn effect."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Magnesium sulphate as a tocolytic is always given by which method, and how should it NOT be given?","options":["Always given by infusion pump; do not give by IV bolus","Always given by IV bolus; do not give by infusion","Always given by IM; do not give by IV","Always given orally; do not give parenterally"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MgSO4 is always given by infusion pump and should not be given by IV bolus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated administration rule."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"To which drug class does terbutaline belong?","options":["Beta-adrenergic agonist","Prostaglandin inhibitor","CNS depressant","Corticosteroid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Terbutaline is a beta-adrenergic agonist.  Why B wrong: Indomethacin is the prostaglandin inhibitor.  Why C wrong: MgSO4 is the CNS depressant.  Why D wrong: Betamethasone is a corticosteroid."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"What is the mode of action of terbutaline?","options":["Relaxes smooth muscles, inhibiting uterine activity and causing bronchodilation","Stimulates uterine contraction","Depresses the CNS","Inhibits prostaglandin synthesis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Terbutaline relaxes smooth muscles, inhibits uterine activity, and causes bronchodilation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other drug classes."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which of the following are maternal side effects of terbutaline? (Select all that apply)","options":["Tachycardia and palpitations","Pulmonary oedema and chest pain","MI and hypotension","Hypokalaemia","Bradycardia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Maternal side effects of terbutaline include tachycardia, palpitations, pulmonary oedema, chest pain, MI, hypotension, and hypokalaemia.  Why E wrong: Terbutaline causes tachycardia, not bradycardia."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which of the following are newborn side effects of terbutaline? (Select all that apply)","options":["Tachycardia","Hypotension","Hypocalcaemia and hyperbilirubinemia","Low sugar level","Hypertonia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Newborn side effects of terbutaline include tachycardia, hypotension, hypocalcaemia, hyperbilirubinemia, and low sugar level.  Why E wrong: Hypotonia (from MgSO4) — not hypertonia — is the relevant neonatal tone effect; hypertonia is not listed for terbutaline."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which of the following are maternal contraindications to terbutaline? (Select all that apply)","options":["Active vaginal bleeding","Pre-eclampsia & eclampsia","High risk labour","Preterm labour before 37 weeks"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Maternal contraindications include active vaginal bleeding, pre-eclampsia & eclampsia, and high risk labour.  Why D wrong: Preterm labour before 37 weeks is the indication for tocolysis, not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which of the following are fetal contraindications to terbutaline? (Select all that apply)","options":["Gestational age >37 weeks","Cervical dilatation >4 cm","Fetal demise & anomalies","Chorioamnionitis, IUGR, and fetal distress","Gestational age of 30 weeks"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Fetal contraindications include gestational age >37 weeks, cervical dilatation >4 cm, fetal demise & anomalies, chorioamnionitis, IUGR, and fetal distress.  Why E wrong: 30 weeks is within the preterm window where tocolysis is indicated, not contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"In nursing care for tocolytic therapy, in which position should the client be placed to improve placental oxygenation and reduce pressure on the cervix?","options":["On her side","Supine flat","Trendelenburg","Prone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The client is positioned on her side to improve placental oxygenation and reduce pressure on the cervix.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the recommended tocolytic positioning."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Anti-D immune globulin should be administered to which mother?","options":["Rh-negative mother who has an Rh positive fetus","Rh-positive mother with an Rh negative fetus","Any mother with pre-eclampsia","Any mother in preterm labour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anti-D should be given to the Rh-negative mother who has an Rh positive fetus (Rh negative women nourishing an Rh positive fetus).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the indication for Anti-D."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"When is the 1st dose of Anti-D immune globulin given during pregnancy?","options":["28 to 32 weeks gestation","At delivery","First trimester","20 weeks gestation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 1st dose is given at 28 to 32 weeks gestation.  Why B wrong: The 2nd dose is after delivery within 72 hours.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated timing."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"When is the 2nd dose of Anti-D immune globulin given after delivery?","options":["Within 72 hours","Within 24 hours","Within 1 week","Within 48 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 2nd dose is given after delivery within 72 hours.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated window is 72 hours."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"By which route is Anti-D immune globulin administered?","options":["Intramuscular (IM)","Intravenous","Subcutaneous","Intradermal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Route of administration of Anti-D is intramuscular (IM).  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated route."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"What is the dose of Anti-D immune globulin?","options":["300 mcg or 1500 IU","100 mcg or 500 IU","0.5 mg","5 IU"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dose of Anti-D is 300 mcg or 1500 IU.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated Anti-D dose."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"The Kleihauer-Betke (KB) test is a quantitative test used to determine:","options":["The amount of fetomaternal hemorrhage (FMH) and, in Rh-negative patients, the dose of Anti-D injection","Serum magnesium level","Fetal lung maturity","Bishop score"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The KB test quantitatively determines fetomaternal hemorrhage (fetal blood in maternal circulation) and, in Rh-negative patients, the dose of Anti-D injection.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are unrelated to the KB test."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Anti-D immune globulin is indicated after which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["After abortion in a client with Rh negative mother","After invasive procedures such as amniocentesis or CVS","Rh-negative mother with Rh positive fetus","Rh-positive mother with Rh negative fetus"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Indications include after abortion in Rh-negative mother, after invasive procedures (amniocentesis, CVS), and Rh-negative mother nourishing an Rh positive fetus.  Why D wrong: Anti-D is for the Rh-negative mother, not the Rh-positive mother with Rh negative fetus."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Rubella vaccine can be given by which route before hospital discharge to a non-immune postpartum client?","options":["Subcutaneous (SC)","Intramuscular","Intravenous","Oral"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rubella vaccine can be given SC before hospital discharge to a non-immune postpartum client.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated route is subcutaneous."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Rubella vaccine should be administered if the rubella titer is:","options":["<1:8",">1:8","<1:16",">1:32"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: It should be administered if rubella titer is <1:8 (indicating non-immunity).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated titer threshold."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Rubella vaccine is contraindicated in a woman with a history of which allergy?","options":["Egg allergy","Penicillin allergy","Latex allergy","Sulfa allergy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rubella vaccine is contraindicated in women with a history of egg allergy because the vaccine is prepared using egg.  Why B/C/D wrong: These allergies are not the stated contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"After rubella vaccination, for how long should the client avoid conceiving pregnancy?","options":["3 months","1 month","6 months","1 year"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The client should avoid conceiving pregnancy for 3 months after immunisation and use additional contraceptives for at least 3 months.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated period is 3 months."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"What is the mode of action of betamethasone in pregnancy?","options":["A corticosteroid that increases surfactant production to promote fetal lung maturity and reduce RDS severity","A prostaglandin inhibitor","A CNS depressant","A beta-adrenergic agonist"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Betamethasone is a corticosteroid that increases surfactant production to promote fetal lung maturity and reduce the severity of RDS.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other drug classes."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"What is the indication for betamethasone?","options":["Pre-term labour","Postpartum haemorrhage","Eclampsia","Rh isoimmunisation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Betamethasone is indicated in pre-term labour (to promote fetal lung maturity).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not its indication."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"According to current guidelines, what is the dosing of betamethasone for fetal lung maturity?","options":["12 mg every 24 hours, 2 doses","6 mg every 12 hours, 4 doses","12 mg every 12 hours, 4 doses","6 mg every 24 hours, 2 doses"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Betamethasone dosing is 12 mg every 24 hours, 2 doses.  Why B wrong: That is the dexamethasone regimen.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the stated betamethasone regimen."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"According to current guidelines, what is the dosing of dexamethasone for fetal lung maturity?","options":["12 mg every 24 hours, 2 doses","6 mg every 12 hours, 4 doses","6 mg every 24 hours, 4 doses","12 mg every 12 hours, 2 doses"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dexamethasone dosing is 6 mg every 12 hours, 4 doses.  Why A wrong: That is the betamethasone regimen.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the stated dexamethasone regimen."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which are side effects/contraindications of betamethasone? (Select all that apply)","options":["Pulmonary oedema","Sodium & fluid retention","Elevated blood glucose (hyperglycaemia)","Hypoglycaemia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Side effects include pulmonary oedema, sodium & fluid retention, and elevated blood glucose/hyperglycaemia.  Why D wrong: Corticosteroids raise glucose (hyperglycaemia), not lower it."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"What is the mode of action of prostaglandins used in obstetrics?","options":["Promote ripening of the cervix (softer), dilation and effacement, and stimulate uterine contractions","Relax uterine muscle","Increase surfactant production","Antagonise dopamine receptors"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Prostaglandins ripen the cervix (making it softer), promote dilation and effacement, and stimulate uterine contractions.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other drug classes."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Prostaglandins for cervical ripening are usually administered by which route?","options":["Vaginally","Intravenously","Intramuscularly","Orally"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Prostaglandins are usually administered vaginally.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies vaginal administration."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Prostaglandins are indicated for pre-induction cervical ripening when the Bishop score is:","options":["<4",">8","<8",">12"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Indication includes pre-induction cervical ripening when Bishop score <4, induction of labour, and induction of abortion as an abortifacient.  Why B wrong: A Bishop score ≥8 means treatment can be discontinued.  Why C/D wrong: The stated threshold for ripening is <4."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which are side effects of prostaglandins? (Select all that apply)","options":["Fever, chills, flushing, headache","Hypotension, diarrhoea, stomach cramping","Tachysystole (>12 uterine contractions in 20 minutes)","Hyperstimulation of the uterus and fetal distress (meconium stained)","Bradycardia and hypertension"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Side effects include fever/chills/flushing/headache, hypotension, diarrhoea, stomach cramping, tachysystole (>12 contractions in 20 min), uterine hyperstimulation, and fetal distress (meconium stained).  Why E wrong: Prostaglandins cause hypotension, not hypertension/bradycardia here."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Tachysystole due to prostaglandins is defined as:","options":[">12 uterine contractions in 20 minutes without an alteration in the FHR pattern",">6 contractions in 10 minutes",">4 contractions in 10 minutes",">20 contractions in 20 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Prostaglandin tachysystole = >12 uterine contractions in 20 minutes without an alteration in the FHR pattern.  Why B wrong: >6 in 10 min is the oxytocin tachysystole definition.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated definition."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which prostaglandin corresponds to misoprostol, given intra-vaginally?","options":["Prostaglandin E1 (PGE1)","Prostaglandin E2 (PGE2)","Prostaglandin F2 alpha (PGF2 alpha)","Prostaglandin I2 (PGI2)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PGE1 = misoprostol, intra-vaginal.  Why B wrong: PGE2 = dinoprostone.  Why C wrong: PGF2 alpha = carboprost.  Why D wrong: PGI2 is not listed among the given examples."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Dinoprostone corresponds to which prostaglandin?","options":["PGE1","PGE2","PGF2 alpha","PGI2"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PGE2 = dinoprostone, given intra-vaginally.  Why A wrong: PGE1 = misoprostol.  Why C wrong: PGF2 alpha = carboprost.  Why D wrong: Not listed."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Carboprost, given intra-vaginally, corresponds to which prostaglandin?","options":["PGE1","PGE2","PGF2 alpha (PGF2α)","PGI2"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: PGF2 alpha = carboprost, intra-vaginal.  Why A wrong: PGE1 = misoprostol.  Why B wrong: PGE2 = dinoprostone.  Why D wrong: Not listed as an example."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which of the following are contraindications to prostaglandins? (Select all that apply)","options":["High risk labours","PPH and PID","Non-reassuring FHR pattern","Mal-presentation & mal-position","Bishop score <4"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Contraindications include high risk labours, PPH, PID, non-reassuring FHR pattern, and mal-presentation & mal-position.  Why E wrong: Bishop score <4 is an indication for prostaglandin cervical ripening, not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"During prostaglandin nursing care, the nurse should ensure that the woman does what before medication administration?","options":["Void before the medication administration","Eat a full meal","Ambulate for 30 minutes","Receive an enema"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should ensure that women void before the medication administration.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated pre-administration nursing action."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"After prostaglandin administration, the client should be placed in the side-lying position for a minimum of how long to prevent hypotension?","options":["30 minutes","10 minutes","60 minutes","2 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After administration, place the client in side-lying position for a minimum of 30 minutes to prevent hypotension.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated minimum is 30 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Prostaglandin treatment can be discontinued when the Bishop score reaches:","options":["≥8","<4","≥4","<8"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If a Bishop score ≥8 is achieved, treatment can be discontinued.  Why B wrong: <4 is the indication to start prostaglandins.  Why C/D wrong: The stated discontinuation threshold is ≥8."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"If prostaglandins are not effective for cervical ripening/induction, which drug should be used next?","options":["Oxytocin","Ergometrine","Magnesium sulphate","Terbutaline"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If prostaglandins are not effective, then use oxytocin.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated next-line drug for ineffective prostaglandins."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"According to the \"Also Remember\" box, the middle layer of the uterus (endometrium) is known as what during pregnancy?","options":["Decidua","Amnion","Chorion","Myometrium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The box states the endometrium is known as decidua during pregnancy (and is the major source of PGF2 alpha).  Why B/C wrong: These are fetal membranes.  Why D wrong: Myometrium is the muscular layer (source of PGI2)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"What is the mode of action of ergot alkaloids?","options":["Stimulate uterine muscles, increase force and frequency of contractions, and produce a firm tetanic contraction","Relax uterine smooth muscle","Increase surfactant production","Inhibit prostaglandin synthesis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ergot alkaloids stimulate uterine muscles, increase force and frequency of contractions, and produce a firm tetanic contraction of the uterus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other drug classes."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Ergot alkaloids carry a possibility of arterial vasoconstriction and vasospasm of which arteries?","options":["Coronary arteries","Renal arteries","Cerebral arteries","Pulmonary arteries"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: There is a possibility of arterial vasoconstriction and vasospasm of the coronary arteries.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies coronary arteries."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"What is the ideal time for administration of ergot alkaloids?","options":["Just after delivery of the placenta","During the first stage of labour","Before delivery of the placenta","At crowning"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ideal time for administration is just after delivery of the placenta; they should not be given before delivery of the placenta.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated ideal timing."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which of the following are examples of ergot alkaloids?","options":["Ergonovine and methergine","Misoprostol and dinoprostone","Oxytocin and pitocin","Terbutaline and indomethacin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of ergot alkaloids are ergonovine and methergine.  Why B wrong: Those are prostaglandins.  Why C wrong: Those are oxytocin preparations.  Why D wrong: Those are tocolytics."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which of the following are indications for ergot alkaloids?","options":["PPH and post-abortal haemorrhage","Preterm labour prevention","Cervical ripening","Fetal lung maturity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Indications for ergot alkaloids are PPH and post-abortal haemorrhage.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are indications for tocolytics/prostaglandins/corticosteroids, not ergot alkaloids."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which is the MOST COMMON side effect of ergot alkaloids, due to their vasoconstrictive action?","options":["Severe hypertension","Hypotension","Hypoglycaemia","Bradypnoea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Severe hypertension is the most common side effect due to vasoconstrictive action; other effects include uterine cramping, bradycardia, vasospasm, angina, and dysrhythmias.  Why B wrong: They cause hypertension, not hypotension.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated side effects."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which of the following are side effects of ergot alkaloids? (Select all that apply)","options":["Uterine cramping and bradycardia","Severe hypertension","Vasospasm, angina & dysrhythmias","Hypotension and tachycardia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Side effects include uterine cramping and bradycardia, severe hypertension (most common), and vasospasm, angina & dysrhythmias.  Why D wrong: Ergots cause hypertension and bradycardia, not hypotension/tachycardia."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Ergot derivatives are absolutely contraindicated in a known case of which condition (AIIMS, DSSSB, RRB, PGI)?","options":["Hypertension","Hypotension","Diabetes","Anaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ergots derivatives are absolutely contraindicated in a known case of hypertension (tagged AIIMS, DSSSB, RRB, PGI).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated absolute contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"During ergot therapy, which parameter must be monitored closely and frequently, with immediate stoppage and reporting if abnormal?","options":["Blood pressure","Blood glucose","Serum potassium","Urine specific gravity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor blood pressure closely and frequently while the client is on ergot therapy; if high BP is noted, immediately stop the drug and report to the on-duty doctor.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the emphasised monitoring parameter for ergot therapy."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"In the difference table, how do ergot derivatives act on the myometrium compared to oxytocin?","options":["Act directly on the myometrium producing tetanic contraction with complete loss of polarity","Act on the physiological uterine contractile system with law of polarity maintained","Relax the myometrium","Have no effect on the myometrium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ergot derivatives act directly on the myometrium producing tetanic contraction with complete loss of polarity.  Why B wrong: That describes oxytocin (physiological system, polarity maintained).  Why C/D wrong: Ergots contract, not relax, the myometrium."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"In the difference table, the onset of action of ergot derivatives compared to oxytocin is:","options":["Comparatively slower","Faster in action","Immediate","Identical"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ergot derivatives have a comparatively slower onset; oxytocin is faster in action.  Why B wrong: That describes oxytocin.  Why C/D wrong: The table states ergots are comparatively slower."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"According to the difference table, the duration of action of ergot derivatives is ___ whereas oxytocin is ___.","options":["Long sustained; short lived","Short lived; long sustained","Long sustained; long sustained","Short lived; short lived"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ergot derivatives have a long sustained duration; oxytocin is short lived.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reverse or misstate the table entries."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"According to the difference table, which is a clinical use of ergot derivatives (not oxytocin)?","options":["To stop haemorrhage following delivery, abortion, or expulsion of dead fetus","Induction of labour","To augment uterine contraction during labour","Cervical ripening"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ergot clinical uses include stopping haemorrhage following delivery/abortion/expulsion of dead fetus and prophylactic third-stage use to hasten placental separation and minimize blood loss.  Why B/C wrong: Induction and augmentation of labour are oxytocin uses.  Why D wrong: Cervical ripening is a prostaglandin use."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"According to the difference table, which hazard is specifically associated with oxytocin?","options":["Water intoxication and uterine rupture","Rarely gangrene of the toe","Rise in blood pressure (most common), stroke","Nausea and vomiting only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin hazards include uterine hyperstimulation, antidiuretic effect/water intoxication, uterine rupture, angina pain, or rarely hypotension.  Why B wrong: Gangrene of the toe is an ergot hazard.  Why C wrong: Rise in BP/stroke is an ergot hazard.  Why D wrong: Nausea/vomiting is listed as an ergot hazard."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"According to the difference table, which hazard is specifically associated with ergot derivatives?","options":["Rise in blood pressure (most common) and stroke; rarely gangrene of the toe","Water intoxication","Uterine rupture from hyperstimulation","Antidiuretic effect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ergot hazards include nausea/vomiting, rise in blood pressure (most common)/stroke, and rarely gangrene of the toe.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are listed as oxytocin hazards."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"According to the difference table, the contraindication for ergot derivatives is ___ whereas for oxytocin it is ___.","options":["Hypertension; tetanic uterus or over-stimulated uterus","Tetanic uterus; hypertension","Hypotension; bronchospasm","Diabetes; hypertension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ergot contraindication = hypertension; oxytocin contraindication = tetanic uterus or over-stimulated uterus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reverse or misstate the table."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"What is the mode of action of lung surfactant?","options":["Enhances production of surfactant and restores surface activity to the lungs to prevent and treat RDS","Relaxes uterine smooth muscle","Increases serotonin levels","Blocks dopamine receptors"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lung surfactant enhances surfactant production and restores surface activity to the lungs to prevent and treat RDS.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other drug classes."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"By which route is lung surfactant administered?","options":["Intra-tracheal route","Intramuscular","Intravenous","Subcutaneous"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The route of administration of lung surfactant is the intra-tracheal route.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies intra-tracheal administration."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"After surfactant administration through the endotracheal tube, suctioning should be avoided for at least how long?","options":["2 hours","30 minutes","1 hour","6 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Suctioning should be avoided for at least 2 hours after administration.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated time is 2 hours."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"During surfactant administration, the nurse should monitor for which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Bradycardia","Lung sounds","Decreased oxygen saturation","Hyperglycaemia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor for bradycardia, lung sounds, and decreased oxygen saturation during administration.  Why D wrong: Hyperglycaemia is not the stated monitoring parameter for surfactant."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which drug is given as the example/choice of drug for lung surfactant use to treat/prevent RDS?","options":["Dexamethasone","Betamethasone","Terbutaline","Indomethacin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Dexamethasone as the example/choice of drug under Lung Surfactant.  Why B wrong: Betamethasone is discussed separately for lung maturity but not named here as the surfactant example.  Why C/D wrong: These are tocolytics."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which condition is the risk after birth of the baby that eye medications aim to prevent?","options":["Ophthalmia neonatorum","Retinopathy of prematurity","Cataract","Glaucoma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ophthalmia neonatorum is the risk after birth, prevented by prophylactic eye medications.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated target of neonatal eye prophylaxis here."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Erythromycin 0.5% and tetracycline 1% eye medications act as bacteriostatic & bactericidal and are effective against which organisms?","options":["Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis","Staphylococcus aureus and E. coli","Streptococcus and Klebsiella","Candida and Aspergillus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Erythromycin 0.5% & tetracycline 1% are effective against Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These organisms are not the stated targets."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Silver nitrate 1% eye prophylaxis protects against only which organism, and its common side effect is:","options":["Neisseria gonorrhoea; chemical conjunctivitis","Chlamydia trachomatis; corneal ulcer","Staphylococcus; cataract","E. coli; retinal detachment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Silver nitrate 1% (rarely used) protects against only Neisseria gonorrhoea; common side effect is chemical conjunctivitis.  Why B/C/D wrong: Silver nitrate targets only Neisseria gonorrhoea and its noted side effect is chemical conjunctivitis."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Why is the newborn at risk for haemorrhage requiring Vitamin K?","options":["Lack of/insufficient synthesis of Vitamin K","Excess Vitamin K stores","Increased platelet count","High prothrombin at birth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The newborn is at risk for haemorrhage due to lack of insufficient synthesis of Vitamin K.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated deficiency mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Newborns are deficient in Vitamin K for the first how many days of life due to lack of intestinal bacteria?","options":["6–8 days","1–2 days","10–14 days","28 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Newborns are deficient in Vitamin K for the first 6–8 days of life because they lack intestinal bacteria that synthesise it.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated period is 6–8 days."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Most coagulation factors are synthesised in which organ in the presence of Vitamin K?","options":["Liver","Spleen","Kidney","Bone marrow"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Most coagulation factors are synthesised in the liver in the presence of Vitamin K.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies the liver."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"According to new guidelines, what is the Vitamin K dose for a newborn weighing more than 1000 gm?","options":["1 mg","0.5 mg","2 mg","0.1 mg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dose is 1 mg if newborn's weight >1000 gm and 0.5 mg if weight <1000 gm.  Why B wrong: 0.5 mg is for weight <1000 gm.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated doses."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"According to new guidelines, what is the Vitamin K dose for a newborn weighing less than 1000 gm?","options":["1 mg","0.5 mg","0.25 mg","2 mg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dose is 0.5 mg if newborn's weight <1000 gm (and 1 mg if >1000 gm).  Why A wrong: 1 mg is for weight >1000 gm.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated doses."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"By which route and site is Vitamin K administered to a newborn?","options":["IM in vastus lateralis muscle of the mid thigh","IV in the umbilical vein","SC in the deltoid","Oral drops"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin K is given IM in the vastus lateralis muscle of the mid thigh.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies IM vastus lateralis."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Antipsychotics are also known by which of the following names (RRB-2019)?","options":["Major tranquilizers, neuroleptics, and D2 blockers","Mood elevators","Minor tranquilizers","Local hormones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Antipsychotics are also known as major tranquilizers, neuroleptics, and D2 blockers (RRB-2019).  Why B wrong: Mood elevators are antidepressants.  Why C wrong: Minor tranquilizers are anxiolytics.  Why D wrong: Local hormones are prostaglandins."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"What is the mechanism of action of first-generation (typical) antipsychotics?","options":["D2 antagonism","5HT2A & D2 antagonism","Serotonin reuptake inhibition","Beta-adrenergic agonism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 1st generation antipsychotics act by D2 antagonism.  Why B wrong: That is the 2nd generation mechanism.  Why C wrong: That describes SSRIs.  Why D wrong: That describes terbutaline."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"What is the mechanism of action of second-generation (atypical) antipsychotics?","options":["5HT2A & D2 antagonism","D2 antagonism only","MAO inhibition","Prostaglandin inhibition"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 2nd generation antipsychotics act by 5HT2A & D2 antagonism.  Why B wrong: D2 antagonism alone is the 1st generation mechanism.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated mechanisms."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which of the following are indications for antipsychotics? (Select all that apply)","options":["Organic or functional psychosis","Schizophrenia","OCD & Tic disorder","Behaviour disorder and eating disorder","Hypertension"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Indications include organic/functional psychosis, schizophrenia, OCD & Tic disorder, behaviour disorder, and eating disorder.  Why E wrong: Hypertension is not an antipsychotic indication."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which of the following are TYPICAL (first-generation) antipsychotics? (Select all that apply)","options":["Chlorpromazine","Prochlorpromazine","Trifluprazine and Trifluopromazine","Fluphenazine and Butyrophenones (haloperidol)","Clozapine"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Typical antipsychotics include chlorpromazine, prochlorpromazine, trifluprazine, trifluopromazine, fluphenazine, and butyrophenones (haloperidol).  Why E wrong: Clozapine is an atypical (second-generation) antipsychotic."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which of the following are ATYPICAL (second-generation) antipsychotics? (Select all that apply)","options":["Clozapine","Risperidone","Olanzapine (ESIC-2024)","Ziprasidone and Quetiapine","Haloperidol"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Atypical antipsychotics include clozapine, risperidone, olanzapine (ESIC-2024), ziprasidone, and quetiapine.  Why E wrong: Haloperidol (butyrophenone) is a typical antipsychotic."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Antidepressants are also known as:","options":["Mood elevators","Major tranquilizers","Neuroleptics","D2 blockers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Antidepressants are also known as mood elevators.  Why B/C/D wrong: Those are names for antipsychotics."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"According to the note, which therapy is the most successful for major depression?","options":["ECT (electroconvulsive therapy)","SSRI","MAOIs","Tricyclics"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states ECT is the most successful therapy for major depression.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are drug classes, not the therapy cited as most successful."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"What is the mechanism of action of antidepressants?","options":["Increase serotonin and norepinephrine levels","Antagonise dopamine D2 receptors","Inhibit prostaglandin synthesis","Deplete serotonin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Antidepressants increase serotonin and norepinephrine levels.  Why B wrong: That is the antipsychotic mechanism.  Why C/D wrong: These contradict the stated mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which of the following are indications for antidepressants? (Select all that apply)","options":["Depression","Anxiety, phobia","OCD & hysteria","Eating disorder","Schizophrenia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Indications include depression, anxiety/phobia, OCD & hysteria, and eating disorder.  Why E wrong: Schizophrenia is an indication for antipsychotics, not antidepressants."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"Which of the following are types of antidepressants? (Select all that apply)","options":["Tri-cyclic","SSRI","MAOIs","Butyrophenones"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Types of antidepressants are tri-cyclic, SSRI, and MAOIs.  Why D wrong: Butyrophenones (haloperidol) are typical antipsychotics, not antidepressants."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"A postpartum eclamptic client on MgSO4 develops a respiratory rate of 10/min, absent knee jerk, and urine output of 20 ml/hr. What is the nurse's PRIORITY action?","options":["Stop the magnesium infusion immediately","Give the next scheduled MgSO4 dose","Increase the infusion rate","Place the client supine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: These findings (RR <12, absent DTR, urine <30 ml/hr) indicate magnesium toxicity; the first priority is to stop magnesium therapy, then give calcium gluconate.  Why B wrong: Repeat/next dose requires +2 DTR, urine >30 ml/hr, RR >12 — all absent here.  Why C wrong: Increasing the rate would worsen toxicity.  Why D wrong: Supine risks hypotensive syndrome; side-lying is preferred."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"A woman in preterm labour at 30 weeks is prescribed a tocolytic and betamethasone. What is the primary purpose of adding betamethasone?","options":["To promote fetal lung maturity and reduce RDS severity","To stop uterine contractions","To prevent Rh isoimmunisation","To control postpartum haemorrhage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Betamethasone is a corticosteroid that increases surfactant production to promote fetal lung maturity and reduce RDS severity in pre-term labour.  Why B wrong: The tocolytic, not betamethasone, halts contractions.  Why C wrong: Anti-D prevents Rh isoimmunisation.  Why D wrong: Ergots/oxytocin control PPH."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"A client with severe postpartum haemorrhage also has a blood pressure of 170/110 mmHg. Which uterotonic is CONTRAINDICATED?","options":["Ergometrine (ergot derivative)","Oxytocin","Misoprostol","Carboprost"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ergot derivatives are absolutely contraindicated in hypertension due to vasoconstrictive action and risk of severe hypertension/stroke; oxytocin is preferred in hypertensive cases.  Why B wrong: Oxytocin is the choice in hypertension/pre-eclampsia.  Why C/D wrong: Prostaglandins are not the contraindicated agent in hypertension per the ergot rule (though other cautions exist)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · OBG medications — MgSO4 therapy, Oxytocic agents & Oxytocin…","question":"A newborn of a mother who received magnesium sulphate shows hypotonia. Which antidote is appropriate for the newborn?","options":["Levallorphan (Lorfan)","Calcium gluconate 10%","Naloxone","Vitamin K"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For newborn magnesium toxicity, Levallorphan (Lorfan) is the antidote; hypotonia is the newborn side effect of MgSO4.  Why B wrong: Calcium gluconate is the maternal antidote.  Why C/D wrong: These do not reverse neonatal magnesium effects."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which class of antidepressants is considered the first-line medicine in the treatment of depression?","options":["Tricyclic antidepressants (TCAs)","Monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs)","Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs)","Mood stabilizers"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: SSRIs are stated as the first-line anti-depressant medicine in depression and are also preferred for prophylaxis of recurrent depression.  Why A wrong: TCAs are older agents, not first-line.  Why B wrong: MAOIs are reserved for resistant/atypical depression.  Why D wrong: Mood stabilizers are used for mania/bipolar disorder, not first-line depression."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"SSRIs are also preferred for which of the following purposes?","options":["Treatment of narcolepsy","Prophylaxis of recurrent depression","Reversal of opioid overdose","Induction of labour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SSRIs are preferred for prophylaxis of recurrent depression.  Why A wrong: Narcolepsy/hypersomnia is an exception for anxiolytic indications, not an SSRI use here.  Why C wrong: Naloxone reverses opioid overdose.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin induces labour."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Besides depression, SSRIs are also used in all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["OCD","Anxiety","Phobic disorders","Parkinson's disease"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: SSRIs are used in OCD, anxiety and phobic disorders; Parkinson's disease is not listed.  Why A, B, C wrong: All are listed SSRI indications."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the mode of action of SSRIs?","options":["Increase GABA level","Block or inhibit reuptake of serotonin, increasing serotonin activity","Break down norepinephrine and dopamine","Decrease serotonin and norepinephrine level"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SSRIs block or inhibit reuptake of serotonin, so more serotonin is available, increasing serotonin activity.  Why A wrong: Increasing GABA is the anxiolytic mechanism.  Why C wrong: That describes MAO enzyme action.  Why D wrong: That describes anti-manic/mood-stabilizer mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which of the following are side effects of SSRIs? (Select all that apply)","options":["Anorexia","Dry mouth","Weight gain or loss","Decreased libido","Seizure activity"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: SSRI side effects include anorexia, dyskinesia, nervousness, nausea, diarrhoea, dry mouth, weight gain or loss, decreased libido, apathy and seizure activity.  All listed options are documented SSRI side effects."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which of the following is an example of an SSRI?","options":["Imipramine","Escitalopram","Phenelzine","Amitriptyline"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SSRI examples include Citalopram, Escitalopram, Fluoxetine, Fluvoxamine, Paroxetine and Sertraline.  Why A, D wrong: Imipramine and amitriptyline are TCAs.  Why C wrong: Phenelzine is an MAOI."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which drug is considered the drug of choice in OCD (Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder)?","options":["Fluoxetine","Imipramine","Lithium carbonate","Haloperidol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fluoxetine is considered a drug of choice in OCD.  Why B wrong: Imipramine is the DOC in enuresis in children.  Why C wrong: Lithium carbonate is DOC for mania.  Why D wrong: Haloperidol is DOC for tic disorder."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the mode of action of tricyclic antidepressants (TCAs)?","options":["Increase GABA levels only","Inhibit reuptake of serotonin and norepinephrine and act as antagonists to SHAM receptors","Inhibit monoamine oxidase enzyme","Enhance reuptake of norepinephrine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: TCAs inhibit reuptake of neurotransmitters (serotonin, norepinephrine) and act as antagonists to SHAM (Serotonin, Histamine, Alpha, Muscarinic) receptors.  Why A wrong: That is the anxiolytic mechanism.  Why C wrong: That is the MAOI mechanism.  Why D wrong: Enhancing reuptake of norepinephrine is lithium's action."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"In the TCA mechanism, \"SHAM\" receptors stand for which set of receptors?","options":["Serotonin, Histamine, Alpha, Muscarinic","Sodium, Hydrogen, Aldosterone, Magnesium","Serotonin, Histamine, Adrenergic, Melatonin","Sympathetic, Hypothalamic, Adrenal, Medullary"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SHAM = Serotonin, Histamine, Alpha, Muscarinic receptors.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the acronym given."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"How long may TCAs take to start working or produce mood-elevating effects?","options":["A few hours","2–3 days","2–3 weeks","3 months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: TCAs may take 2–3 weeks to start working or produce mood elevating effects.  Why A, B, D wrong: These do not match the stated onset."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which of the following are side effects of TCAs? (Select all that apply)","options":["Dry mouth","Constipation","Urinary retention","Sexual dysfunction","Blurred vision"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: TCA side effects include dry mouth, constipation, blurred vision, urinary retention, GI upset, sedation, confusion, weight gain and sexual dysfunctions.  All listed are documented TCA side effects."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which of the following is an example of a tricyclic antidepressant?","options":["Sertraline","Amitriptyline","Selegiline","Moclobemide"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: TCA examples are imipramine, amitriptyline, nortriptyline.  Why A wrong: Sertraline is an SSRI.  Why C, D wrong: Selegiline and Moclobemide are MAOIs."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which drug is considered the drug of choice in enuresis in children?","options":["Fluoxetine","Imipramine","Lithium","Amobarbital"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Imipramine is considered a drug of choice in enuresis in children.  Why A wrong: Fluoxetine is DOC in OCD.  Why C wrong: Lithium is for mania.  Why D wrong: Amobarbital is a barbiturate anxiolytic."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the mode of action of monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs)?","options":["They inhibit the MAO enzyme so norepinephrine, serotonin and dopamine are not broken down, increasing their concentration","They block serotonin reuptake only","They increase GABA levels","They enhance reuptake of norepinephrine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MAO breaks down norepinephrine, serotonin and dopamine; when inhibited by MAOIs, these are not broken down, thus increasing concentration of all three neurotransmitters in the brain.  Why B wrong: That is SSRI mechanism.  Why C wrong: That is anxiolytic mechanism.  Why D wrong: That is lithium's mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"MAOIs are commonly used for which type of depression?","options":["Mild first-episode depression","Resistant and atypical depression","Seasonal depression only","Postpartum depression only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MAOIs are commonly used for resistant and atypical depression.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the stated indication."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which of the following is an example of an MAOI?","options":["Nortriptyline","Fluvoxamine","Phenelzine","Lithium carbonate"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: MAOI examples are Phenelzine, Selegiline, Isocarboxazid and Moclobemide.  Why A wrong: Nortriptyline is a TCA.  Why B wrong: Fluvoxamine is an SSRI.  Why D wrong: Lithium carbonate is a mood stabilizer."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"A patient on MAOI therapy should be instructed to avoid foods high in which substance to prevent hypertensive crisis?","options":["Tyramine","Potassium","Tryptophan","Vitamin K"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Patients on MAOIs should avoid high tyramine foods (aged cheese, cured meats, banana, caffeine, papaya, raisins, red wine, beer, fish) to avoid hypertensive crisis (JIPMER, PGI, AIIMS-2022).  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the substance to avoid for hypertensive crisis on MAOIs."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which of the following high-tyramine foods should be avoided by a client on MAOIs? (Select all that apply)","options":["Aged cheese","Red wine","Cured meats","Fresh apple juice","Beer"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The listed high-tyramine foods are aged cheese, cured meats, banana, caffeine, papaya, raisins, red wine, beer and fish.  Why D wrong: Fresh apple juice is not on the high-tyramine avoid list."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which of the following are signs seen in a patient experiencing a hypertensive crisis (from MAOI–tyramine interaction)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Occipital headache","Neck stiffness","Dilated pupils","Elevated temperature","Chest pain"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Signs of hypertensive crisis include occipital headache, neck stiffness, sweating, nausea, vomiting, dilated pupils, elevated temperature, and chest pain.  All listed are documented signs."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Anti-manic drugs are also known as:","options":["Antipsychotics","Mood stabilizers","Anxiolytics","Antidepressants"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anti-manic drugs are also known as mood stabilizers.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are separate drug classes."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the mechanism of action of anti-manic (mood stabilizer) drugs?","options":["Increase serotonin and norepinephrine level","Decrease serotonin and norepinephrine level","Increase GABA only","Block dopamine receptors"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anti-manic drugs decrease serotonin and norepinephrine level.  Why A wrong: Increasing these would worsen mania/be antidepressant-like.  Why C, D wrong: These are anxiolytic and antipsychotic mechanisms."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Anti-manic drugs are indicated in all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Mania","Bipolar disorder","Schizophrenia (catatonic-excitement)","Narcolepsy"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Indications are mania, bipolar disorder, and schizophrenia (catatonic-excitement); narcolepsy is not listed.  Why A, B, C wrong: All are listed indications."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which of the following is an anticonvulsant used as a mood stabilizer?","options":["Valproic acid / sodium valproate","Fluoxetine","Phenelzine","Chlorpromazine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anticonvulsant mood stabilizers include valproic acid, sodium valproate, CBZ (carbamazepine), lamotrigine, gabapentin.  Why B wrong: SSRI.  Why C wrong: MAOI.  Why D wrong: Antipsychotic."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Apart from anticonvulsants, which agent is listed as the other mood stabilizer?","options":["Lithium carbonate","Diazepam","Amobarbital","Sertraline"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The other mood stabiliser is lithium carbonate.  Why B, C wrong: These are anxiolytics.  Why D wrong: SSRI."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the most common drug (DOC) used for mania?","options":["Valproic acid","Lithium carbonate","Haloperidol","Diazepam"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common drug (DOC) used for mania is lithium carbonate.  Why A wrong: An alternative mood stabilizer but not the DOC.  Why C, D wrong: Antipsychotic and anxiolytic respectively."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which drug classes are included under anti-anxiety/anxiolytic agents? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hypnotics","Sedatives","Anticonvulsants","Aminoglycosides","SSRIs"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Anti-anxiety/anxiolytics include hypnotics, sedatives and anticonvulsants.  Why D wrong: Aminoglycosides are antibiotics.  Why E wrong: SSRIs are antidepressants (not listed under anxiolytics here)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the mechanism of action of anti-anxiety/anxiolytic drugs?","options":["Increase GABA level","Decrease serotonin","Block dopamine receptors","Inhibit MAO enzyme"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anxiolytics increase GABA level.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are mechanisms of other drug classes."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Anxiolytics are indicated for all psychiatric disorders EXCEPT:","options":["Attention deficit disorder","Hypersomnia/narcolepsy","Generalized anxiety","Panic disorder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Indications are all psychiatric disorders except hypersomnia/narcolepsy (and attention deficit disorder is listed as an indication).  Why A, C, D wrong: These are appropriate indications."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which of the following is a barbiturate-type anxiolytic?","options":["Diazepam","Phenobarbital","Lorazepam","Alprazolam"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Barbiturate anxiolytics are amobarbital and phenobarbital.  Why A, C, D wrong: Diazepam, lorazepam, and alprazolam are non-barbiturate (benzodiazepine) agents."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which of the following is a non-barbiturate anxiolytic?","options":["Amobarbital","Phenobarbital","Chlordiazepoxide","Lithium"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Non-barbiturate anxiolytics include chlordiazepoxide, diazepam, oxazepam, lorazepam, alprazolam.  Why A, B wrong: These are barbiturates.  Why D wrong: Lithium is a mood stabilizer."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"According to the normal serum levels of psychotropic medications, what is the therapeutic serum level of lithium?","options":["0.6–1.2 mEq/L","4–12 µg/ml","50–125 µg/ml","200–700 ng/ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The normal serum level of lithium is 0.6–1.2 mEq/L.  Why B wrong: 4–12 µg/ml is carbamazepine.  Why C wrong: 50–125 µg/ml is valproic acid.  Why D wrong: 200–700 ng/ml is clozapine."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the normal serum level of carbamazepine?","options":["0.6–1.2 mEq/L","4–12 µg/ml","2.5–15 µg/ml","95–250 ng/ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Carbamazepine serum level is 4–12 µg/ml.  Why A wrong: Lithium.  Why C wrong: Lamotrigine.  Why D wrong: Amitriptyline + nortriptyline."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the normal serum level of valproic acid?","options":["50–125 µg/ml","4–12 µg/ml","2.5–15 µg/ml","200–700 ng/ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Valproic acid serum level is 50–125 µg/ml.  Why B wrong: Carbamazepine.  Why C wrong: Lamotrigine.  Why D wrong: Clozapine."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the normal serum level of lamotrigine?","options":["4–12 µg/ml","2.5–15 µg/ml","50–125 µg/ml","150–300 ng/ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lamotrigine serum level is 2.5–15 µg/ml.  Why A wrong: Carbamazepine.  Why C wrong: Valproic acid.  Why D wrong: Imipramine + desipramine / desipramine."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the normal serum level of clozapine?","options":["200–700 ng/ml","50–150 ng/ml","95–250 ng/ml","4–12 µg/ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clozapine serum level is 200–700 ng/ml.  Why B wrong: Nortriptyline.  Why C wrong: Amitriptyline + nortriptyline.  Why D wrong: Carbamazepine."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"The combined serum level of amitriptyline + nortriptyline is:","options":["95–250 ng/ml","150–300 ng/ml","50–150 ng/ml","200–700 ng/ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Amitriptyline + nortriptyline serum level is 95–250 ng/ml.  Why B wrong: Imipramine + desipramine / desipramine.  Why C wrong: Nortriptyline alone.  Why D wrong: Clozapine."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"The serum level of imipramine + desipramine is:","options":["50–150 ng/ml","95–250 ng/ml","150–300 ng/ml","2.5–15 µg/ml"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Imipramine + desipramine serum level is 150–300 ng/ml (desipramine alone is also 150–300 ng/ml).  Why A wrong: Nortriptyline.  Why B wrong: Amitriptyline + nortriptyline.  Why D wrong: Lamotrigine."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"The normal serum level of nortriptyline is:","options":["50–150 ng/ml","150–300 ng/ml","95–250 ng/ml","200–700 ng/ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nortriptyline serum level is 50–150 ng/ml.  Why B wrong: Desipramine / imipramine + desipramine.  Why C wrong: Amitriptyline + nortriptyline.  Why D wrong: Clozapine."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which of the following are side effects of antipsychotic drugs? (Select all that apply)","options":["Photosensitivity","Agranulocytosis","Orthostatic hypotension","Galactorrhoea","EPS"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Antipsychotic side effects include photosensitivity, weight gain, agranulocytosis, contact dermatitis, urinary retention, constipation, orthostatic hypotension, jaundice, galactorrhoea, and EPS (most common).  All listed are documented."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which is the most common side effect of antipsychotic drugs?","options":["Galactorrhoea","Extra-pyramidal symptoms (EPS)","Jaundice","Urinary retention"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: EPS is noted as the most common side effect of antipsychotic drugs.  Why A, C, D wrong: Listed side effects but not identified as most common."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which was the first antipsychotic drug (introduced in 1952)?","options":["Haloperidol","Clozapine","Chlorpromazine","Risperidone"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The first antipsychotic drug was Chlorpromazine (1952).  Why A wrong: Haloperidol is DOC for tic disorder.  Why B, D wrong: Clozapine and risperidone are later agents."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which drug is the drug of choice for tic disorder?","options":["Chlorpromazine","Haloperidol","Clozapine","Lithium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Haloperidol is the drug of choice for tic disorder.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not indicated as DOC for tic disorder."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Extra-pyramidal syndrome (EPS) is most commonly seen with which antipsychotic?","options":["Clozapine","Risperidone","Haloperidol","Chlorpromazine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: EPS is most commonly seen with haloperidol.  Why A wrong: Clozapine has fewer EPS side effects.  Why B, D wrong: Not identified as most likely to cause EPS here."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which drug(s) is/are the DOC for negative symptoms of schizophrenia?","options":["Clozapine and Risperidone","Haloperidol","Chlorpromazine","Lithium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DOC for negative symptoms of schizophrenia are Clozapine and Risperidone.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not indicated for negative symptoms here."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which antipsychotic is described as the most effective with the least side effects?","options":["Haloperidol","Chlorpromazine","Clozapine","Risperidone"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Clozapine is the commonly used, most effective antipsychotic with fewer side effects.  Why A, B, D wrong: Not described this way."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the most common side effect of clozapine, requiring periodic CBC testing?","options":["Hypoglycaemia","Agranulocytosis (decreased WBC count)","Hyperkalaemia","Hepatotoxicity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common side effect of clozapine is agranulocytosis (decreased WBC count), so periodic CBC tests should be done.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the noted clozapine effect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"A client on antipsychotic therapy should be advised to avoid which of the following?","options":["Alcohol, tea and coffee","High-protein foods","Green vegetables","Dairy products"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: While on antipsychotic therapy, alcohol, tea and coffee beverages should be avoided.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not restricted per this note."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is stated as the most common side effect of antipsychotics (in the akathisia note)?","options":["Akathisia","Tardive dyskinesia","Pseudo-parkinsonism","Oculogyric crisis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common side effect of antipsychotics is noted as akathisia.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are other EPS reactions, not the one named here."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Lithium is contraindicated during which of the following?","options":["Pregnancy and lactation","Hypertension only","Diabetes only","Old age only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lithium is contraindicated during pregnancy and lactation.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"The extrapyramidal symptom \"Pseudo-parkinsonism\" typically has what onset?","options":["Within 30 days or 4 weeks","Within 3 to 5 days","5–60 days after initiation","3 months onwards"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pseudo-parkinsonism occurs within 30 days or 4 weeks; features include Parkinson-like tremors, shuffling gait, rigidity and drooling.  Why B wrong: 3–5 days is acute dystonia.  Why C wrong: 5–60 days is akathisia.  Why D wrong: 3 months onwards is tardive dyskinesia."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which EPS reaction is described as the \"earliest onset EPS,\" occurring within 3 to 5 days with uncontrolled muscle spasms over face, tongue and neck?","options":["Akathisia","Acute dystonia","Tardive dyskinesia","Pseudo-parkinsonism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Acute dystonia occurs within 3–5 days, is the earliest onset EPS, with uncontrolled muscle spasms mostly over face, tongue and neck.  Why A, C, D wrong: Different onset/features."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Akathisia (restlessness, irresistible desire to move around, inability to sit still) usually has what onset?","options":["Within 3 to 5 days","5–60 days after initiation","3 months onwards","Weeks to months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Akathisia occurs 5–60 days after initiation with restlessness and inability to sit still.  Why A wrong: Acute dystonia.  Why C wrong: Tardive dyskinesia.  Why D wrong: Oculogyric crisis."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which EPS reaction, occurring 3 months onwards, is characterized by protrusion and rolling of the tongue, sucking/smacking of the lips, and chewing motions?","options":["Acute dystonia","Tardive dyskinesia","Akathisia","Pseudo-parkinsonism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tardive dyskinesia occurs 3 months onwards with tongue protrusion/rolling, lip sucking and smacking, and chewing motion.  Why A, C, D wrong: Different onset/features."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Oculogyric crisis (OGC) is best described as:","options":["A rare acute dystonic reaction involving the extraocular muscles with involuntary upward deviation of both eyes, treated as an emergency","A benign self-limiting condition needing no treatment","A late-onset movement of the lips and tongue","Restlessness with inability to sit still"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: OGC is a rare acute dystonic reaction involving the extraocular muscles with involuntary upward deviation of both eyes due to spasm/increased tone, treated as an emergency.  Why B wrong: It is an emergency, not benign.  Why C wrong: That describes tardive dyskinesia.  Why D wrong: That describes akathisia."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the drug of choice for oculogyric crisis?","options":["IV benztropine (antimuscarinic)","Dantrolene","Haloperidol","Lithium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DOC for OGC is antimuscarinics, e.g., IV benztropine.  Why B wrong: Dantrolene is DOC for NMS.  Why C wrong: Haloperidol can cause EPS.  Why D wrong: Lithium is a mood stabilizer."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS) is best described as:","options":["A rare but very dangerous side effect of neuroleptic/antipsychotic medicines","A common, mild reaction to SSRIs","A withdrawal reaction from benzodiazepines","A benzodiazepine overdose syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NMS is a rare but very dangerous side effect of neuroleptic or anti-psychotic medicines, occurring weeks to months after drug initiation.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not associated with those drug classes."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which of the following are features of neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Muscle rigidity","High grade fever/hyperpyrexia (107°C or more)","Unstable blood pressure","Diaphoresis and rapid deterioration of mental status","Stupor and coma"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: NMS features include muscle rigidity, high grade fever/hyperpyrexia (107°C or more), unstable blood pressure, diaphoresis, rapid deterioration of mental status, stupor and coma.  All listed are documented NMS features."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the drug of choice for neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS)?","options":["Dantrolene","Benztropine","Naloxone","Physostigmine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice in NMS is dantrolene, and antipsychotics should be stopped immediately if NMS occurs.  Why B wrong: Benztropine is for OGC/dystonia.  Why C wrong: Naloxone reverses opioids.  Why D wrong: Physostigmine is the antidote of atropine."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the immediate action if NMS occurs in a patient on antipsychotics?","options":["Increase the antipsychotic dose","Immediately stop the antipsychotics","Add a second antipsychotic","Give an antidepressant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Antipsychotics should be immediately stopped if NMS occurs.  Why A, C, D wrong: Contraindicated/inappropriate in NMS."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Lithium is used to treat manic episodes of which disorder?","options":["Schizophrenia","Bipolar mood disorder","Major depression","Panic disorder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lithium is used to treat manic episodes of bipolar mood disorder.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the stated indication for lithium therapy."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Lithium is available in which forms?","options":["Lithium carbonate and lithium citrate","Lithium sulfate and lithium chloride","Lithium phosphate only","Lithium bromide only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lithium is available in lithium carbonate and lithium citrate.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated available forms."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the lithium serum level in acute mania?","options":["0.6–1.2 mEq/L","1.0–1.5 mEq/L",">1.5 mEq/L",">2.5 mEq/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lithium serum level in acute mania is 1.0–1.5 mEq/L.  Why A wrong: 0.6–1.2 mEq/L is the therapeutic/maintenance level.  Why C wrong: >1.5 mEq/L is the toxic level.  Why D wrong: >2.5 mEq/L is severe toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the lithium therapeutic/maintenance level?","options":["1.0–1.5 mEq/L","0.6–1.2 mEq/L",">1.5 mEq/L",">3 mEq/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The lithium therapeutic/maintenance level is 0.6–1.2 mEq/L.  Why A wrong: Acute mania level.  Why C wrong: Toxic level.  Why D wrong: Death level."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"The lithium toxic level is:","options":[">1.5 mEq/L","0.6–1.2 mEq/L","1.0–1.5 mEq/L","4–12 µg/ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The lithium toxic level is >1.5 mEq/L.  Why B wrong: Therapeutic level.  Why C wrong: Acute mania level.  Why D wrong: Carbamazepine level."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"At what lithium serum level does death occur?","options":[">1.5 mEq/L","1.5–2.5 mEq/L",">3–3.5 mEq/L","0.6–1.2 mEq/L"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Death occurs at lithium level >3–3.5 mEq/L.  Why A wrong: Toxic level.  Why B wrong: Moderate toxicity range.  Why D wrong: Therapeutic level."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"When should blood serum level be monitored after the first dose of lithium?","options":["After 6 hours","After 12 hours","After 24 hours","After 48 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Blood serum level should be monitored after 12 hrs of the first dose.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the stated monitoring time."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the mode of action of lithium regarding norepinephrine?","options":["Enhances reuptake of norepinephrine, decreasing norepinephrine level and hyperactivity","Blocks reuptake of norepinephrine","Inhibits MAO enzyme","Increases dopamine activity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lithium enhances reuptake of norepinephrine, which decreases the norepinephrine level in the body, resulting in decreased hyperactivity.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are actions of other drug classes / opposite effects."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Regarding lithium's mechanism in the brain, which of the following is correct?","options":["Lithium decreases presynaptic dopamine activity and inactivates postsynaptic G protein, reducing excitatory neurotransmission","Lithium increases presynaptic dopamine activity","Lithium blocks GABA receptors","Lithium activates postsynaptic G protein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lithium decreases presynaptic dopamine activity and inactivates postsynaptic G protein, reducing excitatory neurotransmission in the brain; it also increases GABA level in the CNS.  Why B, C, D wrong: Opposite of the stated actions."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the early sign of lithium toxicity?","options":["Nausea and vomiting","Tremors","T-wave depression","Knee jerk sign"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The early sign of lithium toxicity is nausea and vomiting.  Why B, C, D wrong: Tremors, hypokalaemia, T-wave depression, muscle weakness (knee jerk sign) are late signs of lithium toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which of the following are late signs of lithium toxicity? (Select all that apply)","options":["Tremors","Hypokalaemia","T-wave depression","Muscle weakness (knee jerk sign)","Nausea and vomiting only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Late signs of lithium toxicity include tremors, hypokalaemia, T-wave depression, and muscle weakness (knee jerk sign).  Why E wrong: Nausea and vomiting is the early sign, not a late sign."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Metabolism and re-absorption of lithium occurs mainly through which organ?","options":["Liver","Kidneys","Lungs","Spleen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Metabolism and re-absorption of lithium is done by the kidneys.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the primary organ for lithium handling."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the lithium dose in the acute phase?","options":["600–1200 mg/day","900–2100 mg/day","300–600 mg/day","2500 mg/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lithium dose in the acute phase is 900–2100 mg/day.  Why A wrong: 600–1200 mg/day is the chronic phase dose.  Why C, D wrong: Not stated doses."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the lithium dose in the chronic phase?","options":["900–2100 mg/day","600–1200 mg/day","1500–2500 mg/day","300 mg/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lithium dose in the chronic phase is 600–1200 mg/day.  Why A wrong: That is the acute phase dose.  Why C, D wrong: Not stated doses."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the antidote for lithium toxicity?","options":["Haemodialysis","Physostigmine","Naloxone","Protamine sulfate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote of lithium toxicity is haemodialysis.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are antidotes for atropine, opioids, and heparin respectively."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"A client on lithium therapy should be instructed to do which of the following?","options":["Restrict all salt from the diet","Take adequate salt in the diet and drink 6–8 glasses of water per day","Restrict fluids to 500 mL/day","Avoid all water intake"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Client should maintain and take adequate salt in the diet and drink 6–8 glasses of water per day to prevent lithium toxicity (serum sodium and potassium must be maintained/monitored).  Why A, C, D wrong: Salt/fluid restriction increases lithium toxicity risk."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which of the following is a contraindication of lithium therapy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Dehydration","Sodium depletion / hyponatraemia","Low serum potassium (hypokalaemia)","Clients on diuretics","Pregnancy and lactation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Contraindications include dehydration, sodium depletion/hyponatraemia, hypokalaemia, clients on diuretics, pregnant women (to prevent congenital malformations), lactating mothers, and thyroid hormone imbalance.  All listed are documented contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Why is lithium contraindicated in pregnant women?","options":["To prevent congenital malformations","To prevent hyperkalaemia","To prevent maternal hypertension","To promote lactation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lithium is contraindicated in pregnant women to prevent congenital malformations.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Lithium toxicity occurs when ingested lithium cannot be detoxified and excreted by which organ?","options":["Liver","Kidneys","Lungs","Skin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lithium toxicity occurs when ingested lithium cannot be detoxified and excreted by the kidneys.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the excretory organ for lithium."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which class of drugs should NOT be administered while a client is on lithium therapy (to prevent toxicity)?","options":["Diuretics","Antibiotics","Antihistamines","Antacids"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Do not administer diuretics while client is on lithium therapy to prevent toxicity.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not specifically contraindicated per this note."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"To prevent lithium toxicity, which electrolyte level should be maintained during lithium therapy?","options":["Serum calcium","Serum sodium","Serum magnesium","Serum chloride"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Maintain serum sodium level while client is on lithium therapy to prevent toxicity.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the electrolyte specified."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Mild lithium toxicity occurs at a serum lithium level of:","options":["1.5 mEq/L","1.5–2.5 mEq/L",">2.5 mEq/L","0.6 mEq/L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mild toxicity: serum lithium level is 1.5 mEq/L, with lethargy, apathy, diminished concentration and coarse hand tremors.  Why B wrong: Moderate toxicity range.  Why C wrong: Severe toxicity range.  Why D wrong: Within therapeutic range."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which signs are seen in mild lithium toxicity? (Select all that apply)","options":["Lethargy and apathy","Diminished concentration","Coarse hand tremors","Tonic-clonic seizures","Coma"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Mild toxicity signs are lethargy & apathy, diminished concentration, and coarse hand tremors.  Why D, E wrong: Tonic-clonic seizures and coma are seen in severe toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Moderate lithium toxicity occurs at a serum lithium level of:","options":["1.5 mEq/L","1.5–2.5 mEq/L",">2.5 mEq/L","3–3.5 mEq/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Moderate lithium toxicity: serum level 1.5–2.5 mEq/L with nausea/vomiting, severe diarrhoea, slurred speech, tinnitus, and blurred speech/vision.  Why A wrong: Mild level.  Why C wrong: Severe level.  Why D wrong: Death level."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which signs are seen in moderate lithium toxicity? (Select all that apply)","options":["Nausea and vomiting","Severe diarrhoea","Slurred speech and tinnitus","Blurred speech and vision","Nystagmus and coma"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Moderate toxicity signs are nausea & vomiting, severe diarrhoea, slurred speech & tinnitus, and blurred speech & vision.  Why E wrong: Nystagmus and coma are severe toxicity signs."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Severe lithium toxicity occurs at a serum lithium level of:","options":["1.5 mEq/L","1.5–2.5 mEq/L",">2.5 mEq/L","0.6–1.2 mEq/L"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Severe lithium toxicity: serum lithium level is >2.5 mEq/L.  Why A wrong: Mild.  Why B wrong: Moderate.  Why D wrong: Therapeutic."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which signs are seen in severe lithium toxicity? (Select all that apply)","options":["Nystagmus and deep tendon hyperreflexia","Visual or tactile hallucinations","Oliguria and anuria","Tonic-clonic seizures, coma leading to death","Coarse hand tremors only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Severe toxicity signs are nystagmus, deep tendon hyperreflexia, visual/tactile hallucinations, oliguria & anuria, impaired level of consciousness, tonic-clonic seizures, coma leading to death.  Why E wrong: Coarse hand tremors indicate mild toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the first priority nursing action in lithium toxicity?","options":["Notify the HCP first","Withhold lithium","Give a diuretic","Increase the lithium dose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first priority action in lithium toxicity is to withhold lithium; then notify the HCP.  Why A wrong: Notifying the HCP comes after withholding lithium.  Why C, D wrong: Contraindicated in toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which of the following are nursing interventions in lithium toxicity? (Select all that apply)","options":["Withhold lithium","Notify the HCP","Monitor vital signs and LOC","Check client's serum sodium level","Monitor and maintain serum lithium level periodically"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Interventions include withhold lithium (first), notify HCP, monitor vitals & LOC, check serum sodium, and monitor/maintain serum lithium periodically.  All listed are documented interventions."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"During lithium therapy, a client should be educated to do which of the following?","options":["Drive during the acute phase of mania","Not drive or operate dangerous machinery until serum lithium levels are stabilised","Increase caffeine intake","Restrict fluids to under 1 litre"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The client should not drive or operate dangerous machinery during the acute phase of mania or until serum lithium levels are stabilised.  Why A wrong: Opposite of the guidance.  Why C wrong: Should avoid excessive caffeine.  Why D wrong: Should maintain hydration (6–8 glasses/day)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Why should a client on lithium therapy notify the doctor if diarrhoea and vomiting occur?","options":["These symptoms cause sodium and potassium depletion which leads to lithium toxicity","These symptoms indicate an allergic reaction","These symptoms increase lithium excretion, reducing effect","These symptoms indicate a viral infection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diarrhoea and vomiting cause sodium and potassium depletion, which leads to lithium toxicity.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which drug is used to treat acetaminophen (paracetamol) toxicity?","options":["Acetylcysteine","Activated charcoal","Naloxone","Physostigmine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Acetylcysteine is used to treat acetaminophen toxicity but should never be given with activated charcoal.  Why B wrong: Activated charcoal should not be co-administered with acetylcysteine.  Why C, D wrong: Antidotes for opioids and atropine."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Acetylcysteine should NEVER be given together with which of the following?","options":["Activated charcoal","IV fluids","Oxygen","Antiemetics"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Acetylcysteine should never be given with activated charcoal.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not contraindicated per this note."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Rifampin and its metabolites turn body fluids (urine, faeces, sputum, tears, sweat) to which colour?","options":["Blue-green","Orange to red","Black","Yellow only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rifampin and its metabolites turn urine, faeces, sputum, tears, and sweat orange to red colour.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the stated discoloration."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Atropine dries up secretions and, by acting on ciliary and iris sphincter muscles of the eye, causes which adverse effect?","options":["Miosis","Mydriasis","Cataract","Increased lacrimation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Atropine lessens the response of ciliary and iris sphincter muscles in the eye, causing mydriasis (pupil dilation) as an adverse effect.  Why A wrong: Miosis is the opposite.  Why C, D wrong: Not the stated effect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"The rubella vaccine is made from duck eggs, so an allergic reaction may occur in clients with which allergy?","options":["Egg allergy","Penicillin allergy","Latex allergy","Sulfa allergy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rubella vaccine is made from duck eggs, so an allergic reaction may occur in clients with egg allergies.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not related to duck-egg-derived vaccine."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Anti-parkinsonian medications commonly cause which of the following?","options":["Vivid dreams and nightmares","Constipation only","Hearing loss","Orange urine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anti-parkinsonian medications cause vivid dreams and nightmares.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated effect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Oxytocin has an antidiuretic effect and can cause which complication?","options":["Fluid overload or water intoxication","Dehydration","Hyperkalaemia","Hyperglycaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin has an antidiuretic effect and can cause fluid overload or water intoxication (Daman Diu-2018).  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated effect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Oxytocin given to induce or augment labour may cause uterine tachysystole, increasing the risk of:","options":["Uterine rupture","Placenta previa","Cervical incompetence","Amniotic fluid embolism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin may cause uterine tachysystole, increasing the risk of uterine rupture.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated risk."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"SSRIs such as fluoxetine affect sleep by doing which of the following?","options":["Decrease rapid-eye-movement (REM) sleep and cause vivid dreams","Increase REM sleep","Eliminate dreaming entirely","Cause narcolepsy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SSRIs (e.g., fluoxetine) decrease rapid-eye-movement sleep and cause vivid dreams.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated effect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which liver enzymes become elevated soon after ingestion of acetaminophen?","options":["SGOT and SGPT","Amylase and lipase","CK-MB and troponin","BUN and creatinine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SGOT and SGPT levels become elevated soon after ingestion of acetaminophen.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the enzymes affected here."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Steroid use tends to have which effect on blood glucose, particularly in diabetic/pre-diabetic clients?","options":["Increases blood glucose levels","Decreases blood glucose levels","Has no effect","Causes hypoglycaemia only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Steroid use tends to increase blood glucose levels, particularly in clients with diabetes and pre-diabetes.  Why B, C, D wrong: Opposite of the stated effect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Magnesium sulphate crosses the placenta and can produce which adverse neonatal effects? (Select all that apply)","options":["Respiratory depression","Hypotonia","Bradycardia","Hyperglycaemia","Hypertension"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Magnesium sulphate crosses the placenta and can produce adverse neonatal effects including respiratory depression, hypotonia, and bradycardia.  Why D, E wrong: Not among the listed neonatal effects."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Furosemide (Lasix) is a potassium-depleting diuretic that can cause:","options":["Hypokalaemia","Hyperkalaemia","Hypercalcaemia","Hypernatraemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Furosemide (Lasix) is a potassium-depleting diuretic that can cause hypokalaemia.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated effect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"A client who reports difficulty swallowing and excessive respiratory secretions 1 hour after receiving pyridostigmine bromide is most likely experiencing:","options":["Cholinergic crisis / excessive acetylcholinesterase medication","Myasthenic crisis","Anaphylaxis","Hypertensive crisis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: These symptoms suggest cholinergic crisis or excessive acetylcholinesterase medication.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not indicated by these specific symptoms after pyridostigmine."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"When given through an I.V. catheter in the hand, phenytoin may cause which condition?","options":["Purple glove syndrome","Grey baby syndrome","Reye's syndrome","Serotonin syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Phenytoin given through an I.V. catheter in the hand may cause purple glove syndrome.  Why B wrong: Grey baby syndrome is caused by chloramphenicol.  Why C wrong: Reye's syndrome is aspirin-induced.  Why D wrong: Not associated with phenytoin here."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Carbamazepine (CBZ) causes agranulocytosis because of the reduction in which blood cells?","options":["Red blood cells","White blood cells","Platelets","Reticulocytes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Carbamazepine (CBZ) causes agranulocytosis because of the reduction in white blood cells (AIIMS BPL-2016).  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the cell type reduced."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Drug hypersensitivity (drug allergy) is the result of which type of reaction?","options":["An antigen-antibody immune reaction occurring when a drug is given to a susceptible client","A direct toxic effect on the liver","An idiosyncratic genetic mutation","A drug-drug interaction only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Drug hypersensitivity/drug allergy is the result of an antigen-antibody immune reaction that occurs when a drug is given to a susceptible client.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the definition given."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Alpha blockers may cause severe orthostatic hypotension and syncope, especially with the first few doses. This is commonly called:","options":["First dose effect","Rebound effect","Withdrawal effect","Cheese reaction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Alpha blockers may cause severe orthostatic hypotension and syncope with the first few doses, an effect called the \"first dose effect.\"  Why B, C wrong: Different phenomena.  Why D wrong: The cheese reaction is associated with MAOIs and tyramine."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which are the most serious complications of aminoglycosides?","options":["Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity","Hepatotoxicity and cardiotoxicity","Neurotoxicity and myopathy","Photosensitivity and rash"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity are the most serious complications of aminoglycosides.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated most serious complications."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which of the following are the most common adverse effects of therapeutic doses of ACE inhibitors? (Select all that apply)","options":["Angioedema of the face and limbs","Dry cough","Hyperkalaemia","Proteinuria","Hypoglycaemia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: ACE inhibitor adverse effects include angioedema of face/limbs, dry cough, dysgeusia, fatigue, headache, hyperkalaemia, hypotension, proteinuria, rash, and tachycardia (JIPMER-2016).  Why E wrong: Hypoglycaemia is not listed for ACE inhibitors."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Nitrates may cause which of the following adverse effects? (Select all that apply)","options":["Flushing","Headache","Orthostatic hypotension","Reflex tachycardia","Constipation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nitrates may cause flushing, headache, orthostatic hypotension, reflex tachycardia, rash, syncope, and vomiting.  Why E wrong: Constipation is not listed for nitrates."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Warfarin may cause which of the following adverse effects? (Select all that apply)","options":["Agranulocytosis","Alopecia (with long-term use)","Tissue necrosis or gangrene","Dermatitis","Hyperglycaemia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Warfarin may cause agranulocytosis, alopecia (long-term use), anorexia, dermatitis, fever, nausea, tissue necrosis or gangrene, urticaria, and vomiting.  Why E wrong: Hyperglycaemia is not listed for warfarin."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the most serious adverse reaction linked to metformin, most likely in clients with renal dysfunction?","options":["Lactic acidosis","Hypoglycaemia","Weight gain","Pancreatitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most serious adverse reaction linked to metformin is lactic acidosis, most likely to occur in clients with renal dysfunction.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated most serious reaction."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Fluconazole may cause transient elevations of which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Liver enzymes","Alkaline phosphatase","Bilirubin","Blood urea nitrogen and creatinine","Blood glucose"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Fluconazole may cause transient elevations of liver enzymes, alkaline phosphatase, and bilirubin levels, as well as dizziness, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, diarrhoea, rash, headache, hypokalaemia, and elevated BUN and creatinine.  Why E wrong: Blood glucose elevation is not listed for fluconazole."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Bile-sequestering drugs may cause which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Bloating","Cholelithiasis","Constipation","Steatorrhea","Diarrhoea"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Bile-sequestering drugs may cause bloating, cholelithiasis, constipation, and steatorrhea.  Why E wrong: Diarrhoea is not among the listed effects."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Gold therapy is used for which condition?","options":["Rheumatoid arthritis","Osteoarthritis","Gout","Osteoporosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Gold therapy is used for rheumatoid arthritis.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated indication."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Prolonged or frequent use of a benzodiazepine can cause which of the following?","options":["Physical dependency and withdrawal syndrome when the drug is stopped","Permanent tooth discoloration","Grey baby syndrome","Hearing loss"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Prolonged/frequent use of a benzodiazepine can cause physical dependency and withdrawal syndrome when the drug is stopped.  Why B, C, D wrong: Associated with tetracycline, chloramphenicol, and loop diuretics respectively."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"In pregnant clients, benzodiazepines increase the risk of congenital malformation if taken during which period?","options":["First trimester","Second trimester","Third trimester","During labour only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In pregnant clients, benzodiazepines increase the risk of congenital malformation if taken in the first trimester.  Why B, C wrong: The risk noted is the first trimester.  Why D wrong: Use during labour causes neonatal flaccidity (a separate point)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Using benzodiazepines during labour may cause which of the following in the neonate?","options":["Neonatal flaccidity","Neonatal hyperglycaemia","Neonatal seizures","Neonatal jaundice"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Using benzodiazepines during labour may cause neonatal flaccidity.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated neonatal effect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Toxic doses of a beta blocker can produce which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Severe hypotension","Bradycardia","Heart failure","Bronchospasm","Hyperglycaemia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Toxic doses of a beta blocker can produce severe hypotension, bradycardia, heart failure, or bronchospasm.  Why E wrong: Hyperglycaemia is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Rapid parenteral administration of a loop diuretic (e.g. frusemide/Lasix) may cause which of the following?","options":["Hearing loss (including deafness) and tinnitus","Purple glove syndrome","Grey baby syndrome","Agranulocytosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rapid parenteral administration of a loop diuretic may cause hearing loss (including deafness) and tinnitus.  Why B, C, D wrong: Associated with phenytoin, chloramphenicol, and CBZ respectively."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"High doses of a loop diuretic can produce profound diuresis, leading to:","options":["Hypovolemia and cardiovascular collapse","Fluid overload","Hyperkalaemia","Hypertension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: High doses of a loop diuretic can produce profound diuresis, leading to hypovolemia and cardiovascular collapse.  Why B, C, D wrong: Opposite/not the stated effect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Permanent discoloration of teeth occurs if tetracycline is given during tooth formation in children younger than what age?","options":["Age 8","Age 5","Age 12","Age 2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Permanent discoloration of teeth occurs if tetracycline is given during tooth formation in children younger than age 8.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated age cut-off."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which drug may cause \"Grey baby syndrome\"?","options":["Chloramphenicol","Tetracycline","Rifampin","Phenytoin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Chloramphenicol may cause grey baby syndrome.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not associated with grey baby syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Digoxin increases the forceful myocardial contraction. This effect is known as:","options":["Positive inotropic effect","Negative inotropic effect","Positive chronotropic effect","Negative dromotropic effect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Digoxin increases the forceful myocardial contraction (positive inotropic effect).  Why B, C, D wrong: These describe other cardiac effects."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Before administering digoxin, the pulse should be checked, and it should NOT be administered if the pulse rate is below:","options":["60 bpm","50 bpm","70 bpm","100 bpm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pulse should be checked before administration of digoxin, and it should not be administered if pulse rate is below 60 bpm (AIIMS Nagpur-2020).  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated cut-off."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which are early signs of digoxin toxicity? (Select all that apply)","options":["Nausea and vomiting","Blurring of vision","Blind spots","Diplopia and white halos","Bradycardia only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Early signs of digoxin toxicity are nausea & vomiting, blurring of vision, blind spots, diplopia and white halos.  Why E wrong: Bradycardia is not listed as the early sign here."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Nitrous oxide is also called:","options":["Laughing gas","Sweet gas","Sleep gas","Marsh gas"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nitrous oxide is also called laughing gas.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated name."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which substance was the first to be used for general anaesthesia?","options":["Ether","Chloroform","Nitrous oxide","Halothane"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ether was the first substance to be used for general anaesthesia.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the first agent used."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Carbamazepine is a drug of choice for which condition?","options":["Partial seizure","Absence seizure","Status epilepticus","Mania"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Carbamazepine is a drug of choice for partial seizure.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated CBZ indication here."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"NSAIDs are contraindicated in peptic ulcer, except which drug?","options":["PCM (paracetamol)","Ibuprofen","Aspirin","Naproxen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NSAIDs are contraindicated in peptic ulcer except PCM (paracetamol).  Why B, C, D wrong: These are NSAIDs contraindicated in peptic ulcer."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Aspirin is contraindicated in all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Vitamin K deficiency","Chickenpox","Influenza","Mild fever in adults"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Aspirin is contraindicated in vitamin K deficiency, chickenpox, influenza, and pregnancy; mild fever in adults is not listed as a contraindication.  Why A, B, C wrong: All are listed contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which is the drug of choice in chronic gout arthritis, and how should it be taken?","options":["Allopurinol, taken with food to prevent gastric irritation","Colchicine, taken on an empty stomach","Aspirin, taken with milk","Probenecid, taken at bedtime"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Allopurinol is a drug of choice in chronic gout arthritis and should be taken with food to prevent gastric irritation.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not stated as DOC for chronic gout here."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Dopamine is used in which conditions? (Select all that apply)","options":["Cardiogenic shock","Hypotension due to poor cardiac output","Cardiomyopathy","Tachycardia","Pheochromocytoma"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Dopamine is used in cardiogenic shock, hypotension due to poor cardiac output, and cardiomyopathy.  Why D, E wrong: Dopamine is contraindicated in tachycardia, arrhythmias and pheochromocytoma."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Dopamine is contraindicated in which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Tachycardia","Arrhythmias","Pheochromocytoma","Cardiogenic shock","Hypotension"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Dopamine is contraindicated in tachycardia, arrhythmias and pheochromocytoma.  Why D, E wrong: These are dopamine indications."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"An antacid containing magnesium has which effect (Trick: MD)?","options":["Laxative effect causing diarrhoea","Constipation","No GI effect","Causes vomiting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Antacid containing magnesium has a laxative effect causing diarrhoea (Trick: MD = Magnesium & Diarrhoea).  Why B wrong: Aluminium-containing antacids cause constipation.  Why C, D wrong: Not the stated effect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"An antacid containing aluminium causes which effect (Trick: AC)?","options":["Constipation","Diarrhoea","Vomiting","Nausea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Antacid containing aluminium causes constipation (Trick: AC = Aluminium & Constipation).  Why B wrong: Magnesium antacids cause diarrhoea.  Why C, D wrong: Not the stated effect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the most common side effect of insulin?","options":["Hypoglycaemia","Hyperglycaemia","Weight loss","Lactic acidosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common side effect of insulin is hypoglycaemia.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the most common insulin side effect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Anti-thyroid medication is contraindicated during which period?","options":["Pregnancy and lactation","Adolescence only","Old age only","Menstruation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anti-thyroid medication is contraindicated during pregnancy and lactation.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Aspirin toxicity affects which cranial nerve, causing tinnitus?","options":["Cranial nerve 8 (vestibulocochlear)","Cranial nerve 5 (trigeminal)","Cranial nerve 7 (facial)","Cranial nerve 10 (vagus)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Aspirin toxicity affects cranial nerve 8, causing tinnitus.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the cranial nerve affected."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the antidote of atropine?","options":["Physostigmine","Naloxone","Protamine sulfate","Deferoxamine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote of atropine is physostigmine.  Why B wrong: Naloxone reverses opioids.  Why C wrong: Protamine sulfate reverses heparin.  Why D wrong: Deferoxamine treats iron intoxication."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which drug is the most commonly used drug in leprosy?","options":["Dapsone","Rifampin","Isoniazid","Ethambutol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dapsone is the most commonly used drug in leprosy.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are primarily anti-tubercular drugs."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the therapeutic level of digoxin?","options":["0.5–2 ng/dl",">2 ng/dl","4–12 µg/ml","0.6–1.2 mEq/L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The therapeutic level of digoxin is 0.5–2 ng/dl.  Why B wrong: >2 ng/dl is the toxic level.  Why C wrong: Carbamazepine range.  Why D wrong: Lithium range."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the toxic level of digoxin?","options":[">2 ng/dl","0.5–2 ng/dl",">1.5 mEq/L","50–125 µg/ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The toxic level of digoxin is >2 ng/dl.  Why B wrong: That is the therapeutic level.  Why C wrong: Lithium toxic level.  Why D wrong: Valproic acid level."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Ethambutol commonly causes which adverse effect?","options":["Visual disturbance","Ototoxicity","Orange urine","Peripheral neuritis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ethambutol causes visual disturbance.  Why B wrong: Streptomycin causes ototoxicity.  Why C wrong: Rifampin causes orange discoloration.  Why D wrong: Isoniazid causes peripheral neuritis."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Streptomycin causes ototoxicity due to damage of which cranial nerve?","options":["8th cranial nerve","5th cranial nerve","7th cranial nerve","2nd cranial nerve"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Streptomycin causes ototoxicity due to damage of the 8th cranial nerve.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the cranial nerve involved."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Rifampin causes orange-red discoloration of body fluids and is noted to be:","options":["Safe in pregnancy","Contraindicated in pregnancy","Ototoxic","Nephrotoxic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rifampin causes orange-red discoloration of urine, sweats, vaginal discharge and tears, and is noted as safe in pregnancy.  Why B wrong: It is noted as safe in pregnancy.  Why C, D wrong: Not the stated effect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Supplementary vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) should be given to clients taking which drug?","options":["Isoniazid","Rifampin","Ethambutol","Streptomycin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Supplementary vitamin B6 or pyridoxine should be given to clients taking isoniazid.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the drug requiring pyridoxine supplementation."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Isoniazid causes which adverse effects? (Select all that apply)","options":["Peripheral neuritis","Hepatotoxicity","Visual disturbance","Orange urine","Ototoxicity"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Isoniazid causes peripheral neuritis and hepatotoxicity (PGI Rai Bareli-2018).  Why C wrong: Ethambutol causes visual disturbance.  Why D wrong: Rifampin causes orange urine.  Why E wrong: Streptomycin causes ototoxicity."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Why should tetracycline not be given to pregnant mothers and children?","options":["Because it damages developing teeth/bone","Because it causes deafness","Because it causes visual disturbance","Because it causes hepatotoxicity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tetracycline should not be given to pregnant mothers and children because it damages developing teeth/bone.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated reason for tetracycline."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Tetracycline drugs, if taken with milk, cause:","options":["Discoloration of teeth","Improved absorption","Ototoxicity","Nephrotoxicity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tetracycline drugs if taken with milk cause discoloration of teeth.  Why B wrong: Milk actually chelates and reduces absorption; discoloration is the effect noted.  Why C, D wrong: Not stated for tetracycline-milk interaction."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Penicillin was the first antibiotic, discovered by:","options":["Alexander Fleming","Louis Pasteur","Robert Koch","Edward Jenner"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Penicillin was the first antibiotic, discovered by Alexander Fleming.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the discoverer of penicillin."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"When ductus arteriosus fails to close at birth (PDA), a small dose of which drug may close it by inhibiting prostaglandin production?","options":["Indomethacin","Ibuprofen syrup only","Paracetamol","Prostaglandin E1"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In PDA, a small dose of indomethacin may be given to close it by inhibiting production of prostaglandin.  Why B, C wrong: Not the drug named here.  Why D wrong: Prostaglandin E1 keeps the ductus open."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"NSAIDs should NOT be administered during which period?","options":["Late pregnancy","Early pregnancy only","Lactation only","Menstruation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NSAIDs should not be administered in late pregnancy.  Why B, C, D wrong: The stated restriction is late pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which of the following anti-tubercular drugs is only bacteriostatic?","options":["Ethambutol","Rifampin","Isoniazid","Streptomycin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ethambutol is only a bacteriostatic drug.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not identified as only bacteriostatic here."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Betamethasone is used for foetal lung maturity as a surfactant to prevent which condition?","options":["RDS (Respiratory Distress Syndrome)","Neonatal jaundice","Necrotizing enterocolitis","Neonatal sepsis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Betamethasone is used for foetal lung maturity as a surfactant to prevent RDS.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated prevention target."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Aminoglycoside drugs may cause which adverse effect?","options":["Deafness (ototoxicity)","Constipation","Visual disturbance","Orange urine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Aminoglycoside drugs may cause deafness (ototoxicity).  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated aminoglycoside effect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Nitroglycerin is used sublingually in which condition?","options":["Angina pectoris","Myocardial infarction only","Heart failure only","Hypertension crisis only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nitroglycerin is used sublingually in angina pectoris.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated indication for sublingual nitroglycerin here."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the drug of choice in vertical transmission of HIV?","options":["Nevirapine","Zidovudine","Tetracycline","Vancomycin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DOC in vertical transmission of HIV is nevirapine.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated DOC here."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the drug of choice for cholera?","options":["Tetracycline","Vancomycin","Quinine","Neostigmine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DOC of choice for cholera is tetracycline.  Why B wrong: Vancomycin is DOC for MRSA.  Why C wrong: Quinine is DOC in malaria.  Why D wrong: Neostigmine is DOC in myasthenia gravis."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the drug of choice for Methicillin Resistant Staphylococcus Aureus (MRSA)?","options":["Vancomycin","Penicillin","Tetracycline","Nevirapine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DOC of MRSA is vancomycin.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated MRSA DOC."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the most common (m/c) side effect of cyclophosphamide, which can be prevented by increasing fluid intake?","options":["Cystitis","Alopecia","Nausea","Nephrotoxicity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The m/c side effect of cyclophosphamide is cystitis, which can be prevented by increasing fluid intake.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not identified as the m/c preventable-by-fluids effect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Erythropoietin is a hormone secreted by which organ to induce red blood cell production?","options":["Kidneys","Liver","Spleen","Bone marrow"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Erythropoietin is a hormone secreted by the kidneys to induce RBC production, and is given exogenously in renal failure clients to prevent anaemia.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the secreting organ."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the drug of choice in myasthenia gravis?","options":["Neostigmine","Atropine sulphate","Edrophonium","Quinine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DOC in myasthenia gravis is neostigmine (RRB-2020). (Note: Edrophonium is used for diagnosis; pyridostigmine bromide for treatment per p.1021.)  Why B wrong: Atropine sulphate is DOC in bradycardia.  Why C wrong: Edrophonium is for diagnosis of MG.  Why D wrong: Quinine is DOC in malaria."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the drug of choice in bradycardia?","options":["Atropine sulphate","Dopamine","Digoxin","Neostigmine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Drug of choice in bradycardia is atropine sulphate.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated DOC in bradycardia."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which of the following are side effects of phenytoin (Trick: HOT MALIKA)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hyperplasia of gums","Hirsutism","Osteomalacia","Megaloblastic anaemia","Ataxia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Phenytoin side effects (HOT MALIKA) = Hyperplasia of gums, Hirsutism, Osteomalacia, Megaloblastic anaemia, Ataxia, Lymphadenopathy, Insulin resistance, vitamin-K deficiency, Arrhythmia.  All listed are documented phenytoin side effects."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Phenobarbitone is also used in which condition?","options":["Pathological jaundice","Myasthenia gravis","Malaria","Bradycardia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Phenobarbitone is also used in pathological jaundice.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated use here."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the drug of choice in malaria?","options":["Quinine","Chloroquine","Tetracycline","Dapsone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DOC in malaria is quinine.  Why B wrong: Chloroquine is DOC in malaria during pregnancy.  Why C, D wrong: For cholera and leprosy respectively."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the drug of choice in malaria during pregnancy?","options":["Chloroquine","Quinine","Doxycycline","Primaquine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DOC in malaria during pregnancy is chloroquine.  Why B wrong: Quinine is DOC in malaria (non-pregnant context here).  Why C, D wrong: Not the stated pregnancy DOC."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Reye's syndrome in children is an aspirin-induced disease which occurs if aspirin is taken in which conditions?","options":["Viral diseases such as flu and chickenpox","Bacterial infections","Fungal infections","Fever of any cause"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Reye's syndrome (in children) is an aspirin-induced disease that occurs if aspirin is taken in viral diseases such as flu and chickenpox.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated trigger."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Lactulose is used in liver disease for which purpose?","options":["Clearance of ammonia from the GI tract","Reduction of bilirubin","Prevention of variceal bleeding","Correction of hypoalbuminaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lactulose is used in liver disease for clearance of ammonia from the GI tract.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated function."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Atropine sulphate is contraindicated in which type of glaucoma?","options":["Closed (narrow) angle glaucoma","Open angle glaucoma","Congenital glaucoma","Secondary glaucoma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Atropine sulphate is contraindicated in closed angle glaucoma.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Opioids are contraindicated in patients with which conditions? (Select all that apply)","options":["Acute or severe bronchial asthma","Respiratory depression","Mild pain","Chronic constipation"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Opioids are contraindicated in those with acute or severe bronchial asthma or respiratory depression.  Why C, D wrong: Not stated contraindications (opioids can cause constipation but that is an effect, not a listed contraindication here)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Whole blood without the plasma removed has a haematocrit of about:","options":["38%","25%","50%","45%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Whole blood without the plasma removed has a haematocrit of about 38%.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Each unit of whole blood or red blood cells contains enough haemoglobin to raise the haemoglobin level in an average-sized adult by approximately:","options":["1 g/L or by 3% HCT","5 g/L or by 10% HCT","2 g/dL or by 6% HCT","0.5 g/L or by 1% HCT"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Each unit raises the haemoglobin level in an average-sized adult by 1 g/L, or by 3% HCT.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Washed PRBCs are commonly used for which clients?","options":["Clients previously sensitized to transfusions","First-time transfusion recipients","Clients with iron deficiency","Clients with dehydration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Washed PRBCs, rinsed with a special solution that removes WBCs and platelets, are commonly used for clients previously sensitized to transfusions, decreasing the chance of a transfusion reaction.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated indication."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Depending on facility policy, how many licensed professionals need to identify the client and blood products at the bedside before administering a blood transfusion?","options":["Two","One","Three","Four"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Two licensed professionals need to identify the client and blood products at the bedside before transfusion to prevent errors and a potentially fatal reaction.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated number."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"If a client is a Jehovah's Witness, what is required for a transfusion?","options":["Special written permission","Physician verbal order only","No consent needed","Court order always"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If the client is a Jehovah's Witness, a transfusion requires special written permission.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated requirement."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which blood administration set types are commonly used to administer blood?","options":["Straight-line and Y-type sets","Micro-drip sets only","Vented sets only","Buretrol sets only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Straight-line and Y-type blood administration sets are commonly used to administer blood.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated set types."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"After a platelet transfusion, when should blood be drawn for a platelet count to determine increments?","options":["1 hour after platelet transfusion","Immediately after","6 hours after","24 hours after"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Prepare to draw blood for a platelet count 1 hour after platelet transfusion to determine the increments for later platelet transfusion.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated timing."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Large-volume transfusions of fresh frozen plasma (FFP) may require correction for which electrolyte abnormality because citric acid in FFP binds calcium?","options":["Hypocalcaemia","Hyperkalaemia","Hypernatraemia","Hypomagnesaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Large-volume FFP transfusions may require correction for hypocalcaemia because citric acid in FFP binds calcium.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated abnormality."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Within what time frame should whole blood be infused using a blood administration set?","options":["Within 4 hours","Within 6 hours","Within 2 hours","Within 12 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Use a blood administration set to infuse whole blood within 4 hours.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated time."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Platelets are used to prevent bleeding caused by which condition?","options":["Thrombocytopenia","Anaemia","Leucopenia","Hypocalcaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Platelets are used to prevent bleeding caused by thrombocytopenia.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated indication."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Platelet transfusions are contraindicated in a patient with which condition?","options":["Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP)","Idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura","Aplastic anaemia","Leukaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Platelet transfusions are contraindicated in a patient with thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP).  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Fresh frozen plasma (FFP) is used to treat which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Coagulation factor deficiencies","Warfarin reversal","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Iron deficiency anaemia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: FFP is used to treat coagulation factor deficiencies, warfarin reversal, and thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura.  Why D wrong: Not an FFP indication."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Albumin is used to treat which condition?","options":["Hypoproteinaemia (with or without edema)","Coagulation factor deficiency","Thrombocytopenia","Iron deficiency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Albumin is used to treat hypoproteinaemia (with or without edema).  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated indication."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Cryoprecipitate is the insoluble portion of plasma recovered from FFP and contains which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Fibrinogen","Factor VIII","Von Willebrand factor (VWF)","Factor XIII","Fibronectin"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Cryoprecipitate contains fibrinogen, Factor VIII, VWF, Factor XIII, and fibronectin.  All listed components are documented."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Fresh frozen plasma is un-coagulated plasma separated from red blood cells and is rich in which coagulation factors?","options":["Factors V, VIII, and IX","Factors II, VII, X","Factors I and XIII only","Factor VIII only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: FFP is rich in coagulation factors V, VIII, and IX.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated factors."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Within what time must cryoprecipitate be administered after thawing?","options":["Within 6 hours of thawing","Within 4 hours of thawing","Within 30 minutes of thawing","Within 24 hours of thawing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cryoprecipitate must be administered within 6 hours of thawing.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated time."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"When administering blood or blood components, a catheter of which diameter should be used?","options":["20G & 18G or larger","24G or smaller","22G only","26G or smaller"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When administering blood or blood components, use a catheter with a diameter of 20G & 18G or larger.  Why B, C, D wrong: Smaller-gauge catheters are not recommended."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Whole blood or packed red blood cells should be obtained from the blood bank within how long of the transfusion start time?","options":["30 minutes","1 hour","15 minutes","2 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Obtain whole blood or packed red blood cells from the blood bank within 30 minutes of the transfusion start time.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated time."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Before administering blood, the bag should be observed for abnormalities. Which of the following should prompt return of the blood to the bank? (Select all that apply)","options":["Abnormal colour","Red blood cell clumping","Gas bubbles","Extraneous material","Correct expiration date"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Check the expiration date and observe the bag for abnormal colour, RBC clumping, gas bubbles, and extraneous material; return outdated or abnormal blood to the bank.  Why E wrong: A correct (non-expired) date is acceptable, not a reason to return."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Before administering blood, which identifiers should be checked? (Select all that apply)","options":["Blood bag identification number","ABO blood group","Rh compatibility","Client's favourite food"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Before administering blood, check the blood bag identification number, ABO blood group, and Rh compatibility.  Why D wrong: Irrelevant to blood administration safety."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"When administering multiple units of blood under pressure, which device should be used to avoid hypothermia?","options":["Blood warmer","Cooling blanket","Pressure cuff only","Micro-drip chamber"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When administering multiple units of blood under pressure, use a blood warmer to avoid hypothermia.  Why B, C, D wrong: Do not prevent hypothermia."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which infection is a risk of post-transfusion exposure in a patient receiving frequent blood transfusions?","options":["Hepatitis-C","Malaria only","Tuberculosis","Tetanus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis-C is exposed post-transfusion in a patient receiving frequent blood transfusions.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated post-transfusion risk here."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"A peripherally inserted central catheter (PICC) usually enters at which vein and terminates where?","options":["Enters at the basilic vein and terminates in the superior vena cava","Enters at the cephalic vein and terminates in the right atrium","Enters at the femoral vein and terminates in the inferior vena cava","Enters at the jugular vein and terminates in the aorta"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A PICC usually enters at the basilic vein and terminates in the superior vena cava.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated anatomy."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"When flushing a central venous catheter, which syringe should be used?","options":["Low-pressure 10-mL syringes","High-pressure 1-mL syringes","3-mL syringes only","50-mL syringes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When flushing a central venous catheter, flush vigorously using only low-pressure 10-mL syringes.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the recommended syringe."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"When flushing a central venous catheter, how much free-flowing blood should you be able to obtain before each flush?","options":["3 to 5 mL","1 to 2 mL","10 mL","0.5 mL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: You should be able to obtain 3 to 5 mL of free-flowing blood before each flush; if you can't, follow facility policy regarding occlusion or malfunction.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated volume."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"When changing the injection cap on a central venous catheter, the client should be instructed to perform which maneuver?","options":["Valsalva's maneuver","Trendelenburg maneuver","Credé's maneuver","Leopold's maneuver"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Instruct the client to perform Valsalva's maneuver while you quickly disconnect the old cap and connect the new injection cap using aseptic technique.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the correct maneuver for this procedure."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"When converting pounds to kilograms, 2.2 pounds is equal to how many kilograms?","options":["1 kilogram","2 kilograms","0.5 kilogram","2.2 kilograms"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When converting pounds to kilograms, 2.2 pounds equals 1 kilogram.  Why B, C, D wrong: Incorrect conversion."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"To convert a client's weight in pounds to kilograms, you should:","options":["Divide the weight in pounds by 2.2","Multiply the weight in pounds by 2.2","Divide by 2","Multiply by 1.2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: To convert weight in pounds to kilograms, divide the weight in pounds by 2.2.  Why B wrong: That converts kg to pounds.  Why C, D wrong: Incorrect factor."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"To convert a client's weight in kilograms to pounds, you should:","options":["Multiply the weight in kilograms by 2.2","Divide the weight in kilograms by 2.2","Multiply by 2","Divide by 1.2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: To convert weight in kilograms to pounds, multiply the weight in kilograms by 2.2.  Why B wrong: That converts pounds to kg.  Why C, D wrong: Incorrect factor."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"2 tablespoons (TBSP) is equal to how many mL?","options":["30 mL (1 fluid ounce)","15 mL","5 mL","60 mL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 2 tablespoons (TBSP) equals 1 fluid ounce or 30 mL (CIP Ranchi-2023).  Why B, C, D wrong: Incorrect conversion."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"1 teaspoon equals how many mL?","options":["5 mL (1 fluid dram)","15 mL","30 mL","10 mL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 1 teaspoon equals 1 fluid dram or 5 mL (NHM & IGNOU-2022).  Why B, C, D wrong: Incorrect conversion."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"To calculate the dose to be administered when a medication is available in units, which equation is used?","options":["Amount of drug in mL / Dose required in units = 1 mL or other measure / Drug available in units","Dose required × Drug available","Amount desired / Amount you have only","BSA × adult dose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The stated equation is: Amount of drug in mL / Dose required in units = 1 mL (or other measure) / Drug available in units.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the stated units-based formula."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which is the basic unit of length in the metric system?","options":["Meter","Liter","Gram","Mole"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A meter is the basic unit of length.  Why B wrong: Liter is volume.  Why C wrong: Gram is weight.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which is the basic unit of volume?","options":["Liter","Meter","Gram","Mole"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A liter is the basic unit of volume.  Why B wrong: Meter is length.  Why C wrong: Gram is weight.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which is the basic unit of weight?","options":["Gram","Meter","Liter","Mole"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A gram is the basic unit of weight.  Why B wrong: Meter is length.  Why C wrong: Liter is volume.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"A paediatric dose can be calculated by dividing the child's body surface area (BSA) by the average adult BSA and multiplying by the average adult dose. What is the average adult BSA used?","options":["1.73 m²","1.5 m²","2.0 m²","1.0 m²"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The average adult BSA used is 1.73 m².  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the most accurate and common way to calculate paediatric drug doses?","options":["The dosage per kilogram of body weight method","The body surface area method only","Fried's rule","Young's rule"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most accurate and common way to calculate paediatric drug doses is the dosage per kilogram of body weight method.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not identified as most accurate/common here."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Using the body-weight method, the paediatric dose is calculated as:","options":["Child's body weight in kg × required number of mg of the drug per kg","Child's BSA × adult dose","Age in years × 10","Weight in pounds × 2.2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Paediatric dose = child's body weight in kg × required number of mg of the drug per kg.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the body-weight formula."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"To solve a two-step dosage calculation, which formula is used?","options":["Amount desired / Amount you have = Equivalent amount desired / Equivalent amount you have","Amount desired × Amount you have","Weight × 2.2","BSA / 1.73 m²"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The two-step dosage formula is: Amount desired / Amount you have = Equivalent amount desired / Equivalent amount you have.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated two-step formula."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the onset of action of regular insulin?","options":["½ to 1 hour","2 to 4 hours","3 to 6 hours","Immediate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Regular insulin's onset of action is ½ to 1 hour, peak is 2 to 4 hours, and duration is 3 to 6 hours.  Why B wrong: 2–4 hours is the peak.  Why C wrong: 3–6 hours is the duration.  Why D wrong: Not immediate."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the peak of action of regular insulin?","options":["2 to 4 hours","½ to 1 hour","3 to 6 hours","8 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Regular insulin peak is 2 to 4 hours.  Why B wrong: That is the onset.  Why C wrong: That is the duration.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the duration of action of regular insulin?","options":["3 to 6 hours","½ to 1 hour","2 to 4 hours","12 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Regular insulin duration is 3 to 6 hours.  Why B wrong: Onset.  Why C wrong: Peak.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which drug is used for the diagnosis of myasthenia gravis?","options":["Edrophonium","Pyridostigmine bromide","Neostigmine","Atropine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Edrophonium is used for the diagnosis of myasthenia gravis.  Why B wrong: Pyridostigmine bromide is used for treatment.  Why C wrong: Neostigmine is DOC for treatment (p.1020).  Why D wrong: Atropine is unrelated to MG diagnosis."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which drug is used for the treatment of myasthenia gravis (per the pyridostigmine note)?","options":["Pyridostigmine bromide","Edrophonium","Atropine","Dopamine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pyridostigmine bromide is used for the treatment of myasthenia gravis.  Why B wrong: Edrophonium is for diagnosis.  Why C, D wrong: Not used to treat MG."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which agents are used to reduce cerebral oedema?","options":["Mannitol or corticosteroids","Loop diuretics only","Beta blockers","ACE inhibitors"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mannitol or corticosteroids are used to reduce cerebral oedema.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated agents."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Mannitol can do all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Reduce intraocular pressure","Prevent acute tubular necrosis","Draw water into the vascular system to increase blood pressure","Cause fluid retention"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Mannitol reduces intraocular pressure, prevents acute tubular necrosis, and draws water into the vascular system; it promotes osmotic diuresis (does not cause fluid retention).  Why A, B, C wrong: These are stated mannitol actions."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the therapeutic phenytoin serum level?","options":["10 to 20 mg/dL","0.5–2 ng/dl","0.6–1.2 mEq/L","4–12 µg/ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A therapeutic phenytoin level is 10 to 20 mg/dL (RRB-2020).  Why B wrong: Digoxin therapeutic level.  Why C wrong: Lithium level.  Why D wrong: Carbamazepine level."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Warfarin inhibits clot formation by interfering with clotting factors dependent on which vitamin?","options":["Vitamin K","Vitamin C","Vitamin B12","Vitamin D"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Warfarin inhibits clot formation by interfering with clotting factors that are dependent on vitamin K.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the vitamin involved."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Mannitol promotes osmotic diuresis by increasing the pressure gradient in which structures, thus increasing urine output?","options":["Renal tubules","Glomerular capillaries only","Collecting ducts only","Ureters"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mannitol promotes osmotic diuresis by increasing the pressure gradient in the renal tubules, thus increasing urine output.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated site."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Phenytoin should be administered slowly at what maximum rate?","options":["50 mg/minute","100 mg/minute","10 mg/minute","25 mg/minute"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Phenytoin should be administered slowly (50 mg/minute).  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated rate."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Acetazolamide, a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor, decreases intraocular pressure by which mechanism?","options":["Decreasing the secretion of aqueous humor","Increasing aqueous humor drainage","Constricting the pupil","Increasing tear production"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Acetazolamide, a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor, decreases intraocular pressure by decreasing the secretion of aqueous humor (AIIMS Rishikesh-2017).  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Furosemide belongs to which class of diuretics?","options":["Loop diuretic","Thiazide diuretic","Potassium-sparing diuretic","Osmotic diuretic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Furosemide is a loop diuretic.  Why B, C, D wrong: Furosemide is not a thiazide, potassium-sparing, or osmotic diuretic."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Urokinase belongs to which drug class?","options":["Thrombolytic drug","Anticoagulant","Antiplatelet","Vasodilator"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Urokinase is a thrombolytic drug.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated class."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Atropine, an anticholinergic drug, has which effect on the pupil?","options":["Mydriatic effect causing pupil dilation","Miosis (pupil constriction)","No change in pupil size","Fixed pinpoint pupils"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Atropine, an anticholinergic drug, has mydriatic effects, causing pupil dilation.  Why B, C, D wrong: Opposite/not the stated effect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Methylprednisolone, an anti-inflammatory drug, is used to decrease which of the following?","options":["Spinal cord edema","Blood glucose","Intraocular pressure only","Blood pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Methylprednisolone, an anti-inflammatory drug, is used to decrease spinal cord edema.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated use."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Heparin has which action?","options":["Prevents clot formation and prevents clot enlargement","Dissolves existing clots","Increases platelet count","Interferes with vitamin K"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Heparin will prevent clot formation and prevent clot enlargement.  Why B wrong: That describes thrombolytics.  Why C wrong: Not a heparin action.  Why D wrong: That describes warfarin."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Vasodilators act directly on which structure to reduce blood pressure?","options":["Smooth muscle","Cardiac muscle","Skeletal muscle","Renal tubules"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Vasodilators act directly on smooth muscle to reduce blood pressure.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated site of action."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Anti-lipidemics lower elevated lipid levels. Which drug is an example?","options":["Atorvastatin","Warfarin","Furosemide","Mannitol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anti-lipidemics lower elevated lipid levels, like atorvastatin.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not anti-lipidemic drugs."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Metformin lowers blood glucose by all of the following mechanisms EXCEPT:","options":["Increasing hepatic glucose production","Decreasing hepatic glucose production","Reducing intestinal glucose absorption","Improving insulin sensitivity by increasing peripheral glucose uptake and utilization"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Metformin decreases (not increases) hepatic glucose production, reduces intestinal glucose absorption, and improves insulin sensitivity by increasing peripheral glucose uptake and utilization.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are correct metformin mechanisms."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Tricyclic antidepressants may inhibit reuptake of norepinephrine and serotonin at which neuronal site, enhancing their concentration in the synaptic cleft?","options":["Presynaptic neurons (central nervous system nerve terminals)","Postsynaptic neurons only","Glial cells","Neuromuscular junction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: TCAs may inhibit reuptake of norepinephrine and serotonin in CNS nerve terminals (presynaptic neurons), thus enhancing their concentration and activity in the synaptic cleft.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated site."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Magnesium sulphate interferes with the release of which neurotransmitter at the myoneural junction?","options":["Acetylcholine","Dopamine","Serotonin","GABA"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Magnesium sulphate interferes with the release of acetylcholine at the myoneural junction.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the neurotransmitter affected."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the antidote for cyanide poisoning?","options":["Amyl nitrite","Deferoxamine","Protamine sulfate","Physostigmine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Amyl nitrite is the antidote for cyanide poisoning.  Why B wrong: Deferoxamine treats iron intoxication.  Why C wrong: Protamine sulfate reverses heparin.  Why D wrong: Physostigmine is the atropine antidote."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Atropine sulfate is the antidote for which type of toxicity?","options":["Anticholinesterase toxicity and organophosphate poisoning","Cyanide poisoning","Iron intoxication","Heparin overdose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Atropine sulfate is the antidote for anticholinesterase toxicity and organophosphate poisoning.  Why B wrong: Amyl nitrite for cyanide.  Why C wrong: Deferoxamine for iron.  Why D wrong: Protamine for heparin."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the antidote for iron intoxication?","options":["Deferoxamine","Amyl nitrite","Edetate calcium disodium","Naloxone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Deferoxamine is an antidote for iron intoxication.  Why B wrong: Amyl nitrite for cyanide.  Why C wrong: Edetate calcium disodium for lead poisoning.  Why D wrong: Naloxone for opioids."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the antidote for digitoxin (digoxin) intoxication?","options":["Digoxin Immune Fab","Deferoxamine","Protamine sulfate","Physostigmine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Digoxin Immune Fab is the antidote for digitoxin intoxication.  Why B, C, D wrong: Antidotes for iron, heparin, and atropine respectively."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Edetate calcium disodium is a chelating agent used to treat which poisoning?","options":["Lead poisoning","Iron poisoning","Cyanide poisoning","Organophosphate poisoning"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Edetate calcium disodium is a chelating agent used to treat lead poisoning.  Why B wrong: Deferoxamine for iron.  Why C wrong: Amyl nitrite for cyanide.  Why D wrong: Atropine/PAM for organophosphate."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Pralidoxime chloride (PAM) is an antidote for which of the following?","options":["Organophosphate poisoning and cholinergic overdose","Cyanide poisoning","Iron intoxication","Heparin overdose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pralidoxime chloride (PAM) is an antidote for organophosphate poisoning and cholinergic overdose.  Why B, C, D wrong: Antidotes for other agents."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Protamine sulfate is used to treat overdose of which drug?","options":["Heparin","Warfarin","Aspirin","Digoxin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Protamine sulfate is used to treat heparin overdose.  Why B wrong: Warfarin is reversed by vitamin K/FFP.  Why C, D wrong: Not reversed by protamine."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Loop diuretics inhibit sodium and chloride re-absorption in which part of the nephron?","options":["Ascending loop of Henle","Distal convoluted tubule","Proximal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Loop diuretics inhibit sodium and chloride re-absorption in the ascending loop of Henle, thus increasing excretion of sodium, chloride and water.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the site of loop diuretic action."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Like thiazide diuretics, loop diuretics increase the excretion of which electrolyte?","options":["Potassium","Calcium","Magnesium","Bicarbonate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Like thiazide diuretics, loop diuretics increase excretion of potassium.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the electrolyte specified."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Spironolactone is a potassium-sparing diuretic that competitively inhibits aldosterone at which site, promoting sodium excretion and potassium retention?","options":["Distal renal tubules","Ascending loop of Henle","Proximal tubules","Glomerulus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Spironolactone, a potassium-sparing diuretic, competitively inhibits aldosterone at the distal renal tubules, promoting sodium excretion and potassium retention.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated site."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Opioid agonists are used to relieve or decrease pain without causing the client to:","options":["Lose consciousness","Feel drowsy","Breathe","Move"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Opioid agonists are used to relieve or decrease pain without causing the client to lose consciousness.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated point."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Opioid agonists can cause which adverse effects due to their binding to receptor sites? (Select all that apply)","options":["Constipation","Respiratory depression","Hypertension","Increased heart rate"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Opioid agonists can cause constipation and respiratory depression because of their binding to receptor sites in the peripheral and central nervous system.  Why C, D wrong: Not the stated effects."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which of the following are commonly administered opioid agonists? (Select all that apply)","options":["Codeine","Morphine","Fentanyl","Naloxone","Oxycodone"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Commonly administered opioid agonists are codeine, morphine, fentanyl, hydrocodone, and oxycodone.  Why D wrong: Naloxone is an opioid antagonist, not an agonist."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which are the most common (m/c) opioid antagonists/antidotes?","options":["Naltrexone and naloxone","Morphine and codeine","Fentanyl and oxycodone","Hydrocodone and tramadol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The m/c opioid antagonists or antidotes are naltrexone and naloxone.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are opioid agonists."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Naloxone is the drug of choice for managing which condition?","options":["Opioid overdose (reversing respiratory depression and sedation)","Lithium toxicity","Cyanide poisoning","Iron intoxication"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Naloxone is the DOC for managing an opioid overdose by reversing respiratory depression and sedation and helps stabilize vital signs within seconds after administration.  Why B, C, D wrong: Managed by haemodialysis, amyl nitrite, and deferoxamine respectively."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What are the three most common pain assessment tools used by clinicians?","options":["Visual analog scale, numeric rating scale, and FACES scale","Glasgow coma scale, APGAR, and Braden scale","Wong-Baker, Braden, and Norton scale","Numeric, categorical, and thermal scales"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The three most common pain assessment tools are the visual analog scale, the numeric rating scale, and the FACES scale.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the three stated tools."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"When using the numeric rating scale, the client rates pain by choosing a number from:","options":["0 (no pain) to 10 (worst pain imaginable)","1 to 5","0 to 100","1 to 3"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: On the numeric rating scale, the client chooses a number from 0 (no pain) to 10 (worst pain imaginable).  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Why are nonopioids the first choice for managing mild pain?","options":["They decrease pain by inhibiting inflammation at the injury site","They block opioid receptors","They cause sedation","They increase serotonin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nonopioids are the first choice for mild pain because they decrease pain by inhibiting inflammation at the injury site.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which of the following are examples of non-opioids? (Select all that apply)","options":["Acetaminophen","NSAIDs","Salicylates such as aspirin","Morphine"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of non-opioids are acetaminophen, NSAIDs, and salicylates such as aspirin.  Why D wrong: Morphine is an opioid."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Which of the following are examples of NSAIDs? (Select all that apply)","options":["Ibuprofen","Naproxen","Diclofenac","Celecoxib","Morphine"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of NSAIDs include ibuprofen, naproxen, diclofenac, celecoxib, mefenamic acid, etoricoxib, indomethacin and aspirin.  Why E wrong: Morphine is an opioid, not an NSAID."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"What is the full form of NSAIDs?","options":["Non-Steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs","New Selective Anti-inflammatory Drugs","Non-Specific Analgesic Inhibitory Drugs","Neuro-Selective Anti-inflammatory Drugs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NSAIDs stands for Non-Steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs.  Why B, C, D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"Opioids work by blocking the release of neurotransmitters involved in transmitting which signals to the brain?","options":["Pain signals","Motor signals","Visual signals","Auditory signals"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Opioids work by blocking the release of neurotransmitters involved in transmitting pain signals to the brain.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated signals."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), mood stabilizers/anti-manic drugs…","question":"If a client is using a bronchodilator and a steroid inhaler, what is the correct sequence and timing?","options":["Use the bronchodilator first, wait 5 minutes, then use the steroid","Use the steroid first, then the bronchodilator immediately","Use both at the same time","Use the steroid first, wait 30 minutes, then bronchodilator"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If a client is using a bronchodilator and a steroid, have him use the bronchodilator first, wait 5 minutes, and then use the steroid (NORCET-5 Prelims 2023).  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the correct sequence/timing."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"When a client requires both a tube feeding and instillation of medication through the tube, which action should the nurse take?","options":["Give the tube feeding first, then the medication","Give the medication first to ensure the client receives it all","Mix the medication into the feeding formula","Hold the medication until after the next feeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If a tube feeding and a medication must both be given, the medication is given first to ensure the client receives all of it.  Why A wrong: Giving the feeding first risks the client not receiving the full medication dose.  Why C wrong: Medication should not be mixed into the feeding formula.  Why D wrong: There is no indication to delay the medication until the next feeding."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"During assessment before a tube feeding, the residual stomach contents measure greater than 100 mL. What is the nurse's most appropriate action?","options":["Continue the feeding as scheduled","Withhold the medication and next feeding and notify the physician","Increase the rate of the feeding","Reposition the client and immediately re-feed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If residual stomach contents exceed 100 mL, the nurse should withhold the medication and next feeding and notify the physician, because excessive residual may indicate intestinal obstruction or paralytic ileus.  Why A wrong: Continuing risks aspiration and worsening of an underlying problem.  Why C wrong: Increasing the rate would worsen the residual and risk aspiration.  Why D wrong: Re-feeding immediately ignores the abnormal residual finding."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"Excessive residual stomach contents before a tube feeding may indicate which condition(s)?","options":["Dehydration or hypovolemia","Intestinal obstruction or paralytic ileus","Gastric ulcer or gastritis","Hyperglycemia or ketoacidosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Excessive residual stomach contents may indicate intestinal obstruction or paralytic ileus.  Why A wrong: Residual volume reflects gastric emptying, not fluid volume status.  Why C wrong: Ulcer/gastritis are not the noted causes of high residual.  Why D wrong: These metabolic states are unrelated to gastric residual volume."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"What is the primary purpose of the Z-track intramuscular injection method?","options":["To speed up drug absorption","To prevent leakage of the drug into the subcutaneous tissue","To reduce the volume of medication needed","To allow rapid I.V. peak levels"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Z-track I.M. injection method prevents leakage of the drug into the subcutaneous tissue.  Why A wrong: Its purpose is preventing leakage/tissue irritation, not speeding absorption.  Why C wrong: It does not change the medication volume.  Why D wrong: Rapid peak I.V. levels describe the I.V. bolus method, not Z-track."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"The Z-track I.M. injection method is typically used to administer drugs that irritate and discolour subcutaneous tissue, primarily which preparation?","options":["Iron dextran","Regular insulin","Heparin","Tetanus toxoid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Z-track method is typically used for drugs that irritate and discolour subcutaneous tissue, primarily iron preparations such as iron dextran.  Why B wrong: Insulin is given subcutaneously, not by Z-track.  Why C wrong: Heparin is given subcutaneously, not by Z-track.  Why D wrong: Tetanus toxoid is given subcutaneously, not by Z-track."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"The Z-track method of I.M. injection may be especially useful in which group of clients?","options":["Neonates","Elderly clients who have decreased muscle mass","Athletes with large muscle mass","Clients on anticoagulant therapy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Z-track method for I.M. injection may be used in elderly clients who have decreased muscle mass.  Why A wrong: The source specifies elderly clients with decreased muscle mass, not neonates.  Why C wrong: Athletes with large muscle mass are not the specified group.  Why D wrong: Anticoagulant therapy is not the criterion mentioned for Z-track use."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"Which injection method allows rapid I.V. drug administration to quickly achieve peak levels in the bloodstream?","options":["Z-track I.M. method","Intradermal injection","I.V. bolus injection","Subcutaneous injection"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The I.V. bolus injection method allows rapid I.V. drug administration to quickly achieve peak levels in the bloodstream.  Why A wrong: Z-track is an I.M. technique, not a rapid I.V. route.  Why B wrong: Intradermal injection is slow and used for tiny volumes into the dermis.  Why D wrong: Subcutaneous absorption is slow, not rapid peak."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"How do transdermal patches deliver their medication, and where should they be applied? (NORCET-2022)","options":["By active transport; apply to hairy areas","By passive diffusion at a constant rate; apply to non-hairy areas","By pressurized injection; apply over a vein","By rapid bolus; apply to the abdomen only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Transdermal patches administer drugs by passive diffusion at a constant rate, and patches should be applied on a non-hairy area (NORCET-2022).  Why A wrong: Delivery is passive diffusion, and hairy areas impair patch adhesion/absorption.  Why C wrong: Patches are not injections.  Why D wrong: Patches are not bolus doses restricted to the abdomen."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"Parenteral drugs are administered through which routes?","options":["Only orally","Through the skin, subcutaneous tissue, muscle, or veins","Only rectally","Only through inhalation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Parenteral drugs are administered through the skin, subcutaneous tissue, muscle, or veins.  Why A wrong: Oral is an enteral route, not parenteral.  Why C wrong: Rectal is not a parenteral route.  Why D wrong: Inhalation is not listed among the parenteral routes."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"The term \"parenteral\" means:","options":["Within the intestines","Outside the intestines","Under the tongue","Through the rectum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Parenteral means outside the intestines.  Why A wrong: This is the opposite; \"enteral\" refers to within the intestines.  Why C wrong: Under the tongue describes the sublingual route.  Why D wrong: Through the rectum describes the rectal route."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"Intradermal medications are injected into which layer of the skin? (FAQs)","options":["The subcutaneous fat","The dermis, or outer layer of the skin","The muscle","A vein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Intradermal medications are injected into the dermis, or outer layer of the skin.  Why A wrong: The subcutaneous fat is the subcutaneous route, not intradermal.  Why C wrong: Muscle is the I.M. route.  Why D wrong: A vein is the I.V. route."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"The volume of a drug given intradermally is:","options":["Less than 0.5 mL","0.5 to 1 mL","1 to 3 mL","Up to 5 mL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The volume of a drug given intradermally is less than 0.5 mL.  Why B wrong: 0.5 to 1 mL is the subcutaneous volume.  Why C wrong: 0.5 to 3 mL is the intramuscular range, not intradermal.  Why D wrong: 5 mL exceeds any of these single-injection volumes."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"To administer an intradermal injection, which syringe should be used?","options":["A 3 mL syringe calibrated in 0.1 mL increments","A 1 mL syringe calibrated in 0.01 mL increments","A 5 mL syringe calibrated in 0.5 mL increments","A 10 mL syringe calibrated in 1 mL increments"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An intradermal injection uses a 1 mL syringe that's calibrated in 0.01 mL increments.  Why A wrong: Coarser 0.1 mL calibration is not specified for intradermal.  Why C wrong: A 5 mL syringe is too large for the tiny intradermal volume.  Why D wrong: A 10 mL syringe is far too large for intradermal dosing."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"The needle used for an intradermal injection should be which gauge and length?","options":["18 to 23G, 1\" to 3\" long","25 to 27G, 3/8\" to 5/8\" long","25 to 28G, 1/2\" to 5/8\" long","20 to 22G, 1\" long"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An intradermal injection uses a 25 to 27G needle that is 3/8\" to 5/8\" long.  Why A wrong: 18 to 23G, 1\" to 3\" is the intramuscular needle.  Why C wrong: 25 to 28G, 1/2\" to 5/8\" is the subcutaneous needle.  Why D wrong: These values are not specified for intradermal."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"Which drugs are commonly injected into the subcutaneous tissue?","options":["Iron dextran, digoxin, and furosemide","Heparin, insulin, and tetanus toxoid","Warfarin, lithium, and phenytoin","Dopamine, dobutamine, and adrenaline"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Drugs commonly injected into subcutaneous tissue include heparin, insulin, and tetanus toxoid.  Why A wrong: Iron dextran is given by Z-track I.M.; digoxin and furosemide are given I.V./oral.  Why C wrong: These are oral/other-route drugs, not the listed SC drugs.  Why D wrong: These vasoactive drugs are given I.V."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"What volume can be injected subcutaneously?","options":["Less than 0.5 mL","0.5 to 1 mL","0.5 to 3 mL","3 to 5 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A volume of 0.5 to 1 mL can be injected subcutaneously.  Why A wrong: Less than 0.5 mL is the intradermal volume.  Why C wrong: 0.5 to 3 mL is the intramuscular volume.  Why D wrong: 3 to 5 mL exceeds the subcutaneous limit."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"The needle used for a subcutaneous injection should be which gauge and length?","options":["25 to 28G and 1/2\" to 5/8\" long","18 to 23G and 1\" to 3\" long","25 to 27G and 3/8\" to 5/8\" long","20 to 22G and 1\" to 2\" long"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Needles used for a subcutaneous injection are 25 to 28G and 1/2\" to 5/8\" long.  Why B wrong: 18 to 23G, 1\" to 3\" is the intramuscular needle.  Why C wrong: 25 to 27G, 3/8\" to 5/8\" is the intradermal needle.  Why D wrong: These values are not specified for subcutaneous injections."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"Which of the following are commonly used sites for subcutaneous injection? (Select all that apply)","options":["Lateral upper arm","Thigh","Abdomen","Ventrogluteal region","Upper back"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Common subcutaneous injection sites include the lateral upper arm, thigh, abdomen, and upper back.  Why D wrong: The ventrogluteal site is used for intramuscular injections, not subcutaneous."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"When giving a subcutaneous injection to a thin client, the nurse should:","options":["Not pinch the skin and insert at a 90-degree angle","Pinch the skin and insert the needle at a 45-degree angle","Spread the skin taut and insert at a 15-degree angle","Pinch the skin and insert at a 10-degree angle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For a thin client, pinch the skin and insert the needle at a 45-degree angle.  Why A wrong: Not pinching and 90-degree insertion is used for an obese client.  Why C wrong: A 15-degree angle is not used for subcutaneous injection.  Why D wrong: A 10-degree angle is not the specified technique."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"When giving a subcutaneous injection to an obese client, the nurse should:","options":["Pinch the skin and insert at a 45-degree angle","Not pinch the skin and insert the needle into the fatty tissue at a 90-degree angle","Spread the skin and insert at a 15-degree angle","Aspirate before injecting into muscle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For an obese client, do not pinch the skin but insert the needle into the fatty tissue at a 90-degree angle.  Why A wrong: Pinching and 45-degree is the technique for a thin client.  Why C wrong: A 15-degree angle is not used for subcutaneous injection.  Why D wrong: Subcutaneous, not intramuscular, injection is being described here."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"After giving an injection of heparin or insulin, the nurse should:","options":["Massage the site vigorously","Not massage the skin","Apply a heating pad","Aspirate for blood return"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Do not massage the skin after giving an injection of heparin or insulin.  Why A wrong: Massaging can cause bruising/tissue damage and altered absorption; it is contraindicated.  Why C wrong: A heating pad is not indicated after these injections.  Why D wrong: Aspiration is not recommended for subcutaneous heparin/insulin, and massaging is what's addressed here."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"A volume ranging from 0.5 to 3 mL may be given intramuscularly, except in a child, who should receive a total of no more than:","options":["0.5 mL","1 mL","2 mL","3 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A volume ranging from 0.5 to 3 mL may be given intramuscularly except to a child, who should only receive a total of 1 mL.  Why A wrong: 0.5 mL is not the stated maximum for a child.  Why C wrong: 2 mL exceeds the stated pediatric limit of 1 mL.  Why D wrong: 3 mL is the adult maximum, not the pediatric limit."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"Which site is considered the safest site for intramuscular injections? (FAQs in Nursing Exams)","options":["Dorsogluteal site","Deltoid site","Ventrogluteal site","Vastus lateralis site"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The ventrogluteal site is the safest site for intramuscular injections.  Why A wrong: The dorsogluteal site risks sciatic nerve injury and is not the safest.  Why B wrong: The deltoid is used for small volumes but is not identified as the safest.  Why D wrong: The vastus lateralis is a common site but not identified as the safest here."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"At what angle should intramuscular injections be inserted?","options":["15-degree angle","45-degree angle","90-degree angle","30-degree angle"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Intramuscular injections should be inserted with the needle at a 90-degree angle.  Why A wrong: 15 degrees is associated with intradermal-type angles, not I.M.  Why B wrong: 45 degrees is used for subcutaneous injections in thin clients.  Why D wrong: 30 degrees is not the I.M. angle."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"After administering an intramuscular injection, the nurse should:","options":["Rub the site vigorously","Apply pressure to the site; it should not be rubbed","Immediately apply ice","Leave the site uncovered and elevate the limb"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After an I.M. injection, pressure should be applied to the site; it should not be rubbed.  Why A wrong: Rubbing is specifically to be avoided.  Why C wrong: Applying ice is not the specified action.  Why D wrong: Elevating the limb is not the specified post-injection action."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"Intramuscular injections are given with a needle of which gauge and length?","options":["25 to 27G, 3/8\" to 5/8\" long","25 to 28G, 1/2\" to 5/8\" long","18 to 23G, 1\" to 3\" long","20G, less than 1/2\" long"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Intramuscular injections are given with an 18-23G needle that is 1\" to 3\" long.  Why A wrong: 25 to 27G, 3/8\" to 5/8\" is the intradermal needle.  Why B wrong: 25 to 28G, 1/2\" to 5/8\" is the subcutaneous needle.  Why D wrong: A 20G needle less than 1/2\" is not the specified I.M. needle."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"Selection of a venipuncture device and site for peripheral I.V. catheter insertion depends on all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["The type of solution to be used","The frequency and duration of infusion","The patency and location of accessible veins","The client's blood type"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Selection depends on the type of solution, frequency/duration of infusion, patency/location of accessible veins, the client's age/size/condition, and (when possible) the client's preference; blood type is not a listed factor.  Why A wrong: The type of solution is a stated factor.  Why B wrong: Frequency and duration of infusion is a stated factor.  Why C wrong: Patency and location of accessible veins is a stated factor."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"When possible, for peripheral I.V. catheter insertion the nurse should choose a vein in the:","options":["Dominant arm or hand","Non-dominant arm or hand","Lower extremity","Antecubital fossa only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If possible, choose a vein in the non-dominant arm or hand.  Why A wrong: The dominant arm is avoided when possible to preserve the client's function.  Why C wrong: The lower extremity is not the preferred choice stated here.  Why D wrong: The antecubital fossa is not specified as the preferred site."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"The preferred venipuncture sites for peripheral I.V. catheter insertion are the cephalic and basilic veins in the lower arm and:","options":["The veins in the dorsum of the hand","The femoral vein","The external jugular vein","The veins of the foot"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The preferred sites are the cephalic and basilic veins in the lower arm and the veins in the dorsum of the hand.  Why B wrong: The femoral vein is a central access site, not a preferred peripheral I.V. site.  Why C wrong: The external jugular is not a preferred peripheral catheter site here.  Why D wrong: Foot veins are not among the preferred sites listed."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"How often are gauze I.V. dressings typically changed?","options":["Every 24 hours","Every 48 hours or when the dressing becomes wet, soiled, or non-occlusive","Every 72 hours","Every 96 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Gauze I.V. dressings are typically changed every 48 hours or when the dressing becomes wet, soiled, or non-occlusive.  Why A wrong: 24 hours applies to changing I.V. solution, not the gauze dressing.  Why C wrong: 72 hours applies to I.V. tubing changes.  Why D wrong: 96 hours is not the stated interval."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"According to standard practice, I.V. tubing is changed every ___ and I.V. solution is changed every ___ (or as needed/per facility policy). (FAQ's)","options":["24 hours; 48 hours","72 hours; 24 hours","48 hours; 72 hours","12 hours; 24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: I.V. tubing is changed every 72 hours (or per facility policy), and I.V. solution is changed every 24 hours or as needed.  Why A wrong: These values are reversed and incorrect.  Why C wrong: These intervals are swapped/incorrect.  Why D wrong: Neither 12 nor these pairings match the source."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"When a transparent semi-permeable dressing is used, how often should the I.V. insertion site be assessed?","options":["Every hour","Every 2 hours","Every 4 hours","Every 8 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The I.V. insertion site should be assessed every 2 hours if a transparent semi-permeable dressing is used.  Why A wrong: Hourly assessment is not the stated interval.  Why C wrong: Every 4 hours is not the stated interval.  Why D wrong: Every 8 hours is not the stated interval."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"When a transparent semi-permeable dressing is NOT used, how often should the I.V. site be assessed and rotated?","options":["Assessed with every dressing change and rotated every 72 hours","Assessed every hour and rotated daily","Assessed weekly and rotated monthly","Assessed every 12 hours and rotated every 48 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Otherwise, the site is assessed with every dressing change and should be rotated every 72 hours.  Why B wrong: Hourly/daily is not the stated schedule.  Why C wrong: Weekly/monthly intervals are far too long.  Why D wrong: 12-hour/48-hour intervals are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"To prevent errors, when should the prescribed I.V. flow rate be checked?","options":["Only at the start of the shift","Before each solution change","Once per day","Only when the client complains"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Check the prescribed I.V. flow rate before each solution change to prevent errors.  Why A wrong: Once per shift is insufficient per the source.  Why C wrong: Once per day is insufficient.  Why D wrong: Waiting for a complaint is not the standard."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"In total parenteral nutrition (TPN), the highly concentrated, hypertonic nutrient solution is administered by an infusion pump through:","options":["A peripheral vein in the hand","A large central vein","A subcutaneous port","An intramuscular route"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In TPN, a highly concentrated, hypertonic nutrient solution is administered by an infusion pump through a large central vein.  Why A wrong: A small peripheral vein cannot tolerate the hypertonic TPN solution.  Why C wrong: TPN is not given subcutaneously.  Why D wrong: TPN is not an intramuscular therapy."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"Total parenteral nutrition provides which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Calories","Restores nitrogen balance","Essential fluids, vitamins, electrolytes, and minerals","Trace elements","Red blood cells"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: TPN provides calories, restores nitrogen balance, and replaces essential fluids, vitamins, electrolytes, minerals, and trace elements.  Why E wrong: TPN does not provide red blood cells; that requires a blood transfusion."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"Hyperglycaemia can occur during TPN administration as a result of which mechanism?","options":["Dextrose infusing too rapidly","Insufficient protein content","Excess electrolyte replacement","Too little lipid emulsion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperglycaemia can occur during TPN as a result of dextrose infusing too rapidly, and may require an adjustment in the client's insulin dosage.  Why B wrong: Protein content is not the cited cause of hyperglycemia.  Why C wrong: Electrolyte replacement is not the cause of hyperglycemia.  Why D wrong: Lipid content is not the cause of hyperglycemia here."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"In a client receiving TPN, how often should glucose levels be monitored?","options":["Every 6 hours initially, then once a day","Every hour continuously","Once a week","Only if symptoms develop"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor glucose levels every 6 hours initially, then once a day.  Why B wrong: Hourly monitoring is not the stated schedule.  Why C wrong: Weekly monitoring is too infrequent.  Why D wrong: Monitoring is routine, not only symptom-driven."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"Clients receiving TPN should be carefully monitored to detect early signs of which complications? (Select all that apply)","options":["Infection","Metabolic problems","Heart failure","Pulmonary edema","Allergic reactions"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor for early signs of complications such as infection, metabolic problems, heart failure, pulmonary edema, or allergic reactions.  Why A–E: All five are listed as potential complications to detect early."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"Which filter should be used when administering total parenteral nutrition containing an I.V. fat emulsion?","options":["A 0.2-micron filter","A 1.2-micron filter","A 5-micron filter","No filter is required"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Use a 1.2-micron filter when administering TPN containing an I.V. fat emulsion.  Why A wrong: The 0.2-micron filter is used for solutions that do NOT contain a fat emulsion.  Why C wrong: A 5-micron filter is not specified for TPN.  Why D wrong: A filter is required for TPN."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"Which filter should be used when administering a total parenteral nutrition solution that does NOT contain an I.V. fat emulsion?","options":["A 1.2-micron filter","A 0.2-micron filter","A 1.0-micron filter","A 5-micron filter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Use a 0.2-micron filter when administering a TPN solution that does not contain an I.V. fat emulsion.  Why A wrong: The 1.2-micron filter is used for solutions that DO contain a fat emulsion.  Why C wrong: A 1.0-micron filter is not specified.  Why D wrong: A 5-micron filter is not specified."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"Which statement regarding adding medications to a TPN solution is correct?","options":["Add medications freely as needed","Never add medications to a TPN solution container once it is actively infusing","Add only electrolytes once infusing","Add medications only during the last hour of infusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Never add medications to a total parenteral nutrition solution container once it is actively infusing.  Why A wrong: Adding medications freely is unsafe and contraindicated.  Why C wrong: No medications, including electrolytes, should be added once actively infusing.  Why D wrong: There is no \"last hour\" exception; the rule is never once infusing."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"Once the administration set is attached, within what time frame should a TPN solution be infused or discarded?","options":["12 hours","24 hours","48 hours","72 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Infuse or discard any TPN solution within 24 hours once the administration set is attached.  Why A wrong: 12 hours is not the stated time frame.  Why C wrong: 48 hours exceeds the stated limit.  Why D wrong: 72 hours exceeds the stated limit."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"When discontinuing TPN, why should the infusion be decreased slowly, depending on the client's current glucose intake?","options":["To prevent air embolism","To minimize the risk of hyper-insulinemia and resulting hypoglycaemia","To prevent fluid overload","To avoid catheter occlusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When discontinuing TPN, decrease the infusion slowly depending on current glucose intake; this minimizes the risk of hyper-insulinemia and resulting hypoglycaemia.  Why A wrong: The stated reason is hypoglycemia prevention, not air embolism.  Why C wrong: Fluid overload is not the reason given for tapering.  Why D wrong: Catheter occlusion is not the reason for slow discontinuation."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"Refeeding syndrome can occur in extremely malnourished clients receiving TPN and is characterized by a rapid drop in which serum levels? (Select all that apply)","options":["Potassium","Magnesium","Phosphorus","Sodium","Calcium"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Refeeding syndrome includes a rapid drop in potassium, magnesium, and phosphorus levels.  Why D wrong: Sodium is not among the listed dropping electrolytes.  Why E wrong: Calcium is not among the listed dropping electrolytes."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"A peripherally inserted central catheter (PICC) can be used for TPN lasting:","options":["Less than 1 week","1 month or less","3 months or more","Exactly 6 weeks only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A peripherally inserted central catheter can be used for TPN lasting 3 months or more.  Why A wrong: Less than 1 week is not the stated duration for a PICC.  Why B wrong: 1 month or less understates the PICC's stated use.  Why D wrong: The source says 3 months or more, not exactly 6 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"A PICC used for TPN can be inserted into which veins, with the tip resting in which vessel?","options":["Femoral vein; inferior vena cava","Cephalic or basilic vein; superior vena cava","Subclavian vein; right atrium","Jugular vein; pulmonary artery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A PICC for TPN can be inserted into the cephalic or basilic vein and threaded so the tip lies in the superior vena cava.  Why A wrong: The femoral vein and inferior vena cava are not the stated route/position.  Why C wrong: The subclavian/right atrium is not the described PICC path.  Why D wrong: The jugular/pulmonary artery is not the described path."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"Clients receiving TPN should ideally gain how much weight per week?","options":["1 to 2 lb (0.5 to 1 kg)/week","3 to 4 lb (1.5 to 2 kg)/week","0.25 lb (0.1 kg)/week","5 lb (2.5 kg)/week"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clients receiving TPN ideally should gain 1 to 2 lb (0.5 to 1 kg) per week.  Why B wrong: 3–4 lb/week exceeds the ideal target.  Why C wrong: 0.25 lb/week is below the ideal target.  Why D wrong: 5 lb/week is excessive and suggests fluid retention."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"In a client on TPN, weight gain greater than which amount per day indicates fluid retention?","options":["1 lb (0.5 kg)/day","0.25 lb (0.1 kg)/day","3 lb (1.5 kg)/day","5 lb (2.5 kg)/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Weight gain greater than 1 lb (0.5 kg)/day indicates fluid retention.  Why B wrong: 0.25 lb/day is below the threshold for fluid retention.  Why C wrong: 3 lb/day is not the stated threshold value.  Why D wrong: 5 lb/day is not the stated threshold value."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"How is renal function monitored in a client receiving TPN, and what may increases indicate?","options":["By monitoring BUN and creatinine; increases may indicate excess amino acid intake","By monitoring glucose; increases indicate infection","By monitoring liver enzymes; increases indicate fat overload","By monitoring hemoglobin; increases indicate dehydration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor renal function by monitoring blood urea nitrogen and creatinine levels; increases may indicate excess amino acid intake.  Why B wrong: Glucose monitoring detects hyperglycemia, not renal function.  Why C wrong: Liver enzymes are not the stated renal marker here.  Why D wrong: Hemoglobin does not assess renal function in this context."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"In a client receiving warfarin therapy, bleeding precautions should be initiated if the PT is longer than ___ and the INR is greater than ___.","options":["PT > 30 sec; INR > 3","PT > 15 sec; INR > 1","PT > 60 sec; INR > 5","PT > 20 sec; INR > 2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If the PT is longer than 30 sec and the INR is greater than 3 in a client on warfarin therapy, bleeding precautions should be initiated.  Why B wrong: PT > 15 sec / INR > 1 are not the stated thresholds.  Why C wrong: PT > 60 sec / INR > 5 are not the stated thresholds.  Why D wrong: PT > 20 sec / INR > 2 are not the stated thresholds."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"The mechanism of action of digoxin includes which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Positive inotropic effect (increases myocardial contraction)","Negative chronotropic effect (decreases heart rate)","Negative dromotropic effect (decreases impulse conduction)","Positive chronotropic effect (increases heart rate)","Negative inotropic effect (decreases contraction)"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Digoxin has a positive inotropic effect (increases myocardial contraction), a negative chronotropic effect (decreases heart rate), and a negative dromotropic effect (decreases impulse conduction).  Why D wrong: Digoxin decreases heart rate (negative chronotropic), not increases it.  Why E wrong: Digoxin increases contraction (positive inotropic), not decreases it."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"A positive inotropic effect of digoxin refers to:","options":["An increase in myocardial contraction","A decrease in heart rate","A decrease in impulse conduction","An increase in heart rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The positive inotropic effect of digoxin increases myocardial contraction.  Why B wrong: Decreased heart rate is the negative chronotropic effect.  Why C wrong: Decreased impulse conduction is the negative dromotropic effect.  Why D wrong: Digoxin decreases, not increases, heart rate."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"The negative chronotropic effect of digoxin refers to:","options":["Increased myocardial contraction","Decreased heart rate","Decreased impulse conduction","Increased impulse conduction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The negative chronotropic effect of digoxin decreases heart rate.  Why A wrong: Increased contraction is the positive inotropic effect.  Why C wrong: Decreased conduction is the negative dromotropic effect.  Why D wrong: Digoxin decreases, not increases, impulse conduction."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"The negative dromotropic effect of digoxin refers to:","options":["Increased myocardial contraction","Decreased heart rate","Decreased impulse conduction","Increased contractility"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The negative dromotropic effect of digoxin decreases impulse conduction.  Why A wrong: Increased contraction is the positive inotropic effect.  Why B wrong: Decreased heart rate is the negative chronotropic effect.  Why D wrong: Increased contractility is the opposite of a dromotropic effect."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Tube feeding/medication administration, gastric residual…","question":"Why should IV furosemide be administered slowly?","options":["Because rapid injection can cause hyperglycemia","Because hearing loss can occur if it is injected rapidly","Because it precipitates in the vein","Because it causes immediate hypertension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IV furosemide should be administered slowly because hearing loss (ototoxicity) can occur if it is injected rapidly.  Why A wrong: Rapid injection is associated with ototoxicity, not hyperglycemia, here.  Why C wrong: Precipitation is not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: Furosemide is a diuretic; the concern is ototoxicity, not hypertension."}];
